window.HIST=window.HIST||{claims:[],sources:[],coverage:[],people:[],events:[],organizations:[],roles:[]};
window.HIST.claims.push(...[{"id":"C2004","text":"Mukhlis, citing Abdullah (2004), reports that H. Abdurrahman Idris left for Mecca in 1927 aged 17, lived/studied there for 13 years, returned to Bima in 1941 and later held the BHS Imam/chair role.","period":"1927–1947; Bima/Mecca","type":"Retrospective individual biography","source_ids":["S1078","S1081"],"confidence":"Moderate that the dissertation reports this life sequence; no travel, study or appointment record accessed.","basis":"The roster, dates and educational account run through Abdullah (2004, pp.169, 184–195); this is one source lineage, not two confirmations. “Returned by Sultan’s call” is also reported by Mukhlis, not documented here by a letter.","next":"Inspect Abdullah pp.184–195 and seek Mecca student/residence records, travel lists, appointment instruments and school employment records.","year":1927},{"id":"C2005","text":"Mukhlis, citing Abdullah (2004), reports that H. Ishaq Abdul Qadir went to Mecca in 1926, stayed seven years and returned to Bima in 1933; he is listed as BHS Penghulu in 1947.","period":"1926–1947; Bima/Mecca","type":"Retrospective individual biography and office list","source_ids":["S1078","S1081"],"confidence":"Moderate that the cited secondary chain reports these details; direct travel/education/appointment records not accessed.","basis":"Mutawalli separately spells the 1945 delegate’s name “Ishak,” Mukhlis lists “Ishaq,” and the court report uses “Ishaq” for its 1964 first chair. The name match suggests but does not by itself prove all references identify one person.","next":"Compare the 1945 representation letter, 1947 roster, 1964 ministerial decision, signatures and local personnel records.","year":1926},{"id":"C2006","text":"Mukhlis, citing Abdullah (2004), reports that H. Yasin H. Nur went to Mecca in 1926, studied/resided there ten years, returned in 1936 and was listed as BHS Lebedala in 1947.","period":"1926–1947; Bima/Mecca","type":"Retrospective individual biography and office list","source_ids":["S1078","S1081"],"confidence":"Moderate that this is the secondary account; direct biographical and office records not accessed.","basis":"The BHS roster and travel chronology share the Abdullah citation lineage; exact full name, school and return date are unconfirmed by contemporaneous records.","next":"Inspect Abdullah’s cited biography and seek Mecca student lists, Bima mosque/madrasah records and the Beslit No.42 original.","year":1926},{"id":"C2007","text":"Mukhlis, citing Abdullah (2004), reports that H. Usman Abidin went to Mecca in 1927, stayed five years, returned in 1932 and was listed as BHS Khatib Tua in 1947.","period":"1927–1947; Bima/Mecca","type":"Retrospective individual biography and office list","source_ids":["S1078","S1081"],"confidence":"Moderate that the cited secondary account reports these details; personal travel and appointment records not accessed.","basis":"The same dissertation also names Usman Abidin as secretary of a pre-BHS Pegawai Kantor Urusan Agama, via Mutawalli’s distinct secondary narrative; do not assume office continuity without rosters or appointment records.","next":"Find the 1946–47 religious-office member lists, Mecca student records, Darul Tarbiyah/BHS files and compare signature variants.","year":1927},{"id":"C2008","text":"Mukhlis, citing Abdullah (2004), reports that H. Abdul Malik Sulaiman left for Mecca in 1929, stayed twelve years and returned in 1941; the 1947 roster calls him Khatib Toi.","period":"1929–1947; Bima/Mecca","type":"Retrospective individual biography and office list","source_ids":["S1078","S1081"],"confidence":"Moderate that the dissertation reports the itinerary and appointment; original biographical and appointment records not accessed.","basis":"The biography is mediated through Abdullah (2004); spelling and office title should be checked against the original decree.","next":"Inspect Abdullah pp.184–195 and seek Mecca registers, Sultan correspondence, BHS original and later religious-office records.","year":1929},{"id":"C2009","text":"Mukhlis says all seven named 1947 BHS members except Haji Abubakar Husain had studied/resided in Mecca for years, but his brief itinerary on pp.141–142 gives dates only for Idris, Ishaq, Yasin, Usman and Abdul Malik, not Saleh Abdul Qadir.","period":"Before 1947; Bima/Mecca","type":"Retrospective biographical coverage gap","source_ids":["S1078","S1081"],"confidence":"High that the dissertation’s group statement and individual list have this asymmetry; no direct travel records read.","basis":"The omission does not mean Saleh did not study in Mecca; it means this passage does not give his dates. Abubakar is the only explicit exception in the group statement, but his broader biography remains unverified.","next":"Read Abdullah pp.184–195 for Saleh and Abubakar; seek individual biographical records, letters, student/residency lists and mosque records.","year":1947},{"id":"C2010","text":"The Bima Religious Court’s 2024 fourth strategic-plan review states that the court was formally established on 16 March 1964 under Minister of Religion Decision B/IV/2/9/6550 dated 9 December 1964; its 2023 LKjIP repeats the same wording. The establishment date precedes the cited decision, and the repeated wording is institutional reuse rather than an independent confirmation.","period":"1964; Bima","type":"Official institutional date contradiction and source dependence","source_ids":["S1083","S1084","S1079"],"confidence":"High that the two official documents print this chronology and the annual report separately identifies the 9 December decision as Ishaq Abdul Qadir’s first-chair appointment; the actual legal dates remain unresolved.","basis":"The 2023 report and 2024 review are issued by the same institution and reproduce the same passage. The 2024 annual activity report only states that the 9 December decision appointed the first chair; none of the documents attaches the original decision. Do not silently repair “16 March 1964” to another date.","next":"Read the original decision and its effective-date clause; locate appointment, court-opening, handover and first-sitting records. Determine whether 16 March is a separate event date, transcription error or another instrument.","year":1964},{"id":"C2011","text":"ANRI’s official NU inventory describes item 2271 as correspondence concerning the takeover of Yayasan Islam Bima, dated 22 January and 10 August 1970, in retained-copy form, two sheets. The inventory entry does not identify actors or the outcome.","period":"1970; Bima","type":"Finding-aid metadata only","source_ids":["S1085"],"confidence":"High that the catalogue contains this description; no confidence assigned to the event’s underlying facts.","basis":"The item itself has not been retrieved; “pengambilalihan” is the catalogue’s title, not proof that a takeover occurred or succeeded.","next":"Request ANRI item 2271 and identify the letters’ senders, recipients, content, attachments, and archival context.","year":1970},{"id":"C2012","text":"Sila’s 2012 article states that Badan Hukum Syara changed its name to Yayasan Islam Bima in 1963.","period":"1963; Bima","type":"Retrospective institutional-history statement","source_ids":["S1088"],"confidence":"High that the article states this; low-to-moderate as a verified legal event.","basis":"No 1963 charter, board minutes, registration or decree is cited in the article; later accounts place YASIM’s formation or legal establishment in 1968 or the transition in 1969.","next":"Locate 1963 YASIM/BHS board records, statutes, tax/registration files, and compare with 1968–69 instruments.","year":1963},{"id":"C2013","text":"Sila reports 75 schools inherited from the BHS period and 28 established by YASIM since 1968, citing a YASIM 2011 report.","period":"1931–2011; Bima","type":"Retrospective count via cited institutional report","source_ids":["S1088","S1089"],"confidence":"High that the 2012 article prints the count; low-to-moderate for the count and date range.","basis":"The 2011 report has not been located; no school-by-school list, founding dates, definitions, or surviving/closed-school treatment is available.","next":"Obtain the cited YASIM report and reconcile counts against individual school registers and founding decrees.","year":1931},{"id":"C2014","text":"Abdillah reports a Bupati decision dated 16 March 1968 creating/establishing YASIM and transferring to it BHS’s non-judicial education, da’wah and waqf functions; he gives inconsistent transcriptions of the decision number in different passages.","period":"16 March 1968; Bima","type":"Retrospective account quoting an uninspected decree","source_ids":["S1087","S1078","S1066"],"confidence":"Moderate that a 1968 decision is reported; low-to-moderate for the exact text and legal effect.","basis":"Variants include I/i/A-16/SKPS/21 in Mukhlis; Pem./I/1/A.16/SPKS/21 and Pem. 1/1/A.16/SKPS/21 in Abdillah. Original decision and registry entry not read. S1066 is by Adlin Sila and shares the local-source chain with S1088.","next":"Retrieve the decree original, Bupati register, board appointment sheet, and any property/school-transfer schedule; compare every number and abbreviation.","year":1968},{"id":"C2015","text":"Retno’s 2017 article gives 1969 as the year Mahkamatus Syar’iyyah became YASIM; its note cites Alan Malingi’s 2015 unpublished encyclopedia entry.","period":"1969; Bima","type":"Secondary statement through unpublished cited source","source_ids":["S1090","S1096"],"confidence":"High that Retno reports the date; low as an established transition date.","basis":"The cited entry is unavailable. The article’s organization chart relies on Adlin Sila 2014, so this is not independent institutional documentation.","next":"Locate Alan Malingi’s cited entry and the 1969 YASIM charter/board minutes; compare with the 1963 and 1968 claims.","year":1969},{"id":"C2016","text":"Biographical sources report that Tuan Guru H.M. Said Amin was born on 1 January 1936 in Tawali, Wera, Bima, to H.M. Amin Hasan and Hj. Thaifah Sanghaji, and was the third of eight siblings.","period":"1936; Tawali/Wera, Bima","type":"Retrospective family/institutional biography","source_ids":["S1091","S1092","S995","S1097"],"confidence":"Moderate; the accounts repeat the same biography and are institutionally or family linked.","basis":"No civil birth entry, contemporaneous household register, or independently sourced genealogy inspected; the sources do not constitute four independent witnesses.","next":"Seek civil/Islamic birth records, family register, school entry file, and interviews with separately identified siblings or relatives.","year":1936},{"id":"C2017","text":"The reported school chronology conflicts: Mutawali et al. say Said Amin began SR in Tawali in 1943 and describe a 1947 failed attempt to continue in Mecca followed by study in Bima; Fathurrahman’s 2023 memorial instead says he completed SDN 1 Bima in 1947 and then continued in Mecca. The 2021 book has a further itinerary detail sequence.","period":"1943–1949; Bima/Mecca","type":"Biography chronology and source discrepancy","source_ids":["S1091","S1097","S995"],"confidence":"High that the accounts differ; low-to-moderate for exact enrollment and travel dates.","basis":"The timeline depends on retrospective biography and recollection; no school registers, passport, pilgrimage/ship list or Mecca enrollment record read.","next":"Compare SR/SDN and Darul Ulum/Al-Falah registers, travel documents, family letters, and the underlying interview notes.","year":1943},{"id":"C2018","text":"The 2019 Wawasan article reports Said Amin studied at Mecca’s Darul Ulum from 1949, then Al-Falah from 1952 and 1954, graduated in 1956, taught hadith for a year, and returned to Bima in 1957; another memorial compresses the early travel and enrollment into a different sequence.","period":"1948–1957; Mecca/Bima","type":"Retrospective educational biography","source_ids":["S1091","S1097","S995"],"confidence":"Moderate that the biography reports this sequence; low-to-moderate for exact dates and institutions.","basis":"The articles draw on the subject’s retrospective account and later institutional biographies; school records not inspected. Said Amin’s claimed 1956 plan to study English in Alexandria was reportedly canceled over citizenship status, also unverified.","next":"Seek Saudi school enrollment/graduation records, Egyptian university application correspondence, Indonesian citizenship/travel records, and PGAP appointment file.","year":1948},{"id":"C2019","text":"Retrospective biographies report Said Amin’s teaching and legal career as PGAP/PGA/PGAN teacher, MAAIN head, IAIN Sunan Ampel Syariah study (BA, 1976), and Bima Religious Court judge (1984–1996), but differ on whether MAAIN leadership began in 1967 or 1968.","period":"1957–1996; Bima/Surabaya","type":"Reported professional career","source_ids":["S1091","S1097","S995"],"confidence":"Moderate for the broad career sequence; lower for exact appointment dates.","basis":"No education transcripts, civil-service appointment files, school payrolls, or judicial appointment/retirement orders reviewed. The 1967/1968 start difference is preserved.","next":"Request personnel files from the Ministry of Religious Affairs, school archives, IAIN records, and court appointment registers.","year":1957},{"id":"C2020","text":"Mutawali et al. and Fathurrahman report Said Amin was a Bima DPRD-GR member representing Golongan Islam from 1966 to 1971.","period":"1966–1971; Bima","type":"Retrospective office/legislative biography","source_ids":["S1091","S1097","S995"],"confidence":"Moderate that later biographies report the term; low-to-moderate until a roster is found.","basis":"No DPRD-GR appointment decree, member roster, meeting attendance, speech, or vote record reviewed. “Golongan Islam” does not identify a party membership and no party affiliation is inferred.","next":"Locate Bima DPRD-GR member list, appointment and replacement decrees, council minutes, and election/organizational records.","year":1966},{"id":"C2021","text":"Later biographies report Said Amin as Ketua III of NU’s Bima branch for 1966–1980 and as a YASIM board member from 1968; the latter is consistent with his name appearing as member no. 8 in the 1968 board list quoted by Mukhlis.","period":"1966–1980; Bima","type":"Reported organizational offices and board membership","source_ids":["S1091","S1092","S1097","S1078","S995"],"confidence":"Moderate that these sources report the roles; low-to-moderate for exact term boundaries.","basis":"The 1968 list is cited by Mukhlis through Abdul Gani Abdullah, not an original appointment decree. Later biographies do not establish that Said Amin led or initiated the 1967 liquidation.","next":"Find NU branch conference minutes, YASIM board register, founding decree attachment, and individual appointment/attendance records.","year":1966},{"id":"C2022","text":"Mukhlis reports that some Bima residents, allegedly supported by the PKI, pressed for BHS’s dissolution; the dissertation uses the hedging word konon and attributes the episode through an older source.","period":"1967; Bima","type":"Hedged allegation in later historical account","source_ids":["S1078","S1081"],"confidence":"High that the dissertation reports the allegation; low for the allegation as an established fact.","basis":"No names, PKI document, meeting evidence, petition, or original source passage has been inspected. The hedged wording must remain visible; it is not evidence of PKI sponsorship.","next":"Inspect Abdullah’s cited text and any DPRD, BHS, party, police or district files identifying petitioners and their stated affiliations.","year":1967},{"id":"C2023","text":"Mukhlis reports DPRD-GR Bima Resolution No.24/DPR/1967, dated 20 June 1967, directing BHS liquidation into a Yayasan Islam/Majelis Islam and asking the regent to form the organization.","period":"20 June 1967; Bima","type":"Later report of an official resolution","source_ids":["S1078","S1081"],"confidence":"Moderate that the number, date and reported contents are accurately reproduced; no independent primary verification.","basis":"The resolution and meeting minutes were not accessed. The report cites Abdullah; later repetition does not independently confirm the wording or implementation.","next":"Retrieve DPRD-GR resolution, council minutes/roll call, Bupati receipt, and implementation correspondence.","year":1967},{"id":"C2024","text":"Mukhlis reports Regent Soeharmadji’s YASIM formation decree No. I/i/A-16/SKPS/21 dated 16 March 1968 and lists Said Amin among the board members as number 8.","period":"16 March 1968; Bima","type":"Later report of decree and roster","source_ids":["S1078","S1081","S1091","S1097"],"confidence":"Moderate that the dissertation gives this date, number and roster; low-to-moderate until the decree is read.","basis":"The passage cites Abdullah (2004); notation differs in Abdillah 2022. Membership is not evidence that Said Amin proposed, voted for, chaired, or controlled the 1967 liquidation.","next":"Obtain decree and original attached board list, compare with Bupati’s register and YASIM minute book.","year":1968},{"id":"C2025","text":"Abdillah describes YASIM as inheriting BHS’s non-judicial education, da’wah and waqf functions, while judicial work followed a separate court transition; the 2022 thesis derives the transfer claim from earlier literature and school-history writing.","period":"1954–1969; Bima","type":"Retrospective institutional-functional account","source_ids":["S1087","S1078","S1066","S1079"],"confidence":"Moderate that later scholarship distinguishes the function sets; low-to-moderate on the exact transfer dates and legal mechanism.","basis":"No signed transfer list, assets register, school title/management change, waqf deed, or YASIM legal charter inspected. Keep court succession and social/educational organization as separate institutional events.","next":"Find judicial handover records, YASIM charter/minutes, waqf ledgers, school-by-school management files and the BHS assets inventory.","year":1954},{"id":"C2026","text":"Mutawali’s 2013 study reports Bima DPRD-GR Resolution No.16/Res/1969, decided 3 July 1969, as expressing a desire for Bima to have Daerah Istimewa status implementing Islamic law.","period":"3 July 1969; Bima","type":"Later report of local council resolution","source_ids":["S1076"],"confidence":"Low-to-moderate; one retrospective study’s text is the only source located in this pass.","basis":"The original resolution, motion, minutes, speakers, attendance and vote were not found. It does not establish implementation or a direct role by Said Amin.","next":"Locate DPRD-GR archive, annual council minutes, Bima government records, and any submitted legal proposal or central-government response.","year":1969},{"id":"C2027","text":"ANRI’s 1970 item 2271 is a separate archival trace from the 1967 resolution, 1968 decree, and 1969 DPRD resolution; the catalogue does not establish that the correspondence concerned those prior events.","period":"1970; Bima/Jakarta correspondence context unknown","type":"Archival lead distinction","source_ids":["S1085"],"confidence":"High for the catalogue separation; no claim about correspondence content.","basis":"Do not connect the 1970 title to the 1967–69 institutional steps until the letters are read.","next":"Retrieve both leaves and any folder cover/adjacent correspondence.","year":1970},{"id":"C2028","text":"ANTARA reported that 45 Bima pesantren leaders met with the Ministry of Religious Affairs and FKUB on 16 July 2011 to discuss Pondok Pesantren Khilafiah Umar Bin Khatab (UBK); the report lists KH Said Amin as an attendee but quotes no statement from him.","period":"16 July 2011; Bima","type":"Contemporary reported meeting/attendance","source_ids":["S1094"],"confidence":"High that the contemporary report lists him; moderate for attendance pending minutes or signed list.","basis":"No minutes, attendee sheet or recording inspected; do not attribute the meeting’s positions or statements to Said Amin.","next":"Find Kemenag/FKUB minutes, invitation, attendance list and any contemporaneous statement.","year":2011},{"id":"C2029","text":"Kahaba reported a 2014 coordination meeting on LDII and a nine-person follow-up team including Said Amin as a religious-leader representative; the report says the meeting ended without a decision and does not quote him.","period":"16 November 2014; Bima","type":"Contemporary reported meeting/committee membership","source_ids":["S1095"],"confidence":"High that the report states this; medium for committee appointment pending minutes.","basis":"The headline paraphrase about LDII repentance belongs to the article’s account of MUI chair Yasin Abubakar, not Said Amin. No team appointment sheet or interview from Said Amin located.","next":"Seek meeting minutes, the nine-member list, follow-up reports, and direct contemporaneous statements.","year":2014},{"id":"C2030","text":"Later publications list more than twenty works attributed to Said Amin and identify works including Menuju Pelaksanaan Syari’at Islam (2002), Sifat Shalat Rasulullah (2012), Ar-Risalah Al-Aminiyah (2013–14 series), and several books on theology, ritual and religious education.","period":"2001–2014; Bima","type":"Bibliographic attribution","source_ids":["S1091","S1092","S1097","S995","S1098"],"confidence":"High that the biographies list these titles; moderate for publication details, authorship and edition history.","basis":"Most original books and manuscripts have not been directly read; a work’s title alone is not evidence of its complete argument or political position.","next":"Obtain copies/editions, compare title pages and colophons, identify manuscript variants and read the cited passages directly.","year":2001},{"id":"C2031","text":"Ahlan’s 2022 thesis interprets Said Amin’s use of hadith as an effort to redirect certain Bima devotional practices while preserving local cultural elements; it also reports specific ritual substitutions.","period":"Undated/recent practice as analyzed in 2022; Bima","type":"Later author’s discourse analysis and impact interpretation","source_ids":["S1086","S1091","S1098"],"confidence":"High that the thesis’s abstract makes this interpretation; low-to-moderate for scale and causal impact.","basis":"The accessible portion is incomplete; the thesis relies in part on Said Amin’s own works and Mutawali’s biography. No independent before/after field series or participant-level data reviewed.","next":"Access restricted chapters, identify fieldwork dates/respondents and compare ritual practices with contemporaneous local accounts.","year":2022},{"id":"C2032","text":"Sources give Said Amin’s death as 30 April 2015; Fathurrahman’s 2023 memorial also says his Al-Ittihad foundation chairmanship continued until 2016, a posthumous endpoint that conflicts with the reported death date.","period":"30 April 2015 / claimed term through 2016; Bima","type":"Retrospective date contradiction","source_ids":["S1091","S1097","S1092","S995"],"confidence":"High that the sources contain the conflict; moderate-high that 2015 is the reported death date.","basis":"No civil death record or foundation handover/appointment record reviewed. Do not silently correct 2016 to 2015 or infer that the role passed to a successor.","next":"Check death registration, funeral record, foundation board succession and chair appointment records.","year":2015},{"id":"C2033","text":"A 2021 biographical book reports Said Amin married Hj. Imo H. Ahmad in 1958 and had eleven children.","period":"1958 onward; Bima","type":"Retrospective family biography","source_ids":["S995"],"confidence":"Moderate as a family biography claim.","basis":"No marriage register or family census was inspected; spellings and number of surviving children may vary across editions or family accounts.","next":"Check civil/religious marriage records and separately sourced family genealogy if these details become relevant.","year":1958},{"id":"C2034","text":"The 2021 biographical volume separates an older TGH Muhammad Said associated with Ngali from Tuan Guru H.M. Said Amin; shared forms such as Muhammad Said are insufficient for identity matching.","period":"Before 1945–2015; Ngali/Tawali, Bima","type":"Identity-disambiguation observation","source_ids":["S995","S1091","S1097"],"confidence":"High that the source treats them as separate profiles; moderate-high identity distinction across the reviewed biographical material.","basis":"No civil identity records compared; do not merge results on name string alone.","next":"Keep separate authority records and match future references by birth place, family, dates, office and source context.","year":1945},{"id":"C2035","text":"The reproduced judgment identifies H. Muhammad AR, BA, as YASIM’s chair and plaintiff in case 14/Pdt.G/2021/PN Rbi, filed 1 March 2021 against 23 Madawau residents including village head Anwar Ibrahim; the object is approximately 50,000 m² (5 ha) at So Tololara Watasan.","period":"2021; So Tololara Watasan, Madawau, Bima","type":"Court-record fact / case scope","source_ids":["S1099","S1100"],"confidence":"High that the reproduced judgment says this; legal and cadastral boundaries remain document-specific.","basis":"Public university repository reproduces the court scan; exact parcel map and original title instruments were not inspected. The 5 ha object is not the separately reported two-hectare school parcel or the wider 24/24.61 ha YASIM land account.","next":"Obtain certified judgment, site inspection map, cadastral coordinates, BPN record and original title exhibits.","year":2021},{"id":"C2036","text":"The court partly granted YASIM’s claim, declared the specific 5 ha So Tololara plot YASIM property, found the defendants’ occupation without YASIM consent unlawful, declared land documents/data issued by defendant I as village head invalid, and ordered vacant return and removal of buildings.","period":"2021; Madawau","type":"Judicial finding and remedy","source_ids":["S1100"],"confidence":"High for the wording of this reproduced dispositive; it is a decision of a first-instance court, not a general adjudication of other parcels.","basis":"The scan is reproduced through a thesis. The outcome applies to the defined object and parties; it does not decide all Madawau land claims or establish execution.","next":"Obtain certified ruling, appellate/PK rulings and execution/land-office records; georeference the object.","year":2021},{"id":"C2037","text":"The same judgment rejected YASIM’s Rp300,000,000 damages request and requested daily coercive fine (dwangsom), while ordering defendants to bear Rp11,720,500 in case costs.","period":"2021; Madawau","type":"Judicial finding and remedy","source_ids":["S1100"],"confidence":"High that the reproduced judgment states these dispositions.","basis":"The damages rejection concerns the submitted proof in this suit, not a finding that no loss occurred outside the claim.","next":"Check certified judgment and any later appeal/PK alteration.","year":2021},{"id":"C2038","text":"The judgment cites a photocopy of Deputy Head of Agraria decree No.41/Depag/1966 dated 25 June 1966 and a Ministry of Home Affairs decision dated 7 February 1974, cited in the scan as SK No.3/HP/DA/74, as part of YASIM’s documentary case.","period":"1966–1974; invoked in 2021 Madawau suit","type":"Court-recorded evidence / historical legal instrument lead","source_ids":["S1100"],"confidence":"High that the judgment reproduces these exhibit references; low-to-moderate on the instruments’ full legal effect until originals are inspected.","basis":"The originals, complete attachments, registry entries, and broader land-transfer instruments were not accessed; a later book snippet uses a different 1974 number form.","next":"Locate originals at Kabupaten Bima, BPN, Ministry of Home Affairs, or court archive; compare exact number, wording, scope and parcel schedules.","year":1966},{"id":"C2039","text":"Wardiansyah’s thesis abstract broadly describes the outcome as cancellation of community SHM certificates; the reproduced dispositive instead expressly declares village-head-issued land documents/data invalid and orders surrender of the defined object, without listing individual SHM certificate numbers in that dispositive.","period":"2021–2022; Madawau","type":"Source-description discrepancy","source_ids":["S1099","S1100"],"confidence":"High that the two accessible texts phrase the legal result differently.","basis":"The court also discusses BPN cancellation orders already issued for some certificates in 2016. Do not infer that the court itself individually canceled every certificate.","next":"Read the restricted thesis chapter only through authorized access; inspect all court pages and the cited BPN decisions.","year":2021},{"id":"C2040","text":"On 16 November 2026 the PN Raba/Bima SIPP row for 14/Pdt.G/2021/PN RBI displayed case status “Pemberitahuan Putusan PK” (notification of a judicial-review decision).","period":"As displayed 2026-11-16; case registered 2021","type":"Official case-registry status","source_ids":["S1101"],"confidence":"High that the portal row displayed this status on the access date; the PK outcome itself is unknown from the retrieved row.","basis":"The case-detail route triggered a Cloudflare verification page. The list provides no PK number, decision date, result or execution history.","next":"Request the case-detail docket, Supreme Court PK number and decision; obtain any certified notification and execution file.","year":2026},{"id":"C2041","text":"In 2020 Anwar told local reporters that a roughly two-hectare site used by SDN Inpres Tololara had been agreed for school use around 1975 with his late father Ibrahim Gani, on promises of compensation and land exchange; he cited SPPT No.52.06.130.010.005.0059.0 and a Madawau DHKP entry at So Sakido Dompu.","period":"Claim refers to agreement around 1975; reported 2020; Madawau","type":"Interested-party claim reported by local press","source_ids":["S1108","S1109"],"confidence":"High that the reports attribute this claim to Anwar; low-to-moderate that the agreement, parcel size and title basis are accurately established.","basis":"No agreement, SPPT, DHKP original, survey map, compensation record or court filing was inspected. The report says “gugat” but provides no case number or judgment.","next":"Locate the 1975 agreement, school construction and compensation files, SPPT/DHKP originals, BPN parcel map and any case registration.","year":1975},{"id":"C2042","text":"A Madapangga UPTD education official said his search of the school’s inventory files did not locate land-title papers, while explicitly cautioning that non-discovery did not prove no legal document existed; a district education chief said he had received but not yet read Anwar’s letter.","period":"2020; SDN Inpres Tololara","type":"Administrative statement reported contemporaneously","source_ids":["S1110","S1111"],"confidence":"High that the article reports these statements; the scope of the search was narrow.","basis":"The search was at school records, not a comprehensive search of government, BPN, village or court archives.","next":"Obtain the written inventory search, district letter log, school land register and county property inventory.","year":2020},{"id":"C2043","text":"The 2020 school-land claimant and the 2021 defendant appear likely to be the same Anwar Ibrahim because the reports place the named claimant in Madawau and the judgment names Anwar Ibrahim, the Madawau village head; no direct identity record was compared.","period":"2020–2021; Madawau","type":"Provisional identity match","source_ids":["S1100","S1108","S1109"],"confidence":"Moderate-high identity inference based on matching name, village and overlapping dispute context; not a verified civil identity match.","basis":"The 2020 reports call him “Anwar” and cite his father as Ibrahim Gani; the 2021 judgment lists Anwar Ibrahim and contains differing inheritance/adoption narratives. The school plot may be separate.","next":"Compare civil/village identity records and cadastral maps before merging all property claims.","year":2020},{"id":"C2044","text":"The 2021 court caption identifies Anwar Ibrahim as a man aged approximately 54 and Head of Madawau village at the time of the suit.","period":"2021; Madawau","type":"Court-caption biographical fact","source_ids":["S1100"],"confidence":"High that the judgment caption says this; age is approximate and fixed to the caption date.","basis":"No birth record or appointment/election decree was inspected; the caption does not establish start or end of his term.","next":"Seek birth/identity record, village-head election and appointment instruments, and term-end record.","year":2021},{"id":"C2045","text":"Kahaba described H. Muhammad AR as YASIM chair for 2015–2020; later reports identify him as Ketua Umum in 2017 and 2022, and the 2021 judgment names him as chair/plaintiff.","period":"2015–2022; Bima","type":"Reported institutional role","source_ids":["S1099","S1103","S1106","S1112","S1123"],"confidence":"High that multiple reports and the judgment identify this office; exact appointment succession and complete tenure are not documented.","basis":"Sources vary between “Ketua Yayasan”, “Ketua Umum”, and the court’s formal “Ketua Umum Pengurus”; no YASIM board appointment minutes were reviewed.","next":"Obtain 2015–2022 board appointment decisions, foundation registry and handover minutes.","year":2015},{"id":"C2046","text":"YASIM’s internal audit supervisor and chair said a nearly three-month audit of 2004–2009 and 2009–2014 management found non-procedural loans and losses estimated around Rp300–400 million; they said an LHP was delivered in early October 2015 and reported no indication then of personal use.","period":"Audit of 2004–2014; reported 2015; Bima","type":"Attributed internal-audit claim","source_ids":["S1103"],"confidence":"High that the article reports these statements; low-to-moderate on the underlying losses until the LHP and records are inspected.","basis":"Internal audit report is unpublished in this archive, audited periods are inconsistently rendered elsewhere as 2009–2015, and no independent audit or court finding was found.","next":"Retrieve the LHP, ledgers, board responses, loan instruments and any recovery or legal proceedings.","year":2004},{"id":"C2047","text":"DPRD member Muhidin alleged in 2015 that 248 ha claimed by YASIM was privately owned land taken by government around 1963–1964; he also alleged that a later Bima regent’s hearing favored residents and a return order was concealed.","period":"Alleged taking 1963–1964; allegation reported 2015; Bima","type":"Political allegation and documentary lead","source_ids":["S1105"],"confidence":"High that the report attributes these claims to Muhidin; low that the land history and concealed order are established.","basis":"No parcel list, return order, hearing record, family title or government correspondence was inspected. The article gives no date for the hearing.","next":"Identify the alleged land parcels and obtain the hearing minutes, decision/order, registry, return notice and claimant list.","year":1963},{"id":"C2048","text":"A Kabupaten Bima government historical PDF lists Junaidin Amir Hamzah as head for 1959–1965, Abidin Ishak for 1965–1968 and Soeharmaji for 1968–1974.","period":"1959–1974; Bima","type":"Official retrospective office chronology","source_ids":["S1102"],"confidence":"High that the PDF prints these dates; moderate for exact tenure pending original appointment records.","basis":"Muhidin’s report does not date the supposed hearing; it may have occurred after the alleged 1963–64 taking. The chronology therefore does not by itself disprove the hearing.","next":"Find appointment decrees, interim service records and the alleged hearing file; preserve the spelling variants Suharmaji/Soeharmaji.","year":1959},{"id":"C2049","text":"Monta heirs told DPRD in October 2015 that 20 ha or more across Kecamatan Monta had been managed by YASIM and the regency since 1974; a DPRD member said both sides had documents and proposed mediation.","period":"Claim concerns management since 1974; reported 2015; Monta","type":"Heirs’ claim and DPRD response","source_ids":["S1104"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the statement and proposed mediation; underlying title and land area remain unverified.","basis":"No parcel-by-parcel inventory or documents were reproduced. This is not the 248 ha allegation, Madawau 5 ha suit, or Pela claim.","next":"Obtain the heirs’ documents delivered to Commission I, exact village/plot list, YASIM land ledger and mediation record.","year":1974},{"id":"C2050","text":"A November 2015 protest by residents identified as from Sape and Monta sought a response on land described as auction land and on an expected DPRD recommendation.","period":"2015-11-17; Sape and Monta, Bima","type":"Contemporaneous protest report","source_ids":["S1107"],"confidence":"High that Kahaba reports the protest; low on parcel identity and exact relation to the October Monta group.","basis":"No participant list, parcel list, meeting minutes or Commission I recommendation was accessed.","next":"Identify the Sape/Monta groups, their land packages, and DPRD correspondence.","year":2015},{"id":"C2051","text":"A 2017 report describes county-wide auction of former village-head guarantee lands (“tanah eks jaminan Kepala Desa”), with transparency complaints; Anwar Ibrahim commented that winning notices did not show bid values.","period":"2017; Kabupaten Bima","type":"Local-government auction report and attributed comment","source_ids":["S1120"],"confidence":"High that the outlet reports this process and quote; the auction documents were not inspected.","basis":"This public county process is distinct from YASIM’s annual cultivation tenders described in the Madawau court record.","next":"Obtain the county tender notice, parcel schedule, bidders, award and accounting records.","year":2017},{"id":"C2052","text":"In January 2017 ARUN reportedly asked the Bima regent to cancel an auction of several hectares of rice land at Desa Pela, Monta, on behalf of heirs of Arsyad Salam and others.","period":"2016 petition reported 2017; Pela, Monta","type":"Reported heirs’ petition","source_ids":["S1121"],"confidence":"High that the report attributes this request to ARUN; low-to-moderate on the underlying inheritance and title claims.","basis":"Petition, land certificates, survey and auction decision were not inspected; direct page was blocked and article details were retrieved from search extract.","next":"Obtain ARUN petition, Arsyad Salam family chain, Desa Pela land book, auction file and cited land-reform papers.","year":2016},{"id":"C2053","text":"The Pela petition reportedly cited Landreform committee materials dated 1969 and 1970, including No. UM.IV/88/KPTS/1999 dated 20 May 1969 and No. AREX.VIII/14/1970, as well as a 1970 plenary resolution and implementation report.","period":"1969–1970; Kabupaten Bima; cited in 2016 petition","type":"Uninspected archival source leads","source_ids":["S1121"],"confidence":"High that the news extract prints these references; low on exact document titles, number transcription and legal effect.","basis":"The 1969 number may contain a typographical or OCR issue; no original paper was found. References are not evidence that the papers said what the petitioners asserted.","next":"Request Landreform committee records and BPN files; compare number and date against the original petition.","year":1969},{"id":"C2054","text":"Residents of Dore reportedly occupied an eight-are plot after YASIM sold it to a foundation employee for Rp80 million; residents said they wanted access for a public lane.","period":"2019-12; Dore, Palibelo, Bima","type":"Contemporaneous dispute report; competing participant accounts","source_ids":["S1113"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the occupation and claims; sale title, price and access history remain unverified.","basis":"No deed, receipt, board resolution, village road plan or waqf registration was inspected.","next":"Obtain sale deed/receipt, YASIM board minutes, parcel map, waqf record and village requests.","year":2019},{"id":"C2055","text":"YASIM chair Muhammad AR said the Dore sale was approved by board meeting, the plot was unproductive, proceeds were used to buy two hectares in Bolo, and the plot was waqf property to be replaced.","period":"2019; Dore and Bolo","type":"Attributed institutional explanation","source_ids":["S1113"],"confidence":"High that the report quotes/paraphrases this explanation; low-to-moderate on the transfer and replacement transaction.","basis":"The board minutes, payment trail, new deed, replacement waqf registration and charity accounting were not inspected.","next":"Obtain the board minutes, sale and purchase instruments, bank/receipt record, and waqf registration.","year":2019},{"id":"C2056","text":"In 2017 Kahaba identified H. Muhammad AR as YASIM’s Ketua Umum and reported his position that MIS Roka staff dismissal followed an internal supervisory review; he advised dissatisfied residents to report concerns to police.","period":"2017; Desa Roka, Belo; YASIM office in Bima","type":"Attributed public statement and role","source_ids":["S1112"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the role and statement; not evidence that the underlying BOS or dismissal allegations were true.","basis":"No investigation report, employment decision, police case file or later outcome was inspected.","next":"Obtain YASIM supervisory report and employment decisions if this separate school dispute is pursued.","year":2017},{"id":"C2057","text":"Bimakini reported a 13 September 2016 confrontation between Madawau and Mangge Na’e residents over a disputed Bima–Dompu boundary parcel at So Doro Kalate Roso; each side claimed the site, and Anwar Ibrahim cited SPPT records for Bima.","period":"2016; Bima–Dompu border area","type":"Contemporaneous local-news report and attributed official claim","source_ids":["S1114","S1115"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the confrontation and statements; low on administrative ownership/boundary.","basis":"No village or district border regulation, cadastral map, SPPT file, police report or provincial boundary determination was inspected.","next":"Obtain signed incident/agreement record, provincial map and final boundary decision; trace 2013 episode independently.","year":2016},{"id":"C2058","text":"A September 2016 interim agreement reportedly barred claiming or cultivating the contested boundary site pending a final outcome; provincial boundary-team review was planned, but November reports still described the dispute as unresolved.","period":"2016; Madawau–Mangge Na’e boundary area","type":"Reported interim administrative action and unresolved outcome","source_ids":["S1116","S1117","S1118","S1119"],"confidence":"High that these reports state an interim agreement and intended review; whether the agreement was carried out and whether a final decision later issued are unknown.","basis":"Agreement signatures, provincial survey, administrative decisions and subsequent court filings were not obtained.","next":"Request the 13/16 September agreement, Bima/Dompu correspondence, provincial review report and boundary decision.","year":2016},{"id":"C2059","text":"The 2016 boundary coverage uses several place forms—So Doro Kalate Roso, Doro Roso and So Kasa’a Mboko—without a map that proves they name the same exact parcel.","period":"2016; border conflict sites","type":"Toponym/parcel disambiguation observation","source_ids":["S1114","S1118","S1119"],"confidence":"High that the articles use these variants; low that the sites are identical.","basis":"Do not merge the place labels or connect them to the YASIM So Tololara object based on name similarity.","next":"Compare original maps, village letters and field coordinates.","year":2016},{"id":"C2060","text":"After the September 2021 decision, Media Dinamika Global reported a November 8 argument between Anwar Ibrahim and YASIM’s land representatives about planting/auction authority and a short pause agreement; it said activity continued afterward.","period":"2021-11; Madawau","type":"Single-outlet report of disputed post-judgment field events","source_ids":["S1122"],"confidence":"High that the outlet reports this account; low on the facts and legal status of each participant’s actions.","basis":"No execution warrant, police report, enforcement inspection, or independent second account was obtained.","next":"Find court execution docket and village/security reports; compare accounts from both sides and local witnesses.","year":2021},{"id":"C2061","text":"A February 2022 report still identifies H. Muhammad AR, BA as YASIM Ketua Umum while reporting his installation of more than 40 mosque personnel.","period":"2022-02; Bima","type":"Contemporaneous institutional-role report","source_ids":["S1123"],"confidence":"High that the outlet reports this role; appointment term dates are not known.","basis":"The foundation’s appointment decree and board registry were not reviewed.","next":"Obtain the 2020–2025 YASIM board roster and signed appointment decisions.","year":2022},{"id":"C2062","text":"A June 2026 report says a proposed Madawau cooperative site remained disputed, the military district command urged postponement pending legal clarity, Anwar Ibrahim described competing claims, and an unnamed “Yayasan Kabupaten Bima” was reportedly willing to donate some land after a court decision.","period":"2026-06; Madawau","type":"Contemporaneous local report with unreferenced institutional/legal claims","source_ids":["S1124","S1125"],"confidence":"High that two outlets published substantially the same account; moderate that the meeting occurred; low on the legal identity, exact parcel and claimed court/gift facts.","basis":"S1125 appears to republish S1124. Neither provides the judgment number, donor resolution, map, gift deed, or proof that the entity is YASIM or the 2021 claimant. Follow-up planned for 8 June was not located.","next":"Obtain the 5 and 8 June minutes, land location, foundation legal name, donation deed and cited court decision.","year":2026},{"id":"C2063","text":"No reviewed public source supplies a verified full expansion of H. Muhammad AR’s initials, birth date, family background or continuous personal biography; the degree suffix BA appears in the court caption and 2022 report.","period":"Public record through 2022; Bima","type":"Biography coverage gap / identity control","source_ids":["S1100","S1123"],"confidence":"High for the limits of this reviewed source set, not proof that no biographical record exists.","basis":"Do not invent a full name or treat “AR” as a known family name; BA is not a substitute for an academic record.","next":"Search YASIM registry, institutional personnel records and attributed oral-history materials; verify identity before connecting namesakes.","year":2022},{"id":"C2064","text":"The evidence concerns multiple distinct land claims: YASIM’s 5 ha So Tololara suit object; Anwar’s reported 2 ha school parcel at So Sakido Dompu; the 20+ ha Monta heirs’ claim; Muhidin’s unitemized 248 ha allegation; and the several-hectare Pela heirs’ claim.","period":"2015–2021; different sites in Bima","type":"Parcel and claim separation","source_ids":["S1100","S1104","S1105","S1108","S1109","S1121"],"confidence":"High that sources identify different locations and claimants; exact parcel boundaries remain incomplete.","basis":"No map or land ledger establishes overlap. Similar YASIM/Bima references do not make the cases one dispute.","next":"Create parcel authority records from cadastral maps and original claim/auction files; retain separate event entries.","year":2015},{"id":"C2065","text":"A former YASIM treasurer/witness told the 2021 court that the foundation’s So Tololara land account was 24.61 ha, that the land was annually auctioned, and that Anwar won the 2013/14 tender for Rp30 million, paid Rp10 million and owed Rp20 million; he said the disputed 5 ha was included in the larger area.","period":"2013–2014 account reported in 2021 trial; So Tololara, Madawau","type":"Witness testimony recorded in court","source_ids":["S1100"],"confidence":"High that the judgment reproduces this witness account; moderate-to-low as a fact about the tender/payment until records are checked.","basis":"The judgment also contains a separate 24 ha figure in plaintiff pleadings. The tender notice, receipt, ledger and cadastral area were not inspected; do not reconcile 24 and 24.61 ha.","next":"Obtain YASIM land inventory, annual tender notice, winning bid, receipts/arrears records and survey plan.","year":2013},{"id":"C2066","text":"Bima Regent Regulation 25/2019 includes Desa Madawau among 82 villages scheduled for the 2019 village-head election and allocates Rp22.64 million in election assistance to it.","period":"2019; Desa Madawau, Madapangga, Bima","type":"Official administrative provision","source_ids":["S1143"],"confidence":"High for what the regulation prescribes; it does not prove the disbursement or election result.","basis":"The regulation’s village list and annex were read. Actual election minutes and financial realization were not obtained.","next":"Obtain Madawau election committee minutes, certified vote count, appointment decree and village-account realization.","year":2019},{"id":"C2067","text":"A local report on the 27 January 2020 swearing-in list identifies “Anwar” as the newly installed Madawau village head.","period":"2020-01-27; Desa Madawau","type":"Contemporaneous report of official appointment","source_ids":["S1144","S1153"],"confidence":"Moderate; the report gives only the first name, though other sources identify Anwar Ibrahim in that office.","basis":"No official appointment roster was accessed in this pass.","next":"Obtain the regent’s appointment decree and 2019 election result for Madawau.","year":2020},{"id":"C2068","text":"Madawau BPD chair Agustina and other BPD-side reports objected to a Rp116 million agricultural-road project, alleging the village government proceeded without the required consultation/agreement and had not answered a clarification request.","period":"2020-12 to 2021-01; Desa Madawau","type":"Attributed institutional complaint","source_ids":["S1126","S1127","S1128"],"confidence":"High that the BPD complaint was reported; undetermined whether the procedure was violated.","basis":"Local reports quote BPD representatives; APBDes revisions, minutes, procurement and inspection files were not examined.","next":"Obtain 2020 APBDes and amendments, BPD minutes/attendance, procurement file, DPMDes response and inspection record.","year":2020},{"id":"C2069","text":"Anwar Ibrahim replied that Rp116 million was residual funding cut because of Covid-19, returned to the village program and spent on an agricultural road that had been completed; he invited administrative review if the BPD disputed the work.","period":"2020-12 to 2021-01; Desa Madawau","type":"Attributed office-holder response","source_ids":["S1129"],"confidence":"High that the report records his response; the budget explanation and completion are unverified.","basis":"No APBDes, contract, work volume, payment or completion record was read.","next":"Compare the amended APBDes, disbursement records, contractor file and physical-inspection record.","year":2020},{"id":"C2070","text":"In a separate 5 January 2021 response, Anwar described Rp165 million as remaining funds after Covid-19 cuts rather than a budget change and challenged the process and documentation of a BPD meeting/letter.","period":"2020-12 to 2021-01; Desa Madawau","type":"Attributed office-holder response","source_ids":["S1130"],"confidence":"High that he is reported to have said this; underlying meeting and fund facts remain unresolved.","basis":"This report gives Rp165 million, while road-project stories cite Rp116 million; do not treat the values as interchangeable without accounts.","next":"Obtain the BPD letter, meeting minutes, APBDes revision and DPMDes file.","year":2020},{"id":"C2071","text":"The 2020–21 Madawau road-budget coverage uses both Rp116 million and Rp165 million, without enough records to establish whether the figures describe one appropriation, different budget components or separate complaints.","period":"2020–2021; Desa Madawau","type":"Source discrepancy / unresolved accounting question","source_ids":["S1126","S1127","S1128","S1129","S1130"],"confidence":"High that the published reports differ; no conclusion about the actual total.","basis":"No village budget, ledger or official reconciliation is available in the reviewed sources.","next":"Reconcile amounts against APBDes and cash-book entries before describing the project cost.","year":2020},{"id":"C2072","text":"A 2022 local report relayed a community allegation that village official Ratu Kardiningsih had been absent from work since 2019 and that the village head had not filled the post.","period":"2019–2022; Desa Madawau","type":"Attributed public allegation","source_ids":["S1131"],"confidence":"High that the allegation was published; low on the factual status of attendance or employment.","basis":"The report contains unverified personal rumors which are excluded; no attendance register or personnel record was inspected.","next":"Obtain the official’s appointment, leave/medical and attendance records and any village staffing decision.","year":2019},{"id":"C2073","text":"Anwar responded that Kardiningsih had submitted a medical certificate, that the absence was due to illness, and that a recruitment process and 2022 budget allocation were planned; he also referred to a salary-backed bank loan affecting the timing.","period":"2022; Desa Madawau","type":"Attributed office-holder response","source_ids":["S1132"],"confidence":"High that the response was reported; medical, financing and recruitment details are unverified.","basis":"No medical note, bank record, village budget or recruitment notice was accessed.","next":"Check staffing file, APBDes 2022, recruitment notice and replacement appointment.","year":2022},{"id":"C2074","text":"In 2017, Roka residents publicly accused the YASIM supervisory team of bias/collusion in a dispute involving MIS YASIM Roka’s head and alleged misuse of BOS funds, BSM support and students’ savings.","period":"2017; Desa Roka, Belo, Bima","type":"Attributed public allegation","source_ids":["S1133"],"confidence":"High that the residents made the allegations; the underlying financial misconduct is unproven.","basis":"The report records a demonstration and statements; no school accounting, BOS audit, police file or court judgment was inspected.","next":"Obtain MIS Roka BOS statements, BSM beneficiary records, YASIM review file and any police/prosecutor case status.","year":2017},{"id":"C2075","text":"YASIM supervisory member M. Yusuf Idris denied collusion, said the team found no indication of BOS/BSM misuse, and said it recommended dismissal of two volunteer teachers after alleged workplace incidents.","period":"2017; Desa Roka, Belo, Bima","type":"Attributed internal-review response","source_ids":["S1134"],"confidence":"High that the supervisor gave this account; the team’s findings and conduct remain unverified.","basis":"The report describes but does not reproduce the review report or teacher employment decisions.","next":"Obtain the supervisory report, interview notes, recommendation and dismissal letters.","year":2017},{"id":"C2076","text":"YASIM chair H. Muhammad AR said Jaharudin and Jumriati were dismissed on 22 July 2017 following the supervisors’ recommendation and advised dissatisfied residents to report the matter to police; his report of a police summons to the school head was hearsay as presented.","period":"2017; Desa Roka and Bima","type":"Attributed office-holder statement","source_ids":["S1112"],"confidence":"High for the reported statement and the reported date; the dismissal record and police action are unverified.","basis":"No employment letter, police summons or case file was accessed.","next":"Obtain YASIM’s decision and police docket; identify any subsequent labor or criminal proceedings.","year":2017},{"id":"C2077","text":"In January 2020 residents complained about financial transparency at Masjid Baitul Hamid; former mosque chair H. Sudirman said financial reports were presented to the mosque committee and that YASIM could examine the supporting records.","period":"2015–2020 reported; Penaraga, Kota Bima","type":"Competing attributed statements","source_ids":["S1135"],"confidence":"High that the statements were reported; the accounts themselves were not verified.","basis":"The article does not include account books, minutes or receipts.","next":"Obtain the mosque committee minutes, LPJ for 2015–2020 and bank statements.","year":2015},{"id":"C2078","text":"In June 2020 committee member MN claimed the five-year LPJ showed receipts of Rp2,468,086,095 and spending of Rp1,417,707,594, with more than Rp1 billion not reflected as a remaining balance; he also cited specific arithmetic differences and omitted land-auction proceeds.","period":"2015-03-31 to 2020-04-30; Masjid Baitul Hamid, Penaraga","type":"Attributed document-based allegation","source_ids":["S1136"],"confidence":"High that MN described those figures; low-to-undetermined as an accurate reconstruction of the accounts.","basis":"The article does not reproduce the LPJ and totals may include cash balances/receipts from multiple sources; government grants were not yet reconciled.","next":"Inspect the full LPJ, receipts, cashbook, bank records, waqf-land tender sheets and physical asset inventory.","year":2015},{"id":"C2079","text":"Former mosque chair H. Sudirman denied the June 2020 accusations and said the supporting records were held by the treasurer and had been submitted to YASIM; he threatened legal action over what he considered reputational harm.","period":"2020; Penaraga, Kota Bima","type":"Attributed response","source_ids":["S1136"],"confidence":"High that the response was reported; the submitted records and threatened action were not verified.","basis":"No underlying documents or court/police filing were accessed.","next":"Locate the submitted LPJ and determine whether any defamation case was filed.","year":2020},{"id":"C2080","text":"As of 26 June 2020, city Kesra and BPKAD officials told Kahaba that they were still searching for files showing whether and how much city/provincial aid had been paid to Masjid Baitul Hamid during 2015–2020.","period":"2015–2020; Kota Bima / NTB; status reported 2020-06-26","type":"Attributed public-record search status","source_ids":["S1138"],"confidence":"High that officials reported an ongoing search; it does not establish that no aid was given.","basis":"No municipal grant ledger or later search result was located in this pass.","next":"Request city/province grant registers, recipient decisions, payment orders and any later document-search response.","year":2015},{"id":"C2081","text":"YASIM supervisors said their internal review would examine five years of records including bank flows, cashbooks, purchases, staff incentives and waqf income, and compare documents with physical works; the release date was delayed for additional checking.","period":"2015–2020 records; review reported June–July 2020","type":"Reported internal audit procedure","source_ids":["S1137","S1140","S1141"],"confidence":"High that the review was described this way; its methods cannot be independently assessed without the workpapers.","basis":"The process was internal to YASIM, and neither its terms of reference nor its report was obtained.","next":"Obtain the audit mandate, workpapers, full LHP and any committee minutes receiving the report.","year":2015},{"id":"C2082","text":"On 28 August 2020 H Muhammad said YASIM’s review found more than Rp49 million in expenditures that could not be accounted for; he said three former mosque officials had signed an undertaking to repay the amount in installments.","period":"2015–2020; reported 2020-08-28; Penaraga, Kota Bima","type":"Attributed internal-audit outcome and repayment undertaking","source_ids":["S1142"],"confidence":"High that the chair reported this; low-to-moderate for the underlying financial finding until the LHP and undertaking are read.","basis":"The article does not reproduce the LHP, signed undertakings, receipts of repayment or any criminal finding.","next":"Obtain the LHP, signed undertakings and repayment ledger; check whether the amount was repaid.","year":2015},{"id":"C2083","text":"The resident’s more-than-Rp1-billion claimed discrepancy and the YASIM chair’s reported Rp49-million-plus internal finding are not reconciled by the public material reviewed; differences in scope, source records and accounting method remain unknown.","period":"2015–2020; reported June–August 2020","type":"Unresolved discrepancy between attributed accounts","source_ids":["S1136","S1142"],"confidence":"High that the public accounts differ; no basis to select one as the final figure.","basis":"The resident’s LPJ calculations, YASIM LHP, bank statements and any official audit are unavailable; do not characterize the disputed sums as proven theft.","next":"Compare the same five-year period and revenue/expense categories using originals; check whether the Rp49m appears within the claimed larger discrepancy.","year":2015},{"id":"C2084","text":"Ministry education registry lists SMAS YASIM as a private high school under Yayasan Islam Bima, with school establishment dated 11 April 1969 and an operational permit dated 7 June 2016.","period":"School record current as accessed 2026-11-16; Kota Bima","type":"Official registry entry","source_ids":["S1145"],"confidence":"High for the fields in the registry; these dates concern the school unit, not the foundation’s establishment.","basis":"The scanned founding/operating decrees linked from the data page were not retrievable.","next":"Obtain the school’s founding decree and foundation registry entry.","year":2026},{"id":"C2085","text":"The reproduced scan of the 2021 Raba Bima judgment gives two variants in YASIM’s pleaded legal history: printed p.5 lists Notarial Deed No.93 as dated 1985 and the AHU approval number as AHU-D1316.50.10.2014, while printed p.43 lists 1986 and AHU-01316.50.10.2014.","period":"1969–2014 claims reproduced in 2021 judgment; printed pp.5 and 43","type":"Internal documentary-text discrepancy","source_ids":["S1100"],"confidence":"High that the two scanned pages contain different variants; no conclusion about the true deed date or approval number.","basis":"Both pages in a university-repository scan reproduction were visually checked. The text summarizes the plaintiff’s version; no original deed or AHU approval was examined.","next":"Compare certified judgment evidence exhibits P-1 to P-4/P-24 with the 1969, 1985/1986 and 2014 notarial originals and AHU approval record.","year":1969},{"id":"C2086","text":"On printed p.43 the judgment reproduction frames YASIM’s legal-lineage paragraph as what the plaintiff alleged and says it corresponded to exhibits P-1, P-2, P-3, P-4 and P-24; those exhibits are not reproduced in that paragraph.","period":"2021; So Tololara litigation, plaintiff’s corporate-history argument","type":"Court-record attribution / evidence-access boundary","source_ids":["S1100"],"confidence":"High that this is how the reproduced judgment paragraph is worded; it does not establish what the exhibits prove.","basis":"The scan’s paragraph says “Penggugat mendalilkan”; the archival record reviewed here contains the judgment reproduction, not the underlying exhibits. Do not treat the pleading summary as an independent determination of YASIM’s entire institutional history.","next":"Obtain the case-file copies of P-1 through P-4 and P-24 and compare with official notary/AHU files.","year":2021},{"id":"C2087","text":"Abdillah’s 2022 thesis gives two variants of the legal account: p.17 attributes the transfer of BHS non-judicial functions to a 16 March 1968 Bupati decision numbered Pem./I/1/A.16/SPKS/21; p.109 cites Ministry of Religion Decision No.23/1960 and a Bupati decision numbered Pem.1/1/A.16/SKPS/21, citing Abdul Gani Abdullah 2015, p.259.","period":"1960–1968; Bima","type":"Retrospective institutional-history variants in one thesis","source_ids":["S1087"],"confidence":"High that the thesis contains both passages and number variants; the wording and legal effect of the original orders remain unverified.","basis":"The thesis does not attach the KMA or Bupati decision; its p.109 footnote points to a 2015 secondary book. Do not silently harmonize SPKS/SKPS or infer that the ministerial decision created YASIM.","next":"Obtain the original KMA No.23/1960, Bupati order, 1967 DPRD-GR resolution, attachments and the cited Abdullah p.259.","year":1960},{"id":"C2088","text":"The current official history page of Pengadilan Agama Manna states that Ministry of Religion Decision No.23/1960 dated 14 November 1960 established the Manna Religious Court as a branch of the Bengkulu Religious Court for South Bengkulu.","period":"14 November 1960; Manna, Bengkulu","type":"Official institutional retrospective","source_ids":["S1146"],"confidence":"High that the official page currently reports this account; the decision text itself was not accessed.","basis":"This concerns PA Manna’s institutional history. It does not show that the same decision had no connection to Bima, but it demonstrates that the cited number is also used for judicial-branch formation and cannot by itself prove YASIM’s legal establishment.","next":"Read the original decision and determine its operative clauses, addressees, territorial scope and relationship to Bima.","year":1960},{"id":"C2089","text":"Abdillah’s 2022 thesis reports that on 12 April 2021 in Kota Bima its author interviewed “Muhammad AR (72), Ketua Yayasan Islam Bima”; the thesis attributes to that interviewee a retrospective account of YASIM as continuing the Sultanate-era Mahkamatus Syar’iyyah and its educational role.","period":"12 April 2021; Kota Bima","type":"Attributed oral-history statement by institutional officeholder","source_ids":["S1087"],"confidence":"High that the thesis identifies and cites the interview this way; moderate for the accuracy of the recollection about institutional continuity.","basis":"The interview recording/transcript was not accessed. The thesis cites separate historical studies for the 1931 and 1934 school dates, so those dates should not be attributed solely to Muhammad AR. “72” is the thesis’s interview-age label, not a verified birth record.","next":"Request the interview audio/notes and compare the chair’s recollection with BHS/YASIM charters, minutes, school registers and legal instruments.","year":2021},{"id":"C2090","text":"Permendagri No.37/2016 states that the Bima–Dompu county line had been agreed by the two regencies with NTB provincial facilitation and central boundary-team approval; its coordinate text places PABU 020 on the Madawau side bordering Manggenae (Dompu) and PABU 021/022 on the Madawau side bordering Katua (Dompu).","period":"2016; administrative boundary between Kabupaten Bima and Kabupaten Dompu","type":"Official legal instrument / administrative boundary description","source_ids":["S1147"],"confidence":"High for the text of the promulgated regulation; no inference about local consent, private land title or the exact location of the 2016 confrontation.","basis":"The official PDF and specified coordinate passages were opened. The cartographic annex was not independently georeferenced; village place-names do not identify a private parcel.","next":"Obtain the official annex map and provincial/local survey coordinates; compare only after georeferencing the So Doro/So Kalate reports and named parcels.","year":2016},{"id":"C2091","text":"In July 2021 Bima Regency administration official H. Masykur said the government relied on Permendagri 37/2016 rather than claiming land unilaterally; he also said Karaku had historically belonged to Dompu and that both sides could revise the line. The report describes a Dompu-side allegation that 99 ha had been taken and disagreement over RTRW versus the ministerial boundary.","period":"2021-07; Karaku and Madawau–Manggena’e border area","type":"Attributed official statement and competing administrative positions","source_ids":["S1152"],"confidence":"High that the outlet reports these statements; low-to-moderate on the boundaries, area and historical jurisdiction until maps and records are reviewed.","basis":"The report is a local-media account of Bima officials’ position; it includes no joint boundary map, parcel list, original inter-regency minutes or survey finding.","next":"Obtain both counties’ signed minutes, the cited RTRW maps, the legal annex and the alleged 99 ha parcel schedule.","year":2021},{"id":"C2092","text":"The NTB provincial DPRD review of the 2021 governor’s accountability report said Bima–Dompu border disputes remained unresolved, naming the Karaku/Manggena’e–Madawau boundary and Manggena’e–Rora boundary; it said the issue emerged after Permendagri 37/2016 and became prominent during Bima’s 2021 RTRW adjustment.","period":"2021 report reviewed in 2022; NTB","type":"Official legislative retrospective and recommendation","source_ids":["S1148"],"confidence":"High that the provincial DPRD document makes this assessment; it is not a boundary judgment or the underlying field survey.","basis":"The report offers an institutional account of unresolved administrative work and describes earlier jurisdictional understanding; it does not resolve parcel ownership.","next":"Obtain provincial/central boundary-team reports, map attachments, Bima RTRW draft versions and local service records.","year":2021},{"id":"C2093","text":"NTB’s government release says its 14 October 2022 facilitation meeting agreed the 5 October technical minutes except coordinates TK 13–PABU 15, recorded coordinate changes and planned two follow-up minutes that month.","period":"2022-10; Bima–Dompu border facilitation","type":"Official meeting retrospective; original minutes not accessed","source_ids":["S1149"],"confidence":"High that the provincial website’s indexed release reports these outcomes; low on exact technical geography until the minutes and maps are obtained.","basis":"Direct page timed out and no original minutes, map, field report or signed follow-up minutes were available. Do not map TK/PABU point codes to a Madawau parcel by assumption.","next":"Request signed minutes of 5, 14 and subsequent October meetings, all coordinate tables and maps, and central review record.","year":2022},{"id":"C2094","text":"On 2 November 2022 the Bima and Dompu regents signed an agreement concerning revision of Permendagri 37/2016; Bima’s official release says it would be submitted to the central government as a basis for revising the regulation and reports later local meetings/site review.","period":"2022-11-02; Bima–Dompu","type":"Official account of an intergovernmental agreement and next procedural step","source_ids":["S1151"],"confidence":"High that the Bima government page reports the signing and intended submission; unknown whether the central regulation was subsequently revised.","basis":"The signed agreement, central submission receipt, later review report and formal amended regulation were not obtained. Do not state that the signature itself changed the regulation.","next":"Obtain the complete signed agreement, central registry/receipt, field-review report and any later Permendagri amendment.","year":2022},{"id":"C2095","text":"The NTB governor’s office reported that public services in Dusun Karaku, Desa Manggena’e, continued under Dompu administration after the 2 November 2022 boundary agreement.","period":"2022-11; Karaku, Manggena’e, Dompu / Bima border","type":"Official statement about public-service administration","source_ids":["S1150"],"confidence":"High that the provincial government publicly described the arrangement; does not establish all boundary coordinates or title to land.","basis":"The announcement does not reproduce the agreement or a cadastral map and describes administrative services rather than private land ownership.","next":"Compare with the signed boundary agreement, Dompu/Bima village administrative registers, election/health/school coverage and later amended regulation.","year":2022},{"id":"C2096","text":"A contemporaneous Kahaba report says 82 village heads elected in Kabupaten Bima’s 2019 simultaneous village elections were sworn in on 27 January 2020.","period":"2019–2020; Kabupaten Bima","type":"Contemporaneous report of public office inauguration","source_ids":["S1153"],"confidence":"High that the report records the collective inauguration; it does not identify individual village heads or prove Anwar Ibrahim’s full name/appointment.","basis":"The article gives the aggregate number and ceremony date but no name list; the separate Bimakini list S1144 says only “Anwar” for Madawau.","next":"Obtain Madawau election tally, certified result and regent appointment decree; compare names against the 2021 court caption and 2016/2026 reports.","year":2019},{"id":"C2097","text":"The reproduced 2021 judgment’s printed p.44 records YASIM’s pleading that Anwar Ibrahim won its 24 ha So Tololara annual land tender for 2013/14 at Rp30 million, paid Rp10 million and owed Rp20 million; the plaintiff referred to exhibits P-7 (auction record) and P-8 (demand letter) and alleged continued occupation of 5 ha from 2015.","period":"2013–2015 claims reproduced in 2021 suit; So Tololara, Madawau","type":"Plaintiff’s pleading and evidence references in court record","source_ids":["S1100"],"confidence":"High that the reproduced judgment records the pleading; low-to-moderate that the tender, payment and arrears occurred as alleged.","basis":"The paragraph reports plaintiff allegations; P-7/P-8, receipts, land ledger and survey were not accessed. The witness account gives a separate 24.61 ha figure; the court excerpt is not a receipt or final finding on each payment.","next":"Obtain certified judgment, P-7/P-8, tender notices, payment ledger/receipts, 2014/15 award, parcel map and any post-trial ruling.","year":2013},{"id":"C2098","text":"The 2024 Bima and Dompu regency laws state that the two regencies share a general boundary and that exact field demarcation is to be established by a Minister of Home Affairs Regulation.","period":"2024; Bima and Dompu legal framework","type":"Official statutory text / unresolved administrative implementation","source_ids":["S1154","S1155"],"confidence":"High for the statutory text; does not identify the operative boundary regulation or decide if the 2022 agreement was implemented.","basis":"The statutes describe general directions of the boundaries and delegate precise field demarcation to a ministerial regulation; they do not cite Permendagri 37/2016 or the 2022 agreement.","next":"Find the ministerial regulation in force after 2022, its amendment/replacement history, official annex map and any central registry entry for the 2022 inter-regency agreement.","year":2024},{"id":"C2099","text":"A Media Dinamika Global archive excerpt reports that the Camat and related departments held a guidance meeting with Madawau village government and BPD on 12 January after disagreement over a 2020 agricultural-road project; the excerpt attributes to DPMD officer El Faisal the view that the APBDes was the legal budget basis and that BPD had agreement/evaluation functions.","period":"2021-01-12; Madawau, Kabupaten Bima","type":"Attributed report of local mediation and official statement","source_ids":["S1156"],"confidence":"High that the archive excerpt reports this meeting and statement; underlying budget compliance remains unresolved.","basis":"Only an indexed archive excerpt was available; article URL, minutes, APBDes and DPMD follow-up were not obtained. The article body gives 12/1/20 despite archive date 12 Jan 2021.","next":"Obtain the original 12 Jan meeting report/minutes, 2020 APBDes and amendments, procurement documents and DPMD file.","year":2021},{"id":"C2100","text":"At a reported 22 January 2021 public hearing, IMPM listed four demands involving BPD staffing, the 2020 APBDes amendment, 2021 village planning and BPD performance; BPD chair Agustina and secretary Suryadin criticized the village government’s consultation, while deputy chair Mus Muliadin said the APBDes and its amendment had been approved and discussed orally.","period":"2021-01-22; Madawau, Kabupaten Bima","type":"Contemporaneous report of internal institutional disagreement","source_ids":["S1157"],"confidence":"High that the article records these distinct statements; no conclusion about which account is accurate.","basis":"Only one local outlet’s report was reviewed. No hearing minutes, signed APBDes amendment, BPD attendance sheet or appointment decision was accessed.","next":"Obtain the hearing record, BPD meeting minutes, APBDes and amendment, and staff-appointment files.","year":2021},{"id":"C2101","text":"On 24 February 2021 IMPM demonstrated outside Madawau BPD and village offices, seeking public information; its spokesperson Firdaus alleged collusion and forged signatures on the APBDes 2020 agreement and said 2021 planning stages had not been completed.","period":"2021-02-24; Madawau, Kabupaten Bima","type":"Reported protest and attributed allegation","source_ids":["S1158"],"confidence":"High that the local report records the protest and allegation; unverified as to forgery, collusion or planning status.","basis":"No signed agreement, handwriting examination, inspection or formal complaint result was included.","next":"Obtain IMPM petition, APBDes agreement, signature originals, inspection file and any police/inspectorate record.","year":2021},{"id":"C2102","text":"The 24 February 2021 report attributes to BPD chair Agustina the claim that only her own signature on the APBDes agreement was genuine and four other members’ signatures had been made by village officials; village head Anwar Ibrahim denied forgery and said the process was proper and witnessed/accepted by the BPD chair.","period":"2021-02-24; Madawau, Kabupaten Bima","type":"Directly competing attributed allegations and response","source_ids":["S1159"],"confidence":"High that the report records both statements; factual status of the signatures is undetermined.","basis":"No agreement original, signature sample, BPD resolution, investigation result or court finding was accessed.","next":"Compare signed originals, BPD attendance/minutes, legal review and any forensic or administrative investigation.","year":2021},{"id":"C2103","text":"A 2 March 2021 local report says a Camat-facilitated meeting led IMPM and residents to agree to reopen the BPD and village offices, with a written statement and a one-month deadline for BPD and village-government actions.","period":"2021-03-02 through reported deadline 2021-04-01; Madawau","type":"Reported mediated reopening and promised administrative steps","source_ids":["S1160"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this arrangement; unknown whether the writing was signed or the promised steps were completed.","basis":"The written commitment, meeting minutes, APBDes 2021, RKPDes 2021 and deadline-compliance review were not obtained.","next":"Request signed statement, Camat minutes, DPMD follow-up and 2021 village planning/budget records.","year":2021},{"id":"C2104","text":"In October 2024 IMPM figures Firdaus and Syuryadin publicly alleged that the selection of three Madawau village-apparatus/Kaur posts was opaque or administratively defective, described the committee/candidate process, and sought suspension or annulment; the articles did not contain a response from the village government or Camat.","period":"2024-10-18 to 2024-10-20; Madawau, Kabupaten Bima","type":"Reported one-side allegations about recruitment","source_ids":["S1161","S1162"],"confidence":"High that the articles published the allegations and request; no basis to determine whether selection rules were breached.","basis":"No committee decree, recruitment announcement, candidate list, test record, result, appointment decree, DPMDes/Camat response or appeal was examined.","next":"Obtain the 2024 selection file and the full text of Bima Regent Regulation 29/2023 and implementing rules.","year":2024},{"id":"C2105","text":"A January 2021 article calls Firdaus IMPM chair; later 2021 and 2024 items give the name Muhammad Firdaus or Firdaus for IMPM-linked public statements. The similar names, village and organization make a same-person link plausible but not identity-verified.","period":"2021-01 to 2024-10; Madawau","type":"Possible identity match across public organizational roles","source_ids":["S1165","S1157","S1158","S1163","S1161"],"confidence":"High for the source-reported roles/names; moderate-to-low for the identity link.","basis":"No civil identity, organizational membership list, elected-officer record or first-person profile was obtained.","next":"Obtain IMPM statutes, officer lists, meeting records and direct confirmation from the named person.","year":2021},{"id":"C2106","text":"A Desa Naru Barat government-site page identifies Bima Regent Regulation 29/2023 as amending the rules implementing Bima Regional Regulation 1/2015 on village apparatus; its attached PDF link returned 404 during review.","period":"2023 regulation page; accessed 2026-11-16; Kabupaten Bima","type":"Official local-site document locator; text inaccessible","source_ids":["S1164"],"confidence":"High that the page identifies the rule title and amendment relationship; no confidence assigned to any substantive provision.","basis":"The attachment was unavailable. A same-number BPK search hit concerned Luwu Utara and is unrelated.","next":"Retrieve an official Gazette/JDIH copy of Bima Perbup 29/2023 and confirm effective date and later amendments.","year":2023},{"id":"C2107","text":"A 2021 report uses “Suryadin” for a Madawau BPD secretary, while two October 2024 reports use “Syuryadin” for an IMPM pioneer/spokesperson and one gives the alias “Surya Ghempar”; current evidence does not establish whether these names refer to the same person.","period":"2021-01 and 2024-10; Madawau","type":"Name and affiliation disambiguation question","source_ids":["S1157","S1162"],"confidence":"High that the publications use these spellings and roles; identity link unverified.","basis":"No membership, employment or civil identity record was accessed.","next":"Obtain BPD personnel/meeting lists and IMPM officer rosters; keep records separate until identity is established.","year":2021},{"id":"C2108","text":"On 18 February 2021, a local report quoted IMPM core member Firdaus as saying Madawau had not yet held the 2021 Musdes and that internal BPD problems were delaying village development; he urged the BPD to press the village government to convene it.","period":"2021-02-18; Madawau, Kabupaten Bima","type":"Attributed public assessment and organizing demand","source_ids":["S1166"],"confidence":"High that the report published this statement; the actual status of planning meetings and budgets is unverified.","basis":"The outlet did not provide a Musdes notice, minutes, 2021 RKPDes/APBDes file or district registry confirming whether the meeting had taken place.","next":"Obtain 2021 Musdes notice/minutes, RKPDes/APBDes and Kecamatan/DPMD filing records; compare with the 22 Jan and 24 Feb reporting.","year":2021},{"id":"C2109","text":"Intelmedia’s 14 March 2021 report gives two different dates for the IMPM MUBES: Saturday night 13 February in one passage and 13 March at 19:30 in another. The actual meeting date is unresolved.","period":"2021-02/03; Madawau, Bima","type":"Conflicting contemporaneous report detail","source_ids":["S1167"],"confidence":"High that the report contains the conflict; date of event unresolved.","basis":"One article, no minutes or participant list; article publication date is 14 March.","next":"Obtain the MUBES invitation, attendance sheet, signed minutes and officers’ handover record.","year":2021},{"id":"C2110","text":"The same report says the MUBES reviewed IMPM’s AD/ART and elected its 2021–22 leadership; it quotes Syuryadin as a pioneer who claimed IMPM had existed for seven years and says Firdaus was elected Duta Eksekutif while Taufik Hidayat was an outgoing officeholder.","period":"2021; Madawau, Bima","type":"Reported organization meeting and attributed self-description","source_ids":["S1167"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the meeting and titles; organization’s founding date and formal offices remain unverified.","basis":"No charter, AD/ART, attendance list, registration or signed election minutes accessed.","next":"Obtain AD/ART, registration file, MUBES minutes and officer roster.","year":2021},{"id":"C2111","text":"A January 2021 article describes Firdaus as IMPM chair and planned cadre cohort XI, while a June 2021 article describes a cohort V. The numbering conflict is not resolved and could reflect different cohort labels, plans or reporting error.","period":"2021-01 to 2021-06; Madawau","type":"Conflicting reports about organizational sequence","source_ids":["S1165","S1168"],"confidence":"High that the two publications use these different numbers; what each number counts is unknown.","basis":"No cohort lists, course reports or annual recruitment registers accessed.","next":"Obtain recruitment invitations and participant lists for the 2021 cohorts and earlier cadre classes.","year":2021},{"id":"C2112","text":"A 2021 IMPM report says the organization sent request 009/P/IMPM/X/2021 for BNN Bima to speak at a seminar planned for 14 October; a BNN officer said the request had been received. The report does not prove the seminar took place.","period":"2021-10; Kabupaten Bima","type":"Reported organizational activity and agency acknowledgment","source_ids":["S1169"],"confidence":"High that the report records the request and BNN response; event completion is unverified.","basis":"No original letter, agenda, attendance list or BNN activity report accessed.","next":"Request the letter and BNN seminar activity/attendance record.","year":2021},{"id":"C2113","text":"In a June 2022 interview, Syuryadin, also called Surya Ghempar, self-reported graduating from SDN Inpres Tololara in 2006 and publicly criticized the school’s conditions; a July article quotes his claim that a land claim was affecting school funding and facilities.","period":"2022-06/07; Madawau","type":"Self-reported biographical detail and attributed advocacy claim","source_ids":["S1170","S1171"],"confidence":"High that the reports attribute these statements to him; education and property claims are not independently verified.","basis":"No school register, school asset inventory, land-title or budget records reviewed.","next":"Obtain school alumni/employee records, asset register, land parcel and relevant administrative or court files.","year":2022},{"id":"C2114","text":"Bimakini reported that a 6 February 2020 Madawau BUMDes meeting ended in an altercation after a disagreement involving SR and meeting chair Junaidin; the account attributed to the police chief says three people were injured and four people were detained at that time.","period":"2020-02-06; Madawau","type":"Contemporaneous reported altercation and police account","source_ids":["S1172"],"confidence":"High that the report records the police account; responsibility, injury details and legal outcome are not independently established.","basis":"Direct article access was blocked; no police, clinic, charge or court records located.","next":"Request police report, medical records, charging decision and court/police disposition.","year":2020},{"id":"C2115","text":"A report on the 26 October 2020 Dena road blockade attributes to protester Denis allegations of BUMDes/Gapoktan Samakai mismanagement, KUR deductions at BNI and non-subsidized fertilizer; village head Abdul Haris said he would summon the parties to clarify.","period":"2020-10-26; Dena, Madapangga","type":"Reported protest and competing attributed statements","source_ids":["S1173"],"confidence":"High that the article records the protest and statements; the financial and fertilizer allegations remain unverified.","basis":"No BUMDes accounts, loan documents, delivery records, inquiry or subsequent hearing result accessed.","next":"Obtain BUMDes/Gapoktan accounts, farmer applications, BNI response, fertilizer manifests and village hearing minutes.","year":2020},{"id":"C2116","text":"On 27 January 2021, Pemuda Peduli Desa (PPD), represented by coordinator Fan Mahayus, demonstrated at Dena village office and demanded revocation of decisions concerning BUMDes and Gapoktan Samakai and a meeting with CV Rahmawati and BNI; the article says an open hearing was planned for the next day.","period":"2021-01-27/28; Dena, Madapangga","type":"Reported demonstration and stated demands","source_ids":["S1174"],"confidence":"High that the archive article reports these demands and planned hearing; no evidence of the hearing’s result.","basis":"Archive search excerpt reviewed; no hearing minutes or follow-up located.","next":"Find 28 January hearing minutes, invitations, village decisions and any later complaint/administrative outcome.","year":2021},{"id":"C2117","text":"A 17 October 2021 report records delayed-KUR complaints from farmers in Dena and Palama, Madapangga; farmer Martinus from Palama said he had signed in September and waited nearly a month. BNI’s response and individual loan records were not included.","period":"2021-10-17; Dena and Palama, Madapangga","type":"Contemporaneous report of attributed borrower complaint","source_ids":["S1175"],"confidence":"High that the article publishes the complaint; delay and bank handling are not independently verified.","basis":"No borrower file, loan contract or bank response; do not treat these farmers as IMPM’s named constituency.","next":"Obtain anonymized case-level application dates, bank response and eventual disbursement/cancellation status.","year":2021},{"id":"C2118","text":"IMPM demonstrated at BNI Cabang Bima on 20 October 2021. Coordinator Muhammad Firdaus alleged that farmers in several Bima subdistricts, especially Madawau, had repaid previous KUR on time but their later applications were not disbursed before planting.","period":"2021-10-20; Kota Bima/Madawau","type":"Reported demonstration and attributed allegation","source_ids":["S1176","S1177"],"confidence":"High that the two reports describe the demonstration and quote IMPM; borrower-level facts remain unverified.","basis":"Neither report identifies borrowers or reproduces application files; BNI response not included.","next":"Obtain IMPM petition, borrower consented records, BNI written response and any subsequent disbursement decisions.","year":2021},{"id":"C2119","text":"The Bimakini and Media Dinamika Global reports cover the same IMPM demonstration and rely on organizer statements; their appearance in two outlets does not independently confirm the individual loan delays.","period":"2021-10-20; Bima","type":"Source-independence and evidence-limit observation","source_ids":["S1176","S1177"],"confidence":"High; comparison of the two reports.","basis":"No named loan recipients or bank documents in either article.","next":"Seek borrower-level and bank records rather than counting the reports as independent verification.","year":2021},{"id":"C2120","text":"After IMPM asked DPRD Kabupaten Bima to summon BNI, Firdaus said the matter had not been resolved; DPRD secretary Edy Tarunawan said the letter was received but a hearing required leadership scheduling. The report gives no evidence that such a hearing occurred.","period":"2021-10-21 to 2021-10-25; Bima","type":"Reported petition and conditional institutional response","source_ids":["S1178","S1179"],"confidence":"High that the reports record the exchange; the later hearing status remains unknown.","basis":"No received-stamp copy, agenda, minutes or follow-up decision obtained.","next":"Request DPRD registry, commission agenda, BNI invitation and meeting minutes.","year":2021},{"id":"C2121","text":"LAPAS NTB held a separate protest at the OJK NTB office in Mataram on 21 October 2021 over KUR complaints involving BNI Cabang Bima; it is a different organization and event from IMPM’s protest at the BNI branch.","period":"2021-10-21; Mataram","type":"Separate organization’s reported protest","source_ids":["S1180"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the event; underlying claims are attributed to LAPAS.","basis":"No loan records or OJK complaint file obtained.","next":"Request OJK registry and LAPAS petition/borrower list; maintain separation from IMPM case.","year":2021},{"id":"C2122","text":"At a reported 1 November 2021 meeting with BNI’s Bima chief at OJK, LAPAS lawyer Muhammad Tohar said the bank’s explanation conflicted with field reports and alleged Rp550,000 deductions for BPJS, stamp and administration costs.","period":"2021-11-01; Mataram","type":"Reported meeting and one-side financial allegation","source_ids":["S1181"],"confidence":"High that the article attributes the statement to Tohar; the alleged deductions remain unverified.","basis":"No loan receipts, fee schedule or full bank response reproduced.","next":"Obtain the meeting minutes, bank response and borrowers’ disbursement slips.","year":2021},{"id":"C2123","text":"Bima Today reports that students first demonstrated about KDKMP Dena and burned a used tire outside the district education office, then met the Camat, police, military post and cooperative representative Wahyuddin at the subdistrict hall on 7 April 2026; student representative Opik questioned civil servants’ presence among cooperative managers, while Wahyuddin and the Camat said the matter was clear.","period":"2026-04-07; Dena, Madapangga","type":"Contemporaneous report of protest, meeting and attributed positions","source_ids":["S1184"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the sequence and speakers; no roster, formal decision or legal ruling is provided.","basis":"Direct HTTP article text read; no membership list, meeting record, cooperative registration certificate or official response located.","next":"Obtain the protest petition, meeting minutes, KDKMP Dena membership/board list, registration record and relevant appointment/assignment orders.","year":2026},{"id":"C2124","text":"Kemenkop Juklak 1/2025 defines KDKMP membership by village/kelurahan residence; it defines Pengurus as cooperative members elected in members’ meetings, requires Pengurus not to be from village leadership and bars specified kinship with other officers/supervisors; Pengelola may be a member or a third party.","period":"2025-04-12; national regulation","type":"Primary-source rule text","source_ids":["S1182"],"confidence":"High for what the official guideline says.","basis":"This does not determine whether any particular Dena person is a member, board officer, village leader or appointed manager; the cooperative’s documents were not examined.","next":"Compare the Dena cooperative’s AD/ART, membership and officer register with the full operative rule and any later amendments.","year":2025},{"id":"C2125","text":"PANRB’s official announcement confirms a national policy briefing on assigning regional PPPK to KDKMP under a joint circular; that staffing policy alone does not establish eligibility for a cooperative board seat or prove Wahyuddin’s individual assignment.","period":"2025-09-26; Indonesia","type":"Official policy announcement; limit of scope","source_ids":["S1183"],"confidence":"High that the ministry announced the assignment policy; individual application to Dena is unverified.","basis":"Original joint circular and Wahyuddin’s assignment decree not obtained.","next":"Obtain the joint circular, Ministerial Decree 1227/2025 if applicable, local tasking letter and cooperative appointment records.","year":2025},{"id":"C2126","text":"Bima Today attributes to Wahyuddin the statement that he is an ASN PPPK at Bima DPRD Secretariat and that cooperative formation has legal status; the report reproduces no identity, assignment or legal-registration document. Its reference to BKN “No. 7 Tahun 2026” differs from the 2025 date appearing in official descriptions of the joint PPPK circular.","period":"2026-04-07; Dena","type":"Attributed public biographical/legal statements and document-number discrepancy","source_ids":["S1183","S1184","S1194"],"confidence":"High that the article makes these statements; individual status and cited instrument application remain unverified.","basis":"No personnel appointment, registration certificate, original circular or full cooperative roster accessed.","next":"Verify Wahyuddin’s public tasking order, cooperative AHU certificate and the exact circular text and number.","year":2026},{"id":"C2127","text":"Official TNI publications report Dena KDKMP construction visits on 2 and 28 January 2026, a 31 January target and about 70% progress at the 28 January visit; these documents concern construction status, not the separate 7 April governance dispute.","period":"2026-01 to 2026-02; Dena","type":"Official construction status reporting","source_ids":["S1185","S1186"],"confidence":"High that the pages report a target and progress estimate; final completion certification is not shown.","basis":"The February page reports 70% after the target date; no completion certificate or audit report reviewed.","next":"Obtain completion/acceptance records and keep them separate from membership and management complaints.","year":2026},{"id":"C2128","text":"In the separate BNI KCP Woha case, RRI reports that nine farmers in Desa Tambe applied for up to Rp50 million each in 2021; eight reportedly received no funds and one received half. The official court record identifies first-instance case 30/Pid.Sus-TPK/2025/PN Mtr and Arif Rahman’s conviction.","period":"2021–2025; Tambe/Woha, Bima","type":"Court proceeding and attributed applicant account","source_ids":["S1187","S1188","S1189"],"confidence":"High that the court record and RRI report identify this case and first-instance disposition; applicant-level facts are reported in court/news context.","basis":"This case is not the Madawau KUR protest; case file and appellate judgments require separate review.","next":"Read the complete official judgment and related decisions; identify borrowers only from public court records and verify the procedural history.","year":2021},{"id":"C2129","text":"RRI reports that Arif Rahman was sentenced to five years, a Rp200 million fine and Rp159.18 million restitution, and that both he and prosecutors appealed; the MA directory now lists cassation 5342 K/PID.SUS/2026, but its disposition was not accessed.","period":"2025-11 to 2026; BNI KCP Woha case","type":"First-instance judgment and later-procedure status","source_ids":["S1187","S1188"],"confidence":"High for the first-instance judgment as reported and cassation listing; current final disposition unknown.","basis":"MA page was inaccessible directly; no cassation decision text was retrieved.","next":"Retrieve appeal and cassation decisions and verify finality, sentence and restitution status.","year":2025},{"id":"C2130","text":"ANTARA reports that a different 2019–20 BNI Cabang Bima investigation involved 1,634 listed farmers/livestock beneficiaries, 12 collection agents, nine suspects and a reported Rp39 billion audit figure; allegations included deductions and fictitious beneficiaries.","period":"2019–2025; Kabupaten Bima","type":"Police investigation and attributed suspected-conduct report","source_ids":["S1190","S1191","S1192"],"confidence":"High that ANTARA reports the police/audit figures; guilt of each suspect is not established by these reports.","basis":"These figures are not the Tambe case or the 2021 Madawau loan-delay complaint; no final court judgment was located in this pass.","next":"Find indictments and trial decisions, clarify the audited period and scope, and track each named or initialed defendant’s separate case.","year":2019},{"id":"C2131","text":"A 29 July 2025 ANTARA report says two collection-agent suspects had missed police summons twice in the larger 2019–20 Bima KUR investigation; a 15 November 2026 Detail NTB report says prosecutors were re-reviewing returned files and notes 790 of 1,634 beneficiaries interviewed.","period":"2025-07 to 2026-11; Kabupaten Bima","type":"Reported investigative status","source_ids":["S1192","S1193"],"confidence":"High that the two reports describe these procedural developments; disposition and exact file status remain uncertain.","basis":"Detail NTB’s headline says Rp35 billion while the body gives Rp39,089,800,000 with a duplicated billion unit; no current official case register was retrieved.","next":"Obtain police and prosecutor case-register entries, file-return letters, BPKP audit and any later court filings.","year":2025},{"id":"C2132","text":"Current evidence does not establish that the 2020 Madawau BUMDes meeting violence, 2020/21 Dena protests, 2021 Madawau KUR complaint, LAPAS NTB protest, Dena 2026 KDKMP dispute, Tambe/Woha prosecution and 2019–20 BNI Bima investigation share the same participants, loan accounts or underlying case.","period":"2020–2026; multiple distinct Bima locations","type":"Evidence-boundary observation","source_ids":["S1172","S1173","S1174","S1175","S1176","S1180","S1184","S1187","S1190"],"confidence":"High that no link was established in the materials reviewed; absence of a found link is not proof no connection exists.","basis":"Different dates, villages, organizations, projects and court files; no cross-case borrower or actor match established.","next":"Keep each matter separate unless records identify shared people, accounts, land or formal referrals.","year":2020},{"id":"C2133","text":"Media Dinamika Global’s publisher-maintained staff page lists Syuryadin, S.Pd.I, M.Pd as editor-in-chief and NTB representative.","period":"Undated page; accessed 2026-11-16","type":"Publisher self-description / reported public role","source_ids":["S1195"],"confidence":"High that the page lists those roles and suffixes; credentials and current tenure are not independently verified.","basis":"The page gives no clear update date and is maintained by the publisher itself.","next":"Seek the publisher’s dated masthead, press registration and independent biographical or credential records.","year":2026},{"id":"C2134","text":"The MDG directory separately lists “Firdaus Alias Wildan” in group development and “Firdaus, S.H.” as a Madapangga bureau representative; no reviewed source establishes whether these entries are the same person as the IMPM Firdaus, or whether the two directory entries refer to each other.","period":"Undated page; accessed 2026-11-16; IMPM reporting 2021–2024","type":"Identity-resolution boundary","source_ids":["S1195","S1167","S1168","S1161","S1162","S1176"],"confidence":"High that the page has two distinct listings and local reports use Firdaus in IMPM roles; identity links remain unverified.","basis":"Names alone and a shared locality do not establish identity; no IMPM roster or publisher employment record was reviewed.","next":"Compare dated staff records and IMPM membership/election documents; retain separate candidate identities until a direct identifier is documented.","year":2026},{"id":"C2135","text":"The June 2021 IMPM report explicitly calls Syuryadin “Surya Ghempar” and describes him as an IMPM pioneer; its self-maintained publisher directory separately lists Syuryadin in MDG editorial positions.","period":"2021; undated directory accessed 2026-11-16","type":"Public-role and alias evidence","source_ids":["S1168","S1195"],"confidence":"High for the alias and reported roles within these publications; the degree suffixes are not independently verified.","basis":"Both items are published by Media Dinamika Global; neither establishes that IMPM’s Syuryadin is the similarly spelled Madawau BPD secretary “Suryadin.”","next":"Obtain IMPM minutes/member roll and BPD appointment records; do not merge the two without direct identity evidence.","year":2021},{"id":"C2136","text":"The March 2021 MUBES report says the meeting reviewed AD/ART and elected 2021–22 officers, but gives both 13 February and 13 March as the event date; Firdaus is described as elected Duta Eksekutif and Taufik Hidayat as the outgoing officer.","period":"2021; Madawau, Bima","type":"Contemporaneous report with internal date conflict","source_ids":["S1167"],"confidence":"High that the article contains these statements; actual meeting date is unresolved.","basis":"No meeting notice, signed minutes or election record is attached.","next":"Retrieve MUBES invitations, signed minutes, member list and leadership handover record.","year":2021},{"id":"C2137","text":"January 2021 reporting describes IMPM’s cohort-XI training as a plan; June reporting documents preparation and an indexed report of an opening for cohort V. The numbers may designate different series or a changed plan, so they cannot yet be treated as a proven contradiction.","period":"January–June 2021; Madawau, Bima","type":"Organizational sequence with differing cohort labels","source_ids":["S1165","S1168","S1196"],"confidence":"High that reports use XI and V for those respective contexts; the relationship between the labels remains unresolved.","basis":"The June 29 article was available only as search-index text; no cohort ledger or training roster is available.","next":"Obtain AD/ART, training modules, dated recruitment announcements and participant registers for each cohort.","year":2021},{"id":"C2138","text":"Official TNI pages report a 2 January site visit with a 31 January completion target and about 70% construction progress on 28 January; a local report describes a 15 February thanksgiving after completion was claimed, and a 8 July regional report quoting the Bima cooperative service says 14 of 84 buildings were 100% complete and Dena received a symbolic key to an operational vehicle.","period":"2026-01-02 to 2026-02-15; Dena, Madapangga","type":"Construction milestone reports","source_ids":["S1185","S1186","S1197","S1199"],"confidence":"High that the sources report these milestones; government completion statement is attributed to the cooperative-service official, while formal acceptance and operation are not established.","basis":"The February local report was available only as indexed text and appears syndicated; the July report directly quotes a named department official. No handover certificate, Dena operating ledger or management roster was retrieved. Neither proves resolution of the 7 April dispute.","next":"Retrieve Agrinas contract close-out and acceptance, vehicle handover record, cooperative licensing, and dated operational/member records.","year":2026},{"id":"C2139","text":"On 17 November 2022, BPKP NTB told ANTARA it had not received a formal request to calculate losses in the Bima KUR case, although police had consulted the investigative unit; the article says police began investigation in 2021 and records 1,634 farmer/livestock recipients.","period":"2021–2022; Bima KUR distribution investigation","type":"Contemporaneous procedural status and attributed case scope","source_ids":["S1198"],"confidence":"High that ANTARA reported BPKP’s statement and the police timeline; original police and BPKP correspondence was not reviewed.","basis":"This is the November 2022 status only and cannot be used as the later case status.","next":"Obtain BPKP receipt/request records and police case register for the original audit request and scope.","year":2021},{"id":"C2140","text":"By 21 April 2025, ANTARA reported police had announced nine suspects in the 2019–20 Bima KUR investigation and cited a BPKP loss audit of about Rp39 billion; this postdates the November 2022 report that no formal audit request had yet arrived.","period":"2019–2025; Kabupaten Bima","type":"Later investigation status / timeline reconciliation","source_ids":["S1190","S1191","S1198"],"confidence":"High that the reports document different procedural points in time; exact audit scope and method remain unverified.","basis":"No primary audit report, case file or indictment reviewed. The two dated reports show a changing timeline rather than proving that either figure or scope is independently correct.","next":"Obtain the signed BPKP audit and police/prosecutor case chronology.","year":2019},{"id":"C2141","text":"Detail NTB reports that seven of nine suspect files were sent to prosecutors on 26 March 2026 and later returned for re-review; files for Sri Rahmawati and Eka Hairani were still being completed, with 14 BNI personnel, 12 collection agents and 790 of 1,634 borrowers reportedly examined.","period":"2026-03-26 to 2026-11-12; Bima KUR investigation","type":"Reported prosecutorial and investigative procedure","source_ids":["S1193"],"confidence":"High that Detail NTB attributed these counts and file movements to the prosecutor; official file-transfer records were not obtained.","basis":"One local news report; no returned-file letter, prosecutor register or completed indictment was reviewed.","next":"Request the prosecutor’s case register, file-return directions, police resubmission date and final P-21 or equivalent status.","year":2026},{"id":"C2142","text":"The 15 November 2026 report names seven alleged suspects and gives their reported roles: M Amin (former BNI Bima head), Irfan Mansyur (former credit division head), Damhuri (former marketing-business head), Dedy (former Bima Regency DPRD member, 2019–24), Ismail (ASN at UPTD Dikbudpora Parado), Ikhsan (former acting head of SMAN 1 Woha), and M Isnaini (BNI Bima employee).","period":"2019–2026; Bima KUR investigation","type":"Reported identities and alleged procedural status","source_ids":["S1193"],"confidence":"High that the report attributed these names and roles to the case; low for independent verification of each role or identity linkage.","basis":"The article calls them suspects; no individual conviction or indictment was reviewed. Mention in an investigation is not proof of guilt.","next":"Check official suspect notices, indictment documents and separate court dockets for each named person; avoid inferring roles from initials or similarly named people.","year":2019},{"id":"C2143","text":"The amount in the latest Detail NTB account is internally inconsistent: its headline says Rp35 billion while its body prints Rp39,089,800,000 followed by a duplicated billion unit; earlier ANTARA reports cite about Rp39 billion.","period":"2025–2026; Bima KUR investigation","type":"Source-text discrepancy","source_ids":["S1191","S1193"],"confidence":"High that the published wording conflicts; the final audited amount is not independently verified.","basis":"The original BPKP audit has not been accessed, and the local report may contain a typographical or unit error.","next":"Compare the exact amount and period in the signed BPKP audit, police evidence summary and eventual indictment.","year":2025},{"id":"C2144","text":"On 8 July 2026, Ekbis NTB quoted the head of Bima Regency’s Cooperative Institutional Affairs saying 14 of 84 KDKMP buildings had been completed; Desa Dena and Desa Pesa were represented at a symbolic operational-vehicle key handover at HJB 386. The official said cooperative operations would rely on member capital and planned soft loans.","period":"2026-07-08; Kabupaten Bima, including Dena","type":"Reported government project status and public handover event","source_ids":["S1199"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the official’s statements; actual Dena cooperative operations and financial transactions are unverified.","basis":"The report provides no signed building acceptance, vehicle receipt, cooperative accounts, business activity or roster. “Symbolic handover” should not be described as proof of active operations.","next":"Get handover certificates, member/manager records and operating accounts; check whether Dena appeared in official program monitoring records.","year":2026},{"id":"C2145","text":"Kampung Media’s 2 June 2026 report quotes NTB Cooperative Service official Rahmadin saying 149 KDKMP had begun operating province-wide and 51 KDKMP outlets were 100% complete; the report gives no unit-by-unit list or definition of “operating.”","period":"2026-06-02; NTB province","type":"Attributed provincial program status","source_ids":["S1200"],"confidence":"High that the portal reports the official’s figures; independent verification and per-cooperative status are unavailable.","basis":"No transcript, provincial dataset, or list of the 149 units is attached; the aggregate does not establish Desa Dena’s operating status.","next":"Review the RRI Mataram audio/transcript and provincial cooperative registry/report with a dated unit-level list.","year":2026},{"id":"C2146","text":"The same report says KDMP Ncandi in Madapangga supplied 250 kg of rice and 30 kg of fruit per week to SPPG Amanah Dena, located in Desa Dena. The reported supplier is Ncandi’s cooperative; the receiving nutrition service point is in Desa Dena, so this is not evidence that Desa Dena KDKMP was the supplier.","period":"2026-06-02; Madapangga, Bima","type":"Reported cooperative-to-SPPG supply relationship / entity boundary","source_ids":["S1200"],"confidence":"High that the published report states this supplier, delivery point and weekly quantities; transaction-level proof not reviewed.","basis":"No supply contract, order, invoice, delivery slip, payment or direct SPPG response is attached. Do not merge with the separate Dena village KDKMP governance/building record.","next":"Obtain KDMP Ncandi and SPPG Amanah Dena registration, contract, invoices, delivery and payment records.","year":2026},{"id":"C2147","text":"BPS Bolo District in Figures 2025 reports 14 named villages for 2024 and does not list Dena.","period":"2024; Bolo, Bima","type":"Administrative village roster","source_ids":["S1201"],"confidence":"High for the roster printed in the BPS yearbook; does not establish later administrative changes.","basis":"Table 1.1 p.8; area data sourced from BPS Village Potential Data (Podes).","next":"Compare with the next yearbook and current Ministry of Home Affairs village-code register.","year":2024},{"id":"C2148","text":"BPS Mada Pangga District in Figures 2025 lists Dena among 11 villages in Madapangga for 2024.","period":"2024; Madapangga, Bima","type":"Administrative village roster","source_ids":["S1202"],"confidence":"High for the printed 2024 roster.","basis":"Table 1.1 p.8, BPS district yearbook.","next":"Check current administrative code data if a post-2024 boundary change is alleged.","year":2024},{"id":"C2149","text":"BPS Bolo District in Figures 2026, using 2025 data, again lists 14 Bolo villages and does not include Dena.","period":"2025 data; Bolo, Bima","type":"Latest retrieved administrative village roster","source_ids":["S1203"],"confidence":"High for the roster in the 2026 BPS yearbook; it does not itself record events after the 2025 data year.","basis":"Table 1.1 p.8 and geography description p.4.","next":"Obtain any 2026 village-creation or boundary instrument if one is claimed.","year":2025},{"id":"C2150","text":"Bima Regency’s SI-KADES coordinate endpoint returns a Dena entry coded 5206132006 under Madapangga; the endpoint returned only 35 records and is not a complete village roster.","period":"Accessed 2026-11-16; Bima Regency","type":"Positive administrative identification and endpoint limitation","source_ids":["S1204","S1205"],"confidence":"High for the returned record and official village website identifying Dena as Madapangga; no negative inference from missing entries.","basis":"JSON fields name the district and village code; the Dena government site gives the same district. The API result is a partial 35-record response.","next":"Retrieve the complete 2026 Kemendagri village-code register and any Bima Regency boundary instruments.","year":2026},{"id":"C2151","text":"The Ministry of Tourism SISPARNAS profile for Kolam Renang Farzadit Family labels the location Desa Dena, Kecamatan Bolo and says the profile was verified by the tourism office.","period":"Undated profile; accessed 2026-11-16","type":"Conflicting database locality label","source_ids":["S1206"],"confidence":"High that the profile states this; low as proof of administrative jurisdiction.","basis":"The profile provides no verification date or underlying administrative source, and BPS lists no Dena village among Bolo’s 14 villages.","next":"Check SISPARNAS data-entry/verification history and the local tourism-office source record.","year":2026},{"id":"C2152","text":"Local reports identify the same Farzadit pool differently: BimaNews (2021) says Dena-Bolo, while IDN Times (2023) and RRI Mataram (2026) place it in Dena-Madapangga.","period":"2021–2026; Farzadit pool, Bima","type":"Cross-source place-label discrepancy","source_ids":["S1208","S1209","S1210"],"confidence":"High that the publications use these labels; the underlying venue location is better supported as Dena-Madapangga by BPS and village-government records.","basis":"The 2021 article and SISPARNAS listing may share a label error; news reports are not boundary records, and RRI’s indexed text is incomplete.","next":"Check the pool’s coordinates against official Dena village boundary data and ask the Bima tourism office for its source record.","year":2021},{"id":"C2153","text":"Gentra’s 5 July report describes the KDKMP vehicle-key recipients as Desa Dena (Kecamatan Bolo) and Desa Pesa (Kecamatan Wawo); EkbisNTB’s 8 July report quotes a Bima cooperative official identifying Dena as Kecamatan Madapangga and Pesa as Wawo.","period":"2026-07-05 to 2026-07-08; Bima Regency","type":"Conflicting event-location reporting","source_ids":["S1199","S1207"],"confidence":"High that the two reports use different administrative labels; the underlying handover recipient is not independently documented.","basis":"Neither article reproduces a signed vehicle receipt or full recipient list. EkbisNTB attributes the Madapangga identification to a named county official; Gentra gives no named Dena representative.","next":"Obtain the HJB 386 handover certificate, vehicle registry, recipient cooperative identifier and event attendance record.","year":2026},{"id":"C2154","text":"The current official Bima village records place the administrative Desa Dena in Madapangga; Bolo yearbooks for 2024 and 2025 list no Dena village. Gentra’s “Desa Dena, Kecamatan Bolo” is therefore likely a location-label error, but the specific July vehicle recipient remains unverified without a primary handover record.","period":"2024–2026; Dena / Bolo / Madapangga, Bima","type":"Evidence-based locality inference with event-identity limitation","source_ids":["S1201","S1202","S1203","S1204","S1205","S1199","S1207"],"confidence":"High for the retrieved village rosters; medium for concluding which cooperative the event article intended.","basis":"The tourism database and 2021 BimaNews article repeat a Dena-Bolo label for Farzadit pool, but this does not establish a second administrative Dena village. No 2026 handover certificate was accessed.","next":"Confirm with Bima Regency, the recipient’s cooperative registration, and signed vehicle handover documents; retain both published labels until primary records are obtained.","year":2024},{"id":"C2155","text":"RGASPI’s online catalog describes a Semaoen personal file as F.495/op.214/file 71/document 1 and indexes Sema(o)en/Semaun Adil Khan, John, Kasim Vasily Semenovich and Musso Manowar as aliases. This is a finding-aid statement, not confirmation that all names identify the historical Semaoen.","period":"Undated Comintern personal-file metadata","type":"Archival catalog description","source_ids":["S1214"],"confidence":"High for the catalog fields; low for identity claims beyond the file title","basis":"The online description exposes metadata only; no sheets or underlying document images were read.","next":"Request the full item, cover, alias register and original language; compare each name against dated letters and travel/police records.","year":null},{"id":"C2156","text":"The same RGASPI inventory separately catalogs a 364-leaf personal file for Musso Manowar at F.495/op.214/file 55.","period":"Undated Comintern catalog; file itself undated","type":"Archival catalog description","source_ids":["S1215"],"confidence":"High for the catalog record","basis":"The 364 leaves were not accessed.","next":"Read the Musso and Semaoen files side by side and check original cover titles, internal name slips and file rearrangement history.","year":null},{"id":"C2157","text":"The online catalog links Musso Manowar as an alias of Semaoen in one record while separately cataloging Musso Manowar’s personal file; Stutje’s account treats Semaoen and Musso as distinct contemporary actors. This is an unresolved catalog-identity conflict, not grounds to merge the people.","period":"1920s; modern catalog metadata","type":"Archival identity discrepancy","source_ids":["S1212","S1214","S1215","S1213"],"confidence":"High that the published catalog records conflict; moderate-high that Semaoen and Musso are distinct historical actors","basis":"No original RGASPI personal files were read; the online alias field may be an archival cataloging or data-linkage error.","next":"Inspect both RGASPI files and contemporary congress, Secretariat and correspondence documents that name Semaoen and Musso separately.","year":1920},{"id":"C2158","text":"RGASPI’s online finding aid describes an 18-leaf Semaoen letter and article to the Comintern International Women’s Secretariat, dated 5 April 1926, on party work among Indonesian women and the women’s movement; it lists Russian and English.","period":"1926-04-05","type":"Archival catalog description / women’s movement correspondence locator","source_ids":["S1217"],"confidence":"High for the finding-aid description; no confidence assigned to contents not seen","basis":"The entry is metadata only; language fields do not show which leaves are in which language or whether the text is complete.","next":"Request F.507/op.3/file 79, all 18 leaves, translation/registration sheets and Secretariat replies; then compare with Indonesian women’s groups and local organizations.","year":1926},{"id":"C2159","text":"Stutje cites IISH Archief Komintern–Partai Komunis Indonesia, inventory 10, as containing 1924 letters from Semaoen to the Comintern; he uses them to discuss Semaoen’s political networks and his efforts to recruit Indonesian students for Moscow study.","period":"1924 letters; dissertation published 2016","type":"Scholarly archival citation and interpretation","source_ids":["S1212"],"confidence":"High that the dissertation identifies this archival file group; moderate for its interpretation pending the letters","basis":"The IISH letters themselves were not opened; Stutje also cites colonial-security files which may have their own informant bias.","next":"Request IISH ARCH01744, inventory/folder 10 and compare Semaoen’s letters with recipients’ replies, travel and university enrolment records.","year":1924},{"id":"C2160","text":"Xie’s chapter cites Semaoen’s 3 June 1926 “Something after the discussions in the British Sub-Secretariat” in IISH Archief Komintern–Partai Komunis Indonesia, ARCH01744, folder 32, alongside a 3 June Indonesian programme draft in folder 2.","period":"1926-06-03","type":"Scholarly archival locator","source_ids":["S1213"],"confidence":"High for the locator as printed in the chapter excerpt; moderate for the documents’ content until read","basis":"The original folder and a directly accessible full chapter were not obtained in this access path.","next":"Request folders 2 and 32; transcribe the programme and Semaoen paper and compare their drafts, distribution and later Secretariat minutes.","year":1926},{"id":"C2161","text":"Stutje reports that Semaoen drew up a seven-page “organisation plan for our national movement” on 23 November 1926, proposing autonomous social structures, self-help organizations, a shadow cabinet and coordinated armed action; Stutje cites a Dutch colonial secret-file entry.","period":"1926-11-23","type":"Scholarly reconstruction from cited colonial-security documents","source_ids":["S1212"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the description in Stutje; moderate for the underlying plan pending the cited file","basis":"The plan itself is not reproduced in the reviewed pages; its source is a Dutch colonial-security record and the interpretation may reflect police access or paraphrase.","next":"Obtain NL-HaNA 2.10.36.51, inv. 291, item G4 (8 March 1927), including annexes; seek Semaoen’s own copy and Hatta’s response.","year":1926},{"id":"C2162","text":"Hatta’s 1975 oral-history transcript recalls that he and Semaoen reached an agreement in The Hague in December 1926: Hatta says the Perhimpunan Indonesia would lead a future national movement/party and that the PKI would accept that leadership and hand over its organizational resources.","period":"1926 event recalled in 1975","type":"Retrospective oral testimony","source_ids":["S1211"],"confidence":"High that Hatta described these terms; moderate for the exact 1926 text and what was implemented","basis":"The transcript is Hatta’s recollection 49 years later; audio is marked unclear in the same passage and no signed convention is reproduced.","next":"Find the signed convention, Hatta/Semaoen drafts, the copy sent to Sudjadi and Dutch police seizure/interrogation records; distinguish agreement text from implementation.","year":1926},{"id":"C2163","text":"The signing date is unsettled in the sources reviewed: Hatta’s 1975 interview says 6 December 1926 in The Hague, while Stutje dates the signed shorter plan to 5 December 1926.","period":"1926-12-05 to 1926-12-06","type":"Conflicting date reports","source_ids":["S1211","S1212"],"confidence":"High that the sources give different dates; low on the exact signing day","basis":"Hatta’s statement is retrospective; Stutje relies on later scholarship and Dutch archives but the document itself was not read.","next":"Locate the original signed sheets, filing stamps, letters dated around 5–6 December and contemporary Dutch reports.","year":1926},{"id":"C2164","text":"Hatta’s 1975 testimony says the arrangement remained in force beyond the immediate days after signing and recalls that Semaoen publicly revoked it on 19 December 1927; Stutje likewise distinguishes the 1927 public revocation from accounts that date its end to 19 December 1926.","period":"1926-12 to 1927-12","type":"Retrospective testimony and scholarly chronology","source_ids":["S1211","S1212"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the existence of a 1927 revocation report; moderate for the agreement’s legal/political duration","basis":"The transcript and dissertation do not replace the signed agreement or a direct reading of Semaoen’s 1927 statement.","next":"Locate De Tribune, 19 December 1927, p.3, “Verklaring van Semaoen”; compare with the convention and later correspondence.","year":1926},{"id":"C2165","text":"Hatta’s 1975 account says Stalin summoned and reprimanded Semaoen after learning of the Hatta–Semaoen agreement and that Semaoen was removed from a Comintern post.","period":"1926–1927, recalled in 1975","type":"Retrospective report of Soviet/Comintern action","source_ids":["S1211"],"confidence":"High that Hatta made this claim; low-to-moderate that the events occurred exactly as recalled","basis":"No Comintern minutes, personnel order or Semaoen letter was read to corroborate the story; Hatta’s interview uses uncertain and unclear passages.","next":"Search RGASPI/ECCI minutes, Semaoen’s personnel file, Secretariat correspondence, payroll/travel records and his later accounts.","year":1926},{"id":"C2166","text":"A 2024 BRIN article reports that in 1953 Iwa Kusumasumantri asked Sukarno to help persuade Soviet authorities to permit Semaoen’s return.","period":"1953; reported in 2024","type":"Scholarly report of a permission request","source_ids":["S1218"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the published report; low for any inference that Semaoen physically arrived in 1953","basis":"The request letter and Soviet response were not read. A request for permission is not proof of approval, passport/nationality change or arrival.","next":"Find Iwa’s request, Sukarno/foreign-ministry correspondence, Soviet reply, nationality and travel documents, passenger manifests and entry records.","year":1953},{"id":"C2167","text":"A 7 February 1955 Kedaulatan Rakjat search-index excerpt asks when Semaoen would be in Indonesia and says he was still in Moscow; it also reports a Murba nomination effort.","period":"1955-02-07","type":"Contemporary newspaper excerpt; direct scan inaccessible","source_ids":["S1219"],"confidence":"Moderate for the indexed excerpt only","basis":"The Perpusnas scan returned 403; the full article, exact nomination wording and any response have not been read.","next":"Obtain Khastara issue 110, year 10, p.2 through Perpusnas or microfilm; cross-check Murba candidate lists and Assembly records.","year":1955},{"id":"C2168","text":"Historia’s 2026 article quotes Leeuwarder Courant of 5 December 1956 as reporting Semaoen’s arrival the previous day, 4 December, and saying his Russian wife, two children and two grandchildren remained in Moscow.","period":"1956-12-04 to 1956-12-05","type":"Secondary quotation of contemporary Dutch newspaper","source_ids":["S1221"],"confidence":"Moderate; stronger than later undated summaries, but original issue remains unread","basis":"The direct LC page and its surrounding article have not been inspected; family details and whether arrival was at Kemayoran depend on Historia’s transcription.","next":"Retrieve Leeuwarder Courant, 5 Dec. 1956, “Semaoen Terug in Indonesië”; cross-check Indonesian arrival coverage and passenger/immigration records.","year":1956},{"id":"C2169","text":"Later sources give incompatible return chronologies: a 2023 journal article presents c.1953 as the return, the official literary encyclopedia says 1957, while 1955 press still places Semaoen in Moscow and Historia quotes a 4 December 1956 arrival report.","period":"1953–1957","type":"Conflicting retrospective and contemporary chronology","source_ids":["S1219","S1220","S1221","S1222"],"confidence":"High that the records conflict; moderate-high for 4 December 1956 as the leading physical-arrival date","basis":"The 1953 claim may collapse Soviet departure, a permission request or a citizenship process into physical return; the 1957 encyclopedia may be rounded or erroneous.","next":"Obtain passport/naturalization, Soviet exit, Indonesian entry, passenger list, contemporaneous Indonesian newspaper and the original 1956 Dutch article.","year":1953},{"id":"C2170","text":"The 1962 official yearbook reproduces Keppres 177/1959: it lists Semaun as a member of BAPEKAN, appointed by the August 13 decree with effect from 15 August 1959.","period":"1959-08-13 to 1959-08-15","type":"Official contemporary yearbook reproducing a presidential decree","source_ids":["S1225"],"confidence":"High for the appointment text as reproduced","basis":"This establishes member status, not chair/deputy chair or specific work performed. The signed decree image should still be obtained.","next":"Compare the official decree scan with BAPEKAN appointment register, first meeting minutes, assigned portfolio and attendance.","year":1959},{"id":"C2171","text":"ANRI’s BAPEKAN finding aid says the body operated from 1 August 1959 to 5 May 1962 and identifies Semaun as a member through dissolution.","period":"1959-08-01 to 1962-05-05","type":"Official archival finding aid","source_ids":["S1237"],"confidence":"High for the inventory’s institutional summary; moderate for individual attendance or ongoing role","basis":"The guide is an archivist’s retrospective description; it does not by itself prove Semaun’s attendance or work in any specific case.","next":"Read the appointment/dismissal file, meeting minutes, reports and member-assignment papers; compare Keppres 166/1962 and Perpres 3/1962.","year":1959},{"id":"C2172","text":"ANRI’s BAPEKAN index maps “Semaun” to item 351, a six-leaf file described as correspondence about Samadikun and Semaun being selected as BAPEKAN members of Depernas; the correspondence itself has not been read.","period":"1959–1961","type":"Archival finding-aid locator","source_ids":["S1237"],"confidence":"High for the catalog locator and item description","basis":"The title concerns two members’ Depernas selection; it does not show what Semaun wrote or that he personally handled the file.","next":"Request item 351 and neighboring items, cover, annexes and any appointment/nomination correspondence.","year":1959},{"id":"C2173","text":"Sekretariat Negara’s award list records “SAMAUN,” former BAPEKAN member, as recipient of Bintang Mahaputera Utama, without a Presidential Decision number, bestowed on 3 February 1961.","period":"1961-02-03","type":"Official State Secretariat recipient register","source_ids":["S1226"],"confidence":"High for the entry in the official register; moderate-high for identifying “SAMAUN” as Semaoen","basis":"The official register links the spelling through former BAPEKAN membership; no award dossier or individual citation was read.","next":"Retrieve any underlying conferment minute, award certificate, decoration registry and BAPEKAN service record.","year":1961},{"id":"C2174","text":"A scholarly chapter cites an ANRI BAPEKAN file for Semaoen’s Padjadjaran University honorary doctorate in economics and gives the ceremony date as 31 July 1961; ANRI’s presidential-newsreel guide separately lists footage of the award.","period":"1961-07-31","type":"Scholarly archival citation plus official newsreel locator","source_ids":["S1227","S1228"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the ceremony and degree; high for the archival/newsreel locator","basis":"The BAPEKAN file and newsreel were not viewed; the degree instrument, senate decision and precise title require primary records.","next":"Request ANRI BAPEKAN file cited by Fakih, Padjadjaran senate/rectorate records, diploma register and PPFN reel 470/cassette 172.","year":1961},{"id":"C2175","text":"参考消息 dated 10 November 1965 reports that Jakarta’s Api Pantjasila serialized articles attributed to Semaoen on 3–5 November 1965.","period":"1965-11-03 to 1965-11-10","type":"Contemporary Chinese-language report about Indonesian newspapers","source_ids":["S1229","S1230","S1231","S1232"],"confidence":"High that the Chinese newspaper made this report; moderate that the listed serial dates accurately match original issues","basis":"No original Api Pantjasila issue was read; exact authorship/signature, Indonesian wording, sequence and publication context remain unknown.","next":"Request Jakarta issues for 3, 4 and 5 November 1965 from Cornell or Library of Congress microfilm; compare edition/printing and byline.","year":1965},{"id":"C2176","text":"The 10 November Chinese report attributes to the articles criticism of the Aidit leadership and April 1965 party line, criticism of Beijing’s position, defense of the Republic, discussion of land policy, and a call to preserve NASAKOM or Soviet-Indonesian ties without PKI participation.","period":"1965-11-03 to 1965-11-10","type":"Positions attributed through a contemporary translation/press summary","source_ids":["S1229"],"confidence":"Moderate for what the Chinese summary says; low-to-moderate for exact original wording or complete context","basis":"The original Indonesian pieces, translator, excerpts/omissions and article bylines were not checked; “rightist paper” is the Chinese source’s label.","next":"Transcribe all three Indonesian issues, identify titles/bylines and compare the Chinese summary against the original language.","year":1965},{"id":"C2177","text":"Cornell and Library of Congress records list Jakarta API and API PANTJASILA as distinct serial titles; the Cornell guide identifies Yayasan Berdikari as Api Pantjasila’s publisher.","period":"1965–1966","type":"Serials-catalog identification","source_ids":["S1230","S1231","S1232"],"confidence":"High for distinct catalog records and described holdings","basis":"Catalogs do not establish political ownership, editor identity or the contents of the November 1965 issues.","next":"Inspect mastheads, publisher registration, editorial board and dated issues; keep the North Sumatra edition separate.","year":1965},{"id":"C2178","text":"CiNii catalogs a 1957 Semaoen book, Uranium Indonesia dalam zaman atoom: kiamat bikinan, published by Puspa-Baru in Jakarta; this verifies a bibliographic record, not the text’s contents.","period":"1957","type":"Bibliographic catalog record","source_ids":["S1235"],"confidence":"High for the existence of the catalog record; moderate for edition details pending the book","basis":"No copy was read; title/author metadata may contain cataloging or transliteration variation.","next":"Locate a library copy or Kyoto University microfiche and inspect title page, colophon and full text.","year":1957},{"id":"C2179","text":"A 1955 newspaper excerpt and a 1956 newspaper quotation both describe a Russian wife and children left in Moscow; RGASPI separately catalogs a Valentina Ilyinichna Semaun personal file. These sources suggest a family record but do not yet establish a verified marriage or complete family tree.","period":"1950s; file undated","type":"Contemporary press reports plus archival catalog lead","source_ids":["S1216","S1219","S1221"],"confidence":"Moderate for the reported family situation; low-to-moderate for identities and legal relationships","basis":"The newspaper scans and Valentina’s personal file were not read; later local interviews give variant names and child counts.","next":"Read RGASPI F.495/op.214/file 71/doc.2 and seek Indonesian/Soviet marriage, birth, residence, citizenship and family-register documents; compare the 1952 photograph source.","year":1950},{"id":"C2180","text":"A later local report quotes interviews with residents and a relative who describe Semaoen’s Pasuruan family ties and give conflicting names/counts for his children and wives; the cited 1959 PKI invitation letter was not shown in the report.","period":"Family memory reported in 2020; alleged letter 1959","type":"Retrospective oral testimony and secondary transcription lead","source_ids":["S1233","S1234"],"confidence":"High that the article records these statements; low for exact kinship, names, count or letter wording","basis":"The interview notes/audio, birth/marriage records, original 1959 letter and the historian’s cited copy were not obtained.","next":"Request the original letter, identify the interviewer/informants and compare civil, marriage, school, passport, Moscow residence and family papers.","year":2020},{"id":"C2181","text":"Ruth McVey, as quoted in a 2015 Ministry of Education publication, and later biographies place Musso’s birth in 1897 in Kediri/Pagu; the exact place-name varies between Pegu, Pagu and Jagung.","period":"1897","type":"Biographical fact / conflicting locality wording","source_ids":["S1238","S1249","S1250","S1265"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium; fact confidence: medium for 1897/Kediri area, lower for exact village; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The official book quotes McVey rather than presenting a birth register; later biographies may depend on the same source chain. No civil birth record was found.","next":"Seek birth, baptism, school, family and village administrative registers; identify what McVey’s p.169 cites.","year":1897},{"id":"C2182","text":"A 2024 article gives Musso’s birth date as 12 August 1897, but its cited basis is a later secondary account and no contemporaneous birth record is shown.","period":"1897-08-12","type":"Specific biographical date claim","source_ids":["S1265"],"confidence":"Source reliability: low for the specific date; fact confidence: low; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The article is internally inconsistent about family identity and other chronology; its cited source was not independently checked.","next":"Trace the cited Hikmah/Trisnando item and search civil, religious, school and family registers before using a day/month.","year":1897},{"id":"C2183","text":"Biographical accounts identify Musso’s father as bank employee Mas Martoredjo, while local family testimony identifies him as an adopted son of KH Hasan Muhyi/Rono Wijoyo and Nyai Juru; neither lineage is established here by a civil or adoption record.","period":"1897 and family recollections","type":"Conflicting family-history claims","source_ids":["S1246","S1250","S1251","S1252","S1265"],"confidence":"Source reliability: mixed; fact confidence: low for exact biological/adoptive relationship; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The reports use different informants and may repeat shared local traditions; one article makes an inference from language ability that is excluded.","next":"Obtain birth/adoption, marriage, mosque, school, land, inheritance and family papers; record informant identities and dates.","year":1897},{"id":"C2184","text":"Oliver Crawford’s scholarly article describes Musso and Alimin as graduates of the Batavia teacher training college and cites McVey for this statement.","period":"1910s / early 1920s","type":"Educational biographical claim","source_ids":["S1238","S1249","S1256"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for Crawford’s publication; fact confidence: medium-high, because Crawford’s cited basis is McVey rather than a school register; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The article supports the educational claim but does not provide Musso’s individual enrollment or graduation file.","next":"Locate Batavia teacher-training enrollment, examination and alumni registers; compare with school archives and contemporary biographies.","year":1910},{"id":"C2185","text":"Crawford’s account places Musso among Indonesians drawn into the ISDV through Sarekat Islam networks; the exact date, branch and office held by Musso require separate evidence.","period":"1910s–1920s","type":"Political affiliation / network claim","source_ids":["S1256","S1238"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for the scholarly account; fact confidence: medium; causal confidence: low for any claim that a specific organization caused later choices.","basis":"Crawford’s narrative concerns wider recruitment networks and cites McVey; it does not establish a complete individual membership card or uninterrupted affiliation.","next":"Search Sarekat Islam and ISDV branch minutes, membership rolls, newspapers, police reports and Musso correspondence.","year":1910},{"id":"C2186","text":"Kankan Xie reconstructs that Musso and Alimin left Java after the 1926 Prambanan meeting and reached Moscow in July 1926 for Comintern consultations; the precise departure itinerary rests on Xie’s archival reconstruction.","period":"1926-07","type":"Travel and Comintern contact","source_ids":["S1254","S1213"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for a full scholarly dissertation; fact confidence: medium; causal confidence: low for any inference from consultation to operational command.","basis":"Xie cites Comintern minutes and retrospective materials, but the underlying Moscow/Comintern files were not read in this tranche.","next":"Request IISH ARCH01744 and RGASPI meeting minutes; compare travel, border, party and police records.","year":1926},{"id":"C2187","text":"Xie dates the arrest of Alimin and Musso in Singapore to 18 December 1926, after British authorities intercepted their travel from Johor.","period":"1926-12-18","type":"Arrest and colonial policing","source_ids":["S1254"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for the dissertation; fact confidence: medium-high; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The dissertation cites contemporary press and British records, but those underlying records were not directly inspected here.","next":"Read the cited Bataviaasch Nieuwsblad report, Singapore police/court files and British Colonial Office correspondence.","year":1926},{"id":"C2188","text":"Xie reports that a Singapore court rejected the Netherlands East Indies extradition request on 19 March 1927 and that Alimin and Musso were subsequently released to continue to Hong Kong.","period":"1927-03","type":"Court action and release","source_ids":["S1254"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for Xie’s archival reconstruction; fact confidence: medium; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"Court record, release order and passenger record were not directly accessed; “released” and actual onward travel need distinct proof.","next":"Obtain Singapore court judgment, detention register, British Colonial Office files and passenger/immigration records.","year":1927},{"id":"C2189","text":"Morriën reports that Musso returned clandestinely to Indonesia on 16 June 1935, probably using an Australian passport, after preparations involving the Dutch Communist Party/Comintern network.","period":"1933–1935","type":"Clandestine travel / foreign-party support","source_ids":["S1244"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium-high as an archival-based study; fact confidence: medium; causal confidence: medium for logistical support, not for command over subsequent actions.","basis":"Morriën explicitly qualifies the passport identification as probable and cites earlier work and party/Comintern records; originals were not read.","next":"Inspect CPN trip-support papers, Comintern travel/finance files, passport or border records and Dutch PID surveillance files.","year":1933},{"id":"C2190","text":"Morriën describes Musso’s 1935 Surabaya base, contacts around the magazine Indonesia Berdjoang, a small clandestine PKI network and Comintern money for the publication.","period":"1935–1936","type":"Underground organization and funding","source_ids":["S1244"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium-high for the author’s archival-based account; fact confidence: medium; causal confidence: medium for the described support mechanism, unresolved for operational effects.","basis":"Morriën cites Comintern and CPN material, but does not reproduce the full source set in the paper; exact amounts and payers remain unknown.","next":"Find publication accounts, CPN/Comintern remittance records, print runs, correspondence, couriers and local police seizure reports.","year":1935},{"id":"C2191","text":"Morriën reports that Musso and Tamzil communicated with Dutch CPN contacts through covering addresses and couriers, with information relayed through Anton Struik in Moscow to Comintern offices.","period":"1935–1936","type":"Foreign communication channel","source_ids":["S1244"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium-high for the cited archival reconstruction; fact confidence: medium; causal confidence: medium for the channel’s existence, low for its influence on particular Indonesian decisions.","basis":"Underlying letters, courier identities and coded-name key were not read; the paper says some names and places used numeric codes.","next":"Retrieve the cited Comintern/CPN correspondence and key; trace each courier, covering address, date, recipient and response.","year":1935},{"id":"C2192","text":"Morriën reports that Musso left Indonesia on 11 May 1936 via Bali, Hong Kong and Shanghai for Moscow after the PID learned of his presence; the paper does not establish a formal arrest or deportation.","period":"1936-05-11","type":"Departure and colonial surveillance","source_ids":["S1244"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium-high for the archival-based study; fact confidence: medium; causal confidence: medium for PID discovery preceding departure, not for who decided the exit.","basis":"Morriën’s text reports PID knowledge and Musso’s departure; no original surveillance report or passenger list was inspected.","next":"Request PID surveillance and port records; trace travel documents and Musso’s post-return Comintern report.","year":1936},{"id":"C2193","text":"Efimova’s archival study dates Musso’s departure from Moscow to early 1948, stay in Prague, and return to Indonesia on 11 August 1948.","period":"1948-early to 1948-08-11","type":"Travel chronology","source_ids":["S1240","S1241","S1243"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for Efimova’s documented research as reviewed by Sidel; fact confidence: medium-high for broad sequence, medium for exact date; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"Sidel reviewed Efimova’s archive-based argument; the 2014 book cites Soviet files, but those originals were not read in this tranche.","next":"Obtain RGASPI travel letters, Czech residence/visa and Indonesian arrival/port records; compare contemporary press.","year":1948},{"id":"C2194","text":"Efimova reports that Musso met PKI member and Republican representative Suripno and Dutch Communist Party leader Paul de Groot in Prague in May 1948 and sent Moscow a report with a document titled Fundamental Tasks of the Communists in Indonesia.","period":"1948-05","type":"International communist meeting and document","source_ids":["S1240","S1243"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for Efimova as summarized in a full scholarly review; fact confidence: medium-high for the reported document/meeting; causal confidence: medium for its role in later party policy.","basis":"The report and attachment are described through Efimova’s archival research; originals and Dutch/Chinese meeting records were not inspected here.","next":"Obtain Musso’s May correspondence, de Groot papers, Suripno files and Soviet Embassy/Foreign Relations Department copies.","year":1948},{"id":"C2195","text":"The Prague draft described party unification, a broad National Front, opposition to the Hatta government and a national-unity government including PKI representation.","period":"1948-05","type":"Political-program content as reported by a scholar","source_ids":["S1240","S1243"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for the review’s accurate report of Efimova’s reading; fact confidence: medium-high for the draft’s reported content; causal confidence: medium for influence on August decisions.","basis":"The exact text should be compared with the original draft, 13 August resolution and later reprints; a draft is not proof of implementation.","next":"Retrieve and collate the Prague document, Politburo minutes, Bintang Merah issue and all Jalan Baru editions.","year":1948},{"id":"C2196","text":"Sidel reports Efimova’s conclusion that Musso’s Prague plans were to a significant extent his own initiative rather than Moscow instructions; this conclusion concerns the Prague program and does not show that Musso lacked Soviet contacts or that no broader Soviet guidance existed.","period":"1948","type":"Historiographical interpretation / agency","source_ids":["S1240","S1243","S1238","S013"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high that Sidel accurately presents Efimova’s argument; fact confidence: medium for the underlying documentary interpretation; causal confidence: medium and limited to the program under discussion.","basis":"Poeze argues for international-communist links; McVey’s account relied on a sparse record. No direct operational order for the Madiun local action was opened here.","next":"Read the cited Soviet documents, Dutch and Chinese records, plus local Indonesian command/communications records; distinguish guidance, strategy, decision and execution.","year":1948},{"id":"C2197","text":"Efimova reports that a version of the Jalan Baru program was approved as a Politburo resolution on 13 August 1948 and that a version reached the Soviet Embassy in Prague in December through Suripno.","period":"1948-08 to 1948-12","type":"Party resolution and document transmission","source_ids":["S1243","S1242","S1263","S1264"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium-high for an archival-based monograph; fact confidence: medium; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"Resolution minutes and embassy copy were not directly inspected; article drafts and later printed texts are not necessarily identical.","next":"Retrieve Politburo minutes, signed resolution, original 1948 Bintang Merah, Suripno transmission record and Embassy receipt.","year":1948},{"id":"C2198","text":"The digitized Jalan Baru text represents later publication and reprint layers, while Efimova and Poeze report textual revisions and collective discussion; it is not a facsimile of Musso’s untouched 1948 manuscript.","period":"1948 draft; 1951–1953 publications","type":"Textual history / attribution","source_ids":["S1243","S1242","S1263","S1264","S013"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for the reviewed later scholarship and digital transcription; fact confidence: medium-high for existence of distinct editions; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"No 1948 draft, September issue or Politburo minutes was obtained; precise passages and revision dates remain unsettled.","next":"Collate all available versions line by line and identify authorship, editors, amendments and approval points.","year":1948},{"id":"C2199","text":"Anderson reconstructs the 18 September Madiun seizure as beginning with local FDR-linked groups and military actors; Musso, Amir Sjarifuddin and Suripno were away from the city at the outset and learned of developments through radio before travelling there.","period":"1948-09-18 and following days","type":"Local action chronology / military history","source_ids":["S1245","S1255"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for a detailed scholarly study plus a separate interested-party testimony; fact confidence: medium-high for leaders’ absence at initial seizure, subject to source review; causal confidence: medium that local conflict shaped escalation.","basis":"Anderson’s synthesis draws on regional/military materials. Aidit’s 1955 statement is a defense, not neutral corroboration. The exact sequence and local chain of command need original radio, order and unit records.","next":"Obtain Madiun radio logs, local military orders, telegrams, district-government records and dated testimony from all factions.","year":1948},{"id":"C2200","text":"In his 24 February 1955 courtroom defense, Aidit asserted that Musso, Amir Sjarifuddin and other national leaders were not in Madiun during the initial disarmament and provisional appointment, and that Musso’s group reached Madiun late on 18 September 1948.","period":"1948 events as recounted 1955-02-24","type":"Interested-party testimony","source_ids":["S1255"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high that this is Aidit’s recorded defense text; fact confidence: medium for the claimed arrival sequence; causal confidence: low for his broad conclusion that PKI/FDR had no seizure plan.","basis":"Aidit was defending the PKI before a court and contesting government charges. The statement establishes what he argued, not the full truth of the command question.","next":"Compare court transcript, radio logs, travel and meeting records, and independent local accounts.","year":1948},{"id":"C2201","text":"Anderson cites a 3 November 1948 Antara report that Musso was killed near Ponorogo on 31 October; a contemporary New Zealand newspaper reprint also attributed a death report to Antara.","period":"1948-10-31 to 1948-11-03","type":"Death report and contemporary press","source_ids":["S1245","S1248"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium for the original report through scholarly citation; fact confidence: medium-high for reported death date, lower for exact site and manner; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The Antara original was not opened; Papers Past page rendered only an iframe and search excerpt. These reports are close in time but may derive from the same news agency.","next":"Find Antara 3 November edition, military/police death report, body transfer and local burial records.","year":1948},{"id":"C2202","text":"Sources use different locality labels for Musso’s final encounter or death: near Ponorogo, Sumanding, and Desa Semanding/Sumoroto; current evidence does not establish whether these identify the same or different stages of pursuit, capture, death and body transfer.","period":"1948-10-31","type":"Place-name discrepancy","source_ids":["S1245","S1246","S1248"],"confidence":"Source reliability: mixed; fact confidence: high that published labels vary, low for exact site; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"Anderson relays Antara; Papers Past is a reprint; Wirawan cites local military/police reports whose originals were not accessed. Toponym spelling and district boundaries may have changed.","next":"Map historical villages and roads; obtain original reports, hospital register, local death/burial records and eyewitness testimony with source provenance.","year":1948},{"id":"C2203","text":"Wahyu Wirawan’s later paper gives a detailed account of Musso being found, escaping an initial encounter and being shot in a village bathroom before his body was brought to Ponorogo hospital; it attributes this narrative to military/police materials.","period":"1948-10-31; published 2017","type":"Retrospective local military narrative","source_ids":["S1246","S1260","S1261","S1262"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium for the paper as a source to the reported narrative; fact confidence: low-to-medium for the detailed sequence; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"ANRI file Penangkapan Musso, Soemadi’s report and the Police Directorate radiogram were not read. The precise capture/death scene is not independently corroborated.","next":"Obtain all three originals with folio numbers, issuing offices and dispatch/receipt registers; compare hospital and burial records.","year":1948},{"id":"C2204","text":"ANRI’s Ministry of Defense finding aid describes item 1802 as three original pages of November 1948 correspondence about an aircraft arriving at Maguwo that was “connected with Musso.”","period":"1948-11; archival metadata","type":"Finding-aid observation / retrieval lead","source_ids":["S1247"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for the official catalog description; fact confidence: high that the catalog lists this file, unknown for any aircraft event; causal confidence: not assessable.","basis":"Only the finding aid was read. It does not identify passengers, aircraft origin, purpose or whether the connection was rumor, investigation or confirmed fact.","next":"Request item 1802 and adjacent correspondence; inspect sender, recipient, attachments, aircraft identity and follow-up.","year":1948},{"id":"C2205","text":"Poeze’s selected endnote cites U.S. Consul John Hamlin’s English translation of a Musso mass-meeting speech, Singapore, 30 September 1948, NARA 856D.00B09.3048.","period":"1948-09-30","type":"U.S. consular reporting / archival lead","source_ids":["S013","S1258"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high that the scholarly note provides this locator; fact confidence: high for the existence of a cited report, not for the speech content; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The State Department file and translated speech were not accessed. Consular reporting proves U.S. collection/reporting interest, not operational contact or intervention.","next":"Retrieve the exact NARA file, identify original speech language, translator, collection context and distribution list.","year":1948},{"id":"C2206","text":"Poeze’s article notes point to Dutch ministerial records identified as Drees 164 and Beel 25 concerning the Musso–Suripno–de Groot contact, presented to Dutch ministers on 8 September 1948.","period":"1948-09-08","type":"Dutch government record lead","source_ids":["S013","S1259"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high that Poeze cites these documents; fact confidence: medium for the reported presentation, low for their contents until read; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"Full archive series, file titles and original documents have not been recovered.","next":"Trace Drees and Beel files in the Dutch National Archives and request the ministerial attachments and circulation copies.","year":1948},{"id":"C2207","text":"Anderson’s footnote on an alleged 17 September 1948 Cochran–Sukarno–Hatta bargain involving U.S. aid relies on oral information relayed by McVey and Anderson’s inference, not a direct U.S. government document.","period":"1948-09-17, alleged","type":"Causal allegation / source-chain assessment","source_ids":["S1245"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for Anderson’s disclosed citation chain; fact confidence: low for the alleged bargain; causal confidence: low.","basis":"No State Department offer, approval, minutes or diplomatic instruction was identified in the cited footnote.","next":"Search Cochran’s papers, State Department telegrams, Sukarno/Hatta records, and the exact McVey oral-history source before asserting an aid commitment.","year":1948},{"id":"C2208","text":"Soerjono’s retrospective text reports a route story connecting Prague, Cairo, Rangoon, Bukittinggi and Campur Darat; the passage is hearsay and does not establish Musso’s flight path.","period":"1948 return journey","type":"Retrospective travel report","source_ids":["S014"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium for the existence of the recollection in the article; fact confidence: low for the route; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The text reports information heard from another person and treats a Catalina aircraft as inference; no manifest, aviation or port record was located.","next":"Trace the original informant and obtain airport, immigration, flight, aircraft and Indonesian arrival records.","year":1948},{"id":"C2209","text":"Soerjono’s text and TEMPO’s later biography report an Indonesian family branch associated with Pagu and a son named Margono; these reports do not establish legal marriage, biological parentage or how this branch relates to other descendants.","period":"Before 1948; retrospective reports","type":"Family-history claim","source_ids":["S014","S1250"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium as retrospective reports; fact confidence: low-to-medium for the reported relationship; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"Some details in Soerjono are second-hand through Pirngadi; TEMPO’s narrative is compiled from interviews and prior accounts. No civil or school record was checked.","next":"Search marriage, birth, school, guardianship and family correspondence records; identify informants and test whether both works share a source.","year":1948},{"id":"C2210","text":"TEMPO’s biography reports a separate al-Gadri/Paulina-Sukarti branch and two children associated with Musso’s Soviet years; BBC Indonesia, through a third-party mirror, reports additional descendants and a different mother-name chain.","period":"1920s–1940s; later family interviews","type":"Conflicting family-history reports","source_ids":["S1250","S1253"],"confidence":"Source reliability: medium-low as a compiled interview narrative and mirror; fact confidence: low for exact names, counts and parentage; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The reports may overlap and do not cite civil, Soviet residence, birth or marriage records. The identity of “Sunar,” “Musman,” “Paulus/Mohammad al-Gadri,” “Mariana” and “Winarno” remains unresolved.","next":"Find Russian and Indonesian civil records, passport/household files, school registers, family letters and interview recordings; do not merge branches before triangulation.","year":1920},{"id":"C2211","text":"McVey’s 1957 study explicitly cautions that surviving 1948 documentation was sparse and sometimes unreliable and draws partly on post-1948 PKI reprints and later collections.","period":"1948; historiography published 1957","type":"Source-base limitation","source_ids":["S1238"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for the author’s stated method; fact confidence: high that this is her disclosed limitation; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"McVey’s work is an early and important scholarly synthesis, but its evidence base differs from later Soviet archival research.","next":"Read the full book and notes; map each 1948 claim to the later documents and editions now available.","year":1948},{"id":"C2212","text":"Efimova describes Soviet party files as fragmented and Soviet bureaucratic reporting as filtered, with inconvenient information sometimes omitted before reaching senior leaders.","period":"1940s–1950s","type":"Archival-method limitation reported by scholar","source_ids":["S1240","S1243"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high that the author states this limitation; fact confidence: medium for its application to each specific file; causal confidence: medium where the book links filtering to leaders’ knowledge.","basis":"This is Efimova’s archival interpretation, not a claim that every Soviet record is unusable or that any particular omission is proven.","next":"Compare drafts, routing notes, annotations, outgoing correspondence and Chinese/Dutch/Indonesian copies for each important report.","year":1940},{"id":"C2213","text":"An official 2015 Ministry of Education publication’s Musso biography derives key schooling, teacher-training, mentor and Tjokroaminoto-household details from McVey rather than from newly presented personal records.","period":"Early 20th century; 2015 publication","type":"Citation-chain / source-dependence observation","source_ids":["S1249","S1238"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for the visible citation chain; fact confidence: high for the dependence relationship; causal confidence: n/a.","basis":"The publication is useful as a government-hosted reference but should not be counted as independent corroboration of the underlying McVey claims.","next":"Inspect McVey p.169 and trace the original Dutch/Indonesian sources in that note.","year":2015},{"id":"C2214","text":"The institutional and party contacts reported for Musso differ by period: 1926 Comintern consultation, 1935 CPN/Comintern logistics, and 1948 Prague strategy discussions are documented in different source chains and do not by themselves prove one continuous operational command structure.","period":"1926, 1935–1936, 1948","type":"Cross-period inference boundary","source_ids":["S1254","S1244","S1240","S1243","S1245"],"confidence":"Source reliability: high for the cited studies; fact confidence: high that each period has distinct reported channels; causal confidence: low for an uninterrupted command claim.","basis":"The underlying organizations, personnel, documentary forms and objectives changed across these periods.","next":"Build date-specific communication matrices from originals before making cross-period causal claims.","year":1926},{"id":"C2215","text":"Later accounts conflict on Soemarsono’s exact birth date: 26 October 1921 versus 22 September 1921; no civil register read.","period":"1921; Kutoarjo","type":"Conflicting biography","source_ids":["S1276","S1278","S1279"],"confidence":"High that sources conflict; low for either exact date; later sources share memoir/interview chains.","basis":"Find civil, school or family register.","next":"Seek Kutoarjo civil, school, family, or personnel records; retain both dates until one is independently documented.","year":1921},{"id":"C2216","text":"ANRI item 561 catalogs Soemarsono alias Sudardjo Marsono Setjodiwirjo as a former Military Governor; it is a 1954 original folder with photos noted.","period":"1954; Indonesia","type":"Archival-catalog observation","source_ids":["S1268"],"confidence":"High for catalog text; folder contents unread.","basis":"Request the full file and photos.","next":"Request the 1954 investigation folder and photographs; transcribe names and identity statements from the original.","year":1954},{"id":"C2217","text":"ANRI item 295 describes the 1 March 1947 honorably requested discharge of Major-General Soemarsono.","period":"1947-03-01","type":"Archival-catalog observation","source_ids":["S1247"],"confidence":"High for catalog description; decision itself unread.","basis":"Retrieve item and personnel file.","next":"Locate and read the 1 March 1947 presidential decision and personnel file.","year":1947},{"id":"C2218","text":"ANRI item 640 lists Soemarsono, H. Moekti and Farid Ma’roef in a damaged allowance draft dated 9 June 1946.","period":"1946-06-09","type":"Archival-catalog observation","source_ids":["S1247"],"confidence":"High that catalog lists the draft; content and amount unknown.","basis":"Inspect item 640.","next":"Inspect the allowance draft and determine the office, rank, and allowance period for each named person.","year":1946},{"id":"C2219","text":"Sugiyama reports parents Setjodiwirjo and Toekinah; DetikX reports schooling, vocational courses, work for a Dutch firm and wartime publicity work.","period":"1921–1945","type":"Later biographical report","source_ids":["S1266","S1276","S1279"],"confidence":"Medium for recurring reports; no civil/school/employment records read.","basis":"Seek family, school, employer and Sendenbu/BPM records.","next":"Search school registers, employment records, and Japanese-era information-office records; identify the original interview basis.","year":1921},{"id":"C2220","text":"Later accounts describe Gerindo activity and Amir Sjarifuddin as a political mentor/contact; this relationship does not prove individual decisions or responsibility.","period":"Before 1945; Jakarta","type":"Reported relationship","source_ids":["S1276","S1278","S1279"],"confidence":"Medium for recurring contact, lower for exact intimacy/influence.","basis":"Find Gerindo records and correspondence.","next":"Locate dated Gerindo, Amir, and Soemarsono correspondence or independent participant testimony.","year":1945},{"id":"C2221","text":"Kemdikbud’s retrospective account says Angkatan Muda Surabaya was formed 25 August 1945 under Soemarsono and Roeslan Widjaja.","period":"1945-08-25; Surabaya","type":"Later official narrative","source_ids":["S1270"],"confidence":"Medium; formation minutes absent.","basis":"Find meeting minutes and membership list.","next":"Compare the cited official narrative with 1945 Surabaya newspapers and Angkatan Muda records.","year":1945},{"id":"C2222","text":"Kemdikbud separately places PRI formation on 23 September 1945; later reporting calls Soemarsono its chair. Angkatan Muda and PRI should not be silently merged.","period":"1945-09-23; Surabaya","type":"Retrospective account / identity distinction","source_ids":["S1270","S1276"],"confidence":"Medium; organizational continuity unresolved.","basis":"Find charters, dated minutes and membership records.","next":"Find PRI formation minutes, notices, officers list, and any merger or succession documents.","year":1945},{"id":"C2223","text":"DetikX reports Soemarsono’s late-October radio appeal and relays a 20,000 PRI member estimate attributed to Harsutejo; no broadcast transcript or roster found.","period":"1945-10-28; Surabaya","type":"Later report quoting secondary/oral sources","source_ids":["S1276"],"confidence":"Medium for reported leadership, low for exact speech and member count.","basis":"Find broadcast log, PRI roster and cited Anderson text.","next":"Search RRI scripts or recordings, contemporary press, and a PRI member roll before repeating the membership estimate.","year":1945},{"id":"C2224","text":"Tambaksari dates differ among 11, 13, 17, 21 and 23 September; later reconstruction supports 13 and 21, while Soemarsono’s memoir is reported to place Pasar Turi on 17 and Tambaksari on 21.","period":"1945-09; Surabaya","type":"Conflicting chronology","source_ids":["S1270","S1272","S1273"],"confidence":"High that sources conflict; medium for later two-meeting reconstruction; original press not read.","basis":"Get Soeara Asia, Soeara Merdeka, KNI notices and memoir.","next":"Review Soeara Asia and Soeara Merdeka issues, KNI notices, and the cited 1948 youth documentation.","year":1945},{"id":"C2225","text":"Later estimates report 500,000 and about 100,000 Tambaksari attendees; neither is supported here by a contemporaneous count.","period":"1945-09; Surabaya","type":"Conflicting estimate","source_ids":["S1272","S1273"],"confidence":"High that estimates differ; low for either number.","basis":"Find 1948 documentation, police estimates and venue evidence.","next":"Trace each estimate to its earliest printed source and test against venue capacity and contemporary reports.","year":1945},{"id":"C2226","text":"Lessmeister dates the Hotel Oranje/Yamato flag clash to 19 September and cites a 17 December British report saying one person died and the event was later mythologized.","period":"1945-09-19; Surabaya","type":"Scholarly reconstruction","source_ids":["S1267"],"confidence":"Medium-high for reconstruction; CAB original unread.","basis":"Retrieve CAB 120/647 and compare contemporary accounts.","next":"Obtain UK National Archives CAB 120/647 and compare its wording with Indonesian, British, and Dutch contemporaneous records.","year":1945},{"id":"C2227","text":"Kemdikbud’s retrospective account gives a more detailed Ploegman/flag sequence than Lessmeister’s cited British-report passage.","period":"1945-09; Surabaya","type":"Later official narrative","source_ids":["S1270","S1267"],"confidence":"Medium for what it says; precise sequence remains unresolved.","basis":"Compare contemporary records.","next":"Identify and inspect the retrospective account’s cited contemporaneous evidence; compare participant names and sequence.","year":1945},{"id":"C2228","text":"A Begandring article reproduces a page attributed to Penetapan Pemerintah 1946 No.9/Um about 10 November as Heroes Day; original gazette not located.","period":"1946; Indonesia","type":"Later legal-text reproduction","source_ids":["S1274","S1289"],"confidence":"Medium that later compilation carries a page with that title; original wording/status needs verification.","basis":"Retrieve official gazette and compare.","next":"Locate the official 1946 gazette or legal compilation and compare the full text and promulgation details.","year":1946},{"id":"C2229","text":"JDIH metadata records Presidential Decree 316/1959 dated 16 December and says it was amended by Keppres 67/1961.","period":"1959–1961","type":"Official legal metadata","source_ids":["S1275"],"confidence":"High for metadata; decree text unread.","basis":"Read both decrees.","next":"Read the full official texts of Keppres 316/1959 and 67/1961, including their schedules and amendments.","year":1959},{"id":"C2230","text":"Tirto attributes the 4 October 1946 BPKRI Heroes Day proposal, forum approval and Sukarno endorsement to Soemarsono.","period":"1946-10-04; Yogyakarta","type":"Later attribution","source_ids":["S1277","S1278"],"confidence":"Medium that attribution circulates; low-medium as established history without minutes.","basis":"Find BPKRI minutes, motion and correspondence.","next":"Obtain 4 October 1946 BPKRI minutes, the proposal text, participant list, and presidential correspondence.","year":1946},{"id":"C2231","text":"Tirto reports that the first 10 November commemoration took place in Yogyakarta in 1946; contemporaneous program and press not reviewed.","period":"1946-11-10; Yogyakarta","type":"Later event report","source_ids":["S1277"],"confidence":"Medium pending contemporary confirmation.","basis":"Find program and press.","next":"Search Yogyakarta newspapers, event programmes, and government notices from November 1946.","year":1946},{"id":"C2232","text":"ANRI RA-24 catalogs Pepolit staffing, instruction, transfers, payroll and printing records, including a July 1948 report on ideology lessons to soldiers.","period":"1946–1948; military","type":"Archival-catalog observation","source_ids":["S1247"],"confidence":"High that records are cataloged; contents and implementation unknown.","basis":"Request listed Pepolit items.","next":"Request the RA-24 Pepolit files and compare personnel, curriculum, payroll, transfers, and printing orders.","year":1946},{"id":"C2233","text":"Kemdikbud 2013 interprets Pepolit as political education whose expansion reflected left/Socialist influence; this is the author’s interpretation.","period":"1946–1948; Java","type":"Retrospective interpretation","source_ids":["S1271"],"confidence":"High that the interpretation appears; underlying files unread.","basis":"Retrieve cited Defense records.","next":"Check each archive citation in the 2013 study against the original file and identify the author’s evidence for political influence.","year":1946},{"id":"C2234","text":"Simatupang’s retrospective account names Soemarsono in Pepolit leadership and criticizes party influence; it is a participant’s view, not a legal finding.","period":"1946–1948; military","type":"Participant account/evaluation","source_ids":["S1281","S1247"],"confidence":"Medium for reported role; low for broad claim of party control.","basis":"Compare orders and staff files.","next":"Read the complete memoir context and compare Simatupang’s account with Pepolit records and other participants.","year":1946},{"id":"C2235","text":"1953 East Java history says Madiun action began about 3 a.m. on 18 September, ended by 7 a.m., and at 10 a.m. a radio report announced a Front Nasional government with Soemarsono as military governor.","period":"1948-09-18; Madiun","type":"Retrospective official account","source_ids":["S1269"],"confidence":"Medium for what publication states; logs/orders needed.","basis":"Find RRI and administrative records.","next":"Obtain 18 September Madiun radio logs, Front Nasional notices, and unit orders; compare the 1953 retrospective wording.","year":1948},{"id":"C2236","text":"Poeze/Schulte Nordholt describe Soemarsono as leader of the Madiun Action Committee and the Front Nasional government as regional, not a national countergovernment.","period":"1948-09; Madiun","type":"Scholarly reconstruction","source_ids":["S058","S1266"],"confidence":"High for authors’ interpretation; local minutes still needed.","basis":"Find local decrees and appointment lists.","next":"Trace the chapter’s underlying sources for the Action Committee and regional-government characterization.","year":1948},{"id":"C2237","text":"The 1949 report attributed to Soemarsono says he met FDR leaders around 10–11 September and was instructed by Musso, Amir, Setiadjit, Wikana and Harjono to disarm Military Police and Siliwangi units.","period":"1948-09; Madiun","type":"Participant account through later scholarship","source_ids":["S1266","S058","S1287"],"confidence":"High that scholars attribute this to the report; unresolved as historical command fact.","basis":"Report written under Dutch custody and later narratives changed.","next":"Retrieve the 11 November 1949 report, Dutch interrogation file, and its chain of custody.","year":1948},{"id":"C2238","text":"Sugiyama reports a 14-page De Madioen Affaire signed 11 November 1949 and records Soemarsono’s later claim that Dutch interrogators were present while he typed it.","period":"1949-11-11; Dutch custody","type":"Document lead / retrospective statement","source_ids":["S1266","S1287"],"confidence":"High that scholarship reports the claim; exact circumstances not independently verified.","basis":"Find the original, interrogation file and interview notes.","next":"Locate the report copy, custody/interrogation logs, and any contemporaneous record of its production.","year":1949},{"id":"C2239","text":"Sugiyama compares Soemarsono’s 1949, late-1990s/2000, 2002 and 2008 accounts; Poeze/Schulte Nordholt’s assessment of plausibility is their interpretation.","period":"1949–2008; memory","type":"Historiographical comparison","source_ids":["S1266","S058"],"confidence":"High that accounts differ; cause of change unresolved.","basis":"Get interviews and 2008 memoir manuscript.","next":"Compare the 1949 report, interview notes/recordings, 2002 interviews, and 2008 memoir line by line.","year":1949},{"id":"C2240","text":"Sugiyama reconstructs capture near Demak, Semarang trial, 30 July release, 29 October rearrest and 13 December escape from Sipayar prison.","period":"1948–1949; Java","type":"Later biographical reconstruction","source_ids":["S1266"],"confidence":"Medium; court and prison records unread.","basis":"Find arrest, court and prison records.","next":"Obtain Semarang court files, Demak arrest and transfer records, Sipayar prison register, and release/escape documents.","year":1948},{"id":"C2241","text":"Later sources report nearly fourteen years in Pematang Siantar and teaching; formal exile versus party discipline remains unresolved.","period":"1950s–early 1960s; Sumatra","type":"Later report/interpretation","source_ids":["S1266","S1279","S1280"],"confidence":"Medium for residence/teaching, low for legal status.","basis":"Get school payroll, party and police records.","next":"Search Siantar school employment, domicile, party, police, and municipal records to test duration and status.","year":1950},{"id":"C2242","text":"Wilson’s essay links Soemarsono to an OPD/Pagar Desa initiative during PRRI/Permesta but marks part of the exile explanation as his own suspicion.","period":"1950s; North Sumatra","type":"Later editorial interpretation","source_ids":["S1280"],"confidence":"Low for direct organizational link.","basis":"Find OPD roster and records.","next":"Locate OPD records and Pagar Desa organizational files; do not infer identity from shared terminology.","year":1950},{"id":"C2243","text":"ANRI RA-8B item 513 catalogs a December 1955 Pagar Desa legal-status file but does not identify Soemarsono or link it to his reported OPD.","period":"1955; Indonesia","type":"Archival-catalog identity boundary","source_ids":["S1286"],"confidence":"High for catalog wording; no personal/organizational link established.","basis":"Inspect item and find separate OPD records.","next":"Request RA-8B item 513 and determine its subject and relation, if any, to Soemarsono.","year":1955},{"id":"C2244","text":"Detention accounts conflict: Sugiyama places arrest around 1965, while a BBC mirror reports 1968; later sources give 1978 release and 1987 Australia move.","period":"1965–1987","type":"Conflicting biography","source_ids":["S1266","S1278","S1279","S1288"],"confidence":"High that sources differ; exact dates low-confidence.","basis":"No detention, release or immigration records obtained.","next":"Seek Indonesian detention, release, passport, and Australian migration records to resolve the 1965/1968 and 1978/1987 dates.","year":1965},{"id":"C2245","text":"YPKP reports Soemarsono died 8 January 2019 and says the author confirmed the news by calling a daughter; no civil certificate obtained.","period":"2019; Australia","type":"Memorial/family report","source_ids":["S1278","S1279"],"confidence":"Medium-high for date; location needs record.","basis":"Seek NSW death registration.","next":"Check Australian civil death registration and identify the daughter’s source or original announcement.","year":2019},{"id":"C2246","text":"FRUS d221 says Cochran was appointed U.S. delegate to the Good Offices Committee on 6 August 1948, replacing Dubois.","period":"1948-08-06","type":"Contemporary diplomatic record","source_ids":["S1282"],"confidence":"High for published text; not physical arrival date.","basis":"Compare with travel/GOC records.","next":"Compare the published FRUS record with the original appointment correspondence and Cochran’s own papers.","year":1948},{"id":"C2247","text":"Swift p.99 reports Cochran arrived Batavia on 9 August, after the alleged July 18–21 Sarangan meeting.","period":"1948-08-09","type":"Secondary chronology","source_ids":["S1257","S1282"],"confidence":"Medium-high; cited Berita Indonesia not directly read.","basis":"Retrieve newspaper and travel documents.","next":"Find Cochran’s travel records and contemporaneous Batavia press; use them to check the arrival date.","year":1948},{"id":"C2248","text":"Vailland’s 1951 account alleged a July Sarangan meeting and $56 million Red Drive deal; later scholarship rejects it, and no primary payment or meeting record was obtained.","period":"1948-07; Sarangan","type":"Contested allegation","source_ids":["S058","S1257"],"confidence":"High that allegation circulated; low that meeting/payment occurred.","basis":"Cochran timeline rules out his attendance, not all possible U.S. July contacts.","next":"Read Vailland’s complete publication and search Sarangan attendance, travel, authorization, remittance, and disbursement records.","year":1948},{"id":"C2249","text":"Lovett’s 17 September memo says U.S. policy sought to contain/eliminate Indonesian Communism through a strengthened Hatta government and feared Dutch intervention would unite nationalism and Communism.","period":"1948-09-17","type":"Contemporary U.S. policy statement","source_ids":["S1283","S1284"],"confidence":"High for intent in memo; low causal confidence re Madiun.","basis":"Policy intent is not operational command.","next":"Trace the policy statement through U.S. decision memoranda and compare with actions and Dutch/Indonesian records.","year":1948},{"id":"C2250","text":"Cochran’s 20 September telegram records Hatta requesting police equipment, ammunition and weapons, and Livengood discussing possible assistance after political negotiations.","period":"1948-09-20","type":"Contemporary U.S.-side diplomatic report","source_ids":["S1285"],"confidence":"High for telegram text; medium-high that exchange occurred as reported; no delivery evidence.","basis":"Find Indonesian notes, approvals, shipment and receipt.","next":"Search U.S. aid approval, procurement, shipment, receipt, and end-use records; keep request distinct from delivery.","year":1948},{"id":"C2251","text":"FRUS d268 attributes Sukarno’s broadcast to one listener; d269 reproduces the Republic’s statement about the 18 September armed change of regional administration.","period":"1948-09-19/20","type":"Contemporary reports with attributed sources","source_ids":["S007","S008"],"confidence":"High for publication/attribution; political claims not independently confirmed.","basis":"Get RRI transcript, signed communique and ANETA dispatch.","next":"Read complete d268/d269 documents and seek the original broadcast, communiqué, and independent local reporting.","year":1948},{"id":"C2252","text":"RA-24 items 1435–1439 catalog Sarangan/Madiun reports; RA-26 item 199 catalogs an October 6 tasking to Colonel Bambang Soepeno.","period":"1948-09–10","type":"Archival-catalog observations","source_ids":["S1247","S347"],"confidence":"High they are cataloged; contents unknown.","basis":"Request originals and attachments.","next":"Request RA-24 items 1435–1439 and RA-26 item 199; compare report authorship, dates, and underlying sources.","year":1948},{"id":"C2253","text":"RA-26 item 703 catalogs a September 18 order to retake Solo using the catalog’s disguised-Siliwangi phrase; items 704–705 catalog broadcasts and transmitter records.","period":"1948-09–11; Solo/Madiun","type":"Archival-catalog observations","source_ids":["S347"],"confidence":"High for catalog wording; originals and broadcast contents unknown.","basis":"Retrieve orders and RRI logs.","next":"Retrieve RA-26 items 703–705, including the order, broadcast scripts, and transmitter request; verify the catalog’s phrasing.","year":1948},{"id":"C2254","text":"The ARCH.02475 collection record identifies Piet/Pieter van Staveren as born in Rotterdam on 9 June 1925 and deceased in Assen on 30 August 2019.","period":"1925–2019","type":"Institutional archival biography","source_ids":["S1292"],"confidence":"High for what the catalogue states; birth and death certificates not examined.","basis":"One collection record; do not count the IISH and Ons Land renderings of the same archive as independent evidence.","next":"Check civil registrations and family records.","year":1925},{"id":"C2255","text":"The IISH archive description says the collection contains Van Staveren's letters to his parents and friends from 1946–1953, prison/court/release papers, pardon petitions, CPN candidacy papers and later additions.","period":"1946–1960","type":"Archival catalogue observation","source_ids":["S1291","S1292"],"confidence":"High for the collection description; underlying documents not read except selected scans in S1290.","basis":"Catalogues describe holdings; they do not establish the content of every document.","next":"Request the complete finding aid and inspect correspondence, court, pardon and release folders.","year":1946},{"id":"C2256","text":"The IISH profile associates Van Staveren with pre-war socialist AJC and post-war communist ANJV experience and reports contacts with SOBSI; it does not by itself establish a formal office or membership in each organization.","period":"Before 1947–1954","type":"Institutional collection profile","source_ids":["S1306"],"confidence":"Moderate as profile-level biography; underlying letters not read.","basis":"The same IISH collection underlies the profile; distinguish contact, experience and membership.","next":"Read dated correspondence, membership cards and organization records.","year":1947},{"id":"C2257","text":"The collection profile and Historisch Nieuwsblad report that Van Staveren was conscripted in July 1946 and arrived in the Dutch East Indies in January 1947.","period":"1946–1947","type":"Archival biography / secondary reconstruction","source_ids":["S1292","S1304"],"confidence":"High that both profiles state this chronology; service and transport registers not examined.","basis":"The historical article relies on the same personal archive and is not an independent documentary chain.","next":"Check conscription, unit and troop transport registers.","year":1946},{"id":"C2258","text":"Historisch Nieuwsblad reports that Van Staveren and associates organized anti-deployment activity among Dutch conscripts, including soldiers' councils and anti-war propaganda; the underlying organizing files were not located in this review.","period":"1946","type":"Secondary historical narrative","source_ids":["S1304"],"confidence":"Moderate as an attributed narrative; underlying papers and full group roster not read.","basis":"Article is based on the IISH papers and may select from that same source base.","next":"Identify co-organizers and locate soldiers' council records, leaflets and military discipline files.","year":1946},{"id":"C2259","text":"In the 1984 interview quoted by Historisch Nieuwsblad, Van Staveren estimated that roughly 3,200 conscripts did not return from embarkation leave and went into hiding, partly due to their organizing; this is his retrospective estimate, not a verified count.","period":"1946–1947; recalled 1984","type":"Attributed retrospective statement","source_ids":["S1304","S1307"],"confidence":"High that the article attributes the estimate to him; low as a numerical total pending independent enumeration.","basis":"A participant's late estimate and a film catalogue do not validate the number.","next":"Compare personnel/absent-without-leave lists and contemporary estimates.","year":1946},{"id":"C2260","text":"Historisch Nieuwsblad says a 13 February 1950 letter to Van Staveren's lawyer attributed his June 1947 decision to information from his friend Jaap Mol, who worked in an army transport unit, that maps indicated an impending operation into Republican territory.","period":"1947; letter dated 1950-02-13","type":"Later letter reported by a secondary source","source_ids":["S1304"],"confidence":"Moderate for the report of what the letter said; the letter and Mol's service record were not inspected.","basis":"The article summarizes a letter from the same personal archive; this records Van Staveren's own account, not proof that the planned operation caused his decision.","next":"Read the full letter and verify Jaap Mol's identity, role and access to planning information.","year":1947},{"id":"C2261","text":"Dutch archive copies and the Historisch Nieuwsblad account place Van Staveren's crossing to Republican territory during the night of 13–14 June 1947; the Republican police report copy gives 14 June.","period":"1947-06-13/14","type":"Contemporary report and later archival reconstruction","source_ids":["S1290","S1304"],"confidence":"High for the date appearing in both record chains; the Republican report is a copy held in a Dutch-collected file.","basis":"The accounts are not fully independent: the article also draws on the personal papers; the report is not an original field copy inspected in its original custody.","next":"Obtain the Republican original, crossing and unit records, and compare the full June 1947 correspondence.","year":1947},{"id":"C2262","text":"Historisch Nieuwsblad reports that four prominent Indonesian nationalists had provided a guarantee letter before the crossing; it does not identify them in the article, and the letter was not read in this review.","period":"1947","type":"Secondary report of a primary document","source_ids":["S1304"],"confidence":"Moderate that the article reports such a letter; identities, wording and route function remain unverified.","basis":"Do not turn an unnamed set of guarantors into a confirmed personal network.","next":"Find the letter among IISH correspondence, Republican records or Dutch police exhibits.","year":1947},{"id":"C2263","text":"A letter dated 18 June 1947, copied in the Dutch archive file, has Van Staveren tell his parents that Republic-side officials received him warmly and that he travelled to Poerwakarta in a TNI regimental commander's car and stayed at a garrison commander's house.","period":"1947-06-18","type":"Contemporary self-report in copied correspondence","source_ids":["S1290"],"confidence":"High for the contents of the surviving copy; moderate for the described arrangements because it is his own account.","basis":"A personal letter may reassure family and is not independent verification of his treatment or escort.","next":"Locate the original letter and compare with TNI transport, lodging and garrison records.","year":1947},{"id":"C2264","text":"A copied Republican Military Police report dated 20 March 1948 describes Van Staveren as a 22-year-old Dutch conscript who crossed on 14 June 1947 and recommends productive work for the Republic together with monitoring.","period":"1948-03-20","type":"Contemporary Republican police report, Dutch-held copy","source_ids":["S1290"],"confidence":"High for the text of the copy reviewed; moderate for the reported administrative action pending original and corroboration.","basis":"The report is a summary/record of findings, not a verbatim interview transcript; it was preserved through Dutch military collection.","next":"Obtain the original report and identify the bureau, interviewer and implementation record.","year":1948},{"id":"C2265","text":"The 20 March 1948 Republican Military Police report attributes to Van Staveren the view that he was being compelled to fight a nation seeking freedom and support Dutch domination.","period":"1948-03-20","type":"Interview summary / attributed motive","source_ids":["S1290"],"confidence":"High that the report records this motive; moderate that it accurately reflects his complete or exact words.","basis":"The report paraphrases an interview; motive evidence should be compared with his letters and the 1950 lawyer letter.","next":"Read the original and compare dated letters before, during and after the crossing.","year":1948},{"id":"C2266","text":"The Republican Military Police copy says that after the Dutch military offensive Van Staveren was taken to Yogyakarta and worked at the Gandjuran arms factory before later assignment; the sequence is the report's account, not independently confirmed here.","period":"1947–1948","type":"Contemporary administrative report copy","source_ids":["S1290"],"confidence":"Moderate for the reported assignment; underlying personnel/work records are absent.","basis":"One copied report with an administrative purpose.","next":"Check Gandjuran factory, Republican labour and military personnel records.","year":1947},{"id":"C2267","text":"The IISH profile says Van Staveren joined the Republican Youth Movement and had contacts in SOBSI; the personal archive's underlying letters and organizational registers were not read for this claim.","period":"1947–1949","type":"Institutional collection biography","source_ids":["S1306"],"confidence":"Moderate for the profile's summary; exact role and dates unresolved.","basis":"Same IISH collection chain; distinguish personal contact from official position.","next":"Identify the relevant youth organization unit, SOBSI branch and dated correspondence.","year":1947},{"id":"C2268","text":"IISH describes saved support postcards addressed to Van Staveren from J. Abdullah in Semarang and from a Pemuda Rakjat department in Sumatra, among others; the cards were not read individually.","period":"1950–1954","type":"Archival collection description","source_ids":["S1306"],"confidence":"High for the catalogue/profile description; individual authors, dates and wording require inspection.","basis":"Postcards are direct artifacts but the accessible page only describes them.","next":"Inspect originals or scans and verify sender identity and dates.","year":1950},{"id":"C2269","text":"Historisch Nieuwsblad reports a May 1948 naturalization request and the later name form Pitojo Koesoemo Widjojo; the Dutch military-police report copy spells an Indonesian name as Pitoyo Kusumowidjojo.","period":"1948–1949","type":"Secondary account and contemporary police spelling","source_ids":["S1290","S1304"],"confidence":"High that the two spellings occur in these sources; low that a legal name change or approved naturalization occurred.","basis":"No naturalization decree or identity document was located; spelling may be transliteration or alias variation.","next":"Find the application, decision, civil record and Republic identity papers; retain spelling variants.","year":1948},{"id":"C2270","text":"Historisch Nieuwsblad, summarizing Van Staveren's 1950 letter, says that in Madiun he was assigned an organizational and liaison role in Radio Front Nasional; this is his attributed retrospective account, not proof of command over the uprising.","period":"1948; reported 1950","type":"Attributed self-account in secondary reconstruction","source_ids":["S1304"],"confidence":"Moderate that he made this claim; exact title and chain of authority unverified.","basis":"Article's account relies on his own papers and does not establish a national command role.","next":"Read the full letter and locate Radio Front Nasional schedules, orders and staff lists.","year":1948},{"id":"C2271","text":"The 2.10.62 finding aid lists a 15 October 1948 PAM Madiun report about participation/role/experience and an 18 October arrest telegram among five physical documents concerning Van Staveren; originals remain unread.","period":"1948-10-15/18","type":"Archival catalogue observation","source_ids":["S154"],"confidence":"High for the catalogue wording and physical status; unknown which arrest or custody event the telegram concerns.","basis":"The entry's visible summary lists four dated pieces although it says five documents; identify the missing fifth and do not assume the October telegram refers to the 1949 Dutch arrest.","next":"Request all five documents and compare their dates, addressees and custody references.","year":1948},{"id":"C2272","text":"The 1949 Dutch military-police report describes Van Staveren as a Dutch-language broadcaster on Republican radio Gelora Pemuda and records his criticism of both Dutch policy and the Sukarno–Hatta government.","period":"1949","type":"Dutch military-police investigation summary","source_ids":["S1290"],"confidence":"High that the report makes these statements; moderate for underlying activity pending Republican broadcast logs.","basis":"Interrogation/investigation summary, not a transcript or neutral assessment; organization acronym in the scan/OCR is unclear.","next":"Inspect complete scans and locate Gelora Pemuda/BKPRI broadcasts, staff records and scripts.","year":1949},{"id":"C2273","text":"The same Dutch report lists contacts with Musso, Amir Sjarifuddin, Sumarsono and Djokosoejono and labels Van Staveren a committed far-left communist; these are the Dutch service's assessments and contact claims.","period":"1948–1949","type":"Dutch military-police intelligence assessment","source_ids":["S1290"],"confidence":"High for what the report asserts; moderate or low for each contact until independently verified.","basis":"The report was made in an adversarial interrogation/investigation context; labels do not establish party membership or operational authority.","next":"Compare Republican party and youth records, personal correspondence, and records from the named individuals.","year":1948},{"id":"C2274","text":"The Dutch report states that investigators could not establish armed action or sabotage by Van Staveren and did not identify him as an active participant in the central Sukarno–Hatta government.","period":"1949","type":"Investigation conclusion, attributed to Dutch military police","source_ids":["S1290"],"confidence":"High that the report states this; it does not prove that no such activity occurred outside the investigation's reach.","basis":"A negative finding is bounded by the investigators' evidence and purpose.","next":"Read the complete file, questioning records and independent operational records.","year":1949},{"id":"C2275","text":"A scanned pamphlet in the 2.10.14 file is titled Een overeenkomst onder Amerikaanse pressie, bears a 7 August 1949 date and is signed with the Peter Volkland name; it urges Dutch soldiers to join an international brigade.","period":"1949-08-07","type":"Contemporary political pamphlet, selected scan read","source_ids":["S1290"],"confidence":"High for visible text and date on the copy; authorship beyond the signed name and distribution extent need corroboration.","basis":"The title and political language state the pamphlet's argument, not an independently established fact about American pressure.","next":"Compare the pamphlet with 2.10.62 item 7086 and surviving originals/distribution records.","year":1949},{"id":"C2276","text":"The pamphlet title and its allegation of American pressure do not establish that the United States exerted pressure in the negotiations it discusses.","period":"1949","type":"Source-critical observation","source_ids":["S1290","S1309"],"confidence":"High as an evidentiary limitation.","basis":"A propagandistic title is evidence of a political message, not a payment, order or policy instrument.","next":"Seek U.S., Dutch and Republican negotiation records before asserting actual U.S. pressure.","year":1949},{"id":"C2277","text":"The arrest chronology has distinct unresolved dates: Historisch Nieuwsblad says Indonesian officers apprehended and handed Van Staveren over on 24 September 1949; Ons Land's person profile says 28 September; the Dutch report copy says he arrived in Solo on 27 September and that a Dutch arrest order followed on 28 September.","period":"1949-09-24/28","type":"Conflicting secondary, archival-profile and investigation claims","source_ids":["S1290","S1292","S1304"],"confidence":"High that these dates appear in the respective sources; actual apprehension, handover, Dutch order and transfer may be separate events.","basis":"Do not collapse Republican custody, the Dutch arrest order and transfer into one date; archive person profile and IISH/Ons Land records may share descriptive sources.","next":"Read Dutch military-police arrest/transfer logs, Republican detention and extradition papers, and court docket.","year":1949},{"id":"C2278","text":"In the April 1952 Senate debate the Justice Minister said two courts convicted Van Staveren for wartime desertion and abandoning post, with seven years' imprisonment less pretrial detention; he said the Higher Military Court confirmed the judgment in October 1950.","period":"1950; stated 1952-04-17","type":"Official parliamentary statement about court proceedings","source_ids":["S1295"],"confidence":"High for the Minister's statement; exact judgment and legal basis should be verified from the court file.","basis":"A minister's summary in debate is not the judgment itself; an opposing MP's speech is advocacy, not a second judicial record.","next":"Obtain both judgments, docket, sentencing calculation and confinement record.","year":1950},{"id":"C2279","text":"The April 1952 ministerial response says the Ministry of Justice found no basis for pardon at that time.","period":"1952-04-17","type":"Official ministerial statement","source_ids":["S1295"],"confidence":"High that this was the minister's answer; the underlying ministerial decision file remains unread.","basis":"Parliamentary answer reports a decision; it is not the decision file.","next":"Retrieve the pardon-policy folder S1294 and related ministerial submissions.","year":1952},{"id":"C2280","text":"At the March 1954 Senate meeting MP Geugjes requested Van Staveren's release and said he had spent nearly five years in prison; these are his advocacy statements.","period":"1954-03-16","type":"Parliamentary advocacy","source_ids":["S1296"],"confidence":"High for what the member said; prison duration and case merits require prison and court records.","basis":"A speech asking for release is not neutral proof of the reasons stated.","next":"Read the complete debate alongside the prison register and pardon petition.","year":1954},{"id":"C2281","text":"The IISH accession profile says letters celebrating Van Staveren's release arrived in summer 1954; Historisch Nieuwsblad specifies August 1954, while an older IISH profile and the Ons Land person biography state that imprisonment continued until 1955.","period":"1954–1955","type":"Conflicting archival profiles and secondary chronology","source_ids":["S1291","S1292","S1304","S1306"],"confidence":"High that sources conflict; exact release date not established by a primary release order.","basis":"The IISH pages may derive from the same archive and later accession; the 1955 claim may reflect an older profile rather than a distinct record.","next":"Read the Leeuwarden release order, prison ledger, 1954 homecoming papers and 1955 correspondence.","year":1954},{"id":"C2282","text":"The July 1952 BVD election report says the CPN included detained, 27-year-old Van Staveren among its 68 candidates, but the report says he was not positioned to win a seat.","period":"1952","type":"Internal security-service report of election activity","source_ids":["S1297"],"confidence":"High for what the report states; confirm candidate status and list position from the official electoral register.","basis":"BVD report is third-party-hosted and records the service's analysis; it is not the official candidate list.","next":"Check official candidate lists and constituency results.","year":1952},{"id":"C2283","text":"IISH says Paul de Groot and the Indonesian unions SBPU and SOBSI sent letters on Van Staveren's release; the underlying letters have not been read.","period":"1954","type":"Archival profile of political solidarity","source_ids":["S1291"],"confidence":"High for the institution's description; exact wording, date and organizational signatories unverified.","basis":"The profile and the collection are not independent sources; letters could establish direct transnational solidarity once read.","next":"Inspect letters and establish each union's full name, branch and signatory.","year":1954},{"id":"C2284","text":"A May 1951 BVD report says the CPN brought Van Staveren forward in a petition campaign; the report characterizes this as communist propaganda, which is the service's judgment.","period":"1951","type":"Internal security-service report","source_ids":["S1308"],"confidence":"High for BVD reporting and framing; campaign scale and public response not established by this passage.","basis":"A secret-service report is direct evidence of surveillance categories and service perspective, not neutral measurement.","next":"Compare petition originals, committee records and newspaper coverage.","year":1951},{"id":"C2285","text":"The BVD weekly report sent 2 May 1952 records an 8 April exhibition about Van Staveren, estimates about 150 attendees, and says speakers alleged that a judge's punishment was inspired by Americans.","period":"1952-04-08; report sent 1952-05-02","type":"Surveillance report of a political event","source_ids":["S1299"],"confidence":"High that the BVD report records these statements and estimate; the actual attendance and allegation are not independently verified.","basis":"The American-influence statement is an event-speaker allegation captured by BVD, not proof of U.S. influence over the judge or case.","next":"Seek event notices, CPN/petition files, court records and any evidence of U.S. contact.","year":1952},{"id":"C2286","text":"A Nationaal Archief catalogue entry describes a CVD/BVD investigation into people who signed objections to Van Staveren's conviction; the entry is marked access-restricted and does not reveal the signers or investigative outcome.","period":"1950–1951 catalogue grouping","type":"Archival catalogue observation","source_ids":["S1300"],"confidence":"High for the description and listed access status; file contents unknown.","basis":"The catalogue entry establishes an investigation record, not the scope or consequences of surveillance.","next":"Ask the National Archives about access/restriction and request the file when available.","year":1950},{"id":"C2287","text":"The BVD's 1954 report records Van Staveren's mother speaking at a public solidarity event and reporting that he hoped to meet Johan van Biemen within months; the report also describes a continuing release campaign.","period":"1954, dates described in report","type":"Surveillance report of event and attributed speech","source_ids":["S1298"],"confidence":"High for what the report records; the issue/date relationship is unclear and the mother's wording is not independently checked.","basis":"BVD language frames the campaign; the report no. 4 and event dates need bibliographic clarification.","next":"Check original event leaflets, family letters, date of release and BVD report cover.","year":1954},{"id":"C2288","text":"The archive record describes a 2021 addition with sentence, imprisonment, release and resistance-pension application documents, as well as a 1959–1960 patent application correspondence series; these records provide retrieval paths but their contents were not read.","period":"1950–1960","type":"Archival catalogue observation","source_ids":["S1292"],"confidence":"High for the catalogue description only.","basis":"Do not infer the nature of a pension entitlement, invention or post-release employment from file titles.","next":"Inspect the named folders and identify post-release occupation, household and technical work.","year":1950},{"id":"C2289","text":"The accessible IISH profile calls the 1984 programme a radio documentary, whereas Historisch Nieuwsblad, Beeld & Geluid and the NFF catalogue describe a VPRO television/long documentary; the media itself was not watched.","period":"1984","type":"Conflicting media catalogue descriptions","source_ids":["S1304","S1305","S1306","S1307"],"confidence":"High for the conflicting descriptions; medium and exact production format unresolved until the recording is inspected.","basis":"These are catalogue and secondary references; no documentary transcript or programme file was examined.","next":"Watch the original through Beeld & Geluid, VPRO or Eye and inspect production records.","year":1984},{"id":"C2290","text":"The NIMH 2024 research note distinguishes refusal of military service, refusal of orders and desertion; it identifies Van Staveren and Poncke Princen as rare deserters who left the Dutch forces in Indonesia, categories often merged in later public usage.","period":"1945–1950; report 2024","type":"Official historical research synthesis","source_ids":["S1301"],"confidence":"High for the report's definitions and framing; the report is not a new judgment about Van Staveren's motives.","basis":"Use the formal conviction and date of refusal to distinguish him from conscripts who refused deployment in the Netherlands.","next":"Compare the contemporary charge and judgment with the report's categories.","year":1945},{"id":"C2291","text":"The Dutch cabinet acknowledged in 2022 that strict and selective policies toward service refusers could produce heavy sentences and harsh outcomes; its verification pathway is limited to cases raised under the stated service/order-refusal criteria.","period":"2022–2024","type":"Official contemporary policy statement","source_ids":["S1301","S1302"],"confidence":"High for the current policy as described in official sources; no person-specific determination found.","basis":"This policy statement does not show that Van Staveren or his family requested verification or that he qualifies for rehabilitation.","next":"Search NIMH decision registers and the family archive for any application, correspondence or outcome.","year":2022},{"id":"C2292","text":"The IISH/Ons Land records report Van Staveren's death in Assen in 2019; no civil death record or post-release biography was read in this review.","period":"2019","type":"Archival profile observation","source_ids":["S1292"],"confidence":"High for the catalogue statement; no independent civil record accessed.","basis":"Same collection description chain as S1291; the profile is not an obituary or death certificate.","next":"Locate civil registration, local obituary, family papers and post-release employment records.","year":2019},{"id":"C2293","text":"Hearman reports that Aidit, Sakirman, Lukman and Njoto had been killed or arrested by the end of 1965, and that the December 1966 arrest of Politburo member Sudisman exposed the next surviving leadership layer.","period":"1965-12 to 1966-12","type":"Secondary historical reconstruction","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for Hearman’s chronology; underlying arrest and death files were not all independently inspected.","basis":"Hearman, pp.71–72, drawing on published work and interviews. “Killed or arrested” is deliberately retained because the source does not assign the same outcome to each person.","next":"Check individual military/police custody, execution and death records; compare PKI exile communications and court files.","year":1965},{"id":"C2294","text":"Hearman identifies two main surviving PKI clusters after the 1965 repression: Sudisman’s group in Jakarta and a Bandung group around Ismail Bakri that Rewang supported; Rewang had chaired the Central Java provincial committee and was a Central Committee secretary/candidate member.","period":"1966-12 to 1967","type":"Secondary reconstruction of underground leadership","source_ids":["S1310","S1477"],"confidence":"Moderate; the grouping and offices are reported through scholarship, interviews and memoirs, not contemporaneous branch minutes.","basis":"Hearman, pp.71–74, compares participant accounts and later memoirs; Hill, p.366, cites Hearman for the domestic leadership chronology and is derivative, not independent corroboration. No complete membership list or command archive has been inspected.","next":"Locate Ismail Bakri, Rewang and provincial committee correspondence, names/roles lists and contemporaneous exile reports.","year":1966},{"id":"C2295","text":"Hearman reports that after Sudisman’s arrest in December 1966 an emergency PKI Central Committee was formed under Ruslan Wijayasastra.","period":"1966-12 onward","type":"Attributed organizational-history claim","source_ids":["S1310","S1477"],"confidence":"Moderate; the source is Rewang’s interview and later organizational accounts; no signed formation resolution or membership roll is reproduced.","basis":"Hearman, pp.72–73 and note 53 reports Rewang's interview; Hill, p.366, explicitly cites Hearman 2010a pp.72, 85–86. Hill is not an independent witness. No signed formation resolution or membership roll is reproduced.","next":"Find the emergency committee’s founding resolution, membership list, minutes, courier records and correspondence.","year":1966},{"id":"C2296","text":"Hearman identifies Oloan Hutapea, Munir, Sukatno and Tjugito as second-echelon leaders drawn into Rewang’s network and reports that these figures were captured in South Blitar in 1968.","period":"1967–1968","type":"Secondary reconstruction; attributed individual roles","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the article’s identification; detention and capture dates require Army/court records.","basis":"Hearman, pp.72–73 and note 52; the source names prior roles from Van der Kroef and other secondary literature.","next":"Check Trisula detainee registers, trial files, identity records and party correspondence; separate each person's capture date and role.","year":1967},{"id":"C2297","text":"Rewang later explained that the move to South Blitar followed the loss of legal protection, the danger of hiding in cities and a decision to prepare armed resistance; this is his retrospective account, not a recovered committee resolution.","period":"1966–1967, recalled 2009","type":"Participant oral testimony about decision and motive","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate for Rewang’s stated rationale; low-to-moderate for its representativeness of the full leadership network.","basis":"Hearman, p.73; interview with Rewang, Solo, 14 February 2009. Compare earlier quoted testimony in Andre Liem.","next":"Locate Rewang’s contemporaneous letters, provincial committee notes and interviews with other participants who disagreed.","year":1966},{"id":"C2298","text":"Hearman reports that South Blitar fugitives relied on local residents for food and shelter and that accounts differ over whether cadres remained mutually isolated or worked through small cells; the article explicitly identifies tension between these recollections.","period":"1967–1968","type":"Oral-history reconstruction of local support and cell structure","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate for the presence of local support and divergent memories; exact numbers and village-level network map remain uncertain.","basis":"Hearman, pp.78–80; participant interviews and Indonesian Social History Institute transcripts. The author flags contradictions rather than resolving them into one uniform structure.","next":"Map named villages, households, food routes, couriers and cells using interviews, local records and separate witness timelines.","year":1967},{"id":"C2299","text":"Hearman reports disagreement among post-1965 PKI leaders over whether armed attacks should wait until an underground mass base was rebuilt or begin immediately; Munir later attributed the defeat to haste that exposed the South Blitar network before preparations were ready.","period":"1967–1973, recalled in 1973 and 2008–2009","type":"Participant and scholarly account of strategic disagreement","source_ids":["S1310","S1411"],"confidence":"Moderate for the existence of disagreement; the surviving statements are retrospective and do not represent all factions.","basis":"Hearman, pp.81–82; cites Guy Pauker’s 1969 report, Munir’s 1973 defense statement, and later interviews with Winata and Tuti. The RAND memorandum is USAF-sponsored contemporaneous analysis, but its text was not obtained; the dispute is accessible here only through Hearman’s quotation. It is an outside analyst’s assessment, not a PKI record.","next":"Retrieve Pauker’s full memorandum and Munir’s original defense transcript; compare with captured party papers and regional military intelligence.","year":1967},{"id":"C2300","text":"Hearman describes Trisula as a Kostrad-linked counterinsurgency led by Colonel Witamin that began in June 1968 and formally ended on 7 September; local administrators were replaced with caretakers, village movement was checked, and local men were recruited for patrol, radio carriage and detainee handling.","period":"1968-06 to 1968-09","type":"Secondary reconstruction using military history and survivor testimony","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the operation dates and described methods; underlying unit orders and village rosters are not reproduced in full.","basis":"Hearman, pp.82–85, including citations to Semdam VIII Brawijaya, Operasi Trisula Kodam VIII Brawijaya. The unit monograph itself was not independently opened.","next":"Obtain the Trisula unit history, Kostrad/Brigif Linud 18 orders, village caretaker appointments, patrol lists, detainee records and local testimony.","year":1968},{"id":"C2301","text":"A 14 November 1968 U.S. National Intelligence Estimate said only a few hundred PKI activists had gone to the hills in Central and East Java after 1965 to build a guerrilla force the party had lacked in October 1965; it said recent operations had set that effort back.","period":"1965–1968, assessed 1968-11-14","type":"Contemporary intelligence estimate","source_ids":["S1312"],"confidence":"High for the estimate’s wording; low-to-moderate for the actual number, organizational cohesion and operational capacity.","basis":"FRUS d220, paragraph 8; estimate is U.S. interagency analysis, not an Indonesian headcount.","next":"Compare PKI rosters, captured documents, district military reports, local village records and independent interviews.","year":1965},{"id":"C2302","text":"The same 1968 U.S. estimate placed the Malayan Communist Party below 1,000 armed members near the Thai–Malaysian border, Sarawak guerrillas near 500, and the remaining Huk armed nucleus in the Philippines near 150.","period":"1968","type":"Comparative contemporary intelligence estimate","source_ids":["S1312"],"confidence":"High for what the U.S. estimate stated; low-to-moderate for the force counts and its political labels.","basis":"FRUS d220, paragraph 8. “Huk” and “Sarawak guerrillas” are the estimate’s categories; they are not interchangeable with later party formations.","next":"Check Malaysian, Thai, Philippine and Sarawak party/state rosters, border records and later scholarly estimates.","year":1968},{"id":"C2303","text":"The 1968 U.S. estimate assessed the Burma Communist Party’s White Flag force at about 4,000, described China as supplying arms and training in Yunnan, and placed roughly 1,500 Thai insurgents in the northeast plus several hundred northern highland recruits.","period":"1968","type":"Comparative contemporary intelligence estimate","source_ids":["S1312"],"confidence":"High for the estimate’s contents; low-to-moderate for force numbers, attribution of Chinese support and local recruitment details.","basis":"FRUS d220, paragraphs 10–11; the estimate’s Cold War threat frame and foreign-control assumptions need independent testing.","next":"Compare party publications, Burmese/Thai records, Chinese and regional archives, and later scholarship on borderland support.","year":1968},{"id":"C2304","text":"Bob Bergin’s 2016 account says the Thai Communist Party’s armed struggle began in 1965 after an earlier commitment to armed struggle, and describes recruitment among minority communities and refugees in remote border areas; it reports an insurgent strength estimate of about 1,200 in 1965.","period":"1952–1965, retrospective account published 2016","type":"Retrospective intelligence-history synthesis","source_ids":["S1313"],"confidence":"Moderate; the article is a later account relying partly on Thai intelligence and official sources, not a roster or party membership census.","basis":"Bergin, pp.25–26. The article disclaims U.S. Government endorsement of its factual and analytic claims.","next":"Compare party congress materials, CPT publications, Thai police/military records and local minority/refugee histories.","year":1952},{"id":"C2305","text":"Ho Kee Chye’s article reports that the Malayan Communist Party operated from southern Thailand after the first Emergency, experienced an internal factional crisis in the mid-1960s, and announced preparations for southward armed operations in 1968.","period":"1950–1968","type":"Secondary political and military history","source_ids":["S1314"],"confidence":"Moderate; this is a short secondary account and no original CPM plenary resolutions were opened.","basis":"Ho Kee Chye, Inter-Asia Cultural Studies 16(1), pp.56–66; article discusses the 1961 New Directive, internal split and 1968 statement.","next":"Retrieve the CPM New Directive, 1960s Plenum records, 1968 statement and Thai/Malaysian border operational files.","year":1950},{"id":"C2306","text":"Joseph Scalice’s dissertation dates a split in the Philippine PKP to 1967, the founding of the separate Communist Party of the Philippines to 1968, and the establishment of its New People’s Army to 1969.","period":"1967–1969","type":"Document-based dissertation chronology","source_ids":["S1315"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the dated organizational sequence; cross-check founding statements and surviving party records.","basis":"Scalice, dissertation chapters 17–21, pp.299–393; full dissertation read.","next":"Compare CPP founding congress documents, NPA founding statement, PKP minutes, contemporary newspapers and police files.","year":1967},{"id":"C2307","text":"Scalice argues that the 1965–1966 destruction of the Indonesian PKI influenced the Philippine PKP’s search for new international communist ties and its subsequent alignment within the Sino-Soviet dispute; this is the dissertation author’s causal interpretation.","period":"1965–1967","type":"Scholarly causal interpretation","source_ids":["S1315"],"confidence":"High that the dissertation makes this argument; causal weight is not settled and remains the author’s interpretation.","basis":"Scalice, abstract and chapter 14, pp.249–260; distinguish documentary sequence from his larger theoretical account.","next":"Compare PKP correspondence, Moscow/Beijing records, Indonesian exile networks and other scholarship on the Sino-Soviet split.","year":1965},{"id":"C2308","text":"Cribb’s 2002 review states that published estimates of deaths in 1965–1966 ranged from 100,000 to two million and that the often-cited 78,000 Fact-Finding Mission figure was recorded while killings were still under way.","period":"1965–1966 estimates reviewed in 2002","type":"Historiographical casualty-estimate observation","source_ids":["S1311"],"confidence":"High that the article reports these estimate ranges and timing; the actual death toll remains unresolved.","basis":"Cribb, Asian Survey 42(4), pp.557–558. The figures have different dates, methods and institutional purposes.","next":"Compare later regional victim lists, Komnas HAM files, provincial registers, demographic studies and the original Fact-Finding Commission report.","year":1965},{"id":"C2309","text":"Cribb reports that he had found no systematic national collection of survivor, perpetrator and witness testimony suitable for casualty statistics, and that comparison of 1961/1971 census totals by district did not yield a clear excess-loss measure.","period":"Knowledge reviewed in 2002","type":"Methodological assessment of casualty data","source_ids":["S1311"],"confidence":"High for what Cribb states; the conclusion is time-bounded and does not establish that no later dataset or unpublished list exists.","basis":"Cribb, pp.558–559; he notes only a small number of identified remains and describes national totals as informed estimates.","next":"Search post-2002 Komnas HAM, YPKP, local victim databases and burial investigations for ethnicity-coded individual records.","year":2002},{"id":"C2310","text":"Cribb and Coppel’s estimate, as reported in Woolgar’s 2023 article, places ethnic-Chinese deaths in the vicinity of 2,000 against an approximate national total of 500,000; the arithmetic ratio is about 0.4 percent, but neither number is a complete individual count.","period":"1965–1966","type":"Secondary casualty estimate and derived ratio","source_ids":["S231","S1311"],"confidence":"Low-to-moderate for the estimated count and ratio; high that Woolgar reports Cribb and Coppel's estimate. The 0.4% quotient uses two rounded estimates and is not a measured national proportion.","basis":"Woolgar, printed p.1977 and footnote 110, reports Cribb and Coppel’s estimate near 2,000; its footnotes 111–112 discuss the approximate national denominator and cite Cribb and Coppel, 2009, pp.449–450. The denominator is an approximate 500,000 national death estimate and the numerator is an attributed estimate near 2,000. No victim-level ethnic roster supports the quotient; urban/rural and regional variation is substantial.","next":"Trace the estimate's underlying provincial reports; seek named victim, burial, community and civil-register data with ethnicity fields. Do not infer national Javanese, Balinese, Sundanese or other group rates from census population shares.","year":1965},{"id":"C2311","text":"In the sources opened for this pass, no national victim-level dataset was found that identifies ethnicity for each person killed; Cribb’s methodological review finds the evidence base inadequate for reliable national ethnic percentages.","period":"1965–1966 killings; search status as of 2026-11-19","type":"Research-status and evidence-limit observation","source_ids":["S1311","S231"],"confidence":"High for the materials reviewed and Cribb’s stated limitation; not proof that such records do not exist elsewhere.","basis":"Cribb, pp.557–559; Woolgar, printed p.1977 and footnotes 110–112; Ananta et al. census-method study. The approximate Chinese estimate supplies no Javanese, Balinese, Sundanese or other national breakdown. A 1961 census that omitted ethnicity cannot be combined with non-existent victim-level identities to derive group mortality rates. This is a statement about sources reviewed, not proof no records exist elsewhere.","next":"Search Komnas HAM/YPKP person-level records, provincial/village lists, oral-history databases, cemeteries and civil registration; record ethnicity only when the source explicitly supports it.","year":1965},{"id":"C2312","text":"Woolgar’s West Java study reports that only a few thousand people were killed there in 1965–1966 and that killings of ethnic Chinese appeared rare in that province, contrasting the pattern with northern Sumatra, Medan and Aceh.","period":"1965–1966","type":"Regional scholarly estimate and comparison","source_ids":["S231"],"confidence":"Moderate; regional count and ethnic classification depend on the article’s cited sources, not a complete West Java victim registry.","basis":"Woolgar, p.1980, with notes 123–124; the article distinguishes regional patterns and does not claim nationwide uniformity.","next":"Retrieve West Java village-level victim lists and compare with North Sumatra, Aceh and Medan records using the same definitions.","year":1965},{"id":"C2313","text":"Woolgar reports that in West Java anti-Chinese harassment and political restrictions continued into 1966, including takeovers of Chinese-language schools and association property, registration measures, pressured name changes and an August 1966 expulsion order affecting some Chinese association members.","period":"1966","type":"Regional account of state/civilian discriminatory measures","source_ids":["S231"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the article’s documented policy/event sequence; individual execution and the scope of each measure require local files.","basis":"Woolgar, pp.1980–1981, notes 128–136; these post-purge measures are distinct from deaths during 1965–1966.","next":"Read the cited West Java military orders, school/association inventories, registration rolls, deportation records and contemporaneous Chinese-language press.","year":1966},{"id":"C2314","text":"In the 1966 postmortem reproduced by Roosa, Supardjo said the operation’s leadership was divided among Aidit’s group, Sjam’s group and Untung’s group, and argued that operations needed a single command.","period":"1965 operation, document dated 1966","type":"Participant postmortem about operational command","source_ids":["S252"],"confidence":"High for the surviving typed text as reproduced and attributed; moderate for the exact structure because the original manuscript is not extant in the examined copy.","basis":"Roosa, Dalih Pembunuhan Massal, Appendix 1, pp.323–343; Roosa says the trial-court copy was typed from an original, with possible copying errors.","next":"Find Supardjo’s original manuscript, Mahmilub case file and corroborating notes by Untung, Sjam, Soejono and other participants.","year":1965},{"id":"C2315","text":"Supardjo’s postmortem characterized the plan as a Jakarta “firecracker” expected to ignite popular and regional action, and criticized inadequate verified support, communications and contingency planning.","period":"1965 operation, document dated 1966","type":"Participant military critique; causal interpretation","source_ids":["S252"],"confidence":"High for what the document says; moderate for the operational diagnosis because Supardjo was a participant assigning responsibility after defeat.","basis":"Roosa, Appendix 1, pp.328–339; includes Supardjo’s regional courier and command criticism.","next":"Compare dated courier routes, provincial instructions, RRI logs, unit movements, party branch reports and the original G30S operation papers.","year":1965},{"id":"C2316","text":"Roosa’s published account of Iskandar Subekti’s confidential 1986 analysis says Aidit met Sjam in August 1965 to discuss a possible action against the alleged Council of Generals, and then approached a small Politburo working group; this is a later participant account mediated through the historian.","period":"1965-08, recalled in 1986 and published 2006/2008","type":"Retrospective participant account and historian reconstruction","source_ids":["S252"],"confidence":"Moderate; Subekti was an Aidit aide and Politburo note-taker but did not attend Sjam’s meetings with military officers, and the original memo was not independently examined here.","basis":"Roosa, chapter 5, pp.150–153; Subekti’s vantage point and limitations are discussed in the text.","next":"Locate the 1986 original analysis, compare other working-group members, captured Sjam papers, trial transcripts and contemporaneous calendars.","year":1965},{"id":"C2317","text":"Subekti’s account, as presented by Roosa, says the Politburo did not meet during September 1965; Roosa infers that the body could not have collectively reviewed Sjam’s detailed operational contacts, but this does not by itself settle what individual leaders knew.","period":"1965-09, recalled in 1986","type":"Participant account with scholarly inference","source_ids":["S252"],"confidence":"Moderate for Subekti’s attributed recollection; lower for the inference of complete non-knowledge because original attendance records and parallel communications are absent.","basis":"Roosa, pp.150–153; compare Sudisman’s 1967 court defense (S260) and the 1966 party self-criticism (S261).","next":"Find September Politburo and Working Committee calendars, minutes, travel/courier records and individual testimony.","year":1965},{"id":"C2318","text":"Roosa argues that the Army’s official account extended Sjam’s trial testimony from an Aidit-directed operation to a decision by the PKI Central Committee as a whole; this is a historiographical challenge, not an established finding that no other senior figures participated.","period":"1967–1968 trials; analysis published 2006/2008","type":"Historiographical interpretation of official party-responsibility claim","source_ids":["S252","S260"],"confidence":"High that Roosa makes this argument; the underlying level and scope of party authorization remain contested.","basis":"Roosa, chapter 5, pp.135–160; compare Sjam’s coercive trial setting, Subekti’s 1986 analysis, Sudisman’s 1967 defense and Supardjo’s 1966 report.","next":"Read complete Mahmilub transcripts, original party papers and independent witness accounts; separate individual action, Politburo awareness and formal Central Committee authorization.","year":1967},{"id":"C2319","text":"A CIA Directorate of Intelligence report in September 1967 discussed the Fifth Force and Sukarno’s health as possible political context but said evidence to confirm its speculation was lacking.","period":"1967-09","type":"Contemporary intelligence analysis; attributed hypothesis","source_ids":["S1316"],"confidence":"High for the report’s wording; low for the underlying causal hypotheses.","basis":"The indexed passage explicitly characterizes the hypothesis as unconfirmed; the direct PDF request redirected, so only the relevant official OCR excerpt was reviewed.","next":"Retrieve the full report scan and compare it with Sukarno’s health records, Indonesian Fifth Force proposals, military memoranda and Chinese records.","year":1967},{"id":"C2320","text":"Rewang later stated that the PKI as an institution had not decided to launch the 30 September Movement and recalled that roughly ten Politburo members were confused after it occurred.","period":"1965-09 to 1965-10; recollection published 2004","type":"Retrospective participant testimony","source_ids":["S1317","S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate for what Rewang said; low-to-moderate for a universal negative about all party knowledge.","basis":"Liem quotes Rewang’s 2000 interview; no Politburo minutes or signed decision register is available, and Rewang joined the Politburo only in mid-1965.","next":"Find the original interview recording/transcript and compare other Politburo members’ notes, calendars and captured party papers.","year":1965},{"id":"C2321","text":"After Sudisman’s arrest in December 1966, Rewang proposed relocating surviving cadres to a rural base because urban hiding had become unsafe and legal political activity had disappeared.","period":"1966-12 to 1967","type":"Retrospective participant testimony and scholarly reconstruction","source_ids":["S1317","S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate; the proposal and motive are reported retrospectively and no recorded vote or directive has been found.","basis":"Liem and Hearman rely on Rewang’s later account; the party’s surviving structures were fragmented and this cannot be treated as a nationwide order.","next":"Locate Rewang’s original interview, any emergency committee directives and the East Java regional committee’s correspondence.","year":1966},{"id":"C2322","text":"Hearman’s reconstruction identifies at least two surviving leadership groupings, one around Sudisman in Jakarta and another around Ismail Bakri in Bandung, with Rewang aligned with the latter; it does not show one unified underground command.","period":"1966-1968","type":"Scholarly reconstruction from participant accounts and later memoirs","source_ids":["S1310","S1317"],"confidence":"Moderate; the two-group map is a reconstruction and rosters/communications are incomplete.","basis":"Hearman, pp.71–74, compares Rewang, Soedjono and Liem accounts; no complete membership list or command archive has been inspected.","next":"Find Ismail Bakri’s own papers, each group’s membership and courier lists, and independent reports from regional branches.","year":1966},{"id":"C2323","text":"Liem’s interview material describes an emergency Central Committee above provincial leadership and South Blitar project committees; Rewang recalled nine Central Committee members, regional committee figures and cadres from several provinces participating.","period":"1967-1968","type":"Retrospective participant testimony","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"Moderate for the described structure; low for a complete roster, national reach or effective command.","basis":"Rewang’s testimony in the chapter’s Kompro section; names, dates and formal authority remain uncorroborated by internal minutes.","next":"Obtain the original interview, party rosters, minutes and captured command papers; map each committee’s dates and geographic remit.","year":1967},{"id":"C2324","text":"A Muyatno interview quoted by Liem describes cells, guerrilla detachments and ideological education, while the article says proposed STPR and KKPR schools were plans; the chapter does not establish that the schools operated as designed.","period":"1967-1968","type":"Retrospective testimony and scholarly reconstruction of military materials","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"Moderate for the reported plans and testimony; low for implementation at scale.","basis":"The article paraphrases documents seized by the Army and survivor accounts; original documents and school attendance lists were not examined.","next":"Find the captured files, curriculum, attendance lists, instructor names and evidence of actual training.","year":1967},{"id":"C2325","text":"Muyatno recalled that Gatot Taryo was the only project-committee contact he knew and also named Joko Untung; he could not identify Joko’s committee role or the wider membership.","period":"1967-1968","type":"Retrospective participant testimony","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"Moderate for the interview’s contents; low for full identities and duties.","basis":"The same testimony stresses compartmentalization and limited mutual knowledge; spellings may vary and no personnel records were checked.","next":"Search trial files, party lists and local records for Gatot Taryo/Gatot Sutaryo and Joko/Djoko Untung; do not merge variants without identification evidence.","year":1967},{"id":"C2326","text":"Rewang recalled that the start date for a planned armed struggle had not been decided; the emergency leadership expected project preparations and a national conference before authorizing a coordinated campaign.","period":"1967-1968","type":"Retrospective participant testimony","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"Moderate; no minutes confirm the recalled rule or its reach.","basis":"The testimony distinguishes preparation of structures from a formal start of organized armed struggle; dispersed cadres may not have followed the same timetable.","next":"Find the planned conference agenda, emergency committee resolutions, project reports and regional communications.","year":1967},{"id":"C2327","text":"Liem’s interview-based account says some attacks, disarmings and raids occurred outside Kompro decisions and increased military suspicion before the leadership had agreed to begin a coordinated armed struggle.","period":"1967-1968","type":"Retrospective testimony and scholarly reconstruction","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"Moderate; individual incidents and responsibility require case-by-case corroboration.","basis":"Rewang and other participants recall actions the committee had not authorized; Army accounts may describe them differently and no incident roster was checked.","next":"Identify each raid, location, date, perpetrators, targets, casualties and arms taken; compare Army logs and local testimony.","year":1967},{"id":"C2328","text":"Muyatno retrospectively criticized some cadres for seeking quick victory instead of a prolonged Maoist strategy; this is one participant’s diagnosis of internal tactical weakness, not a consensus explanation of PKI’s defeat.","period":"1967-1968; testimony published 2004","type":"Retrospective participant interpretation","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that the interviewee made this criticism; moderate-to-low as a general explanation.","basis":"Liem quotes Muyatno and Rewang’s related criticism; both were recalling an internal debate after the defeat.","next":"Compare dissenting participant accounts, committee papers and the actions each group actually undertook.","year":1967},{"id":"C2329","text":"Liem’s interviews describe South Blitar villagers providing housing, food, agricultural work and everyday care to some fugitives; other testimony shows people’s knowledge and participation varied by village and person.","period":"1967-1968","type":"Retrospective local testimony","source_ids":["S1317","S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate for the attributed cases; not generalizable to all residents.","basis":"No household roster, village supply ledger or courier register was examined; residents’ assistance could take different forms and need not imply party membership.","next":"Locate village-level witness lists, land and household records, food movements, local arrests and independently recorded oral histories.","year":1967},{"id":"C2330","text":"Leksana’s reconstruction describes early-October 1965 demonstrations, property attacks and clashes in East Java as initially local and uneven, followed from mid-October by more coordinated Army-civilian collaboration.","period":"1965-10","type":"Scholarly reconstruction citing Kodam V Brawijaya records","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the article’s reconstruction; underlying documents were quoted or summarized rather than opened here.","basis":"The article cites East Java military archive collection G30S/PKI tahun 1965, inventory no.316-a. It does not establish uniform enforcement across every district.","next":"Inspect inventory 316-a and compare dated orders, district reports, detention registers and local press.","year":1965},{"id":"C2331","text":"Leksana reports that the East Java commander’s 21 October 1965 Pancasila Operation required battalions to send local-condition reports to Surabaya every six hours and allowed pro-Army civilian forces to assist operations against the September 30 Movement; the instruction was distributed to Korem 081–083 and East Java battalions, with copies sent to Army headquarters, Kostrad, and the Diponegoro and Udayana commands.","period":"1965-10-21 onward","type":"Scholarly quotation of a contemporaneous military order","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"High for the order’s reported language; moderate for actual implementation by each battalion.","basis":"The order is quoted from Kodam VIII Brawijaya inventory 316-a through Leksana, not from a signed original reviewed here. Distribution is evidence of intended circulation, not proof that every addressee received, implemented, or reported under it.","next":"Obtain the signed Perintah Operasi No. 5 Pantja Sila, its distribution/receipt logs, six-hour situation reports, and unit/auxiliary implementation records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2332","text":"Leksana reports that a 26 October 1965 radiogram placed progressive revolutionary organizations supporting the Army under Puterpra, provided for combative training, and directed district-to-subdistrict investigation teams adapted to local conditions.","period":"1965-10-26 onward","type":"Scholarly quotation of contemporaneous military radiogram","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"High for the article’s account of the radiogram; moderate for implementation and geographic reach.","basis":"The account is mediated through Leksana’s reading of inventory 316-a; the original telegram, complete addressees, team composition, and evidence of training or investigation in each locality were not inspected.","next":"Obtain Radiogram T. 298/1965, acknowledgements, Puterpra and investigation-team rosters, training attendance, and resulting arrest/interrogation records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2333","text":"Leksana reports that a 23 November 1965 radiogram ordered military-aligned civilian groups to register residents from neighborhood and village levels through districts and institutions, provide information and indoctrination, and assist ABRI operations and psychological-warfare tasks.","period":"1965-11-23 onward","type":"Scholarly quotation of contemporaneous military radiogram","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"High for the provision as quoted; local registration completion is unverified.","basis":"The directive is quoted from Brawijaya inventory 316-a through a scholarly article; completion and use of any lists remain unverified. The document's abbreviation AA is not expanded; the author considers an Ansor/religious-group meaning plausible but not certain.","next":"Inspect Radiogram T. 702/1965 as cited for 23 November, local registration books, list custodians, handover/use records, and the original order's definition of AA.","year":1965},{"id":"C2334","text":"Leksana argues that civilian participants in East Java also pursued varied interests, including party competition, revenge and material or employment opportunities; these motives should be tested locally rather than treated as one common civilian motive.","period":"1965-10 to 1966","type":"Scholarly interpretation of local collaboration","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"High that the author advances this analysis; moderate-to-low as a generalization beyond cases documented in the article.","basis":"The article’s interpretation does not prove any individual’s motive without local evidence and should be compared with participant testimony and economic records.","next":"Compare individual perpetrator accounts, property transfers, hiring lists, party records and village-level disputes.","year":1965},{"id":"C2335","text":"Robinson’s regional comparison argues that military command choices and local capacity produced sharply different outcomes: killings were swift and extensive in Aceh, while West Java’s commander Ibrahim Adjie chose mass arrests rather than similar large-scale killing.","period":"1965-10 to 1966","type":"Scholarly causal interpretation from regional records","source_ids":["S043"],"confidence":"High that Robinson makes this comparison; moderate as a general causal model and not a rule for all provinces.","basis":"The article supports military agency and variation but underlying district records differ and this comparison cannot be universalized to Bali, Java or the archipelago.","next":"Compare each province’s force deployment, command continuity, detention and killing records, and civilian auxiliary chain.","year":1965},{"id":"C2336","text":"Robinson describes KAP-Gestapu as initiated by Army leadership and later covertly assisted financially by the U.S. government; the 2 December Green cable records concurrence with a proposed 50 million rupiah contribution but not a verified transfer or expenditure.","period":"1965-11 to 1965-12","type":"Scholarly synthesis compared with contemporaneous U.S. diplomatic record","source_ids":["S043","S175"],"confidence":"High for Robinson’s published interpretation and Green’s documented concurrence; transfer, receipt and use remain unverified.","basis":"S175 is a proposal/concurrence with redactions; Robinson’s footnote synthesizes later financial assistance. Do not equate approval intent with proof of cash delivery.","next":"Find CIA/State authorization, courier or bank records, Adam Malik receipt, KAP-Gestapu accounts and expenditure evidence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2337","text":"Liem reports that military intelligence on South Blitar began by mid-1967, Soeharto issued an operational order on 20 February 1968, and the Trisula task force was formed on 5 May; the chapter cites official effective operations from May through July, while Hearman dates the named operation’s start to June.","period":"1967-06 to 1968-09","type":"Scholarly reconstruction with attributed military records","source_ids":["S1317","S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate-high for the dated stages; the difference between planning, task-force formation and the main field phase requires the original orders.","basis":"Liem reports distinct intelligence, cleanup-operation, task-force and effective-attack stages; Hearman’s June date may mark the named field operation rather than all preparation.","next":"Read the 20 February order, 5 May formation order, phase reports and 7 September closure record; distinguish phase start from operation name.","year":1967},{"id":"C2338","text":"Liem reports an Army announcement that 37 firearms were captured after Trisula; the figure is an Army-reported seizure total and not an independent audit of all party weapons or armed activity.","period":"1968","type":"Later scholarly quotation of military operational results","source_ids":["S1317","S1392"],"confidence":"High for what Liem says the Army announced; low-to-moderate for completeness and accuracy of the total.","basis":"Liem reports the Army announced 37 captured firearms; Arief et al.’s 1999 history also attributes the same 37-firearm figure to Yasin’s 9 August 1968 press conference and adds four grenades (printed p.45). These may descend from one official count, not independent corroboration. No physical inventory, post-operation reconciliation or independent arms list was examined.","next":"Obtain the Army seizure inventory, unit receipts, prosecution exhibits and any party-side weapons records.","year":1968},{"id":"C2339","text":"Liem reports that the Army replaced village officials with 234 Army caretakers in South Blitar; the number is attributed to a military source, not a complete appointment roll checked here.","period":"1967-1968","type":"Scholarly quotation of military history","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High for the article’s attributed report; moderate for exact count and locality coverage.","basis":"The cited Army monograph and the underlying appointment list were not independently opened.","next":"Obtain the 234 names, appointment dates, villages, replacement reasons and local administration files.","year":1967},{"id":"C2340","text":"Ananta and coauthors report that Indonesia did not collect ethnicity in the 1961 census and that the 2000 census was the first post-colonial census to resume it; the 2010 coded data included 1,331 ethnic, sub-ethnic and sub-subethnic categories.","period":"1930-2010","type":"Demographic methodology and census-history finding","source_ids":["S1318"],"confidence":"High for the paper’s methodological account; it says nothing about victim-level ethnicity.","basis":"Population census categories and dates cannot supply ethnicity for people killed decades earlier; 2010’s 1,331 raw categories require classification.","next":"Find any local civil, cemetery, victim or family roster that records both identity and outcome; document category mapping and missingness.","year":1930},{"id":"C2341","text":"Cribb and Coppel estimate that approximately 2,000 ethnic-Chinese people were killed in 1965–66, but provide no national victim-level roster; this remains an attributed scholarly estimate and a contested interpretation.","period":"1965-1966","type":"Scholarly casualty estimate and interpretation","source_ids":["S1322","S231","S612"],"confidence":"Moderate for the estimate as a published scholarly position; low-to-moderate for precision because no complete victim list supports it.","basis":"Their 2009 article relies partly on earlier work and is polemical in framing; compare Woolgar on West Java and Melvin on Aceh, whose regional evidence can capture different kinds of anti-Chinese violence.","next":"Trace the estimate’s underlying sources and compare local ethnic and political identity lists, deaths, disappearances, property attacks and time boundaries.","year":1965},{"id":"C2342","text":"The ECCC guide summarizes a distinct Cambodian sequence: WPK adopted revolutionary violence in January 1965, ordered zones to prepare armed struggle in September 1966, Samlaut fighting in 1967 arose amid compulsory grain acquisition, and CPK combatants attacked police posts on 17 January 1968.","period":"1965-1968","type":"Institutional summary of judicial findings","source_ids":["S1319"],"confidence":"High for what the ECCC guide says the Case 002/02 judgment found; moderate as a comparative chronology.","basis":"The guide summarizes judgment paragraphs 202–212, not party minutes; the Samlaut conflict’s local actors should not be treated as all centrally directed.","next":"Compare the cited judgment paragraphs with Cambodian party documents, village testimony and police records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2343","text":"Zeller argues that the Pathet Lao/Lao People’s Party relied substantially on Vietnamese Workers’ Party cadres and forces, making the Lao armed movement structurally different from the PKI’s national legal mass-party network.","period":"1950s-1960s","type":"Scholarly comparative interpretation","source_ids":["S1320"],"confidence":"High that the author advances this interpretation; moderate as a comprehensive description of every Lao unit.","basis":"Open-access chapter synthesizes party and external records but is not a full personnel or force census.","next":"Compare Lao and Vietnamese party rosters, military orders, supply and command records with Indonesian sources.","year":1950},{"id":"C2344","text":"Baird’s Hmong-focused CPT study describes recruits entering through basic education and military training, some motivated by schooling and opposition to discrimination, while reporting no Hmong member on the CPT Central Committee and some Hmong provincial-level leadership.","period":"1960s-1980s","type":"Retrospective oral history and social-history comparison","source_ids":["S1321"],"confidence":"Moderate; based on a focused group study and extensive retrospective interviews, not a full CPT roster.","basis":"Recruitment motives and leadership representation vary by period; the Hmong case cannot stand for all CPT members or be directly mapped onto Indonesian ethnic groups.","next":"Compare party membership, training and leadership records for other CPT constituencies and assess the interview sample.","year":1960},{"id":"C2345","text":"The combined evidence supports testing two distinct causal layers: PKI’s leadership concentration, reliance on a small officer-contact network and weak clandestine contingency planning may help explain G30S operational failure and post-purge vulnerability, while Army command, state detention and killing machinery, civilian collaboration and external support belong to the separate mechanisms that destroyed the party’s mass organizations.","period":"1965-1968","type":"Analytical synthesis; causal hypothesis, not authorial political judgment","source_ids":["S252","S260","S261","S043","S1310","S1311","S1317","S1319","S175"],"confidence":"Moderate as a framework for testing; causal weight and political evaluation remain unsettled.","basis":"Evidence describes distinct processes and sources have differing vantage points; this does not establish a single internal PKI cause for mass killing or assign the author’s final political position.","next":"Build province-by-province process tracing from orders, unit logs, victim records, party communications, property changes and foreign-operation chains.","year":1965},{"id":"C2346","text":"Liem’s retrospective chapter says the PKI had no party-led armed-struggle plan before G30S and operated through the existing political system; after the party was banned and its leaders and members were attacked, Sudisman shifted the surviving party toward an armed-resistance strategy during 1966.","period":"Before 1965-09-30; strategy shift during 1966","type":"Scholarly reconstruction based on participant interviews and attributed party/military accounts","source_ids":["S1317","S1310","S261"],"confidence":"Moderate for Liem’s reconstruction; not proof that no individual or affiliated group held military ideas or training.","basis":"Liem, opening section and South Blitar narrative, says pre-G30S the party had no plan to launch armed struggle, remained close to Sukarno and worked within existing politics, then describes Sudisman’s 1966 shift after repression. This must be distinguished from the Fifth Force debate, volunteer training, BTI/HANREV activities, veterans in party networks and post-1965 planned resistance; contemporary party records remain to be checked.","next":"Compare 1964–65 PKI/BTI/Gerwani/Fifth Force documents and military training records with Sudisman’s 1966 self-criticism, internal directives, provincial files and later exile correspondence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2347","text":"In a 1963 public report, Aidit retrospectively described the 1950–51 debate between immediate armed struggle and rebuilding the party while preserving its legal status; he said the party chose the latter after judging its organization weak and the revolutionary situation ebbing.","period":"1950–1951; account published 1963","type":"Contemporaneous party-leader retrospective","source_ids":["S1329"],"confidence":"High for what Aidit stated; moderate as a complete account of the internal debate","basis":"Aidit’s programmatic report is an interested account defending the post-1951 line; it is not a transcript of all Central Committee debate.","next":"Compare the 1951 CC minutes, rival-line documents, membership and branch reports, and Madiun-era security records.","year":1950},{"id":"C2348","text":"Aidit’s 1963 report says the 1951 reconstruction used Central Committee-appointed provincial commissariats because local organizations were confused, while inter-island communications and transport remained a serious problem.","period":"1951–1963","type":"Party organizational self-description","source_ids":["S1329"],"confidence":"High for the report’s statement; moderate for nationwide implementation and scale","basis":"The text explains the party’s chosen central-supervision structure and explicitly notes the archipelago communications problem; no provincial reports were independently checked.","next":"Retrieve appointment, courier, travel, transport, membership and provincial committee records for multiple islands.","year":1951},{"id":"C2349","text":"Aidit’s 1963 report publicly called for links between the armed forces and the people and used the “rifle in one hand, hoe in the other” slogan; this is evidence of militarized political language and a mass-line conception, not proof of a PKI field army, weapons delivery or combat order.","period":"1963","type":"Party programmatic text; distinction between rhetoric and capability","source_ids":["S1329","S133","S1317"],"confidence":"High for published language and catalogue descriptions; low for any inferred force capability","basis":"Aidit’s text is not an operational order. ANRI entries 257–259 remain catalogue locators only, and participant accounts describe a limited post-1965 underground project.","next":"Read BTI items 257–259 and HANREV files; identify participants, instructors, chain of command, weapons and actual use.","year":1963},{"id":"C2350","text":"The September 1966 Politburo self-criticism says party leaders involved themselves in the 30 September Movement without organizational procedure or a mass base, calls this adventurism, and says the leadership then entrusted the party’s fate to Sukarno after the movement failed.","period":"September 1965–September 1966","type":"Post-defeat internal party self-criticism","source_ids":["S1331","S261"],"confidence":"High that the document says this; moderate-to-low as an independently verified causal diagnosis","basis":"The text itself says the Politburo bore greatest responsibility, but was produced under military terror after severe leadership losses and as the surviving group redirected party strategy. It does not specify which individuals approved what in September 1965.","next":"Compare drafting and circulation records, surviving leaders’ criticism, Sudisman’s 1967 defense, Rewang interviews, Supardjo’s report, Subekti’s analysis and contemporaneous party minutes.","year":1965},{"id":"C2351","text":"The 1966 self-criticism attributes past organizational weakness to the leadership’s failure to develop criticism/self-criticism, its reported suppression of criticism from below, and an overly loose, legalistic mass-party structure.","period":"1951–1966 as assessed in September 1966","type":"Internal organizational self-criticism","source_ids":["S1331","S261"],"confidence":"High for the document’s diagnosis; moderate for the history it describes","basis":"This diagnosis is a politically situated post-defeat text advocating a new Maoist armed line; independent party records and survivor accounts are needed to test whether these weaknesses were uniform and decisive.","next":"Find party education and discipline records, cadre evaluations, recruitment statistics, internal criticism, provincial security plans and communications.","year":1951},{"id":"C2352","text":"The September 1966 self-criticism says the pre-1965 “three forms of struggle”—rural guerrilla activity, urban worker action and work among armed forces—were separated and pursued along a peaceful route, effectively abandoning armed struggle; the statement presents this as a failure, not a contemporaneous operational description.","period":"1950s–1965; retrospective assessment in 1966","type":"Internal party retrospective doctrine and strategic reinterpretation","source_ids":["S1331","S261","S1329"],"confidence":"High for the later document’s wording; contested as a description of actual practice","basis":"Aidit’s 1963 report simultaneously used armed-force link and rifle/hoe language. The documents differ in purpose and context; neither alone establishes armed capacity or a uniform party line.","next":"Compare 1956, 1959, 1962–65 party congress resolutions, armed-forces committees, BTI training files and local branch implementation.","year":1950},{"id":"C2353","text":"Woolgar’s 2025 study characterizes the PKI as a hybrid of cadre/vanguard party, mass party and social movement: growth increased bureaucratic hierarchy while also producing a looser, less coherent organization.","period":"1951–1965, with West Java as the case study","type":"Recent archival-based scholarly interpretation","source_ids":["S1330"],"confidence":"High that this is the author’s argument; moderate as a general model and weaker outside West Java","basis":"Based on the author’s stated use of party/KOTI archives, publications and oral histories; only the accepted introduction was read, not the full monograph or underlying files.","next":"Read the full chapters on Guided Democracy, mass organizations and destruction; compare with Central/East Java, Bali, Sumatra, Sulawesi and party rosters.","year":1951},{"id":"C2354","text":"Woolgar argues that fast growth and attempted central control did not eliminate local differentiation: in West Java the party faced an entrenched Siliwangi military presence and increasing opposition, while provincial events could exceed leaders’ control.","period":"1957–1965","type":"Regional scholarly interpretation","source_ids":["S1330","S231"],"confidence":"Moderate; West Java evidence is stronger than any nationwide inference","basis":"The accepted monograph introduction summarizes deeper chapters; S231 is another West Java study by the same author, not wholly independent corroboration.","next":"Inspect the book’s source notes and compare district-level party/military records from other provinces.","year":1957},{"id":"C2355","text":"Woolgar interprets the leadership’s post-October 1965 paralysis as a combination of shock and strategic miscalculation that exposed the vulnerability of the mass membership; he links this to unresolved tensions between mass-party, movement and vanguard roles.","period":"October 1965 onward","type":"Recent scholarly causal interpretation","source_ids":["S1330","S043","S1311"],"confidence":"Moderate as one explanatory model; not a settled sole cause","basis":"The source is a West Java-centered monograph whose introduction summarizes a broader chapter; mass killing and detention were actions of state and military actors and are separate from PKI organizational vulnerability.","next":"Compare central and provincial leadership responses, military orders, arrests, killings and communications with time-stamped local records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2356","text":"Komnas HAM’s report gives at least 1,956 witness-based victims in the legal category “murder” and at least 85,483 in the distinct category “extermination”; it does not present these as a complete national death census or disaggregate them by ethnicity.","period":"1965–1966; report published 2012","type":"Official retrospective human-rights investigation and legal categorization","source_ids":["S859"],"confidence":"High for the report’s categories and figures; low as a national total","basis":"The report’s legal categories use witness statements and cover multiple regions; its own methodology says the team examined 349 people but could not investigate every region or witness because of geographic scope, budget and the passage of time. Other violation categories may overlap, so counts must not be summed into one victim total. No national ethnicity table or ethnic denominator was found; underlying BAPs remain unopened.","next":"Inspect underlying witness BAPs and regional annexes; determine geographic coverage, duplicate handling, category definitions and whether any victim records contain ethnicity fields. Do not convert the “extermination” category into a death count or derive ethnic percentages without person-level records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2357","text":"Komnas HAM records that October 1965 attacks in Makassar targeted alleged PKI-linked people and ethnic-Chinese residents who were perceived as connected to the PRC; it supplies no count that can produce an ethnic share of nationwide deaths.","period":"October 1965; report published 2012","type":"Official report of witness-based local violence","source_ids":["S859"],"confidence":"High that the report records this account; moderate pending review of underlying testimony","basis":"The report describes local crowd attacks, looting and destruction but does not provide a complete Makassar or national ethnicity-coded victim list.","next":"Read the cited BAPs, local police/military records, Chinese association records, and burial or civil registers.","year":1965},{"id":"C2358","text":"The approximately 2,000 ethnic-Chinese deaths estimate divided by an approximately 500,000 overall estimate yields about 0.4% arithmetically, but this is not a measured victim share and cannot establish comparative ethnic risk; reliable national percentages for other groups were not found.","period":"1965–1966","type":"Derived arithmetic from contested scholarly estimates; data-gap finding","source_ids":["S1322","S231","S1311","S1318","S859"],"confidence":"High that the ratio is only derived from approximate estimates; very low as a measured population statistic","basis":"Neither numerator nor denominator is a complete individual-level roster with matched ethnic identity, geography and period. The 1961 census did not collect ethnicity; post-2000 census data do not fill a 1965 victim roster.","next":"Locate and de-duplicate provincial/village victim registers and identify whether ethnicity is recorded; calculate only for defined samples with denominators.","year":1965},{"id":"C2359","text":"A 2025 South Kelud study traces post-1945 occupation and cultivation of abandoned plantation land by former workers, landless residents, refugees and veterans, with BTI and SARBUPRI involved in organizing or supporting some claimants.","period":"1945–1966","type":"Local agrarian history based on archives and period press","source_ids":["S1323"],"confidence":"Moderate for the study’s reconstruction; lower for individual land transactions not checked in source originals","basis":"The authors cite ANRI Ministry of Agriculture records and newspapers; those original files were not independently opened. Local findings cannot be generalized to all East Java or all PKI-linked land activity.","next":"Request cited ANRI files and compare plantation maps, labor rolls, district correspondence, BTI/SARBUPRI files and land-reform orders.","year":1945},{"id":"C2360","text":"The South Kelud study reports that about 515 Gambar residents petitioned to legalize cultivation of roughly 196 hectares; the figure is the study’s report of a local petition, not an independently checked land registry.","period":"Post-1945, before 1965","type":"Locally reported petition and land claim","source_ids":["S1323"],"confidence":"Moderate for the reported petition; unverified as a completed land transfer","basis":"The article’s cited files and petition were not separately obtained. A petition and cultivation do not equal legal title or completed redistribution.","next":"Locate the Gambar petition, cadastral records, estate title and district response.","year":1945},{"id":"C2361","text":"The South Kelud study says Ministry decisions SK49/Ka/64 and SK50/Ka/64 made some plantations land-reform objects and reports a provincial plan to distribute about 74 inactive estates; the plan is not evidence that all land was redistributed.","period":"1964","type":"Local secondary report of administrative land-reform decisions and plan","source_ids":["S1323","S1326"],"confidence":"Moderate for the article’s account; implementation remains unverified","basis":"The Court judgment later reproduces Sakri’s reliance on SK49, but does not adjudicate the ownership or implementation of all parcels.","next":"Obtain signed SK49/SK50, land schedules, implementation reports, beneficiary registers and HGU/title records.","year":1964},{"id":"C2362","text":"The South Kelud study cites a 1958 Kodam Brawijaya order against unauthorized land use and reports that 433 hectares at Petungombo were taken under military custody and later used by PUSKOPAD; the underlying order, map and transfer record were not opened.","period":"1957–1965","type":"Local study’s account of military land control","source_ids":["S1323"],"confidence":"Moderate as a reported local sequence; lower pending primary-file verification","basis":"Part of the account relies on later newspaper reporting and archival citations. Custody, occupation, title and management are distinct legal and practical states.","next":"Find Kodam order 011/1958, Petungombo parcel maps, PUSKOPAD records, estate accounts, and 1964 land-reform files.","year":1957},{"id":"C2363","text":"A 30 November 1965 Berita Yudha report alleged SARBUPRI sabotage under “Gerakan 1.7”; the South Kelud study says whether sabotage occurred or whether the allegation justified taking land is unclear.","period":"1965-11-30 and after","type":"Contemporaneous newspaper allegation reported by later local study","source_ids":["S1323"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the allegation; low that the underlying allegation is true","basis":"The newspaper original and investigation records were not independently examined; the accusation must not be converted into fact.","next":"Retrieve the newspaper issue, police/military investigation, company damage records and SARBUPRI correspondence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2364","text":"The South Kelud study reports that 365 households were evicted from plantation land after 1965, with homes dismantled and little compensation; this is a local article’s account, not a verified national total.","period":"After October 1965","type":"Local post-1965 dispossession account","source_ids":["S1323"],"confidence":"Moderate for the article’s reported count; lower for complete enumeration and compensation status","basis":"The cited newspapers and archival documents were not independently opened; district and parcel scope requires confirmation.","next":"Find the cited 1980s newspaper reports, eviction orders, household lists, compensation files and land concession records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2365","text":"In Constitutional Court case 55/PUU-VIII/2010, farmer Sakri from Soso, Blitar, asserted that he cultivated state land formerly under an erfpacht controlled by PT Kismo Handayani and relied on SK49/KA/64; the judgment also reproduces the government’s account of a July 2008 demonstration and Sakri’s suspended sentence.","period":"1964 decision cited in a 2008 dispute; judgment 2011","type":"Court-reproduced petitioner and government submissions","source_ids":["S1326"],"confidence":"High that the judgment records these submissions; not a judicial finding on ownership or every disputed event","basis":"The Court’s account does not settle the land title; the district trial record and Sakri’s P-7 exhibit were not inspected.","next":"Obtain full Constitutional Court case file and P-7, district court judgment, land registry and the underlying ministerial decision.","year":1964},{"id":"C2366","text":"The Constitutional Court struck down Plantation Law 18/2004 Article 21, its explanation and Article 47(1)–(2) as unconstitutional; it did not decide who owned the South Kelud parcel claimed by Sakri.","period":"2011-09-19","type":"Official judicial holding and scope limitation","source_ids":["S1326"],"confidence":"High","basis":"The operative order concerns statutory provisions and cannot be used as a title judgment for the underlying land dispute.","next":"Compare the operative judgment with subsequent land case rulings and current cadastral/title records.","year":2011},{"id":"C2367","text":"Hearman’s South Blitar study describes military evacuation, camps, village surveillance and food scarcity during the 1968 counterinsurgency; villagers were pressured to assist with food, patrol or control, and accounts vary by locality.","period":"1968","type":"Scholarly reconstruction using military history and oral testimony","source_ids":["S1324","S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate for the described mechanisms; local variation and scale require primary orders and village lists","basis":"The article relies partly on retrospective oral histories and military sources. The actual operation orders, ration books, evacuation lists and camp registers were not independently opened.","next":"Read cited Trisula histories and seek village evacuation, detention, food-distribution and caretaker records.","year":1968},{"id":"C2368","text":"The source combination supports a bounded explanation of PKI military weakness: armed rhetoric, contacts, training proposals and some training do not by themselves prove a centralized guerrilla army; leadership decision structure, communications, command failure, Army territorial power and the later destruction of mass organizations must be tested as separate mechanisms.","period":"1951–1968","type":"Cross-source analytical proposition for later testing","source_ids":["S1329","S133","S1331","S1330","S1317","S1310","S478","S043","S295"],"confidence":"Moderate as a research model; no single source establishes the whole chain","basis":"Aidit is a party text, the BTI inventory is a finding aid, self-criticism is post-defeat, underground accounts are retrospective, and Army power varies by province. It is not a final political judgment or single-cause verdict.","next":"Build province-by-province maps of PKI armed assets, Army territorial units, civilian mobilization, arrests, killings and links between written orders and implementation.","year":1951},{"id":"C2369","text":"Chandra’s East Java estimate of one-time district-level population change compares census-based expected population with later observed counts; it combines deaths above normal, lower fertility and one-time migration, so it is a proxy for disruption rather than a direct death count.","period":"1961–1980; used to estimate 1965–1966","type":"Peer-reviewed demographic method","source_ids":["S1332"],"confidence":"High for what the method measures","basis":"The study is East Java-specific and depends on population-growth assumptions and changing district boundaries.","next":"Compare the 491 district estimates with local victim registers, burial evidence, migration testimony and boundary harmonization; do not label the proxy “deaths.”","year":1961},{"id":"C2370","text":"In East Java, Chandra finds that districts with greater access to main roads tended to show larger estimated one-time population losses; the author argues transport access may have enabled the reach and scale of the anti-PKI campaign.","period":"1965–1966; study published 2019","type":"Peer-reviewed spatial association and author interpretation","source_ids":["S1332"],"confidence":"Moderate for the reported association; not proof of a specific killing operation","basis":"The measure uses a 1971 main-road map, excludes minor roads and other transport, and population change also includes fertility and migration. Correlation does not by itself show army or civilian action in a particular district.","next":"Match road accessibility to dated transport orders, truck logs, detention/transfer records, local testimony and mass-grave evidence by district.","year":1965},{"id":"C2371","text":"Chandra and Zhang’s 2023 abstract says their East Java spatial analysis compares kecamatan population-loss estimates with Army command-center and politico-religious organization-center locations and identifies possible military/civilian-allied hotspots; the authors state the model cannot prove any named group or individual took part.","period":"1965–1966; study published 2023","type":"Peer-reviewed spatial-demographic study, abstract-level claim","source_ids":["S1333"],"confidence":"Moderate for the abstract’s stated design and limit; low for results pending full text","basis":"The full paper and model specification were not accessed. Population-loss proxy is not a victim list; locations of institutions do not establish operational control.","next":"Read full paper, maps, robustness checks, organization lists and citations; compare each hotspot with primary local orders, personnel and testimony.","year":1965},{"id":"C2372","text":"Hearman’s account of Rewang’s testimony frames South Blitar primarily as a refuge from persecution; Rewang assessed organized armed resistance after 1966 as negligible, and only one detachment of young men had received training by the 1968 Trisula operation.","period":"1966–1968","type":"Participant recollection reported in researcher-authored secondary source","source_ids":["S1334","S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate for Rewang’s recollected assessment; low for completeness of all local activity","basis":"The short essay and peer-reviewed article share author and overlapping oral-history source base. Army deployment figures in the essay are attributed to a newspaper account, not independently checked.","next":"Read Rewang’s 2017 memoir and full interview recordings; obtain Kompro rosters, training attendance, weapons and operation records, and local testimony.","year":1966},{"id":"C2373","text":"The Ministry of Home Affairs library catalog identifies a 2017 Indonesian-language book Saya Seorang Revolusioner and says it contains Rewang testimony on G30S, South Blitar and the Politburo self-criticism’s formation; the catalog is a lead to a participant source, not evidence of the testimony’s exact content.","period":"Book published 2017; testimony dates unspecified","type":"Official catalog record / source-location finding","source_ids":["S1335"],"confidence":"High for the catalog metadata and its description; no confidence assigned to unseen testimony","basis":"The actual book was not read; catalog metadata does not establish whether Rewang was interviewed directly, how the text was edited, or the statement’s date and completeness.","next":"Acquire the book; record edition, editor/interviewer, chapter/page references, interview dates, and textual differences against Liem and Hearman.","year":2017},{"id":"C2374","text":"Woolgar’s West Java study repeats the Cribb–Coppel estimate of about 2,000 ethnic-Chinese deaths and argues they were not nationally overrepresented relative to an approximate 500,000 death estimate and roughly 2% population share; it also documents regional variation and anti-Chinese harassment, detention, property attacks and expulsions.","period":"1965–1967","type":"Scholarly interpretation combining approximate national estimates with regional history","source_ids":["S231","S1322","S612"],"confidence":"Moderate as a published interpretation; not a measured national victim-ethnicity share","basis":"There is no national victim-level ethnic roster in these sources; the denominator and numerator are approximate, and region-specific experiences differ. “Not disproportionately represented” does not mean no anti-Chinese targeting or no local overrepresentation.","next":"Trace the estimate’s underlying records and compare regionally bounded ethnic victim lists, population denominators, deaths, detention and expulsion separately.","year":1965},{"id":"C2375","text":"Winward’s Yogyakarta reconstruction places Suharto’s announcement of Kopkamtib on 10 October 1965 as a parallel security command, RPKAD deployment to Central Java on 17 October, and RPKAD arrival in Yogyakarta on 20 October; this is a scholarly chronology, not a substitute for the underlying orders.","period":"1965-10-10 to 1965-10-20","type":"Secondary reconstruction of military command and deployment","source_ids":["S1336"],"confidence":"Moderate for the chronology reported; underlying orders need verification","basis":"The 2018 article cites military histories and interviews; the underlying command orders were not independently opened in this review.","next":"Obtain Kopkamtib founding order, RPKAD movement/operation orders, Diponegoro command dispatches, and local police/military logs.","year":1965},{"id":"C2376","text":"Winward reports that Yogyakarta’s local jails overflowed after the 20 October rally, with some 50-person cells holding more than 130 people, leading military and police to use schools, libraries and offices as makeshift detention sites.","period":"1965-10-20 onward","type":"Scholarly reconstruction combining retrospective interviews and military publications","source_ids":["S1336"],"confidence":"Moderate for the specific interview-backed example; not a complete detention census","basis":"The numbers are recalled by respondents and refer to reported facilities; detention rolls and site-by-site capacity records were not checked.","next":"Compare prison registers, military detention lists, food/transport records, hospital and family accounts.","year":1965},{"id":"C2377","text":"Winward reports that RPKAD and military police solicited suspected-PKI lists from local religious/nationalist organizations and universities, then used civilian accusations and seized organizational lists in arrest operations; this could widen detention beyond people with substantial PKI ties.","period":"1965-10 onward, Yogyakarta/Central Java","type":"Scholarly account of intelligence and targeting mechanisms","source_ids":["S1336","S1337"],"confidence":"Moderate for recurring mechanism in the studied locations; frequency and reach require more records","basis":"Examples combine interviews with cited university orders and military publications; the 2020 appendix says direct knowledge of list formation came from few elderly respondents and could reflect faulty memory or self-presentation.","next":"Retrieve UGM and other institutional purge orders, original lists, military intake files and individual case records; compare names and outcomes.","year":1965},{"id":"C2378","text":"Winward reports that RPKAD and military police selected ten civilians, armed them and authorized them to lead auxiliary units, with legal-immunity assurances described by one interviewee; this is a localized account of state-enabled militia formation, not a nationwide militia count.","period":"Late October 1965; Yogyakarta","type":"Retrospective participant account within a scholarly case study","source_ids":["S1336"],"confidence":"Moderate for the attributed Yogyakarta account; low for numerical generalization","basis":"The statement relies on a former militia leader’s confidential interview; weapon issue and legal guarantees have not been matched to official records.","next":"Find the auxiliary-unit roster, training and arms receipts, appointment/protection orders, and comparison with East Java and other regions.","year":1965},{"id":"C2379","text":"Winward’s Yogyakarta case argues that the Army both initiated and organized the campaign and sometimes restrained civilians, while local groups supplied intelligence, manpower and public support and at times acted beyond military orders; the author treats this as an interaction, not an Army-versus-civilians binary.","period":"1965-10 to 1966-03; Yogyakarta","type":"Scholarly causal interpretation of civil–military relations","source_ids":["S1336","S1337"],"confidence":"Moderate as a bounded case interpretation; national generalization requires provincial evidence","basis":"Interviews and military accounts support the process reconstruction, but sample is nonrepresentative and actors’ memories or self-presentation may be selective. Army custody of detainees and transport/organization remain distinct from militia violence outside authorization.","next":"Compare provincial orders, local force loyalties, detention/execution pathways, militia links and stop/discipline directives.","year":1965},{"id":"C2380","text":"Winward’s 2020 abstract proposes that low intelligence capacity increased civilian information-gathering and broadened targeting, while detention/logistical limits increased lethal treatment; its appendices explicitly record competing explanations and nonrepresentative interview limits.","period":"1965–1966; Central Java, with West/East Java comparison","type":"Published analytical hypothesis and disclosed methodological caveats","source_ids":["S1337","S1332"],"confidence":"Moderate that this is the author’s stated mechanism; causal strength unassessed without full article","basis":"Only abstract and appendices were accessed. Census-based district change in S1332 mixes death, fertility and migration; the interview sample is not statistically representative; individual orders and local records need verification.","next":"Read full article and appendix-linked source trail; test district-by-district against command orders, prison capacity, lists, military units and local victim evidence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2381","text":"Winward contrasts Yogyakarta’s weaker pre-existing militia networks and lack of a large-scale land-reform campaign with East Java’s denser pesantren/Ansor-Banser networks and sharper local agrarian conflicts; despite those differences, Yogyakarta violence escalated after RPKAD deployment and civilian-military coordination.","period":"Before 1965 through 1966","type":"Regional comparative interpretation","source_ids":["S1336","S1337","S295","S043"],"confidence":"Moderate as a comparison to test; not a claim that Yogyakarta had no local disputes or that East Java was uniform","basis":"Winward notes small isolated land disputes in Yogyakarta; regional comparisons are qualitative and categories such as “militia network” and “land conflict” need localized measurement.","next":"Compare district-level pre-1965 land actions, party strength, armed-unit loyalties, civilian organization rosters and violence timing in Yogyakarta, Central/East/West Java.","year":1965},{"id":"C2382","text":"ANRI's 2014 photographic inventory lists Supardjo's Mahmilub hearing at Bappenas from 24 February to 9 March 1967 and describes courtroom photographs; the inventory establishes an archival lead and event metadata, not the content of testimony.","period":"1967-02-24 to 1967-03-09","type":"Official archive finding aid / event metadata","source_ids":["S1338"],"confidence":"High for the inventory entry and dates; no inference about guilt or testimony","basis":"Catalogue description and captions were read; underlying negatives, complete docket and court transcript were not inspected.","next":"Request Supardjo's full Mahmilub docket, signed testimony, exhibits, defense file and original negatives; compare with the typed report reproduced in S252.","year":1967},{"id":"C2383","text":"The Indonesian online text of Sudisman's 21 July 1967 defense speech is the same underlying speech translated in S260; it can be used to check wording but does not provide independent corroboration.","period":"Speech 1967-07-21; transcription accessed 2026-11-17","type":"Textual/version cross-check","source_ids":["S1339","S260"],"confidence":"High that both versions identify the same speech; translation and editorial variants remain","basis":"The Indonesian transcription and existing full English translation were compared. Both represent one courtroom statement made by a detained defendant.","next":"Compare against original Indonesian printed edition and court file; log substantial translation differences with printed pages.","year":1967},{"id":"C2384","text":"Sy. Datuk R.A.'s compiled West Sumatra cases total 2,374: 2,169 detained, 187 killed and 18 sentenced.","period":"West Sumatra cases compiled in a 2015 publication","type":"Regional victim and former-detainee list","source_ids":["S1340"],"confidence":"High for the compiler's printed counts; low as a complete regional total","basis":"The appendices list names and statuses collected by the compiler. The sampling frame and raw sheets were not available; categories and periods may not be exhaustive.","next":"Locate the author's working lists and cited district records; de-duplicate names and reconcile detention, death and sentence outcomes.","year":2015},{"id":"C2385","text":"The West Sumatra tables reviewed in Datuk's book have no ethnicity field, so they cannot yield a victim-ethnicity percentage and names or local origin should not be used to infer ethnicity.","period":"West Sumatra cases compiled in 2015","type":"Dataset field audit / limitation","source_ids":["S1340"],"confidence":"High for the tables reviewed; not a claim about every unreviewed appendix","basis":"Reviewed printed appendices pp.128–165. No ethnicity column appears in the inspected tables.","next":"Review remaining appendices and underlying lists; seek explicit self-identification or reliable family/community records before coding ethnicity.","year":2015},{"id":"C2386","text":"ANTARA reported YPKP 65's claim that it had submitted a cumulative 346 alleged mass-grave locations to Komnas HAM, including 112 locations previously submitted in 2015; this is a reported submission count, not a verified grave or victim count.","period":"2015 and 2019 submissions, reported 2019-10-03","type":"News report of organizational claim","source_ids":["S1341"],"confidence":"High that ANTARA reported the statement; low pending the underlying site list and field checks","basis":"Article reports the organization's chair and says the supporting material was submitted; the reporter did not independently inspect or verify all locations.","next":"Obtain the 2015 and 2019 submission annexes and Komnas intake records; deduplicate sites and verify each with local burial, witness and land records.","year":2015},{"id":"C2387","text":"Tirto's 3 October 2019 account reports the same YPKP 346-site submission and 16-province scope as ANTARA; it is a second article, not independent confirmation of the underlying count.","period":"2019-10-03","type":"Correlated news report","source_ids":["S1341","S1342"],"confidence":"High for what Tirto reported; no independent validation of YPKP's dataset","basis":"Both reports rely on YPKP's account of the same submission.","next":"Inspect the submitted site-level table and distinguish alleged sites, verified graves, estimated interments and identified persons.","year":2019},{"id":"C2388","text":"YPKP's 2023 post says it resubmitted its 346-site report and additional records on Buru forced labor, sexual violence and cases from Pekalongan; the post does not publish the annexes.","period":"2023-02-17 meeting; post 2023-02-19","type":"Victim-organization statement","source_ids":["S1343"],"confidence":"High that YPKP made this statement; contents and evidentiary quality of attachments unreviewed","basis":"Only the organizational post was accessible; no intake receipt, attachments or investigator assessment was linked.","next":"Request the 2023 submission and case-level annexes, receipt confirmation and any subsequent Komnas/Kejagung field assessment.","year":2023},{"id":"C2389","text":"Pohlman reports that SekBer '65's own database contained 1,167 survivor listings and that it had investigated 18 grave sites in several Central Java areas as of the period covered by the 2013 article.","period":"Data reported by 2013","type":"Advocacy-group self-report quoted in peer-reviewed review","source_ids":["S1349"],"confidence":"Moderate for the group's reported counts; low as a death estimate or comprehensive list","basis":"These are survivor database entries and investigated sites, not identified deaths, a national roster or ethnicity-coded data; underlying dataset was not accessed.","next":"Locate SekBer '65's original dataset, inclusion criteria, interview files and grave-site reports; compare with district detention and civil registers.","year":2013},{"id":"C2390","text":"Amnesty International Netherlands' Indonesia 1965 archive reports more than 200 historical documents tagged by time, place, violation and victim affiliation, religion or ethnicity; privacy rules prevent person-name search, so it is a document archive rather than a searchable victim roster.","period":"Documents dated 1967 onward; archive pages accessed 2026-11-17","type":"Archive scope and access-method description","source_ids":["S1350"],"confidence":"High for the archive's stated design and available categories","basis":"Homepage, About and How-to pages read. Tags describe documents and do not amount to consistent victim-level demographic fields.","next":"Search document-level tags for region and ethnicity; manually extract only explicit names/identities and preserve each document's source chain.","year":1967},{"id":"C2391","text":"Cribb's 2002 review described the available personal testimonies as too sparse for statistical analysis and the death estimates then in circulation as ranging very widely; this documents the state of the literature in 2002, not the absence of all later local datasets.","period":"Review published 2002","type":"Historiographical and methodological review","source_ids":["S1311"],"confidence":"High for the author's stated 2002 assessment; not a current inventory","basis":"The article is earlier than later victim registries and regional case studies; it does not justify extrapolating that no local list exists today.","next":"Compare its citation chain with post-2002 province-level studies, Komnas files, YPKP submissions and local victim lists.","year":2002},{"id":"C2392","text":"The CPP's 26 December 1968 Rectify Errors text criticizes the previous Filipino party leadership's urban/legal and organizational strategy; this is the new party's own factional retrospective critique, not an impartial history.","period":"1968-12-26","type":"Party congress primary text / self-criticism","source_ids":["S1344"],"confidence":"High for the published CPP position; independent validity of each historical accusation unassessed","basis":"Text was read in a later web reproduction. It is a polemical party document written to establish a new line.","next":"Compare with old PKP/Huk internal records, party membership and operational files, and independent histories of the postwar defeat.","year":1968},{"id":"C2393","text":"The CPP's 26 December 1968 program declares a peasant army, rural base areas and armed people's democratic revolution as its strategy; this establishes a stated line, not actual force capacity.","period":"1968-12-26","type":"Party congress primary text / declared program","source_ids":["S1345"],"confidence":"High for the program's text; not a census of implementation","basis":"The program is available as a later digital transcription; its strategic declarations do not establish recruitment, weapons, territory or combat effectiveness.","next":"Compare the program with NPA founding records, unit rosters, recruitment, arms and field-operation evidence from 1969 onward.","year":1968},{"id":"C2394","text":"A Ban Phasuk local study dates the CPT's first deadly armed clash to 7 August 1965 and describes its later commemoration as First Gunfire Day; this is a village case, not a national start-date census.","period":"1965-08-07; study published 2017","type":"Local oral-history and historical case study","source_ids":["S1346"],"confidence":"Moderate for the studied community's chronology and memory; limited geographic scope","basis":"Study uses village interviews and a local community history; exact CPT-wide armed-start chronology may differ by definition and location.","next":"Compare CPT regional committee records, Thai state security files, neighboring village histories and contemporary press.","year":1965},{"id":"C2395","text":"The published FRUS summary of an 11 August 1966 CIA memorandum describes the Thai communist insurgency as embryonic and short of cadres; the full eight-page memorandum is not exposed in the edition read.","period":"1966-08-11","type":"Contemporary U.S. intelligence assessment, summary-level access","source_ids":["S1347"],"confidence":"High for the official editorial summary; low for the underlying estimate as ground truth","basis":"The document page identifies a secret intelligence memorandum but only its summary was reviewed; underlying analytic sources and full text were not accessible.","next":"Obtain the full declassified memorandum and compare its collection base with Thai police, military, CPT and village records.","year":1966},{"id":"C2396","text":"The 14 November 1968 NIE gives approximate force estimates for regional communist movements, including a few hundred PKI activists in Java's hills and estimates for CPM, Sarawak guerrillas, Huk remnants, CPT and CPB; the definitions and evidence bases are not symmetric.","period":"1968-11-14","type":"Contemporary interagency U.S. intelligence estimate","source_ids":["S1348"],"confidence":"High that the NIE gave these estimates; low as actual comparable force counts","basis":"The NIE is a U.S. analytic snapshot. It aggregates different categories, regions and source quality, and does not define a standardized measure of membership or armed strength.","next":"For each party, compare contemporaneous internal roster, regional command reports, arms and recruitment records; preserve the NIE as evidence of U.S. perception.","year":1968},{"id":"C2397","text":"Kislenko's regional study describes U.S.-assisted Thai counter-insurgency coordination through a CSOC established in December 1965 and U.S. assistance to Thai border and paramilitary police; this is evidence about Thailand, not U.S. intervention in Indonesia.","period":"Thailand, 1965 onward; study published 2004","type":"Scholarly regional history based on declassified U.S. material","source_ids":["S1351"],"confidence":"Moderate for the institutional account; article's cited underlying records should be checked for particular operations","basis":"The article combines declassified records with secondary literature; country and operation scope is Thailand.","next":"Read the cited U.S. aid and CSOC primary documents and compare Thai police orders; keep separate from Indonesian channels.","year":1965},{"id":"C2398","text":"ANRI's 2024 guide catalogs PFN 1966 films titled Njono di Depan Mahmilub and Untung di Depan Mahmilub and summarizes courtroom scenes and attributed statements; the film footage itself has not been viewed.","period":"Film production 1966; guide published 2024","type":"Official audiovisual archive finding aid","source_ids":["S1352"],"confidence":"High for the finding-aid descriptions; not verbatim testimony","basis":"The guide is a later catalogue narrative about state-produced newsreels, not a transcript. Its language may reproduce the film's framing.","next":"Request/view both PFN films and compare on-screen captions, narrator, courtroom footage and printed Mahmilub transcripts.","year":1966},{"id":"C2399","text":"The printed Perkara Untung volume records the Mahmilub hearing from 23 February through 5 March 1966 at Bappenas; the case chronology is also summarized in ANRI's audiovisual guide.","period":"1966-02-23 to 1966-03-05","type":"Court record and archive-guide event metadata","source_ids":["S1354","S1352"],"confidence":"High for dates stated in the published court volume and ANRI guide","basis":"The accessible volume is a searchable OCR reproduction, not a certified court copy. This establishes hearing dates and venue, not procedural fairness.","next":"Compare the reproduced transcript with a physical copy, official docket and court order in ANRI or military judicial archives.","year":1966},{"id":"C2400","text":"In his recorded courtroom account in Perkara Njono, Njono described three formal Politburo meetings in August and said the 28 August meeting chose to report the alleged General Council danger to Sukarno, request preventive action and inform party cadres; he said the Politburo did not approve the officers' initiative to act first.","period":"Meetings in August 1965, recounted at trial in 1966","type":"Defendant's courtroom testimony in a military tribunal","source_ids":["S1353","S1481"],"confidence":"High that the reproduced transcript records this defense account; the underlying meetings and letter are unverified","basis":"Printed pp.68–70 as represented by searchable OCR. Njono's trial account says the Politburo did not approve the officers' initiative to act first; Safwan's 1981/82 state biography instead reports an expanded 28 Aug Politburo meeting and full delegation to Aidit, citing the 1978 KOPKAMTIB white paper. Neither is an authenticated minute, and they may describe different moments.","next":"Locate Politburo attendance and minutes, the alleged 28 August letter and dispatch copies, Njono's signed interrogation record, and statements by Aidit, Lukman, Sudisman, Rewang and Peris Pardede.","year":1965},{"id":"C2401","text":"The Perkara Njono judgment says a prior signed preliminary-interrogation statement, which the court asserted was given without prosecutorial coercion, showed unanimous Politburo approval of a pre-emptive military operation and formation of a Revolutionary Council; the same volume records Njono's courtroom denial of that approval.","period":"Preliminary interrogation and trial in 1966; underlying alleged decision in August 1965","type":"Conflicting courtroom record: judicial finding versus defendant testimony","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that the judgment and courtroom exchange are both printed in the volume; low as to which account accurately represents the meetings","basis":"The judgment is not an independent meeting record and the interrogation statement itself was not located. The trial took place in a mass prosecution environment; the court's statement that there was no coercion cannot independently establish voluntariness.","next":"Obtain the signed preliminary-interrogation minutes, defense objections, interrogation custody log and any contemporaneous recording; compare exact wording against the judgment's paraphrase.","year":1966},{"id":"C2402","text":"The Perkara Njono judgment attributes to the preliminary interrogation a plan to allocate military matters to Aidit and political matters to the Politburo daily bureau, plus about 2,000 Lubang Buaya reserve trainees; these are court-reported claims, not verified rosters or proof of actual readiness.","period":"Alleged plan in August–September 1965; documented in the 1966 judgment","type":"Court's reported finding about party and reserve-force organization","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that the court printed these claims; low for actual headcount, training and division of command","basis":"The judgment appears to rely on a prior statement and trial witnesses. No attendance roll, training file, independent headcount or command order has been examined. In the same volume Sujono’s 16 Feb 1966 courtroom testimony separately speaks of roughly 1,500 sudden arrivals and, in cross-examination, a report of “1,000-something” candidates; these are not equivalent counts or confirmation of a 2,000-person roster.","next":"Search original interrogation statements and Sujono testimony/defense records; obtain ANRI BTI 257–259, Air Force training rosters, budgets, participant files, weapons receipts and transport records; compare each source’s date, category and counting method.","year":1965},{"id":"C2403","text":"In Perkara Untung, Untung said he believed the Council of Generals planned a coup but acknowledged that he had no concrete facts or documentary proof; elsewhere he attributed his information to reports from Supardjo and other contacts.","period":"August–September 1965, recounted during the 1966 trial","type":"Defendant's courtroom statement and reported intelligence chain","source_ids":["S1354"],"confidence":"High that the OCR reproduction contains the statements; low as to the truth of the alleged coup or full source chain","basis":"The statements appear in a military prosecution transcript; there is no independently verified Council of Generals coup plan in the sources reviewed.","next":"Compare the trial transcript to Untung's signed interrogation, Supardjo's original correspondence, the alleged tape and its chain of custody, SUAD-I/BPI records and the 21 September military meeting documentation.","year":1965},{"id":"C2404","text":"The trial reproductions consulted are OCR searchable versions hosted on Scribd; their text contains recognition errors and the copy's provenance has not been established, so citations need printed page references and later comparison with official or library copies.","period":"Digital access checked 2026-11-17","type":"Source provenance and transcription limitation","source_ids":["S1353","S1354"],"confidence":"High from direct access and visible OCR condition","basis":"The digital host is a third-party platform and no certified scan metadata or chain of custody was visible.","next":"Locate and compare physical copies through ANRI, Indonesian legal libraries, CiNii/WorldCat holdings and military judicial archives.","year":2026},{"id":"C2405","text":"Holtzappel's 2014 essay argues that Njono's trial account supports a Politburo choice to report the General Council danger to Sukarno rather than authorize Untung's action; this is a strongly argued interpretation that must be tested against the same volume's judgment and other evidence.","period":"Interpretive essay published 2014 about August 1965","type":"Non-peer-reviewed secondary interpretation of primary trial material","source_ids":["S1355","S1353","S1354"],"confidence":"High that the essay makes this argument; no confidence in its broader conclusions","basis":"The essay's claims about Suharto, Untung and party authorization go beyond the transcript. It directly conflicts with the judgment's reading of Nyono's earlier statement, and its institutional status is not peer reviewed.","next":"Audit every cited page against physical court volumes; compare Roosa, Rewang, Subekti, Sjam's trial statements and the missing interrogation and party records.","year":2014},{"id":"C2406","text":"The title pages and front matter of the 1966 Njono and Untung case volumes identify the Army Legal Education Center (Pusat Pendidikan Kehakiman A.D., AHM–PTHM) as publisher, P.T. Pembimbing Masa as sole distributor, and cite an Army order directing publication of Mahmilub results for public study; the printed transcripts are official Army-produced records, not independent or necessarily verbatim transcripts.","period":"Publication and permits in March 1966; proceedings from 1966","type":"Primary-source provenance and institutional framing","source_ids":["S1353","S1354"],"confidence":"High that the reproduced front matter states this; exact print and scan provenance should be checked against physical copies","basis":"The title pages name the publisher/distributor and display a military publication permit; the introductory text cites an Army order of 3 March 1966. The volumes remain selected institutional publications, with OCR errors and no independent stenographic control.","next":"Compare physical copies in ANRI, Indonesian military-law libraries and WorldCat/CiNii holdings; obtain the cited Army order, publication instructions, editorial file and complete docket.","year":1966},{"id":"C2407","text":"Taher's appendix A groups listed people under unclear detention, abduction, disappearance and killing; appendix B separately groups detainees disappeared, killed or dead in custody. The subtotal of 333 belongs to that printed list section and is not a national death toll.","period":"1965–1968; list published 2010","type":"Description of an advocacy compilation and its categories","source_ids":["S1356"],"confidence":"High that the book prints these categories and subtotal; low that the entries are complete or independently verified","basis":"Printed appendix headings and subtotal, pp.193–206. It is a partial organizational list with mixed case outcomes, not a representative count of deaths.","next":"Obtain the underlying LPR-KROB records and coding rules; validate each person's fate against family, detention, court, burial and civil records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2408","text":"Taher's printed victim-list columns include number, name, age, address, regency/city and occupation or organization but no ethnicity field.","period":"List published 2010","type":"Direct observation of a victim-list schema","source_ids":["S1356"],"confidence":"High for the printed table schema","basis":"The absence of an ethnicity column in this list cannot establish that no ethnicity-coded dataset exists elsewhere; identity must not be inferred from names.","next":"Inspect the underlying LPR-KROB database and the Komnas HAM files said to have received it for any additional fields.","year":2010},{"id":"C2409","text":"Taher's book describes the printed names as a small part of thousands collected by LPR-KROB and says, attributing the statement to its chair, that the list was submitted to Komnas HAM; no submission receipt or Komnas intake record was inspected.","period":"Claim about advocacy records, book published 2010","type":"Attributed archival lead","source_ids":["S1356"],"confidence":"High that the book makes this attribution; unverified whether and in what form Komnas HAM received the material","basis":"The book names data worker Sumargo and attributes submission to chair Sumaun Utomo. This is not independent proof of national coverage or official intake.","next":"Request the source database from LPR-KROB, submission correspondence and Komnas HAM receipt, annex and catalog record.","year":2010},{"id":"C2410","text":"Pauker's 1964 report characterizes PKI leadership strategy as shifting the political balance through mass mobilization and elite maneuvering, and quotes Aidit describing peaceful parliamentary transition as the ideal if other political groups would permit it; Pauker's suggestion that the public dismissal of armed struggle may be tactical is his inference.","period":"PKI political strategy through 1964; report published 1964","type":"Contemporary U.S.-sponsored analysis quoting public party statements","source_ids":["S1357"],"confidence":"High that the report presents this interpretation and attributed quotation; moderate for its account of the PKI's overall strategy","basis":"Printed pp.9–10. The report cites party texts but is not an internal operational record and explicitly distinguishes public statements from its own inference.","next":"Compare the cited Aidit report and original Indonesian party publications with internal directives, training records and regional implementation.","year":1964},{"id":"C2411","text":"Pauker's 1964 report cites Harian Rakjat publication of Aidit speeches to the Army, Navy and Air Force staff schools and Police Academy, then hypothesizes that such access could influence some officers; the speeches document public institutional access, not officer conversion or a conspiracy.","period":"1962–1964; report published 1964","type":"Contemporary analysis based on period press citations","source_ids":["S1357"],"confidence":"High that Pauker cites the speech publications; low for the inferred effect on military officers","basis":"Printed pp.31–32, notes 41–42. Harian Rakjat originals, attendance records and officer responses were not independently checked.","next":"Retrieve the cited Harian Rakjat issues, school invitations and attendance lists, speech manuscripts and subsequent officer correspondence.","year":1962},{"id":"C2412","text":"Pauker's 1969 report says the surviving PKI leadership apparently chose South Blitar as an East Java base from which to rebuild the party and attributes that decision to Tjugito, Rewang and Hutapea.","period":"1967–1968; report published 1969","type":"Contemporary U.S.-sponsored analyst reconstruction","source_ids":["S1358"],"confidence":"High that the report makes this attribution; moderate-to-low for the underlying decision because the wording is inferential and no minutes are cited there","basis":"Printed p.61. Hearman's later account overlaps with participant recollections and is not independent confirmation of every detail.","next":"Find South Blitar leadership minutes, project committee records, Rewang's original interview or memoir, and Army intelligence copies of captured PKI papers.","year":1967},{"id":"C2413","text":"Pauker's 1969 report describes a disagreement among underground leaders over whether to rebuild a mass-based clandestine structure before armed struggle or to begin isolated attacks sooner.","period":"1967–1968; report published 1969","type":"Contemporary analytical account of reported internal debate","source_ids":["S1358"],"confidence":"High that the report states this disagreement; moderate for the debate's scope and factional representation","basis":"Printed pp.61–62; the passage does not establish how many leaders held either position, whether attacks were centrally authorized, or how Pauker learned the details.","next":"Compare the claim with captured party documents, Rewang, Munir and Winata testimony, local incident files and Trisula intelligence reports.","year":1967},{"id":"C2414","text":"Pauker's 1969 report states that about 2,000 underground PKI members and leaders were killed in the summer of 1968 and its footnote cites two New York Times reports; this is an attributed contemporary estimate, not a verified victim count.","period":"1968-06 to 1968-08; report published 1969","type":"Contemporary casualty estimate with a newspaper citation chain","source_ids":["S1358"],"confidence":"High that Pauker printed the estimate and cited the papers; low-to-moderate for the number itself","basis":"Printed p.62 and footnote 71 cite NYT reports dated July 12 and October 29, 1968. No named roster, inclusion rule or count method is supplied. The October report is described by Les Evans in March 1969 as saying “killed or captured” totaled 2,000; Hearman later presents differing versions of the August newspaper estimate. Thus confidence is high for Pauker’s printed claim, low for 2,000 deaths as a unique or verified total.","next":"Obtain the original NYT July 12, August 11, October 29, 1968 and mid-February follow-up pages through TimesMachine/ProQuest/microfilm; then compare each category and time window with army operation lists, court/prison intake registers, village death lists, families and burial evidence.","year":1968},{"id":"C2415","text":"On March 20, 1961, NSC staffer Robert H. Johnson sent Rostow a condensation of three Pauker RAND papers for possible presidential reading and said they might inform a Sukarno visit and Indonesia policy; he warned that parts were speculative or impressionistic, and the published note says there is no indication Kennedy read it.","period":"1961-03-20","type":"Official record of research-to-policy circulation","source_ids":["S1359"],"confidence":"High for the memorandum and editorial note; no evidence that the President read the summary or adopted its arguments","basis":"The three underlying RAND papers and the 10-page condensation were not inspected. Johnson also challenged a key argument's factual and logical support.","next":"Obtain the attached condensation and three RAND papers from the cited Kennedy Library file; search presidential briefing and reading records.","year":1961},{"id":"C2416","text":"The October 26, 1965 Jakarta Embassy telegram records an early U.S. assessment that alleged Aidit and other top PKI leaders were almost certainly involved in planning, while stating that many facts remained unknown, the goals of participants may have differed, and Chinese provision of several thousand guns was not established.","period":"1965-10-26","type":"Contemporaneous Embassy analytic judgment and attributed allegation","source_ids":["S1360"],"confidence":"High for what the cable says; low for its substantive allegations without independent Indonesian or Chinese records","basis":"Telegram 1184, paras.1–3 and 6–8. Its rapid narrative moves between reported events, inference and uncertain claims; its China statement explicitly says the possibility was not established.","next":"Trace the cited Airgram 300, Chinese and Indonesian arm manifests, party and officer files, and the CIA assessment cited in the editorial note.","year":1965},{"id":"C2417","text":"The FRUS editorial note to the October 26 Embassy telegram reports that a separate CIA October 28 assessment said elements of the PKI were involved but the role of party leadership remained obscure.","period":"1965-10-28; published in FRUS","type":"Official editorial note describing a separate contemporaneous intelligence assessment","source_ids":["S1360"],"confidence":"High that the FRUS note reports this; the underlying CIA memorandum and sources have not been inspected","basis":"OCI No.2342/65 is cited to the Johnson Library National Security File. This records a difference in U.S. assessments, not a final finding about PKI authorization.","next":"Obtain OCI No.2342/65 and its source notes from the Johnson Library; compare with Supardjo, Subekti, trial records and party papers.","year":1965},{"id":"C2418","text":"A FRUS editorial note citing the Jakarta Embassy's October 23, 1965 telegram 1166 says the central policy question was how to help the Army win without revealing the assistance; it establishes a covert policy concern, not the specific approval, delivery, receipt or effect of any aid.","period":"1965-10-23; published in FRUS","type":"Official editorial note quoting a contemporaneous U.S. policy cable","source_ids":["S1360"],"confidence":"High that the published note records this wording; the full telegram and operational follow-through remain unreviewed","basis":"The note cites RG 59 POL INDON–US; separate later records document individual assistance proposals and transfers with varying levels of completion.","next":"Read telegram 1166 and adjacent records; trace each aid proposal through authorization, purchase, shipment, recipient receipt and use.","year":1965},{"id":"C2419","text":"Richard Helms's January 1966 memorandum describes U.S.-supported Thai counterinsurgency programs spanning civilian police, village communications, security roads and rural development, under regular Special Group counterinsurgency review.","period":"Thailand, 1965–1966","type":"Contemporaneous U.S. policy and assistance record for regional comparison","source_ids":["S1361"],"confidence":"High for what the signed memorandum records; no conclusion about program effectiveness","basis":"This concerns Thailand, not Indonesia. The memo is a U.S. institutional perspective and two paragraphs remain classified in the published version.","next":"Compare U.S. and Thai approval, budget, delivery and use records; keep this comparator separate from U.S. actions in Indonesia.","year":1965},{"id":"C2420","text":"The KODAM-I/KOHANDA annual report describes demonstrations across Aceh from 6 October to 2 November 1965 and says the mobilization escalated to killings of PKI/associated-organization and Baperki figures; it tabulates 1,941 people across eight Kosekhan sectors.","period":"1965-10-06 to 1965-11-02 (report section); report compilation date uncertain","type":"Military institutional self-account and aggregate count","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High that the report prints the total and sector breakdown; low-to-moderate for the underlying count and categorization","basis":"Printed pp.6–7. The eight entries sum to 1,941. No named roster, counting rules, date-by-date attribution or independent cross-check is supplied. Do not present it as an independently verified provincial death toll.","next":"Find district death/custody registers, victim names, civil records and underlying Kosekhan returns; compare with Melvin’s map and local lists.","year":1965},{"id":"C2421","text":"Immediately after its 1,941 table, the report states that “the people” suffered no human or property losses during the movement to suppress Gestapu and its affiliates.","period":"1965, report compilation date uncertain","type":"Military institutional self-description and category framing","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High for wording in the report; not evidence that no civilians or other people were harmed","basis":"Printed p.7. The same section has just listed deaths of people classified as PKI/organization/Baperki figures. This records the report’s category boundary and legitimating narrative, not an independently verified casualty statement.","next":"Compare the report’s categories with named victims, district registers, BAPs, detention and death records, and civilian property claims.","year":1965},{"id":"C2422","text":"The Aceh military chronology dates two South Aceh mass-killing reports to 14 October 1965: at Blang Pidie it records five deaths, four classified as Tionghoa and one as Indonesian, with victims’ property seized; at Sama Dua it records ten deaths and identifies four named Tionghoa victims as Hap Tjok, Min On, Bun Tok Seng and Sio Nai (Melvin transcribes the last name as Sio Nam).","period":"1965-10-14","type":"Regional military chronology; attributed incident report","source_ids":["S855","S612"],"confidence":"High that the inspected chronology and Melvin’s transcription report these categories and names; moderate-to-low for the underlying events and totals","basis":"Chronologis p.8. The report does not name perpetrators. In the Sama Dua ten-person total, six victims are not ethnicity-coded in the entry (two named without an ethnicity label and four unnamed). Preserve original spellings; no Chinese-character names or standardized transliterations were found. The article calls the entries separate events, but states that the four named Chinese victims in the Sama Dua entry were all from Blang Pidie; this may denote origin/residence rather than duplicate deaths. Until Chronologis 1966 p.8 is inspected, do not combine the two event rows into unique-person totals or aggregate ethnic shares.","next":"Seek the original South Aceh police/military daily log, village/civil death records, family lists, property seizure records and independent oral testimony; compare the full chronology line by line.","year":1965},{"id":"C2423","text":"Melvin reports that 21 members of the ethnic Chinese community are listed as killed in the 21-page Aceh military chronology; she expressly states this excludes victims whose bodies were not left on public display and is not a final total.","period":"1965; published 2013","type":"Scholarly count of names in a military chronology","source_ids":["S612","S855"],"confidence":"High that Melvin makes this count and caveat; low as a complete victim count","basis":"S612 note 20 cites Chronologis 1966 pp.1–21 and says the 21-person count excludes victims whose bodies were not left on public display. S855 is the same underlying institutional chronology, so these are not two independent counts. The chronology includes a 6 Dec 1965 entry of a Chinese Indonesian shot by a military post; the article does not identify whether that case is included in the 21. No denominator or complete victim roster is supplied.","next":"Reconstruct all 21 entries from the scan and match them case by case to the 6 Dec Beutong shooting, district death/custody registers, families, civil records and burial records; identify unlisted non-public deaths.","year":1965},{"id":"C2424","text":"For Bireuen on 11 October 1965, the Aceh chronology records that a crowd detained PKI member Aminuddin, Army personnel took custody of him and found him bound and injured, and three additional PKI members were rescued by ABRI.","period":"1965-10-11","type":"Military chronology entry about crowd action and Army intervention","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High that the chronology reports this sequence; moderate for independent event confirmation","basis":"Chronologis p.5. The report uses a military framing and supplies no names for the three additional people or separate police/medical records. One incident cannot establish a uniform Army posture across Aceh.","next":"Find Bireuen police, hospital, detention and military logs; identify the three unnamed people and compare with local witness accounts.","year":1965},{"id":"C2425","text":"The annual report says an Aceh screening team was created under an instruction dated 4 October 1965; a 10 October Pepelrada radiogram required meetings and demonstrations to obtain prior permission; and a 25 October Pangdahan A decision placed named vital state installations under Pepelrada supervision.","period":"1965-10-04 to 1965-10-25","type":"Military report’s summary of regional security orders","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High that the report records these order dates and mechanisms; execution and legal scope need the signed instruments","basis":"Printed pp.8–9. The report cites order numbers but the signed originals were not inspected; reported control covered PNKA, Postel, PLN and the Tjot Girek project.","next":"Retrieve the cited Pepelrada/Pangdahan orders, distribution lists and implementation records for the named agencies and sites.","year":1965},{"id":"C2426","text":"The report says Instruction MSR-10/G-I/11092400/65 directed district commanders to arrest, detain and question leaders, cadres, propagandists and members of PKI, Partindo and Baperki, seize their movable and immovable property, and block or control their bank accounts.","period":"1965, instruction cited in annual report","type":"Military report’s account of a regional arrest and asset-control instruction","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High that the annual report cites and summarizes this instruction; low-to-moderate for the full scope and implementation without the original","basis":"Printed p.9. The signed instruction, issuing authority, recipients, case files and person/property lists are not yet inspected. The report’s inclusion of Baperki is explicit.","next":"Obtain original Instruction MSR-10, receipt/distribution records, arrest and property inventories, bank circulars and subsequent case registers.","year":1965},{"id":"C2427","text":"The report says Pangdahan A ordered subordinate units to form “sukwan serba guna” auxiliaries and task forces made up of workers and organizations loyal to the Army as replacements if strikes, sabotage or closures occurred; it also records permission to lend firearms to trusted, trained heads of vital offices.","period":"1965-11, dates/order references printed on report p.9","type":"Military report’s account of civilian auxiliary mobilization and weapons lending","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High for the report’s stated mechanism; actual formation, size and weapons issue remain unverified","basis":"Printed p.9. No roster, training record, firearm receipt or deployment log is supplied in the inspected pages. “Sukwan” and “task force” should not be relabeled as death squads without case-specific evidence.","next":"Find subordinate formation orders, member lists, training attendance, arms receipts/returns and deployment reports; compare with local accounts of civilian auxiliaries.","year":1965},{"id":"C2428","text":"The report says KODAM-I/Iskandarmuda was appointed regional defense command A on 1 August 1965, formalized on 25 August with two KOSUBDAHAN and eight KOSKEHAN, and renamed KOHANDA Aceh in October; a 14 October instruction established a War Room whose communications were operating although implementation was incomplete.","period":"1965-08-01 to 1965-10-14","type":"Military institutional account of territorial command restructuring","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High that the report gives these dates and structure; implementation is partly described as incomplete","basis":"Printed pp.16–17. The report cites formal orders but the originals and subordinate-unit records have not been checked.","next":"Retrieve Kep-3/8/1965, War Room Instruction 1/10/1965, unit establishment tables, signal logs and command traffic.","year":1965},{"id":"C2429","text":"The annual report says that from 1 October all KOHANDA Aceh forces were deployed in operations to suppress Gestok, focused on intelligence and territorial work, while full tactical capacity was held ready to enlarge the striking force; the same section says intelligence staffing from command to district level was only about 70 percent and trained counter-intelligence personnel were scarce.","period":"1965-10 onward; report compilation date uncertain","type":"Military institutional self-assessment of operational posture and intelligence capacity","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High for the report’s stated posture and self-assessment; actual readiness and capacity require operational records","basis":"Printed pp.16–17. The report presents both force posture and material limits but does not give unit strength, orders of battle, daily operation logs or independent measures of intelligence effectiveness.","next":"Compare the account with KOHANDA daily orders, personnel tables, intelligence reports, unit deployment logs, civilian auxiliary rosters and casualty/detention records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2430","text":"The Aceh report retrospectively alleges local PKI planning for revolutionary councils, armed protection groups and support for Gestapu, and alleges Baperki supplied material or financial support; it attributes parts of this account to interrogations and searches.","period":"Claims about 1965 events, recorded in the 1965 annual report","type":"Military accusation and retrospective causal narrative","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High that the Army report makes these allegations; low that the allegations are true without independent evidence","basis":"Printed p.5 and associated sections. The inspected report does not provide interrogation transcripts, chain-of-custody records for seized objects, party minutes, financial accounts or independent corroboration. This is not proof that the PKI or Baperki centrally authorized G30S.","next":"Obtain signed interrogations, search inventories, party and Baperki records, local meeting minutes, bank/account books and contemporaneous independent reports; compare with trial and participant accounts.","year":1965},{"id":"C2431","text":"The Aceh chronology’s 6 December 1965 entry says a 27-year-old Tionghoa Indonesian citizen from Meulaboh was shot dead by a member of the Beutong post in Aceh Pidie; it says he had fled that post the prior evening, used two names and allegedly tried to seize a post member’s weapon.","period":"1965-12-06","type":"Military chronology entry reporting a fatal shooting by a military post and its stated rationale","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High that the chronology records the shooting and the military’s explanation; low-to-moderate for the victim’s identity and circumstances without independent records","basis":"Chronologis p.20. The report does not provide a securely legible personal name or an independent witness account. Its allegations about identity and attempted weapon seizure are the post’s reported explanation, not independently established facts. The case is not matched to Melvin’s 21-person ethnic-Chinese count.","next":"Seek Beutong post log, PMTOH bus passenger/driver records, police and hospital/death registrations, burial and family records; identify the victim and determine whether included in the 21-person list and 1,941 aggregate.","year":1965},{"id":"C2432","text":"The 25 October 1965 chronology entry reports that five unidentified men wearing white cloth masks approached a Chinese person’s residence in Banda Aceh, allegedly intending to abduct a Chinese guest but failing; the same men then searched another Chinese person and took Rp2,000.","period":"1965-10-25","type":"Military chronology entry about reported anti-Chinese intimidation/property crime","source_ids":["S855"],"confidence":"High that the report prints this account; low for perpetrator identity, motive and exact location wording","basis":"Chronologis p.11. The wording places the first home in a location described as a Sinabang Chinese person’s home on/near Pakanbaru in Banda Aceh; the precise geographic referent and the second site are unclear. It does not say the assailants entered the house.","next":"Check local police, military, port/boat and property records; identify the two victims, exact addresses and perpetrators; compare with Chinese community oral histories.","year":1965},{"id":"C2433","text":"Melvin’s note 20 gives 21 ethnic-Chinese deaths in the 21-page Aceh chronology but does not list those 21 names in the article; the same chronology separately reports the 6 December Beutong-post shooting of a Chinese Indonesian, which is not discussed in the article’s narrative. Whether the shooting is among the 21 is unresolved.","period":"1965; article published 2013","type":"Cross-source reconciliation question / unresolved list inclusion","source_ids":["S612","S855"],"confidence":"High for the article’s 21-count footnote and the chronology’s shooting entry; no confidence yet in whether the individual is counted","basis":"S612 note 20 and main text, compared with S855 Chronologis p.20. Do not add the shooting as a 22nd victim or assume it is included until the individual-level basis for the 21 is reconstructed.","next":"Compare every Chronologis entry used by Melvin with the 6 Dec report, names, public-body status, burial records and any list/notes retained by the researcher or archive.","year":1965},{"id":"C2434","text":"Lendy Ramadhan's 2012 thesis appends a transcript of an interview with former PKI Politburo member Rewang conducted in Jakarta on 30 March 2011.","period":"2011 interview; published 2012","type":"Source-provenance fact","source_ids":["S1362"],"confidence":"High that the thesis gives this interview date and transcript; medium that the transcript is complete and faithful","basis":"The appendix gives the date and identifies Rewang as the interviewee, but the audio, transcript preparation and editing history were not available.","next":"Locate the original recording, interviewer notes, consent record and any unedited transcript.","year":2011},{"id":"C2435","text":"In the 2011 interview Rewang said he was a Politburo candidate member from 1961, became a full member around 1964, and moved to Jakarta in July 1965.","period":"1961–1965","type":"Retrospective biographical statement","source_ids":["S1362"],"confidence":"High that the transcript contains this self-report; medium for exact dates","basis":"The source is a later oral-history transcript; Hearman (S1310) places his full Politburo membership in 1965, creating an unresolved chronology difference.","next":"Check Rewang's memoir, party rosters, appointment records, correspondence and other contemporaneous references.","year":1961},{"id":"C2436","text":"In the 2011 interview Rewang rejected the interviewer's framing that Aidit alone carried the Leninist line while Njoto was a revisionist excluded from a six-person G30S decision, and instead attributed responsibility for the mistaken line collectively to the Politburo.","period":"2011 interview about 1965","type":"Retrospective testimony / non-confirmation","source_ids":["S1362"],"confidence":"High that this is the answer recorded in the transcript; low as a complete account of the 1965 decision process","basis":"The interviewer supplied a leading characterization; Rewang's response does not establish who knew of which operational step, and he did not confirm a six-person decision or deliberate exclusion of Njoto.","next":"Compare the complete recording, other Politburo testimony, party minutes, interrogation records and contemporaneous correspondence.","year":2011},{"id":"C2437","text":"Munir's reproduced 1973 defense speech is dated 2 March 1973 at 04:30 and signed from the Military Detention House on Jalan Budi Utomo, Jakarta.","period":"1973-03-02","type":"Document date and place as reproduced","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that the web text contains this signature block; medium that the transcription exactly reproduces the court copy","basis":"No original court copy or recording was inspected; the blog does not establish a complete custody or transcription chain.","next":"Locate the original pledoi in the military court file and compare the complete text and pagination.","year":1973},{"id":"C2438","text":"Munir wrote that Aidit had told him the Politburo Daily Board approved the young officers' idea to pre-empt a Council of Generals coup and form a Revolutionary Council.","period":"1965 event described in 1973 defense","type":"Retrospective participant statement","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that Munir made this claim in the reproduced plea; low-to-moderate for the underlying private communication","basis":"The statement is a defendant's retrospective testimony in a capital case. No Daily Board minutes or independent record of this conversation has been found.","next":"Seek the original plea, Aidit's notes, Politburo Daily Board records, Sjam/Rustomo/Munir trial files and other participants' accounts.","year":1965},{"id":"C2439","text":"Munir described the 27/28 August 1965 meeting as Aidit's briefing to Politburo and Central Committee members present in Jakarta, and said no decision or assent/dissent was taken at that briefing.","period":"1965-08-27/28 as recalled in 1973","type":"Retrospective participant statement about meeting procedure","source_ids":["S1376","S1481"],"confidence":"High that this is Munir's account; low-to-moderate for the meeting's full procedure","basis":"This describes Munir's account of the briefing itself and does not negate his separate claim about an earlier Daily Board agreement. Safwan's later state narrative reports a 28 Aug expanded meeting and full delegation to Aidit via KOPKAMTIB; no contemporaneous minutes reconcile the different descriptions.","next":"Compare with other attendees' accounts, interrogation records, the 1966 self-criticism and party calendars/minutes.","year":1965},{"id":"C2440","text":"Munir stated that Aidit separately told him the Revolutionary Council would counter the Council of Generals and accelerate formation of a Nasakom cabinet.","period":"1965 event described in 1973 defense","type":"Retrospective participant statement","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that the plea makes this assertion; low for the conversation absent corroboration","basis":"Munir explicitly distinguished this private communication from what he said was included in the August briefing; no independent note or recipient record is available.","next":"Find the original plea and cross-check Aidit's contacts, Nasakom cabinet planning records, Rustomo and other Politburo accounts.","year":1965},{"id":"C2441","text":"Munir said Aidit assigned him to Surabaya to assist East Java Special Bureau head Rustomo; he attended a meeting Rustomo led with military personnel and briefed East Java provincial secretary Suwandi on the party line.","period":"1965","type":"Retrospective participant statement","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that Munir reported these actions; medium for the events pending corroboration","basis":"The account appears in a courtroom defense and casts the purpose as opposing a Council of Generals coup; no meeting record or military participants are identified by name.","next":"Search East Java Special Bureau records, Rustomo/Suwandi files, military-unit logs and trial testimony.","year":1965},{"id":"C2442","text":"Munir argued that Biro Chusus was an Aidit-created parallel channel absent from the PKI constitution and not reported to ordinary party committees.","period":"Before and during 1965; stated 1973","type":"Retrospective institutional claim in a legal defense","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that Munir made this argument; medium that it accurately captures formal constitutional status","basis":"The statement is self-defensive and should be compared with 1966 self-criticism, party documents, officers' accounts and evidence of actual operating practice.","next":"Locate the PKI constitution editions, Bureau rosters, reporting lines, correspondence and original trial evidence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2443","text":"Munir described the post-1966 South Blitar relocation as an effort to rebuild an illegal party from rural bases and shelter members and their families from pursuit and violence.","period":"1966–1968, as recalled in 1973","type":"Retrospective participant account of motive and organization","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that the plea states this rationale; medium for its representativeness across the whole network","basis":"Munir's account presents his own and the leadership's stated purpose; it does not show that all local activity followed a single plan.","next":"Compare Rewang and other survivor interviews, village records, military operational histories and party correspondence.","year":1966},{"id":"C2444","text":"Munir said South Blitar had not yet met the three conditions he considered necessary to begin an armed revolution: a strong party, broad mass support, and a workers' and peasants' revolutionary army.","period":"1967–1968, as recalled in 1973","type":"Retrospective strategic assessment","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that this was Munir's assessment in the plea; medium for the condition of every local unit","basis":"A defense argument after capture is not a neutral force audit; it is consistent with but not identical to other testimony that training and arms remained limited.","next":"Find membership and village-support lists, training rosters, weapon inventories and records of armed actions.","year":1967},{"id":"C2445","text":"Munir said South Blitar had village guerrilla militias and semi-regular guerrilla detachments, but no People's Liberation Army (TPR).","period":"By 1968, as recalled in 1973","type":"Retrospective organizational statement","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that the plea says this; medium for exact strength and organization","basis":"No roster, command chart or unit inventory accompanies the statement; it uses Munir's own terminology in a legal defense.","next":"Compare the original plea with Trisula reports, regional military records, participant accounts and captured-document inventories.","year":1968},{"id":"C2446","text":"Munir reported that Army operations in South Blitar began in January 1968, that the Politburo chose defensive resistance and escape from encirclement in April, and that the base fell in August.","period":"1968","type":"Retrospective operational chronology","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that the plea gives these dates; medium for the chronology of operations","basis":"His January–August sequence may refer to a wider period of encirclement and local operations, not the named Trisula campaign, which Hearman dates from June; do not merge the two labels.","next":"Obtain the plea original, army operational orders, Trisula chronology, provincial command logs and village-level records.","year":1968},{"id":"C2447","text":"Munir attributed South Blitar's defeat partly to leadership rebuilding too hastily and exposing activity before preparations were mature, and partly to the Army attacking with overwhelming force.","period":"1968 defeat, interpreted in 1973","type":"Retrospective causal interpretation","source_ids":["S1376","S1310"],"confidence":"High that Munir offered this explanation; medium that haste and exposure contributed; low as a complete causal account","basis":"Munir was a captured defendant and Hearman's account draws partly on overlapping survivor testimony; neither alone measures the relative cause of defeat.","next":"Compare independent local testimony, captured correspondence, Army intelligence and operation files, food/arms records and arrest chronology.","year":1968},{"id":"C2448","text":"In the reproduced plea Munir presented a post-1966 'three-banner' line centered on rebuilding a Leninist party, armed people's struggle led by workers and peasants, and a revolutionary united front.","period":"Post-1966 line as described in 1973","type":"Retrospective statement of political doctrine","source_ids":["S1376"],"confidence":"High that Munir stated this line in the plea; medium as a description of the broader party position","basis":"The speech is an individual leader's retrospective exposition; it must be checked against the original 1966 Politburo self-criticism and other survivor groups' programs.","next":"Compare the 1966 self-criticism manuscript, surviving party pamphlets, regional instructions and contemporaneous underground documents.","year":1966},{"id":"C2449","text":"TAPOL reported in 1982 that Munir was SOBSI General Secretary and Ruslan deputy General Secretary at the time the federation was banned, while describing Munir as deputy General Secretary from 1952.","period":"1950–1965","type":"Advocacy-bulletin biographical account","source_ids":["S1363"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL printed these titles and career dates; medium for the exact office chronology","basis":"Hearman 2016 and 2024 use different translated titles and describe different dates/offices. The discrepancy may reflect rank changes over time or terminology; it should not be silently harmonized.","next":"Check SOBSI congress proceedings, executive rosters, Indonesian union newspapers and WFTU records.","year":1950},{"id":"C2450","text":"TAPOL reported that Mohammad Munir was born in Madura in 1925.","period":"1925","type":"Biographical claim","source_ids":["S1363"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL reports this; medium as an exact birth year","basis":"No civil registration or family record was checked.","next":"Seek civil, school, union and family records.","year":1925},{"id":"C2451","text":"TAPOL reported that Munir began full-time SOBSI work in 1950 and became a deputy General Secretary in 1952.","period":"1950–1952","type":"Biographical/organizational claim","source_ids":["S1363"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL reports these dates; medium for exact appointment chronology","basis":"TAPOL is an advocacy bulletin; later scholarly sources use different titles but corroborate a senior SOBSI/WFTU role.","next":"Check SOBSI congress and executive records.","year":1950},{"id":"C2452","text":"Hearman reports that Munir was a WFTU General Council member and Ruslan a SOBSI deputy secretary-general/WFTU General Council member; her 2016 article uses a different SOBSI rank for Ruslan than her 2024 article.","period":"1953 and later career chronology","type":"Scholarship and office-title comparison","source_ids":["S337","S845"],"confidence":"High that the articles report these distinctions; medium for exact rank-and-date reconciliation","basis":"These are two articles by the same scholar and are not independent sources; translated titles may vary across time or publication.","next":"Check WFTU congress minutes, SOBSI leadership lists and the original language used in organizational publications.","year":1953},{"id":"C2453","text":"TAPOL reported that Ruslan had fought Dutch forces after the 1945 independence proclamation, commanded Pesindo troops, and entered SOBSI leadership after the transfer of sovereignty.","period":"1945–1965","type":"Biographical claim from advocacy bulletin","source_ids":["S1363"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL reports these details; medium for exact unit and command title","basis":"No military service file or Pesindo roster was consulted.","next":"Find Pesindo rosters, veteran registration, SOBSI files and family records.","year":1945},{"id":"C2454","text":"TAPOL and Amnesty report Munir's South Blitar arrest/prison entry as 14 July 1968, while Hearman's reconstruction dates his capture to 13 July; the one-day discrepancy remains unresolved.","period":"1968-07-13/14","type":"Cross-source date discrepancy","source_ids":["S1363","S1365","S1366","S1310"],"confidence":"High that the sources give these dates; no confidence in choosing one","basis":"Arrest, capture, registration or transfer may be dated differently; no arrest register or contemporaneous family notice was found.","next":"Obtain military/police arrest registers, prison intake record, trial file, detention transfer papers and family correspondence.","year":1968},{"id":"C2455","text":"Amnesty reported that Ismail Bakri was arrested in June 1967, sentenced in September 1973, had his life sentence commuted in 1978 to 20 years from arrest, and was released in August 1983.","period":"1967–1983","type":"Human-rights case chronology","source_ids":["S1364"],"confidence":"High that Amnesty's 1984 annual report gives this chronology; medium pending prison and court records","basis":"The report is Amnesty's human-rights summary and its prisoner-of-conscience classification, not the judgment or prison file.","next":"Locate the original sentence, commutation decree, remission calculation, prison register and release record.","year":1967},{"id":"C2456","text":"Amnesty reported that Munir received a death sentence on 12 April 1973, confirmed by the High Court on 23 November 1981 and Supreme Court on 28 March 1983, with presidential clemency rejected in October 1984.","period":"1973–1984","type":"Human-rights case chronology","source_ids":["S1366"],"confidence":"High that the July 1985 report gives these dates; medium pending original judgments and clemency documents","basis":"Amnesty cites reports and case information; the underlying decisions and clemency file have not been inspected.","next":"Retrieve trial, high-court and Supreme Court decisions, execution authorization, clemency petition and presidential decision.","year":1973},{"id":"C2457","text":"Amnesty's May 1985 urgent action and the UN Special Rapporteur's report repeat 14 May 1985 as Munir's execution date, while Amnesty's fuller July 1985 report gives 15 May.","period":"1985-05-14/15","type":"Cross-source date discrepancy","source_ids":["S1365","S1366","S1375","S1367"],"confidence":"High that the documents report both dates; unresolved as to the actual execution date","basis":"The UN report repeats information provided to it; Amnesty's July report is later and fuller but still not an execution register. Amnesty's August follow-up also repeats 14 May.","next":"Locate the execution order, firing-squad/post-operation log, body handover record, family burial and death registration.","year":1985},{"id":"C2458","text":"Amnesty's May urgent action said Munir's wife was not granted permission to visit before execution, but its July report says he met his wife and family for about three hours on 14 May.","period":"1985-05-11 to 14","type":"Cross-report discrepancy about family access","source_ids":["S1365","S1366"],"confidence":"High that Amnesty reports both; medium for the visit itself pending family testimony","basis":"The May text allows that nearby relatives visited; the July text reports a later family meeting. The difference may reflect a changed permission decision or corrected early information.","next":"Seek family letters, prison visitor logs, execution notification, photographs and interviews with relatives.","year":1985},{"id":"C2459","text":"TAPOL reported that Len Murray of the British TUC cabled Suharto on 1 September 1982 to oppose executing Munir and Ruslan, and that Terry Duffy also appealed to Suharto and Kopkamtib head Sudomo.","period":"1982-09-01","type":"International labor advocacy","source_ids":["S1363"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL published this account and cable text; medium for transmission/receipt absent cable records","basis":"This is post-1965 labor advocacy concerning death sentences, not evidence of covert foreign intervention in the 1965 events.","next":"Find the TUC cable and AUEW correspondence, Indonesian presidential and Kopkamtib registries, and replies.","year":1982},{"id":"C2460","text":"Amnesty reported that after Munir's execution Dutch, Australian and European Community officials appealed against further executions, and that Dutch minister Eegje Schoo met Suharto and the Justice Minister on 25 May 1985.","period":"1985-05 to 06","type":"International diplomatic and human-rights response","source_ids":["S1366","S1367"],"confidence":"High that Amnesty reported these contacts and appeals; medium for individual communications pending government archives","basis":"Amnesty summarizes diplomatic reports; Dutch, Australian, Indonesian and European Community files were not directly inspected.","next":"Retrieve Dutch foreign-ministry notes, ambassador cables, Schoo's meeting record, Australian diplomatic files and EC appeal text.","year":1985},{"id":"C2461","text":"A UN Special Rapporteur report reproduced the Indonesian mission's 22 July 1985 statement that Munir's sentence and execution followed normal legal proceedings and due process.","period":"1985-07-22","type":"Government position reproduced in UN report","source_ids":["S1375"],"confidence":"High that the UN report reproduces the government's statement; this is not independent proof of due process","basis":"The UN report also records allegations about trial fairness; the government release is an official position, not a judicial re-evaluation or independent finding.","next":"Obtain the government's original press release, trial transcripts, counsel access records and appellate judgments.","year":1985},{"id":"C2462","text":"Amnesty's 1990 report lists Ruslan as tried in a Special Military Court but says in its narrative that the Central Jakarta Subversion Court sentenced him on 15 July 1974.","period":"1974; report published 1990","type":"Internal source discrepancy / court identification","source_ids":["S1368"],"confidence":"High that one Amnesty report gives both court labels; no confidence in choosing one","basis":"The underlying judgment and court register have not been located; do not treat the table or narrative as a verified court caption.","next":"Retrieve the trial judgment and court docket, appeal records and sentence warrant.","year":1974},{"id":"C2463","text":"Amnesty listed Ruslan as age 70 in February 1990 and age 77 in its 1995 briefing; TAPOL had described him as about 65 in 1982.","period":"1982–1995","type":"Approximate-age discrepancy","source_ids":["S1363","S1368","S310"],"confidence":"High that the publications give these approximate ages; no exact birth date established","basis":"The figures may be estimates or rounding, and do not support a precise birth year.","next":"Seek civil registration, identity papers, family records, school/military records and prison intake files.","year":1982},{"id":"C2464","text":"Amnesty's 1995 trade-union report calls Ruslan a former ILO 'Secretary General,' while the official ILO officeholder list gives David A. Morse as Director-General through 1970 and Hearman identifies Ruslan with SOBSI/WFTU offices.","period":"Through 1970 and Ruslan's 1950s–1960s career","type":"Title discrepancy / likely institutional misidentification","source_ids":["S1374","S1373","S337","S845"],"confidence":"High that the title is inconsistent with ILO officeholder records; medium-high that it is a report error or conflation","basis":"This does not establish all of Ruslan's union titles; the likely conflation may involve SOBSI General Secretary or WFTU activity.","next":"Check the original Amnesty report and source notes, WFTU leadership lists and SOBSI congress records; do not repeat the ILO title as fact.","year":1970},{"id":"C2465","text":"Cornell's WFTU finding aid catalogs a 1953 Congress report on colonial and semi-colonial trade unions with Ruslan Widjajasastra as reporter.","period":"1953-10-10 to 21","type":"Archival locator","source_ids":["S1372"],"confidence":"High that the indexed finding-aid entry names Ruslan and the report; no confidence about report contents","basis":"The guide's old URL now redirects; the Box 3, Folder 9 contents and report have not been read.","next":"Search Cornell's new archives catalog and request Box 3 Folder 9; obtain the report itself and compare name/title variants.","year":1953},{"id":"C2466","text":"ANRI's 1955 photo inventory catalogs a 9 January SOBSI congress-opening photograph and names Ruslan Widjajasastra and Ny. Munir among participants.","period":"1955-01-09","type":"Archival image-catalog metadata","source_ids":["S1370"],"confidence":"High that the inventory entry lists these names; no direct visual-identification confidence","basis":"The photograph image was not opened; the inventory is not a biographical or attendance register.","next":"Open the image and neighboring congress files; compare with SOBSI proceedings and family/photo records.","year":1955},{"id":"C2467","text":"A 2023 military-law journal article gives case-number leads for Ismail Bakri and Ruslan, but the original judgments were not located in the searches reviewed.","period":"1973-10-03 and 1974-07-15","type":"Archival/legal retrieval lead and failed search","source_ids":["S1371"],"confidence":"High that the article/search index gives these locators; no confidence that they reproduce the full case captions accurately","basis":"The article PDF returned 404 and the cited judgments were not accessed; case numbers must remain locator leads, not findings.","next":"Search Supreme Court and military court archives, Mahkamah Agung catalogs, legal repositories, ANRI and relevant military law libraries.","year":1973},{"id":"C2468","text":"Amnesty's April 1995 trade-union report labels Ruslan 'former Secretary General of the ILO,' a claim not corroborated by official ILO officeholder records or the opened SOBSI/WFTU scholarship.","period":"1995 report about Ruslan","type":"Unverified institutional-title claim","source_ids":["S1374","S1373","S337","S845"],"confidence":"High that the Amnesty report uses the label and that opened ILO history does not list Ruslan; medium-high that it is a misidentification","basis":"The label might reflect inaccurate translation or conflation in the advocacy report; avoid attributing intentional deception.","next":"Inspect the full Amnesty report and its cited sources; search Indonesian union rosters and ILO archives for any distinct staff post.","year":1995},{"id":"C2469","text":"Hearman's 2024 article attributes to former SOBSI leader Adam Soepardjan the claim that more than 500 detainees died of starvation in Surabaya's Kalisosok prison in 1968.","period":"1968; reported in 2024 scholarship","type":"Retrospective testimony reported in scholarship","source_ids":["S845"],"confidence":"High that the article reports Soepardjan's claim; medium-to-low for the count absent a prison roster","basis":"The article says Soepardjan drew on firsthand experience and research but no person-level list or independent count was inspected. Do not treat it as an audited number.","next":"Find prison registers, death records, survivor notebooks, family lists, local hospital/cemetery records and Soepardjan's original prison notebooks.","year":1968},{"id":"C2470","text":"The 1961 NSC/Pauker and 2024 WFTU articles add contextual leads but do not provide a nationwide, ethnicity-coded victim roster; the searches reviewed still cannot support a national victim percentage for Javanese, Balinese, Sundanese, Madurese, Chinese or other groups.","period":"1961–2024 research and 1965–1968 violence","type":"Coverage limitation / negative search result","source_ids":["S1318","S231","S612","S1310","S845","S855","S1362","S1363","S1366","S1375"],"confidence":"High that no such roster appears in the reviewed materials; not a claim that no dataset exists anywhere","basis":"Reviewed materials include regional estimates and partial lists with different units; 1961 census data lack an ethnicity question, and regional population-loss rates are not victim ethnicity shares.","next":"Continue seeking provincial/civil victim registers, self-identified ethnicity fields, census microdata, burial and family lists; require common period, category, denominator and de-duplication.","year":1961},{"id":"C2471","text":"Suryadinata, Arifin and Ananta state that Indonesian population censuses from 1961 through 1990 contained no direct ethnicity question and that the 2000 census was the first to include direct ethnic-composition data.","period":"1961–2000 census series","type":"Demographic-method fact, reported by scholarly demographic study","source_ids":["S1377"],"confidence":"High that the authors make this conclusion; high for the reviewed census series","basis":"The study reviews published census materials and says earlier ethnicity estimates depended on the 1930 census or indirect proxies such as language and religion.","next":"Compare the full 1961 census questionnaire and tables with BPS field manuals; retain any provincial or special survey exceptions separately.","year":1961},{"id":"C2472","text":"The official BPS catalogue confirms a 1961 Indonesian population census publication released on 6 June 1962, but the catalogue abstract does not list an ethnicity variable.","period":"1961–1962","type":"Official catalogue fact and limitation","source_ids":["S1383"],"confidence":"High for title, date and catalogue description; low for any inference from the abstract about every variable in the book","basis":"A catalogue record is metadata, not the full census questionnaire or tabulation volume.","next":"Download and inspect the complete census publication and any original questionnaire.","year":1961},{"id":"C2473","text":"The reviewed materials still do not provide a deduplicated national victim roster with person-level ethnicity, common event-period coding and a matched population denominator; national ethnic shares among those killed therefore remain uncalculable from this archive.","period":"1965–1966 evidence coverage","type":"Coverage limitation / bounded negative finding","source_ids":["S1356","S1377","S231","S612","S855"],"confidence":"High about the materials reviewed; not a claim that no such source exists anywhere","basis":"Regional event lists, rough totals, detention lists, population-loss estimates and post-1967 expulsions use different units and cannot be combined into national victim shares.","next":"Search local victim/family lists and provincial records; require identity, event, period, ethnicity source, denominator, deduplication and inclusion rules.","year":1965},{"id":"C2474","text":"Cribb and Coppel estimate roughly 2,000 ethnic Chinese deaths against an overall estimate near 500,000 and an approximate 2% Chinese population share, and argue that this does not show national overrepresentation; Woolgar reports a similar national-level conclusion while documenting harassment and regional violence.","period":"1965–1967","type":"Contested national estimate and scholarly interpretation","source_ids":["S1322","S231"],"confidence":"High that these scholars state the estimates and interpretation; medium-to-low for the underlying counts and comparison","basis":"Woolgar, printed p.1977, reports the approximate 2,000 estimate and its national comparison at footnotes 110–112; neither figure is drawn from a complete ethnicity-coded victim register. The ratio is an estimate-based comparison, not an observed victim proportion.","next":"Inspect article footnotes and underlying provincial estimates; compare with any national-level records and alternative scholarship.","year":1965},{"id":"C2475","text":"Melvin argues that documentary and testimonial records in Aceh show episodes of violence in which ethnic-Chinese identity was a targeting factor, challenging a blanket claim that ethnicity never shaped the killings; this regional evidence does not establish a nationwide ethnic percentage.","period":"Aceh, 1965–1966","type":"Regional scholarly interpretation / counter-evidence","source_ids":["S612","S855"],"confidence":"High that the article makes the argument; medium for each event pending original archival documents and witness recordings","basis":"Melvin’s evidence is geographically bounded and does not yield a nationwide denominator; Cribb and Coppel’s national-level argument and Melvin’s local evidence answer different questions.","next":"Inspect the cited KOHANDA files and testimony records; identify victims and distinguish targeted killing, expulsion, detention and property violence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2476","text":"Hearman reports that South Blitar cells were organized in small three-person groups with differentiated tasks including organization, education/outreach and armed struggle; interviewees’ evidence indicates regrouping and contact work remained central.","period":"1967–1968 South Blitar","type":"Scholarly reconstruction from oral histories and trial / military materials","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this structure; medium for exact prevalence and operation across all cells","basis":"Hearman, printed pp.79–80 and notes 100–101, cites interviews with Winata and Tuti; she explicitly says exact cell activities are difficult to establish and limits the inference mainly to identifying/reconnecting with cadres and supporters. One interview does not define every unit.","next":"Locate surviving cell members’ testimony, committee records, communications and individual trial files.","year":1967},{"id":"C2477","text":"Hearman reports Rewang’s account that arms could come from military deserters, theft and black-market purchase, while the military said it found 34 firearms, some old, and a few blowpipes in two captured youth detachments.","period":"1967–1968 South Blitar","type":"Retrospective oral testimony juxtaposed with military-reported seizure count","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that the article records both accounts; medium-to-low for the full weapons inventory","basis":"Hearman, printed p.80 and notes 102–106; the seizure count comes from the 1969 military history and no complete arms ledger or independently audited inventory is available. Rewang's proposed supply channels are retrospective testimony.","next":"Find Trisula seizure inventories, captured-detachment reports, court exhibits and Rewang’s original interview recording.","year":1967},{"id":"C2478","text":"Hearman reports Rewang saying any decision to undertake armed conflict should be democratically approved at a meeting of provincial committees; no minutes or signed resolution for such a meeting were inspected.","period":"South Blitar, 1967–1968","type":"Retrospective testimony about a proposed decision procedure","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that the interview reports this statement; low-to-medium that the described procedure was implemented","basis":"Hearman, printed p.80 and note 104, records Rewang's interview statement. It is neither a contemporaneous rule nor proof a provincial-committee meeting occurred; other survivor accounts differ. No minutes, signed resolution or independent participant confirmation was located.","next":"Search surviving provincial committee records, correspondence, cell reports and testimony from other participants.","year":1967},{"id":"C2479","text":"The South Blitar article juxtaposes military claims of armed training, Rewang’s account of negligible arms and slow preparation, and former fugitives’ denials of plans to attack; the evidence does not support collapsing the base into either a mature army or a wholly non-political refuge.","period":"1966–1968 South Blitar","type":"Source disagreement / bounded synthesis","source_ids":["S1310","S1376"],"confidence":"High that these competing accounts are present; medium for the extent and timing of preparation","basis":"Military reporting, defendant’s plea and survivor interviews have different incentives and vantage points; no comprehensive party or military operational file was located.","next":"Triangulate with full trial exhibits, captured documents, oral histories and local civilian records.","year":1966},{"id":"C2480","text":"Ong’s working paper dates CPM preparations to revive armed struggle to 1967 and its formal announcement to 1 June 1968; its account attributes the 1961 reorientation partly to Chin Peng’s later recollection of Deng Xiaoping’s persuasion.","period":"1960–1968","type":"Scholarly chronology with retrospective attribution","source_ids":["S1379"],"confidence":"High for the paper’s stated dates; medium for the exact internal decision path","basis":"The 1961 influence claim is attributed through Chin Peng’s memoir, and the paper also uses Malaysian security officials; it is not a CPM minute.","next":"Locate CPM congress/plenum documents, Chin Peng and Rashid Maidin memoirs, Thai border command records and Malaysian intelligence files.","year":1960},{"id":"C2481","text":"A village case study in Northeast Thailand identifies 7 August 1965 as the first deadly clash later commemorated locally as “First Gunfire Day”; this is a local commemorative chronology, not proof that all CPT armed activity began nationally that day.","period":"Northeast Thailand, 1965","type":"Local oral-history and commemoration fact","source_ids":["S1346"],"confidence":"High that the village study reports the local date and commemoration; medium for its designation as a national insurgency onset","basis":"The study is a single-village case with interviews, not a complete CPT operational chronology.","next":"Compare provincial security records, CPT histories and other regions’ first-clash chronologies.","year":1965},{"id":"C2482","text":"Bob Bergin’s retrospective study says the CPT had announced armed-struggle doctrine earlier but began its sustained insurgency in 1965; it describes rural recruitment, limited Chinese assistance as recounted by a Thai intelligence official, and a Thai People’s Liberation Army established in late 1968.","period":"1952–1968 Thailand","type":"Retrospective counterinsurgency history with attributed estimates and testimony","source_ids":["S1380"],"confidence":"High that the article states these points; medium for exact force size and assistance quantities","basis":"The study relies on Thai security perspectives and the author explicitly disclaims official US endorsement; its account is not a neutral party archive.","next":"Compare CPT internal histories, Voice of the People of Thailand broadcasts, Thai regional military records and Chinese archives.","year":1952},{"id":"C2483","text":"The CPB was already a long-running insurgent party before 1965; on 1 January 1968 its forces crossed from China into northeast Burma and opened a border-based campaign that differed structurally from the PKI’s previous public mass-party organization.","period":"1948–1968 Burma / Indonesia comparison","type":"Comparative historical statement","source_ids":["S1381","S1382"],"confidence":"High for the dated UCDP event narrative and CPB’s pre-1965 insurgency; medium for the comparative characterization","basis":"The comparison distinguishes organizational form; UCDP is a dataset narrative and Peng’s article principally covers 1969 onward.","next":"Review CPB congress and operational documents, Burmese archives, Chinese border records and organizational rosters.","year":1948},{"id":"C2484","text":"Zhang’s study follows two Beijing youths who joined the CPB army and finds their motives included political danger, escape from difficult rural resettlement and hopes for personal/family security, complicating a purely ideological account of Chinese volunteers.","period":"1968–1969 Burma / PRC","type":"Peer-reviewed biographical interpretation from two cases","source_ids":["S1378"],"confidence":"High for these two life histories; low for generalizing the motives to all volunteers","basis":"Two biographies and interviews cannot establish the composition or total size of the volunteer force.","next":"Compare other volunteers’ oral histories, Chinese sent-down youth records, CPB personnel files and family correspondence.","year":1968},{"id":"C2485","text":"The UCDP Burma–Shan profile describes CPB military expansion through alliances and recruitment among several borderland ethnic groups, while also recording disputes with some ethnic armed actors; this was not a homogeneous national party-army model.","period":"1968 onward Burma","type":"Conflict-dataset narrative","source_ids":["S1381","S1382"],"confidence":"High that the dataset and scholarly article identify an ethnically diverse border arena; medium on each alliance and strength without cited local records","basis":"“Ethnic group” and political affiliation are not interchangeable; boundaries and alliances shifted by time and locality.","next":"Trace each alliance to dated CPB, Kachin, Kokang, Wa and Shan records and separate membership from tactical cooperation.","year":1968},{"id":"C2486","text":"The 1968 re-established CPP’s own program called for a peasant-led armed force and rural base areas; its congress documents prove the party’s declared strategy, not the size or maturity of the force in 1968.","period":"Philippines, 1968","type":"Party program statement with implementation limit","source_ids":["S1344","S1345","S1315"],"confidence":"High for the text of the program; medium for its contemporaneous implementation","basis":"The party texts are political programs and later digital reproductions; organizational capacity requires independent records.","next":"Compare the CPP founding congress materials, first NPA roster, Huk veterans’ records, military reports and local land/organizing histories.","year":1968},{"id":"C2487","text":"The Communist Party of the Philippines was re-established in December 1968 and the New People’s Army was founded in 1969, according to the scholarly account and the party’s contemporary program sequence.","period":"Philippines, 1968–1969","type":"Organizational chronology","source_ids":["S1315","S1345"],"confidence":"High for the standard dates; medium for exact initial personnel and operational strength","basis":"Program ratification and later scholarship establish dates, but initial troop counts vary by source and should not be compared directly with mass-party membership figures.","next":"Check the founding congress communiqué, 1969 NPA declaration, military records and member biographies.","year":1968},{"id":"C2488","text":"The contemporaneous 1968 US NIE treated Thailand, Burma, Malaysia, Indonesia and the Philippines as different insurgency conditions, but its strength estimates are not standardized and reflect a US intelligence-policy frame.","period":"1968 Southeast Asia","type":"Contemporary intelligence assessment / comparative-source limitation","source_ids":["S1348","S1347"],"confidence":"High that the NIE makes these assessments; low-to-medium for estimated force numbers and causal judgments","basis":"The report’s language is Cold War strategic analysis and several estimates have no common definition of combatant, sympathizer, armed cadre or territorial influence.","next":"Compare its cited embassy and intelligence reporting with each party’s records and country-specific scholarship.","year":1968},{"id":"C2489","text":"Peng’s archival study cautions against treating CPB support as a single fixed Chinese-state policy: it finds Yunnan border counties continued practical aid and border access even as central policy shifted from the mid-1970s.","period":"Burma–China border, 1969–1989","type":"Peer-reviewed archival interpretation of foreign-support mechanism","source_ids":["S1382"],"confidence":"High that the study advances this finding; medium pending independent inspection of cited Yunnan files","basis":"This concerns the CPB and Yunnan after 1968, not Chinese assistance to the PKI or any operation in Indonesia.","next":"Inspect cited Yunnan Border Foreign Affairs Office files and compare them with Beijing central directives, CPB requests and Burmese diplomatic records.","year":1969},{"id":"C2490","text":"KontraS and ICTJ describe a documentation project begun in February 2011 that created event-based records and conducted interviews with victims/witnesses in Blitar and Buton.","period":"2011–2012; Indonesia, with field work in Blitar and Buton","type":"Source-method statement / institutional self-description","source_ids":["S1384"],"confidence":"High that the report describes this method; medium that the resulting database exhaustively represents the two regions","basis":"The report states its own project design and advocacy purpose; the underlying full database and interview set were not opened.","next":"Request the project protocol, database schema, full interview index and any anonymization/verification rules.","year":2011},{"id":"C2491","text":"KontraS’s report says Munir and Ruslan joined the East Java regional committee in late 1966 and, with secretary Suwandi, went to South Blitar in March 1967 to begin implementing the project committee.","period":"Late 1966–March 1967; East Java and South Blitar","type":"Later documentary reconstruction based on anonymous interviews and participant materials","source_ids":["S1384"],"confidence":"High that the report gives this sequence; medium for the precise dates and full group movement","basis":"The report cites anonymous interview codes FC/HCC and Munir’s 1973 defense; those interviews and the original court text were not inspected here. This may describe different arrivals/implementation stages than other accounts.","next":"Obtain the report’s interview key/recordings where available, Munir plea original, and Rewang/other participants’ dated travel accounts.","year":1966},{"id":"C2492","text":"In André Liem’s account, Rewang recalled proposing a move out of Jakarta after Sudisman’s arrest; the proposal was accepted, and East Java contacts had prepared a village considered suitable for a base.","period":"After December 1966; Jakarta to South Blitar","type":"Retrospective participant testimony recorded in oral-history research","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that Liem records this recollection; medium for the exact collective decision and timing","basis":"Liem’s chapter relies largely on 2000 interviews and some military documents; no CC minutes or dated transfer order was located.","next":"Inspect Rewang’s complete memoir/interview recording, East Java regional correspondence and any evacuation or project-committee instructions.","year":1966},{"id":"C2493","text":"Rewang told Liem that the underground leadership had formed an emergency CC, provincial leaderships, project committees and the beginnings of guerrilla detachments, while discussing when armed struggle could formally begin.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar and other proposed Java bases","type":"Retrospective participant testimony about organization and procedure","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that the interview text says this; medium for the exact completeness and operational reach of the structures","basis":"Liem reports that rosters were deliberately compartmentalized and interviewees could not identify all personnel; no complete party list or minutes were found.","next":"Find CC/Kompro rosters, appointment notes, meeting records, courier reports and dated unit returns.","year":1967},{"id":"C2494","text":"Rewang said a fully organized armed struggle would begin only after the project committees were ready and a national conference had reviewed party reorganization and political conditions; he described the timing as still under discussion.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Retrospective testimony about declared decision procedure","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that Rewang made this account; medium for whether every local unit knew or followed that rule","basis":"The national conference and its proposed review have not been located in a contemporaneous record; his recollection may summarize formal policy after the defeat.","next":"Locate draft conference convocations, CC circulars, provincial committee minutes and reports from local detachments.","year":1967},{"id":"C2495","text":"Rewang characterized some local disarmings and attacks against people he called “executioners” as actions outside the CC program and as premature actions that could alert the Army.","period":"Late 1967–early 1968; South Blitar and nearby districts","type":"Retrospective participant account and strategic interpretation","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that Liem records this statement; medium that the actions lacked all higher-level knowledge or approval","basis":"This is Rewang’s later explanation, not an extant order or a complete account of every operation; actors and motives varied by incident.","next":"Build an incident-by-incident table from local testimony, captured case files, Army intelligence chronology, court exhibits and any underground orders.","year":1967},{"id":"C2496","text":"Muyatno told Liem that the project-committee contact he knew was Gatot Taryo, whom he understood to be assigned as committee chair, and Joko Untung; he emphasized that members were not meant to know the full roster.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Participant testimony about a compartmentalized organization","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that the chapter records this testimony; medium for name spelling, formal title and committee-wide membership","basis":"The account itself says the witness did not know whether all named people were members or leaders; Gatot’s name also appears in variant spellings.","next":"Check original interview recording/transcript, aliases in captured files and project-committee appointment or contact lists.","year":1967},{"id":"C2497","text":"KontraS’s report describes a reorganized leadership in which Ruslan headed the Politbiro, Rewang led Agitprop and Munir led armed struggle; it says Munir completed the Perjuta concept in early September 1967 for circulation to other bases.","period":"1967; South Blitar and other PKI bases","type":"Later reconstruction from interviews and a participant’s legal defense","source_ids":["S1384"],"confidence":"High that the report states these roles and dates; medium for the complete roster, formal adoption and distribution","basis":"The report’s cited support includes anonymous interview codes and Munir’s retrospective 1973 defense; original appointment records, Perjuta manuscript and circulation list were not inspected.","next":"Locate the original Perjuta text, dispatch/courier records, CC appointments and source interviews behind FC/HCC.","year":1967},{"id":"C2498","text":"A Koran Sulindo article quoting Rewang’s 2017 memoir dates the emergency CC meeting to 23 May 1967 and names Ruslan as chair, with organizational work, Agitprop and armed-struggle departments assigned respectively to Ruslan, Rewang and Munir.","period":"1967-05-23; South Blitar","type":"Journalistic report of a later memoir, not direct inspection of the memoir","source_ids":["S1385","S1388"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this; low-to-medium for the exact meeting date and appointments","basis":"The memoir was not accessible in full, the article supplies no page citation, and the 23 May date has not been verified against minutes. Other sources date travel/Kompro implementation to January–March; those stages may differ rather than contradict.","next":"Inspect the memoir’s relevant chapter and notes; seek original CC/Politbiro records, copies of minutes and participant diaries.","year":1967},{"id":"C2499","text":"Arah Juang lists Tjugito, Iskandar Subekti, Sukatno and Suwandi among the emergency CC members and separately names Ruslan, Oloan Hutapea, Rewang and Munir as a new Politbiro.","period":"1967; underground PKI leadership","type":"Later political movement synthesis","source_ids":["S1387"],"confidence":"High that the article prints this roster; low-to-medium for a complete, formally adopted roster","basis":"The piece cites survivor memoirs and later political histories but does not provide item-level minutes; the roster may overlap with the same Rewang account reported elsewhere.","next":"Compare with full memoir, KontraS source interviews, organization papers in trials, military capture rosters and dated internal party documents.","year":1967},{"id":"C2500","text":"Koran Sulindo reports that a South Blitar guerrilla detachment had 150 armed fighters intended eventually to form a regular force.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Retrospective memoir claim relayed by secondary journalism","source_ids":["S1385","S1388","S1310","S1376"],"confidence":"Low for the underlying number; high only that the article reports it","basis":"No named roster, date, weapon count or unit definition accompanies the figure; Hearman and Munir describe limited weapons/capacity, while military seizure numbers are incomplete and differently scoped.","next":"Find the memoir page and exact term, Detga lists, training roll, weapon issue records, captured unit files and independently dated headcounts.","year":1967},{"id":"C2501","text":"KontraS’s report says Tjugito and Pratomo prepared a guerrilla-training text titled “Memimpin Perang Rakyat”; Tirto repeats this association.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Later report and journalism, with source dependence unresolved","source_ids":["S1384","S1386"],"confidence":"High that both later sources state the association; medium for authorship and actual distribution","basis":"Neither the manual nor its circulation records were examined; multiple retellings may depend on a shared participant or secondary source.","next":"Locate a surviving copy or capture inventory, identify the text’s authorship and date, and compare it with the stated training course and attendees.","year":1967},{"id":"C2502","text":"Koran Sulindo says the Army obtained more specific South Blitar intelligence after the December 1967 arrest of a person it names Kusno/Yudo and after Pratomo’s capture, then used the information to target the underground network.","period":"Late 1967–1968; East Java military operations","type":"Secondary summary of military and memoir accounts","source_ids":["S1385","S1310"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this sequence; low-to-medium for its dates, interrogation content and causal effect","basis":"The article does not supply the interrogation transcript or intelligence file; the detention, aliases and information chain need a provenance and coercion review.","next":"Obtain the relevant Trisula intelligence reports, arrest/interrogation records, Pratomo case file, and match names/dates against the official operation history.","year":1967},{"id":"C2503","text":"Koran Sulindo, summarizing M. Jasin’s memoir, says the Trisula operation initially lacked central funding, that Jasin borrowed 20 million rupiah from East Java governor Mohammad Noer, that an Air Force commander contributed two companies, and that Jakarta funding arrived later.","period":"1968; East Java / Trisula operation","type":"Later military memoir as paraphrased by journalism","source_ids":["S1385"],"confidence":"High that the article gives this account; low-to-medium for the exact amount, timing and budget mechanism","basis":"Jasin’s memoir and receipts, provincial finance records, command orders and Air Force deployment orders were not inspected; the story may frame a negotiated or staged funding process as a single personal loan.","next":"Inspect Jasin’s memoir pages, Kodam budget and requisition records, provincial treasury entries, Air Force orders and central transfer documentation.","year":1968},{"id":"C2504","text":"Liem’s chapter distinguishes South Blitar refugees gathering in 1966–67 and preparations beginning in early 1967 from the official New Order version it quotes, which says the PKI only began forming the South Blitar Kompro in October 1967.","period":"1966–1967; South Blitar","type":"Conflicting chronology as recorded by oral-history study","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that Liem records this contrast; medium on the underlying formal start dates","basis":"Liem, printed p.164, says the chapter’s interview and reconstruction differ from the official military chronology; he does not reproduce a dated party decree or the complete underlying military document. “Refugees arrived,” “site preparation,” and “formal Kompro formation” may be separate events.","next":"Read the cited 1969 Semdam and 1968 Skets Masa histories page by page; find dated Kompro/CC records, captured files, Rewang memoir pages and travel/communications records.","year":1966},{"id":"C2505","text":"Rewang recalled that after Sudisman was arrested in December 1966 he proposed leaving Jakarta for village areas to reorganize and prepare resistance; he said the proposal was accepted and East Java contacts had prepared a village considered suitable as a base.","period":"December 1966–early 1967; Java","type":"Retrospective participant testimony reproduced in oral-history chapter","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that Rewang gave this account as reproduced by Liem; medium for the exact sequence and timing","basis":"Liem, printed p.174, reports Rewang’s later interview. No decision minutes, communications from the East Java contact or dated travel records were inspected.","next":"Obtain the Rewang recording/transcript, the named East Java contact’s testimony, and dated travel or committee records.","year":1966},{"id":"C2506","text":"Liem reports Rewang’s recollection that roughly nine PKI Central Committee members, East Java regional figures and cadres from other areas began work in the project committees, while their structure and membership were compartmentalized.","period":"1967; South Blitar and other proposed bases","type":"Participant testimony and oral-history reconstruction","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that the chapter gives this approximate composition; medium for the number and full membership","basis":"Liem, printed pp.177–178, attributes the account to Rewang and Muyatno. No dated attendance list or complete roster is available; the chapter itself notes that participants often did not know other members.","next":"Compare names with the full memoir, captured lists, trial records and independent testimony; do not assume “nine” counts every CC member or all arrivals.","year":1967},{"id":"C2507","text":"Liem says the intended Kompro structure extended from district to village, while the “neat” chart shown in military publications may overstate how complete or consistently functioning the underground organization was.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Comparison of confiscated military material with survivor interviews and historian assessment","source_ids":["S1317","S1390"],"confidence":"High that Liem makes this methodological comparison; medium for how far the military chart overstates actual functioning","basis":"Liem, printed pp.177–178, says the military’s organizational chart presents a clear, effective structure but interviewees’ recollections suggest more limited and compartmentalized organization. The underlying confiscated papers and the cited military volume were not directly re-examined in this pass.","next":"Inspect the 1969 military history and captured documents; determine when each chart was created, which units existed on paper, and which were active.","year":1967},{"id":"C2508","text":"Hearman reports that some South Blitar participants described three-person cells responsible for organization, education/outreach and armed-struggle work, each linked to an outside contact; she says the cells’ exact activities are difficult to establish beyond locating and reconnecting with members and supporters.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Retrospective oral-history reconstruction with explicit limits","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that the cited interviews and article describe this structure; medium for prevalence and uniformity across the base","basis":"Hearman cites interviews with Winata and Tuti and an anonymous contact, and explicitly cautions that the cells’ activities cannot be reconstructed precisely. This is not a full organizational manual or proof every cadre belonged to a cell.","next":"Obtain interview transcripts/recordings, identify cells only where sources permit, and compare with captured papers and trial exhibits.","year":1967},{"id":"C2509","text":"Hearman reports that a local participant described cell work as identifying and regrouping with party members and sympathizers in surrounding areas; this supports a network-rebuilding function but does not establish that every cell conducted armed operations.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar and surrounding areas","type":"Historian’s bounded inference from participant interviews","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that Hearman states this; medium for how representative the described cell was","basis":"The article says it is difficult to determine what cells did beyond identifying and reconnecting cadres and supporters. The evidence is a limited set of interviews and does not describe every cell or command relationship.","next":"Seek cell-specific reports, correspondence, training records and interviews from people in different roles and project sites.","year":1967},{"id":"C2510","text":"Muyatno told Liem that South Blitar military training described to him remained limited to political/ideological study and practical skills such as knife-throwing, ambush and self-defence; a planned Malang Selatan school and war-cadre courses are described as plans, not demonstrated completed institutions.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar and South Malang","type":"Retrospective participant testimony and reported organizational plans","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that Liem records this testimony and plans; medium for the total training delivered across all units","basis":"Liem, printed pp.178–179, attributes basic skills and the distinction between plans and operating schools to Muyatno. Nearby interviews describe Maoist “village encircles city”/people’s war theory, a 10:1 guerrilla-force rule and Vietnamese tunnel experience as subjects discussed, not measured capabilities. No instructor rosters, attendance lists, course materials or school operation records were opened.","next":"Search captured materials, military interrogation files, student lists, training notes and the original manuals; distinguish plan from completed course.","year":1967},{"id":"C2511","text":"Rewang told Liem that caves and dug shelters in South Blitar were small, sporadic and unfinished, intended chiefly for refuge rather than combat; he described a long-term plan to connect shelters while building the party, using Vietnam as a model.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Retrospective participant testimony about shelters and strategy","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that Rewang made this statement as reproduced by Liem; medium for the extent and use of all shelters","basis":"Liem, printed pp.179–180, records Rewang’s recollection that the shelters were still initial, not connected and used to hide/protect people. Military depictions of a coherent underground network vary; no mapped shelter survey or full infrastructure inventory was examined.","next":"Compare military maps and photographs, local cave/shelter surveys, captured engineering notes and testimony from residents and different cadres.","year":1967},{"id":"C2512","text":"Liem records Rewang’s claim that children and older residents sometimes carried warnings or messages and that some local protection was voluntary; the same chapter records farmer Ruslan saying a village head ordered him and other men to carry military packs and supplies, while Hearman describes couriers, escorts, evacuation and other compelled civilian logistics.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Competing participant testimony and oral-history reconstruction about local support","source_ids":["S1310","S1317"],"confidence":"High that these accounts are reported; medium for the frequency and voluntariness of support across communities","basis":"Accounts refer to different people and actions. Rewang’s general description cannot stand for every resident; Ruslan’s single account concerns a specific military party and route-mapping excursion. Hearman records additional assistance and pressure, but the underlying military and village rosters are absent. Do not turn either account into unanimous support or opposition.","next":"Build village-specific evidence from resident interviews, evacuation and administrative rosters, courier/contact testimony, and military reports.","year":1967},{"id":"C2513","text":"Hearman reports divergent testimony about whether fugitives in South Blitar intended or participated in violence: some interviewees denied involvement or described refuge/family reunion, while others acknowledged that selected attacks, weapons theft or armed actions occurred.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Historian’s comparison of conflicting survivor testimony","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that Hearman documents divergent testimony; low-to-medium for the prevalence and precise scope of actions","basis":"Interviewees had different roles, information and legal/personal risks; Hearman notes that some actions were not widely acknowledged. Testimony does not establish command authorization, a universal movement policy or exact casualty totals.","next":"Match each alleged action to dated district/police/military files, victim and family testimony, court exhibits and party communications.","year":1967},{"id":"C2514","text":"Hearman reports that military evacuations in June 1968 moved thousands of residents from hamlets and villages into temporary holding camps for several days; one cited military-history example covers 22 June–5 July and names 12 village meeting points.","period":"1968-06 to 1968-07; South Blitar","type":"Oral-history reconstruction cross-referenced to an official military history","source_ids":["S1310","S1324","S1390"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this and identifies its page-based source; medium for the full population count and each site’s actual occupancy","basis":"Hearman, printed p.85 and note 139 cites Semdam VIII/Brawidjaja, Operasi Trisula, p.206, for a 22 June–5 July example with twelve named village meeting points; the article also cites Mrs. J.'s interview for warnings in camps. The military book, evacuee registers and camp occupancy lists were not directly reviewed.","next":"Read p.206 and adjacent pages; locate village orders, household lists, camp registers, return dates and testimony from each named site.","year":1968},{"id":"C2515","text":"Hearman says speeches in the holding camps warned residents not to assist communists or they would be killed, and aircraft dropped leaflets with similar messages.","period":"1968-06 to 1968-07; South Blitar","type":"Witness testimony and historian reconstruction","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports this account; medium until the underlying leaflet, order or independent testimony is inspected","basis":"The speech claim is footnoted to an interview with Mrs. J.; the underlying leaflets and operation instructions were not retrieved.","next":"Locate leaflet originals/photos, air-tasking orders, camp briefing scripts, and accounts from additional named villages.","year":1968},{"id":"C2516","text":"Liem records two separately interviewed local witnesses, Paijo and Maryono, describing soldiers bringing roughly forty male detainees to a South Blitar riverside, shooting them and compelling local men to bury or handle the bodies; Paijo describes bound detainees and a threat against the eleven forced laborers, while Maryono recalls eight victims put in a cave and thirty-two buried in two pits.","period":"1968; Luweng Tikus / South Blitar","type":"Specific witness testimony recorded by a later oral-history study","source_ids":["S1310","S1317"],"confidence":"High that both interviews are separately recorded and concern the same reported episode; medium for the event particulars absent contemporary records, named victim verification or forensic evidence.","basis":"Hearman, printed pp.83–84 and notes 133–138 reproduces Paijo's account; Liem's chapter separately records Paijo and Maryono interviews. These remain retrospective oral accounts with approximate counts and no victim-by-victim roster, forensic survey or contemporaneous operational log.","next":"Locate the original ISSI recordings/transcripts; identify the site without merging locality variants; seek family testimony, burial/missing-person records, forensic survey and military/camp registers; verify each remembered name and whether the cave/pits belong to this same event.","year":1968},{"id":"C2517","text":"Hearman reports that Suwandi, East Java regional secretary, was arrested on 12 June 1968 and paraded at public meetings urging people to surrender; the paper also reports a museum photograph showing him bound during interrogation.","period":"1968-06-12 onward; South Blitar","type":"Scholar’s reconstruction from a military history, participant accounts and a museum photograph","source_ids":["S1310","S1390"],"confidence":"High that Hearman documents the arrest date and public appeals; medium for the sequence pending direct file review","basis":"Hearman cites the military history and interviews; a witness reported hearing that Suwandi had been tortured for three days, which is an allegation, not a verified interrogation record. The photograph caption labels him as under interrogation but does not by itself establish when or how he was treated.","next":"Inspect the cited operation-history pages, Suwandi’s arrest/interrogation and court file, original photograph/caption provenance, and independent testimony.","year":1968},{"id":"C2518","text":"Hearman says the Brawijaya military history characterized the July–early August 1968 arrest wave as the product of “tactical interrogations,” a method said to induce detainees to cooperate with authorities.","period":"1968-07 to 1968-08; South Blitar","type":"Contemporary institutional terminology as cited by a later scholar","source_ids":["S1310","S1390"],"confidence":"High that Hearman cites this term to the army history; low-to-medium for what the procedure entailed in each interrogation","basis":"Hearman cites pp.137 and 140 of the military history; the underlying text, interview logs, coercion protocols and interrogation records were not opened. The label may be euphemistic or operationally specific; do not infer voluntariness from the term.","next":"Read the cited pages and original interrogation reports; compare detainee testimony, physical evidence, legal records and the Army’s own definition of the method.","year":1968},{"id":"C2519","text":"Hearman, citing the Brawijaya military history, reports that Ruslan Wijajasastra and Munir were captured on 13 July 1968 and Rewang on 20 July; separate Amnesty/TAPOL sources use 14 July for Munir and Ruslan, and a 2017 Tirto profile says Rewang was captured on 21 July.","period":"1968-07-13 to 1968-07-21; South Blitar","type":"Conflicting capture-date record across military-history citation, later scholarship and human-rights reporting","source_ids":["S1310","S1363","S1366","S1386","S1390"],"confidence":"High that the sources report these different dates; medium/low for the exact date of capture pending registers","basis":"Hearman cites *Operasi Trisula*, pp.137 and 142; Amnesty/TAPOL may record official detention/admission or prison intake rather than field capture. Tirto gives no record image for 21 July. Keep capture, formal arrest, notification and prison registration timestamps separate.","next":"Obtain operation logs, capture reports, interrogation booking sheets, prison intake registers, family notifications, trial files and original Amnesty source correspondence.","year":1968},{"id":"C2520","text":"Hearman says that by late August 1968 several senior South Blitar leaders, including Rewang, Ruslan, Iskandar Subekti, Sukatno, Djadiwirosoebroto and Mardjoko, had been transferred to Jakarta for trial.","period":"1968-08; East Java to Jakarta","type":"Retrospective participant testimony and historian reconstruction","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports this transfer; medium for each individual date and destination without transfer registers","basis":"The article attributes the timing to Rewang’s interview. Individual trial venues and detention facilities still need to be checked against court dockets and prison registers.","next":"Find transfer warrants, Lowokwaru/Madura prison ledgers, Jakarta detention registers, trial summonses and contemporaneous family notices.","year":1968},{"id":"C2521","text":"Arief, Soedarsono and Sumarno’s 1999 official history says that, after the 1967 leadership change in East Java, some strategically placed or vacant civilian offices—including village head, regent and rector—were temporarily filled by ABRI personnel.","period":"1967 onward; East Java","type":"Retrospective institutional account of military participation in civilian administration","source_ids":["S1392"],"confidence":"High that the report states this; moderate for the scope and duration of appointments because no roster or appointment orders were checked.","basis":"Printed p.40. The report names categories but does not list every officeholder or establish that every vacancy was filled by a soldier.","next":"Find governor and district appointment decisions, village caretaker lists, dates, and civilian/military chains of command.","year":1967},{"id":"C2522","text":"The 1999 provincial history says Kodam VIII/Brawijaya, acting as the core of the regional Kopkamtib special executive, directed Korems to conduct intelligence and territorial operations with civilian agencies; it describes intelligence as assessing unrest and remaining PKI capacity, and territorial operations as restoring government authority.","period":"1967–1968; East Java","type":"Retrospective official account of security and civil-military administration","source_ids":["S1392"],"confidence":"High for the stated institutional policy and stated aims; moderate for execution and local variation because orders and reports were not inspected.","basis":"Printed pp.43–44. The account’s use of “restoring authority” reflects the government’s own administrative framing.","next":"Obtain the original Kodam/Korem instructions, Laksus Kopkamtib records, six-hour reporting files, local civil-administration orders, and village-level implementation records.","year":1967},{"id":"C2523","text":"The 1999 history describes a separate secret Operation Sambar Raga in Surabaya from January through August 1968 and says authorities arrested 712 people called PKI remnants; its footnote points to Sketsmasa’s 1969 third-edition Trisula booklet, pp.40–41.","period":"1968-01 to 1968-08; Surabaya","type":"Retrospective official account; cited military pamphlet not opened","source_ids":["S1392","S1393"],"confidence":"High that the history reports 712 and cites this pamphlet; low-to-moderate for number, category, and actual operation scope absent registers.","basis":"Printed p.44 and note 17. This is Surabaya/Sambar Raga, not a Trisula detainee count and must not be added to the Blitar figure.","next":"Locate Sambar Raga orders, arrest and intake registers, Korem Surabaya reporting, Sketsmasa third edition pp.40–41, and individual case records.","year":1968},{"id":"C2524","text":"The 1999 official history claims that by late 1967 East Java’s “new-style PKI” had seven urban Triko and eight Kompro networks; it describes Blitar Kompro as having two guerrilla detachments, 17 village guerrilla groups, STPR/KKPR training and distribution of trained cadres elsewhere.","period":"Late 1967; East Java and South Blitar","type":"Retrospective military-aligned organizational account","source_ids":["S1392"],"confidence":"High that this is the state-sponsored history’s description; low-to-moderate that all listed bodies were active at the stated strength, since rosters, course records and captured documents were not read.","basis":"Printed pp.41–42. The report cites official military history for some details and uses the New Order category “PKI gaya baru”; participant-based studies describe uneven preparation, compartmentalization and contested recollection.","next":"Compare each named Triko/Kompro, Detga, Gerda and school against party papers, course registers, names, weapons receipts, village testimony and the original military chart; distinguish planned, named, staffed and operational units.","year":1967},{"id":"C2525","text":"A 1999 East Java history attributes to Kodam commander Major General M. Yasin’s 9 August 1968 Malang press conference an Operation Trisula total of 850 detainees and seizures of 37 firearms and four grenades.","period":"1968-08-09; South Blitar / Malang press conference","type":"Later government history reporting a contemporaneous military announcement","source_ids":["S1392","S1317","S1395"],"confidence":"High that the 1999 history attributes these figures to Yasin; low-to-moderate that the figures are complete or comparable to other totals until the press text and underlying ledgers are found.","basis":"Printed p.45. Liem reports a different official figure of 257 arrests for the May–July effective attack period, citing the 1969 Brawijaya book p.127 (C2337). Amnesty International’s 1984 report says Jasin claimed more than 800 arrests in June–July, mainly in South Blitar, and “many” PKI members killed, but gives no death total; this may relay the same official announcement and is not independent verification. The 9 Aug figure is a checkpoint during an operation whose follow-on phases continued; press text and registers remain absent.","next":"Find the 9 Aug 1968 press transcript and contemporary Malang/Surabaya newspapers; inspect S1390 p.127 and Trisula arrest lists; define whether counts include surrendered persons, noncombatants, repeat arrests, other operational sectors or the post-July phase.","year":1968},{"id":"C2526","text":"The 1999 provincial history says Trisula proceeded through reconnaissance, destruction, consolidation and clearance; it then places civil-territorial consolidation/rehabilitation through 7 September 1968 and a final territorial-development phase through 16 December.","period":"1968-05 to 1968-12-16; East Java","type":"Retrospective official operational chronology","source_ids":["S1392"],"confidence":"High for the chronology printed in this retrospective account; moderate for dates and boundaries until operation orders and closure reports are inspected.","basis":"Printed pp.44–45. Its described post-combat phases matter for the period of village administration and surveillance; they should not be collapsed into the named combat phase.","next":"Compare PRINOP orders, Trisula command logs, 7 Sep closeout, territorial rehabilitation instructions, and 16 Dec final-phase records.","year":1968},{"id":"C2527","text":"S1392’s 1999 chapter describes the 1967–68 consolidation as a project to restore governmental authority and later “New Orderization”; its introduction frames the New Order development era as overcoming crises attributed to the Old Order and the G30S/PKI rebellion.","period":"1965 onward; retrospective narrative published 1999","type":"Official memory and political framing in a government-sponsored history","source_ids":["S1392"],"confidence":"High for the text’s framing; this is evidence of later state historiography, not independent proof of the allegations or the success claimed.","basis":"Introduction printed p.1 and chapter 3 printed pp.39–45; its citation base includes official military and national histories.","next":"Compare with 1960s orders and press, provincial civilian records, village-level accounts, and later state commemorative materials; trace how wording and classifications change by edition.","year":1965},{"id":"C2528","text":"S1392’s bibliography lists a third edition of Sketsmasa’s Operasi Trisula, published by Grip in Surabaya in 1969, citing pp.34 and 40–41; this differs from the 1968 second edition listed in the NII/CiNii catalogue (S1391).","period":"1968–1969; source publication history","type":"Bibliographic discrepancy / edition lead","source_ids":["S1391","S1392","S1393"],"confidence":"High for the two different bibliographic statements; low for edition contents and pagination until copies are collated.","basis":"S1391 is a catalogue record for a 44-page second edition; S1392 printed p.55 cites a third edition. Same title and publisher do not establish that text or pagination is identical.","next":"Request both editions from IDE-JETRO/NII or Japanese/Indonesian library networks; compare title pages, contents, print statements, pagination and cited claims.","year":1968},{"id":"C2529","text":"Hearman’s 2010 scholarly article says the 1969 Brawijaya Trisula volume was fast-tracked for publication on 1 October 1969, the fourth anniversary of the coup attempt.","period":"1969-10-01; publication chronology","type":"Secondary source statement about an official military publication","source_ids":["S1310","S1390"],"confidence":"High that Hearman makes this statement; moderate for the exact imprint date until the book’s title page/colophon is inspected.","basis":"The book itself has not been opened. “Fast-tracked for publication” is the historian’s statement, not a verified title-page or print-shop date.","next":"Acquire the volume and inspect title page, colophon, edition and production files; compare the claimed date with any accession/receipt records.","year":1969},{"id":"C2530","text":"Hearman’s 2010 and 2017 accounts cite the 11 August 1968 NYT report for Trisula-era figures, but render the figure differently: about 2,000 “party members” captured/killed or killed, alongside arrests/detention and displacement. Her 2018 essay returns to the captured-or-killed wording and notes that local villagers were likely included; the original newspaper remains inaccessible.","period":"1968 figures as presented in 2010 and 2017 scholarship","type":"Cross-version comparison of a cited contemporary press count","source_ids":["S1334","S1394","S1396","S1425"],"confidence":"High that Hearman’s publications present these formulations and cite the same NYT item; low for the precise number and fate categories because the newspaper and any roster are unavailable.","basis":"Do not treat three Hearman texts as independent corroboration: they derive from one author’s research and the same NYT/Army reporting chain. The 2017 “2,000 killed” wording conflicts with the 2010/2018 captured-or-killed formulation; categories cannot be reconciled without the underlying article and method.","next":"Retrieve the 11 August 1968 NYT text and any Army briefing/operation registers; keep killed, captured, arrested, detained and displaced separate and check civilian inclusion.","year":1968},{"id":"C2531","text":"Amnesty International’s 1984 report says East Java commander Jasin claimed that more than 800 Communists had been arrested during June and July 1968, mainly in South Blitar, and that many PKI members had been killed.","period":"1968-06 to 1968-07; report published 1984","type":"Human-rights report relaying a military commander’s claim","source_ids":["S1395"],"confidence":"High that Amnesty reports this claim; low as independent verification of either the arrest count or killings.","basis":"The report gives no number of deaths and does not identify an underlying press article in this paragraph. It may descend from the same Jasin announcement later reported as 850 detainees; this is not an independent count.","next":"Locate Jasin’s statement and contemporary Malang/Surabaya reports; reconcile “more than 800 arrested,” “850 detainees,” count cut-off, geography and whether deaths are separate.","year":1968},{"id":"C2532","text":"Hearman 2010 says around 1,000 “party members” were captured or killed in a passage on Trisula, but footnote 61 gives Philip Shabecoff’s NYT article date as 13 February 1968, before the operation. Les Evans’s 3 March 1969 article says the 29 October 1968 NYT report put killed-or-captured at 2,000 and a mid-February follow-up cut that number in half, making the printed year a likely citation anomaly.","period":"1968-02-13 citation in a later account of May–September 1968 events","type":"Citation chronology anomaly","source_ids":["S1310","S1397","S1507"],"confidence":"High that Hearman prints the 1,000 figure and footnote date, and that Evans reports a mid-February follow-up after October 1968; low for the original NYT date, exact wording and what the 1,000 measured.","basis":"Evans is a derivative, politically committed newspaper account, not the original NYT article. The article page remains blocked. “Likely citation anomaly” is a chronological inference, not a bibliographic correction. Do not use 1,000 as a Trisula death total.","next":"Read the NYT TimesMachine/ProQuest/microfilm article, verify the issue date and paragraph context, and inspect Hearman’s original notes or an erratum.","year":1968},{"id":"C2533","text":"Hearman 2017 reports that Jasin described PKI political support in South Blitar as very high before October 1965, citing p.8 of his 1968 parliamentary briefing.","period":"Before 1965-10; South Blitar; claim reported in 2017","type":"A military commander’s retrospective statement, quoted by a historian","source_ids":["S1394","S1398"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports and cites the statement; moderate-to-low as a measure of actual support because the briefing and any underlying survey are unavailable.","basis":"The source is a command briefing rather than an election or membership census. It cannot establish a percentage in 1968 or show how every village related to the underground base.","next":"Obtain Jasin’s briefing p.8; compare 1955 election results by subdistrict, party/mass-organization records, 1961 census limits and village-level oral histories.","year":1965},{"id":"C2534","text":"Hearman reports that U.S. Consul D. T. Kenney and another consular official praised Trisula during a June 1969 visit as a counterinsurgency success while describing South Blitar as a “soft target.”","period":"1969-06; post-operation U.S. consular visit","type":"Scholarly report of a diplomatic field assessment","source_ids":["S1310","S1399"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports the cable’s assessment; no evidence this demonstrates U.S. participation in planning or conducting Trisula.","basis":"The June 29 airgram itself was not accessed; this is a year-after-operation assessment cited by a historian.","next":"Retrieve the full airgram and identify the officials, itinerary, interlocutors, operational access, and any follow-up decisions.","year":1969},{"id":"C2535","text":"A 2016 study citing the 29 June 1969 U.S. consular airgram reports that Kenney recommended food-for-work assistance, believing poverty reduction could deter renewed communist infiltration; the airgram also described government resettlement along roads to facilitate control.","period":"1969; South Blitar post-operation reconstruction","type":"U.S. consular policy advice as quoted/paraphrased in scholarship","source_ids":["S1324","S1399"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports this recommendation and resettlement statement; low that any U.S. funds were authorized or delivered.","basis":"The original cable, U.S. aid decision, Indonesian request, budget, shipment, implementing agency and recipient list were not obtained. Advice is not implementation.","next":"Read airgram pp.2 and 5; search AID/State/USOM food-for-work records, Jakarta/Surabaya decisions, Indonesian labor/social-affairs plans, disbursements and local project registers.","year":1969},{"id":"C2536","text":"A 1971 IKIP Surabaya/Balai Penelitian Pendidikan report on a foster-care survey for children affected by G30S/PKI in South Blitar and prospective foster families in Bagor, Nganjuk and Blitar municipality is cited by Hearman; her article connects the report to efforts to place children orphaned during Trisula with new families. Suwandi’s 1985 thesis says the 1971 pilot concerned children who lost both parents and reports child removals from prior family environments in two grandmother cases.","period":"1971; South Blitar, Bagor/Nganjuk and Blitar municipality","type":"Bibliographic record and later scholarly account of a state/university child-placement survey","source_ids":["S1394","S1400","S1401"],"confidence":"High that the report is cited and the article describes such a policy; low for the number of children, how placements were administered, consent and long-term results.","basis":"No copy of the 1971 report or child-specific register was accessed. “Orphaned” and “lost both parents” may describe a narrower pilot group, not all children affected by Trisula. The thesis offers ethnographic cases from 1980 fieldwork, while both survey citations remain secondary references; no child-level totals or case records are available.","next":"Obtain report and appendices; seek social-affairs case files, consent/guardian forms, birth/family records, placement addresses, return/contact histories and interviews with children and kin.","year":1971},{"id":"C2537","text":"Raharjo Suwandi says the fieldwork for his ANU thesis ran from October 1979 to March 1981 and identifies 1980 as his Blitar fieldwork year.","period":"1979-10 to 1981-03; Blitar fieldwork in 1980","type":"Methodology/provenance statement in an ethnographic thesis","source_ids":["S1401"],"confidence":"High that the thesis states these dates; the statement identifies the author’s fieldwork window, not the date of every interview or the child-placement events.","basis":"Printed preliminary pages and p.5. The thesis was completed June 1985.","next":"Check field notebooks/interview dates if exact timing of individual South Blitar accounts matters.","year":1979},{"id":"C2538","text":"In his retrospective South Blitar account, Suwandi describes children of peasants killed in the violence being taken from their prior family environment; he gives two grandmother cases, one whose grandson and another whose two grandchildren were removed, and says one grandmother tried to reclaim them.","period":"Reported in 1985 thesis about events in the 1970s; South Blitar","type":"Retrospective ethnographic observation and interpretation","source_ids":["S1401"],"confidence":"High that the thesis reports these cases; moderate as evidence of the particular experiences because names, dates and administrative records are absent. It does not establish a count for all children or every placement.","basis":"Printed pp.173–174. Suwandi uses “forced adoption” as his description; he records one local authority’s “sinful environment” phrase. The report is ethnographic, based on Blitar fieldwork in 1980, not a contemporaneous case file.","next":"Find the 1971 foster-care survey, child/caregiver case files, local social-affairs correspondence, guardianship/placement orders, and interviews with affected children and kin.","year":1985},{"id":"C2539","text":"Suwandi says a 1971 South Blitar foster-care survey detailed implementation of a pilot affecting children who had lost both parents.","period":"1971; South Blitar","type":"Later thesis citation to a government/university survey","source_ids":["S1400","S1401"],"confidence":"High that the thesis describes the survey this way; low-to-moderate for the pilot’s actual eligibility and scope until the 1971 report is read.","basis":"Suwandi printed p.174 identifies the report and the both-parents-lost subset. Hearman 2017 independently supplies a fuller bibliographic title, but both are secondary references to an unaccessed report.","next":"Obtain the report and establish numbers, definitions of orphanhood, survey method, funding, consent/guardianship, proposed versus completed placements and outcomes.","year":1971},{"id":"C2540","text":"NARA describes the Department of State’s 1963–1973 RG 59 central Subject-Numeric File as divided into a 1967–1969 segment that includes airgrams; its box lists identify exact boxes and are available in the Archives II Research Room.","period":"Archival access structure for 1967–1969 records","type":"Official archival access/arrangement description","source_ids":["S1402","S1403"],"confidence":"High for NARA’s description of the series and finding-aid access. This does not establish that the specific A-12 airgram survives or is in a particular box.","basis":"NARA’s guide says documents should be identified with date and telegram/airgram number; the scholarly citation gives A-12, 29 June 1969, but its longer locator notation should be checked against the physical finding aid.","next":"Use RG 59 Subject-Numeric Files, 1967–1969 box lists and request a search under POL 18 INDON / Surabaya / 29 June 1969 / Airgram A-12; record box and document number only after direct inspection.","year":1967},{"id":"C2541","text":"NARA’s indexed 1965–1973 Department of State classification-manual entry defines POL 18 as “Provincial, Municipal, and State Government.”","period":"1965–1973; Department of State filing taxonomy","type":"Official classification-manual entry, available in search-index excerpt","source_ids":["S1404"],"confidence":"Moderate: the exact subject label appears in the official NARA-indexed excerpt, but the full manual PDF could not be opened in this session. The classification is a filing category, not an event finding.","basis":"The citation “POL 18 INDON” in scholarship may indicate this file category; additional suffixes and exact box/series location remain unresolved.","next":"Read the complete manual and inspect the cited document’s header, routing stamps and folder label at NARA before normalizing the archival citation.","year":1965},{"id":"C2542","text":"Hearman’s 2015 article cites a 1970 Department of Social Affairs/field-survey report titled “Laporan Survey, Masalah Kurang Makan Salah Makan” for Bakung, Sumberdadi and surrounding South Blitar; the article cites its pp.18, 25 and 29.","period":"1970 survey; Bakung, Sumberdadi and surrounding South Blitar","type":"Bibliographic record and scholarly citation to an unaccessed primary report","source_ids":["S1324","S1405"],"confidence":"High that the article cites a report with this title, agency/publisher and page references; moderate for exact institutional wording because the report title page and full text are unavailable.","basis":"Hearman’s bibliography gives Yogyakarta and Balai Penelitian dan Peninjauan Sosial; a later bibliography has a variant “Dinas Research Disorganisasi Sosial.” Do not silently standardize the agency name.","next":"Search Indonesian government and university catalogs, Ministry of Social Affairs and local archives; request title-page image, full report, questionnaire, tables and appendices.","year":1970},{"id":"C2543","text":"Hearman reports that a 1970 government survey gave 1969 sale-crop figures for Bakung and Sumberdadi together: population 2,538; sweet potatoes 25 tonnes, maize 12 tonnes and rice 4 tonnes; she cautions these were marketed quantities and did not appear to include rice/cassava grown for household consumption.","period":"1969 crops; two villages, as reported in 1970 survey","type":"Quantitative data reported by a later scholarly article from a government survey","source_ids":["S1324","S1405"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports these figures and their market-output interpretation; moderate for the underlying counts until the survey tables and definitions are inspected. The two-village total cannot be generalized to all South Blitar.","basis":"Printed article pp.92–93, note 64 cites survey p.25. Distinguish marketed output from total production, household consumption and nutritional adequacy.","next":"Obtain survey p.25 and tables; inspect units, harvest period, population denominator, crops for sale versus subsistence, and possible household-level variation.","year":1969},{"id":"C2544","text":"Hearman quotes the 1970 survey as reporting that surveyed households generally could not afford tea, sugar and coffee and continued to drink unboiled water.","period":"1970; surveyed households in South Blitar","type":"Scholarly quotation/transmission of government survey findings","source_ids":["S1324","S1405"],"confidence":"High that Hearman quotes this statement; moderate for its exact representativeness because the original page, question wording, sample and translation are unavailable. It is not evidence that every household had the same diet or water practice.","basis":"Printed p.93, note 69 cites survey p.29. The article reproduces a short English translation from Indonesian.","next":"Read report p.29 and questionnaire; identify respondent count, question wording, drinking-water sources and whether the statement is a survey result or narrative generalization.","year":1970},{"id":"C2545","text":"ANRI’s KOTI inventory item 515 describes a two-leaf copy of RRI broadcast-news notes for 1–3 October 1965, including the Revolution Council list; the catalog also associates it with 28 September 1965.","period":"1965-09-28 catalog date; broadcasts 1965-10-01 to 1965-10-03","type":"Official archival finding-aid description / retrieval lead","source_ids":["S228"],"confidence":"High for the catalogue description and locator; unresolved for the date discrepancy and unseen contents.","basis":"Official inventory PDF, item 515, printed p.69/70. The described broadcast dates postdate the catalog-associated date. It is a copy, two leaves; no folder image or underlying RRI log was examined.","next":"Request KOTI item 515; check cover, foliation, copy provenance and attached broadcast notes; compare RRI program logs and contemporary newspaper reproductions.","year":1965},{"id":"C2546","text":"The Cornell reproduction of Revolution Council Decision No. 1 lists Tjugito as No. 14 under “Communist Group”; the separate scholarly affiliation table and Embassy A-398 roster identify him as a PKI figure and Central Committee member.","period":"1965-10-01; roster reflects the Embassy’s understanding after the May 1965 CC plenary","type":"Crosswalk of a reproduced contemporary list and a U.S. diplomatic roster","source_ids":["S253","S189","S1310"],"confidence":"High that the reproduced list and A-398 roster contain the name; moderate that the entries refer to the same person and reflect current formal affiliation.","basis":"The original movement list reproduced in S253 and Embassy roster in S189 are different documents; the Council-member affiliation table is later scholarly annotation. The PKI Oct. 5 statement said named members were not informed or asked. No evidence establishes Tjugito’s prior knowledge, consent, attendance or operational role.","next":"Locate the original Decision No. 1, PKI May-plenum roster/minutes, Tjugito’s own testimony and any Council records; compare name forms and dates.","year":1965},{"id":"C2547","text":"The CIA’s 1968 report says the PKI Secretariat was told on 26 September that a military action might occur before 5–6 October without a fixed date; Peris Pardede requested and received assignment to Medan, and the report says he was given timing information on 29 September and departed early on 1 October.","period":"1965-09-26 to 1965-10-01","type":"CIA retrospective account based on interrogation testimony","source_ids":["S251"],"confidence":"High that the CIA report makes this account; moderate-to-low as an independently verified reconstruction.","basis":"Printed pp.159–161. The CIA itself says its conspiracy reconstruction depends entirely on interrogations. The report includes Pardede’s reported recollection, but no Secretariat minutes, travel/mission order or independent regional receipt was opened.","next":"Obtain the original interrogation/transcript and Pardede trial file; seek PKI Secretariat notes, travel records, Medan CDB minutes and contemporary party communications.","year":1965},{"id":"C2548","text":"The CIA report says Sudisman briefed CC/PKI member Suwardiningsih about officers’ planned action and the projected Revolutionary Council, then sent her to Palembang; it reports her 1 October arrival, oral instructions to the CDB, mobilization discussions and four meetings on 1, 3, 5 and 6 October.","period":"1965-09-29 (CIA report’s broader briefing chronology) to 1965-10-06","type":"CIA retrospective account attributed to an Army-interrogated PKI member","source_ids":["S251"],"confidence":"High that the CIA report records this narrative; moderate-to-low for the uncorroborated details.","basis":"Printed pp.157–160. The report says the account came from Suwardiningsih’s interrogation; it does not provide the original interrogation, party minutes, travel record or Palembang CDB statement. Its narrative must not be treated as independent confirmation of the CIA’s wider interpretation.","next":"Locate Suwardiningsih’s trial and interrogation file, travel record, Palembang CDB minutes/support statement, contemporaneous local press and recipients’ testimony.","year":1965},{"id":"C2549","text":"The CIA report infers that the Revolution Council was part of a prior plan rather than an improvised response, using the decree’s reported preparation/signature and broadcast sequence.","period":"1965-10-01","type":"CIA analytical inference from retrospective testimony and event chronology","source_ids":["S251","S253"],"confidence":"High that the report argues this; moderate as an interpretation of the available chronology.","basis":"CIA printed pp.25–27; Cornell’s reproduced decision and radio chronology are a separate source for public documents. The CIA conclusion does not identify who saw, approved or understood the decree before broadcast, and does not prove Politburo-wide authorization.","next":"Read the original signed decree, RRI transmission logs, signatories’ records, CDB messages and contemporaneous party minutes; align time stamps and document provenance.","year":1965},{"id":"C2550","text":"The CIA report hypothesizes that Aidit probably drafted the PKI Political Bureau statement issued on 5 October because it resembled reported local instructions and a reported letter to East Java; it does not present a recovered draft.","period":"1965-10-02 to 1965-10-05","type":"CIA authorship hypothesis about a public party statement","source_ids":["S251","S115"],"confidence":"High that CIA makes the inference; low-to-moderate for actual authorship.","basis":"CIA printed pp.79–80 says the evidence “suggests” Aidit drafted it. Cornell S115 reproduces the statement from newspaper texts and dates it 5 October; neither source supplies the draft, author’s copy, approval record or publication instruction.","next":"Find the Indonesian/Javanese newspaper originals, original party statement, drafts, dispatch/circulation instructions, and Aidit/Politburo correspondence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2551","text":"ANRI’s 2024 Guide to Sukarno-era defense/security archives lists KOTI item 365 (Army newspaper clippings on G30S/Council, 1–11 October), item 366 (October 1965 chronology report) and item 369 (newspaper copies of the 1 October announcement received by the economic ministry).","period":"1965-10-01 to 1965-10-11","type":"Official archival guide / multiple retrieval leads","source_ids":["S1352"],"confidence":"High for the guide entries and locators; no conclusion yet about the folders’ contents.","basis":"Printed p.116, Nos.365–366 and 369. The descriptions identify copies/printouts and one-sample folders; the underlying records were not opened. These leads are distinct from KOTI inventory item 515 (S228).","next":"Request all three folders and record original publication dates, source newspaper/RRI, copy marks, attachments and filing context; compare with item 515.","year":1965},{"id":"C2552","text":"The public Political Bureau statement reproduced by Cornell is dated 5 October and its Indonesian/Javanese text is said to have appeared in Ariwarti Waspada on 8 and 9 October; Sudisman’s 1967 testimony refers to a Political Bureau statement dated 6 October; a later Unhas thesis cites another statement dated 10 October.","period":"1965-10-05 to 1965-10-10; 1967 retrospective testimony","type":"Date and document-identity discrepancy across contemporary text and retrospective/secondary references","source_ids":["S115","S251","S260","S679"],"confidence":"High that the sources report these dates; unresolved whether the 6 October reference is a date error or another text, and the 10 October text is separately reported but not authenticated.","basis":"S115 provides the Oct. 5 document/date and newspaper publication dates; CIA S251 calls it issued Oct. 5; S260 says “dated October 6”; S679 reports a separate Oct. 10 statement. No Oct. 6 original or authenticated Oct. 10 original was obtained.","next":"Compare Ariwarti Waspada Oct. 6, 8–9 and Oct. 10 issues, identify separate Politburo releases, retrieve the IISH source cited in S679 and inspect Sudisman’s Indonesian trial transcript.","year":1965},{"id":"C2553","text":"A 2024 Unhas thesis reproduces text attributed to a separate Political Bureau CC PKI statement dated 10 October 1965; it refers back to the 5 October statement and Sukarno’s 6 October cabinet message and urges calm, unity and resistance to provocation.","period":"1965-10-10; local Makassar response later in October","type":"Secondary transcription of a claimed contemporary party statement","source_ids":["S679","S1406"],"confidence":"Moderate that the thesis transcribes a distinct text and cites an IISH source; low-to-moderate for exact wording/date until the original is read.","basis":"Printed p.42 and footnote 33 of the thesis cite IISG via an obsolete prog-ind.net URL. The old page was inaccessible; IISH’s guide identifies a 261-microfiche PKI documents/serials collection but no item-level record for this statement.","next":"Search the current IISH catalogue under PKI documents/serials, request microfiche/scans for Oct. 10 statement, identify its publication and compare with the thesis transcription.","year":1965},{"id":"C2554","text":"In his July 1967 defense, Sudisman says he personally agreed with the officers’ action and was individually involved; he says he expected their Revolutionary Council to work with Sukarno to defend the left government’s anti-imperialist and limited anti-landlord policies.","period":"1965 events as recalled at the 1967 trial","type":"Detained participant’s retrospective self-attribution and political rationale","source_ids":["S260"],"confidence":"High that the published defense records this position; moderate-to-low as a complete contemporaneous account.","basis":"The testimony places Sudisman’s personal stance alongside claims that the Central Committee as a whole had not adopted a formal decision. He was a detained defendant, prepared the speech from memory, and lacked the original meeting record. Individual assent must not be converted into proof of a unanimous Politburo or whole-party resolution.","next":"Compare original Indonesian transcript/audio, other defendants’ testimony, Politburo/Secretariat minutes, Aidit’s Oct. 6 letter, and local CDB records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2555","text":"Sudisman’s 1967 analysis attributes failure partly to subjectivism, failure to calculate the balance of forces and choose timing/leadership effectively, and isolation from any mass upsurge; he also criticizes political and organizational errors including weak internal criticism, liberalism and commandism.","period":"PKI strategy through the 1965 defeat; written in 1967","type":"Participant’s retrospective internal self-criticism","source_ids":["S260","S1331","S261"],"confidence":"High that the 1967 defense and 1966 self-criticism contain these diagnoses; moderate as an exhaustive causal account.","basis":"Sudisman’s trial speech and the September 1966 Politburo self-criticism are situated post-defeat documents produced under repression and ideological realignment. They identify internal explanations but cannot alone establish the relative weight of military capacity, repression, foreign assistance, class conflict or local conditions.","next":"Compare the 1966 Indonesian text, leadership drafts/dissent, county-level party records, military capacity and repression orders, mass-organization records, and Southeast Asian party comparisons.","year":1965},{"id":"C2556","text":"Sudisman says he prepared his analysis after more than seven months in detention, 14 interrogations over 18 days totaling at least 70 hours, more than 40 preliminary interrogations, and without books or a library, relying on memory.","period":"1966-12 to 1967-07","type":"Testimony about detention and statement-production conditions","source_ids":["S260"],"confidence":"High that Sudisman’s published text states these conditions; the underlying interrogation logs and cell records remain unverified.","basis":"Opening of the 21 July 1967 defense speech. His statement is direct testimony about his experience but has not been checked against detention/interrogation records; this caveat limits how the speech is used as an account of earlier decisions.","next":"Obtain Mahmilub transcript, interrogation schedules and records, prison medical/visitation log, counsel files and the 1975 Indonesian/English publication history.","year":1966},{"id":"C2557","text":"Hilmar Farid’s 2008 discussion of John Roosa argues that G30S leaders lacked a common view and a clear contingency plan after failure, distinguishes the movement from the subsequent mass killings, and identifies the order to kill the generals as unresolved.","period":"Historiography of 1965; essay published 2008","type":"Historiographical interpretation / secondary argument","source_ids":["S1407","S1376","S253"],"confidence":"High that Farid advances this argument; it is not independent corroboration of the underlying events.","basis":"The essay is a book discussion based on Roosa and participant materials; it makes a useful research distinction but does not replace original records or settle who knew, ordered or carried out each act.","next":"Read the specific documents Roosa and Farid rely on, then compare Supardjo, Subekti, Sudisman, trial files, radio/Army records and death-scene evidence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2558","text":"In André Liem’s published interview chapter, Rewang says the PKI as an institution did not decide to launch G30S; he recalls roughly ten Politburo members as confused afterward, says the party hoped Sukarno would restrain the Army, and places any Aidit involvement outside a formal Politburo decision.","period":"1965-09 to 1965-10; recollection published 2004","type":"Retrospective participant testimony reproduced in oral-history scholarship","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that the chapter records these statements; low-to-moderate as a complete account of who knew or authorized what.","basis":"Rewang says he joined the Politburo in mid-1965. This is his retrospective account in a 2004 chapter based on interviews, not Politburo minutes. It does not negate Sudisman’s separate claim of personal agreement/participation or CIA’s interrogation-derived account of limited briefings.","next":"Compare with Sudisman’s full Indonesian defense, CIA interrogation files, dated Politburo/Secretariat records, provincial CDB statements and RRI logs; distinguish institution-wide authorization from individual knowledge.","year":1965},{"id":"C2559","text":"After Sudisman’s arrest in December 1966, Rewang says he proposed that surviving leaders leave Jakarta, organize resistance from rural areas, and move to a base; he recalls that the proposal was accepted and that East Java CDB contacts had prepared villages.","period":"1966-12 onward; exact relocation dates disputed","type":"Retrospective participant testimony in oral-history scholarship","source_ids":["S1317","S1384","S1385","S1388"],"confidence":"High that Rewang’s proposal and acceptance are recorded in the interview; moderate for the decision sequence; exact date and implementing roster remain unverified.","basis":"Liem records the interview rather than contemporaneous orders. Later reports give different dates for arrival, base preparation and Kompro formation; do not collapse those events into one move.","next":"Obtain Rewang memoir pages, travel/identity records, CDB correspondence, village contact records and captured committee papers.","year":1966},{"id":"C2560","text":"The South Blitar organization is described as a layered emergency structure: an Emergency CC above provincial leadership and base-project committees (Kompro); Rewang recalls about nine CC members and says formation of guerrilla detachments had begun, while a participant describes the network as compartmentalized.","period":"1966–1968; South Blitar and other planned bases","type":"Retrospective interviews and later synthesis","source_ids":["S1317","S1384","S1385","S1387","S1388"],"confidence":"High that interviewees describe these layers; moderate for the precise roster, scope and operational effectiveness.","basis":"The article says names and reporting lines were deliberately restricted and that interviewees did not know the full Kompro roster. “Detachments begun” does not mean a completed or combat-capable army.","next":"Find Emergency CC and CDB minutes, appointment circulars, Kompro rosters, captured papers, orders, communications and unit/personnel records.","year":1966},{"id":"C2561","text":"Rewang says the leadership had not fixed a start date for organized people’s war and was waiting for a national conference to assess political conditions and organizational readiness; the planned conference had not yet occurred.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Retrospective participant testimony","source_ids":["S1317","S1384","S1385"],"confidence":"High that Rewang makes this claim; moderate about how consistently it governed dispersed networks.","basis":"No national conference resolution, attendance list or authenticated directive has been obtained. The memoir-derived May 23, 1967 Emergency CC date is not confirmed by Liem’s interview chapter.","next":"Find the proposed conference notice, CDB delegate lists, cancellation/postponement records, and all surviving instructions on when armed struggle could begin.","year":1967},{"id":"C2562","text":"Rewang describes local acts including disarming plantation guards and attacking people he called past “executioners” as acts outside the Kompro/CC program; he retrospectively calls some “armed propaganda,” while also acknowledging that they constituted armed action in a concrete sense.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Retrospective participant interpretation of local operations","source_ids":["S1317","S1384","S1385"],"confidence":"High that the chapter records this distinction; low-to-moderate as a complete inventory of actions and authorization across all cells.","basis":"Rewang distinguishes a formal, organized people’s war—which still awaited preparation and a national conference—from concrete local military acts already occurring. His “armed propaganda” phrase does not erase the actions or their consequences. No dated action order, complete target/perpetrator list or independent record identifies every incident; this is a participant’s retrospective assessment.","next":"Identify each reported disarmament, guard-post incident and attack from local police/Army logs, village testimony, detainee files and party correspondence; compare dates and chain of command.","year":1967},{"id":"C2563","text":"A Muyatno interview in Liem’s chapter identifies “Gatot Taryo” as the contact who introduced him to the South Blitar Kompro and describes him as its chair; Muyatno says he could identify few others because contacts were compartmentalized.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Retrospective participant testimony","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that the interview transcript reports this; moderate for the individual’s role; no independent appointment record found.","basis":"The name form “Gatot Taryo” should not be silently equated with similarly named figures in other sources. The witness explicitly says he did not know the full roster.","next":"Compare name variants against Rewang memoir, military capture/interrogation records, local civil identity files and Kompro documents.","year":1967},{"id":"C2564","text":"A later article citing Rewang’s memoir dates the Emergency CC’s formation to 1967-05-23; Liem’s earlier interview chapter describes its structure and decisions but does not supply that exact date.","period":"1967-05-23; date of Emergency CC formation claimed retrospectively","type":"Secondary report citing a participant memoir, cross-checked against an oral-history chapter","source_ids":["S1317","S1385","S1388"],"confidence":"High that the later article prints this date; low-to-moderate for the exact date until the cited memoir page or a contemporaneous record is inspected.","basis":"Several later reports may depend on the same Rewang memoir and do not constitute independent confirmation. Liem’s non-dating is not proof against May 23.","next":"Read Rewang memoir at the cited pages and look for meeting minutes, dated appointment notices, letters and participant calendars.","year":1967},{"id":"C2565","text":"Published accounts disagree on the date of the Communist Party of Thailand’s first armed clash: Cui Feng (citing a Thai military history) and a northeastern village study give 7 August 1965, while Ian Baird’s 2025 article gives 8 August. The exact date remains unresolved; neither date alone establishes the insurgency’s scale or readiness.","period":"1965-08-07; Thailand","type":"Secondary historical account citing a 1986 Thai military history","source_ids":["S1408","S1313","S1321","S1346","S1441"],"confidence":"High that the reviewed publications differ; moderate on the incident date pending direct comparison of the underlying Thai military records and local chronology.","basis":"Cui Feng’s 2022 article cites Saiyud 1986 p.180; the village case study identifies the date through local oral history/commemoration; Baird’s 2025 article cites other secondary histories. None of the cited 1965 incident reports was inspected.","next":"Retrieve Saiyud 1986 p.180, the village study’s underlying interviews, Thai security incident reports and CPT records; determine whether 7/8 August refers to different locations or event stages.","year":1965},{"id":"C2566","text":"A 1968 U.S. National Intelligence Estimate contrasted a few hundred PKI activists in Central/East Java trying to build a guerrilla force with a Malayan Communist Party force estimated below 1,000 at the Thai border, about 500 Sarawak guerrillas, roughly 150 armed Huks, and a roughly 4,000-member Burma Communist Party/White Flag insurgency; it described Laos as territorially distinct and dependent on North Vietnamese forces.","period":"1968; Southeast Asia","type":"Contemporary U.S. intelligence snapshot, not a comparable force census","source_ids":["S1312","S1313","S1314","S1315","S1320","S1321"],"confidence":"High that the estimate uses these categories and numbers; low-to-moderate for comparability and actual strength.","basis":"The estimates differ in date, definition and source quality; the NIE uses Cold War language and sometimes infers external direction without disclosed evidence. Huk estimates also differ across U.S. documents (about 140 in a 1967 note versus about 150 in the 1968 NIE).","next":"Build separate profiles using each party’s records, state incident files, territorial control, trained combatant vs sympathizer definitions and dated local rosters.","year":1968},{"id":"C2567","text":"Cui Feng’s article describes a distinct regional foreign-support channel: U.S./Thai assistance to KMT forces on the Burma–Thailand frontier in the early 1950s, followed by Thai state use of remaining KMT units in northern border operations against the CPT from late 1969.","period":"1950s–1969; Burma/Thailand","type":"Peer-reviewed secondary reconstruction of regional Cold War military channels","source_ids":["S1408"],"confidence":"High that the article describes the channel; moderate for the underlying transfers because the article relies on earlier scholarship and did not expose the original CIA/Thai logistics files.","basis":"This is outside Indonesia and must not be used as evidence for U.S. support to the Indonesian Army or for U.S. involvement in G30S. The late-1969 Thai arrangement followed a different state/foreign-force mechanism.","next":"Inspect cited CIA, Thai police and ROC records; compare arms approval, transfer, receipt, deployment and payment stages.","year":1950},{"id":"C2568","text":"The available evidence differentiates three Indonesian forms of militarization: a public Fifth Force proposal and limited, partly unverified civilian training; the Indonesian Army’s established territorial command and local auxiliary structures; and the post-1966 PKI’s small, compartmentalized underground cells and planned guerrilla detachments. They are not equivalent forces.","period":"1964–1968; nationwide and South Blitar","type":"Bounded cross-source organizational comparison","source_ids":["S1329","S133","S295","S139","S1310","S1317","S1312"],"confidence":"Moderate-high as a distinction across the sources; exact coverage and strength of each form remain incomplete.","basis":"A proposal is not a formed force; a training file title is not attendance or arms receipt; an Army territorial command is not proof of uniform local execution; a planned underground detachment is not proof of an operational army.","next":"Compare provincial military rosters/orders and civilian training attendance with PKI unit lists, budgets, weapons evidence, commands and action reports.","year":1964},{"id":"C2569","text":"Leksana reports that a 13 October 1965 order established Pupelrada at Korem 083 in Malang under Colonel Sumadi, extending Pepelrada coordination to the Korem/regency level; the article says the framework carried powers to restrict movement and detain people for up to 30 days.","period":"1965-10-13 onward; East Java","type":"Scholarly transcription and interpretation of contemporaneous regional command records","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"High that the article identifies the order and describes these powers; moderate for the exact legal scope and local use until the original decree is reviewed.","basis":"Leksana cites Pepelrada decree Kep-15/10/65 and an October 16 radiogram. No signed original, complete legal text, appeal record, or local order was opened. The legal powers cannot be assumed to have been exercised identically across districts.","next":"Obtain Kep-15/10/65, district Pupelrada installation orders, detention registers, travel restrictions, review/appeal files, and local reports.","year":1965},{"id":"C2570","text":"Leksana reports a 6 November 1965 radiogram ordering Puterpra units to be armed, including assignment of a Technical Assistance Unit to weaker units.","period":"1965-11-06; East Java","type":"Scholarly quotation of a regional military radiogram","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"High that the article quotes this instruction; low-to-moderate for arms actually issued, unit strength, and combat use.","basis":"The original is cited as Radiogram T. 658/1965 to Kodim 0818–0825 and 0831. An arming order establishes planned authorization, not serial-numbered weapons delivery or use.","next":"Inspect T. 658/1965, arms receipts and returns, Puterpra/Technical Assistance Unit rosters, training logs, and operations reports.","year":1965},{"id":"C2571","text":"Leksana reports that a 27 November 1965 radiogram ordered mass movements to cease, routed the ambiguously labeled civilian group AA through Hansip, provided uniforms, and placed the movements under Army direction.","period":"1965-11-27; East Java","type":"Scholarly quotation of a regional military radiogram","source_ids":["S295","S1410"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this order; moderate for date and serial until the underlying record is checked.","basis":"The 2020 article and Leksana’s 2023 monograph repeat T.702/1965 for both the 23 and 27 November instructions. This confirms the persistent printed citation, not the original serial/date; the two publications draw on the same inventory 316-a and are not independent confirmation.","next":"Read both original radiograms and the archive checklist; verify serial, dispatch date, recipients, definition of AA, uniforms issued, and local implementation.","year":1965},{"id":"C2572","text":"Hammer reports that a 26 October 1965 meeting in Solo chaired by Sarwo Edhie decided to involve pre-existing anti-communist village guards and augment them with newly recruited formations; RPKAD provided brief training to the new auxiliaries.","period":"1965-10-26 onward; Central Java","type":"Regional scholarly reconstruction citing military history and field interviews","source_ids":["S139"],"confidence":"High that Hammer reports the decision and training; moderate for exact implementation by district.","basis":"The 26 October meeting is cited through the 1971 Diponegoro military history and Jenkins/Kammen; training duration, personnel, attendance, weapons and individual unit conduct are not established by the order alone. This is Central Java evidence, not a national template.","next":"Obtain the 26 October meeting minutes, village-guard rosters, new auxiliary recruitment lists, RPKAD training reports, weapons receipts, and district deployment logs.","year":1965},{"id":"C2573","text":"Leksana’s East Java evidence shows a shift in the stated task from early public-order management to organized anti-left coordination: an 11 October instruction tasked Zeni Battalion 5 with assisting Puterpra to control demonstrations and prevent property destruction, while the later 21 October Pancasila Operation directed operations against communists and allowed pro-Army civilians to assist.","period":"1965-10-11 to 1965-10-21; East Java","type":"Scholarly reconstruction from quoted military records","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"High for the different stated purposes in the cited instructions; moderate for how consistently the shift was implemented.","basis":"The 11 October order is Radiogram T. 582/1965; the 21 October order is Perintah Operasi No. 5 Pantja Sila. Early local violence and anti-left civilian organization were already present; changing orders do not mean every earlier incident was non-political or that every later locality followed the same sequence.","next":"Read both signed orders, event logs before and after 21 October, recipient-unit records, and district reports to map timing and divergence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2574","text":"Leksana’s account attributes East Java’s delayed provincial command action partly to political realignment and troop shortages: it reports that eight of sixteen provincial battalions were deployed elsewhere, cites a report that roughly 30 percent had been implicated in G30S, and relays a U.S.-intelligence-based claim that Basuki Rachmat said he had sufficient forces to continue the purge only by 19 November.","period":"October–November 1965; East Java","type":"Scholar’s synthesis of retrospective military studies, an intelligence-officer report, and a dissertation’s U.S. cable citation","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"High that Leksana presents these constraints and cites this source chain; moderate-to-low for exact manpower and for the causal weight assigned to it.","basis":"The battalion figures are transmitted through Anderson/Robinson and the 19 November claim through Setiyawan’s use of U.S. intelligence, not through original rosters or the cable inspected here. Political hesitation, troop availability and alliance formation may interact; the evidence does not reduce the delay to one cause.","next":"Obtain 1965 Brawijaya battalion rosters and movement orders, the 29 November East Java report, the cited U.S. cable, and Basuki Rachmat/Colonel Widjaja Sukardanu correspondence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2575","text":"The Amnesty International Indonesia 1965 document archive has an ethnicity-of-victim taxonomy, but states that its document collection concerning the 1965 tragedy covers 1967–2016; the Chinese, Dayak, Javanese and other tags are document-retrieval categories, not a 1965–66 victim-level death register.","period":"Archive metadata checked 2026-11-17; documents span 1967–2016","type":"Directly inspected archive metadata and category pages","source_ids":["S1350"],"confidence":"High for the archive’s stated scope and indexing design.","basis":"The site says it contains more than 200 public PDFs and avoids an individual-name search. A document carrying an ethnicity tag may discuss later detention, court cases, discrimination or historical context; tag presence does not enumerate deaths or establish that a particular tagged person was killed.","next":"Use the archive to locate Amnesty reports and then extract dated individual cases only where the PDF supports them; do not aggregate category results as victim counts or ethnic shares.","year":2026},{"id":"C2576","text":"Leksana reports that Brawijaya Commander Basuki Rachmat and Pepelrada chief of staff Widjaja Sukardanu were considered hesitant to launch large-scale operations, amid a divided East Java political environment and troop shortage; she notes that anti-communism and personal Sukarno loyalty were not mutually exclusive among Brawijaya officers.","period":"October–November 1965; East Java","type":"Secondary military-political interpretation based on earlier scholarship and regional reports","source_ids":["S295"],"confidence":"High that the article advances this interpretation; moderate for individual motives and causal explanation.","basis":"The underlying November 1965 intelligence-officer report and cited command rosters were not independently read. “Sukarnoist” describes reported political loyalty, not proof of support for the PKI or opposition to anti-communist action.","next":"Read the full 29 November East Java report, officers’ service and posting records, command minutes, troop movement logs and correspondence with Jakarta.","year":1965},{"id":"C2577","text":"Leksana reproduces a Korem 083/Kopur Siaga III detention log with reported totals of 2,472 detainees on 4 November 1965, 6,259 by noon on 12 December, 5,798 by midnight that day, and 4,431 on 29 December; the table separates military personnel, civilians in the armed forces, and public civilians on some dates.","period":"1965-11-04 to 1965-12-29; Korem 083 Malang-Besuki","type":"Scholarly transcription of a contemporaneous military detention log","source_ids":["S1410","S295"],"confidence":"High that Leksana’s publications reproduce these totals; moderate-to-low for the underlying register counts and their completeness until the original log is checked.","basis":"The totals are detainees present at listed reporting times, not cumulative arrests, deaths, or a victim count. The original counting method and transfers, releases, deaths, duplicate registrations and subdistrict coverage are unspecified. S295 and S1410 are the same researcher and archive chain.","next":"Inspect the original Laporan G30S/PKI di Daerah Kopur Siaga III/83 Malang-Besuki pp.8–18, daily intake/outflow registers, Kodim returns, prisoner transfers and death records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2578","text":"The published Korem 083 detention table has major internal anomalies: it prints 1,509 detainees on 23 November between 4,020 on 21 November and 5,006 on 27 November, and leaves total fields blank for 4 and 23 December even though the component columns sum to 5,542 and 5,617 respectively.","period":"1965-11-21 to 1965-12-23; table published 2020/2023","type":"Direct audit of a scholarly transcription of a military table","source_ids":["S1410","S295"],"confidence":"High for the table as published; unknown whether the anomaly belongs to the military original, the archive copy, transcription, or table layout.","basis":"The 2023 book repeats the same table drawn from inventory 316-a. A sharp entry change cannot be interpreted as mass release, killing, transfer, or reclassification without the original daily report and count definitions.","next":"Compare the scan page-by-page with the original archive table; inspect 21–28 November entries, totals and date headers; reconcile unit returns and transfers.","year":1965},{"id":"C2579","text":"Leksana reports that a 30 November 1965 radiogram required PKI-linked political or mass organizations’ claimed self-dissolution to be accepted by the Dandim as head of Pupelrada and witnessed by Panca Tunggal and National Front organizations.","period":"1965-11-30; East Java","type":"Scholarly quotation of a regional military radiogram","source_ids":["S295","S1410"],"confidence":"High that both versions of Leksana’s study report the clause; moderate for exact implementation and political pressure in each organization.","basis":"This procedure places acceptance under a district military commander, complicating any unqualified description of organizational dissolution as voluntary. Both publications rely on the same Brawijaya archive record; the original T.715/1965 was not examined.","next":"Read T.715/1965, dissolution letters and meeting minutes; identify the Dandim, witnesses, organizations, dates, and whether leaders were coerced or detained.","year":1965},{"id":"C2580","text":"A Korem 083 report cited by Leksana records four killing cases on 16 November 1965, including one event in which four bodies were found in a rice field; the report’s late-October-to-December language often names the dead but leaves the killers unidentified.","period":"1965-11-16; Malang-Besuki / East Java","type":"Contemporaneous military reporting as transcribed by a historian","source_ids":["S1410","S295"],"confidence":"High that the historian reports these entries; moderate for the underlying incident details pending the original report and individual identifications.","basis":"“Unidentified killer” is the report’s wording, not evidence that investigators did not know or that the killers were civilians. No victim names, autopsies, burial records or incident investigations were reviewed.","next":"Inspect inventory 316-a daily pages, police/civil death reports, cemetery and family records, and compare the 16 November entries against detention/transfer records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2581","text":"Leksana reports that a 4 December 1965 Korem 083 report told the Brawijaya commander that PKI in the Korem 083 area had been “terminated”; this is a military status assertion, not a count of people killed.","period":"1965-12-04; Korem 083","type":"Contemporaneous military report quoted in a later study","source_ids":["S1410","S295"],"confidence":"High that the report contains this characterization as quoted; low as a literal description of every person or cell in the area.","basis":"The same historical record includes thousands detained and local operations continuing after the command claim. “Terminated” may refer to organization, operational resistance, or an official narrative; it cannot be translated automatically into deaths.","next":"Read the report’s original Indonesian sentence and context, distribution chain, supporting unit returns, detention rolls, and January 1966 operation reports.","year":1965},{"id":"C2582","text":"A 1966 South Malang military report, as cited by Leksana, identifies a local Pancasila Operation under P.O.004/1965 dated 19 November 1965, begun on 29 November and continuing to 18 January 1966: troops linked with Tjatur Tunggal and village heads in Donomulyo; Army raider units, Puterpra, Marines and Brimob operated in successive areas, with Brimob also guarding detainees.","period":"1965-11-19 to 1966-01-18; South Malang and Wates, Blitar","type":"Later scholar’s reconstruction from an identified contemporaneous military operation report and a 2019 interview","source_ids":["S1410"],"confidence":"High that the cited report and interview are described this way; moderate for the sequence and unit assignments pending the original report and daily logs.","basis":"The South Malang operation began more than five weeks after the province-wide Pancasila Operation order of 21 October. The report was produced by the operating command; its labels, chronology and self-presentation need comparison with village testimony, arrest registers and incident records.","next":"Obtain the 1966 Laporan Singkat Selama Operasi Pantjasila Malang Selatan, P.O.004/1965, daily orders, task force rosters, Donomulyo Koramil records, detention-site logs and local death/family records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2583","text":"The South Malang military report claimed that 90 percent of Donomulyo residents were PKI and that twelve firearms were found in western Donomulyo; it also described 24 remaining PKI/PR/BTI/Lekra activists for screening and used the term diselesaikan, translated in Leksana’s text as “terminated.”","period":"1965–1966; Donomulyo, South Malang","type":"Military operational report quoted by a historian","source_ids":["S1410"],"confidence":"High that the official report is quoted this way; low for the alleged 90 percent, the weapon-discovery context and what “diselesaikan” meant in each case.","basis":"The 90-percent figure is a military allegation, not a population survey or measured party-membership rate. The report presents itself as restoring order and is addressed to superiors; “secured,” “screened,” “disbanded” and “terminated” require original-language/context review and survivor comparison.","next":"Inspect original P.O.004 operation report and screening lists; identify the 24 persons, legal/physical outcomes, firearm provenance, village denominator and whether “diselesaikan” meant processing, expulsion, detention or killing.","year":1965},{"id":"C2584","text":"Jono, a local guard and former Catholic Youth/PMKRI activist interviewed in Donomulyo in 2016, told Leksana that detainees were taken from police custody to a public cemetery, tied and shot; he said the Army carried out killings and Ansor members sometimes assisted or killed.","period":"1965 operations remembered in a 2016 interview; Donomulyo, South Malang","type":"Retrospective eyewitness testimony reproduced in scholarly research","source_ids":["S1410","S295"],"confidence":"High that the published chapter records this testimony; moderate for the described incidents pending other eyewitness, burial and family corroboration.","basis":"This is one local witness’s account, not a complete incident list or region-wide description. The interview recording/transcript and precise victim identities were not accessed. In the same passage, the military report frames events as “security” and the historian notes that official narrative underreports killings.","next":"Seek the full interview and consent/anonymity conditions, identify cemetery graves and named victims, locate other guards, families, detainees and Ansor/Army participants, and compare operation reports.","year":1965},{"id":"C2585","text":"Leksana’s monograph describes a July–September 1967 Brawijaya “New Orderisation” apparatus: Java Army commanders, Kostrad and RPKAD met in July; a provincial guidance team was authorized in September with military, action-front, mass/political-organization, Golkar, civil-service and press members; Korem/Kodim teams followed the territorial command chain, replaced village heads with Army caretakers in former PKI areas and postponed village elections.","period":"1967–January 1968; East Java","type":"Scholar’s reconstruction from Brawijaya records and earlier military-political studies","source_ids":["S1410"],"confidence":"High that the chapter reports the cited meeting, order and measures; moderate for province-wide execution and duration.","basis":"Underlying Siaran Kilat No.1/1967, Kep-001/ORBA/9/1967 and team procedure annex in inventory 316-b were not inspected. The source’s claim about village-head replacement/election postponement needs district appointment lists and local election files.","next":"Obtain inventory 316-b order originals, July meeting participants/records, September guidance-team structure and work plans, caretaker appointment lists and postponed election registers.","year":1967},{"id":"C2586","text":"Leksana reports that Radiogram T.587/1965, dated 14 October 1965, directed Kodim to work with local police and Panca Tunggal in investigating Gerwani and Pemuda Rakyat members about Jakarta volunteer training and their knowledge or implementation of G30S in their regions.","period":"1965-10-14; East Java","type":"Scholar’s transcription of a contemporaneous military order","source_ids":["S295","S1410"],"confidence":"High that the publications report this instruction; moderate pending the original radiogram and its implementation records.","basis":"Leksana says the investigation concerned organizational knowledge/training, but whether this order directly affected killings is unclear. The 2020 paper and 2023 chapter share the same author and Brawijaya archive chain.","next":"Obtain T.587/1965 and attached Kodim replies; identify interrogated people, procedures, detention outcomes and links, if any, to later arrests or killings.","year":1965},{"id":"C2587","text":"Hearman’s reconstruction distinguishes post-1965 leadership positions: the Bandung group around Ismail Bakri, supported by Rewang, criticized the earlier legal-parliamentary strategy and reliance on Sukarno; the Jakarta group around Sudisman remained closer to Aidit’s line, although Sudisman later supported the September 1966 Politburo self-criticism.","period":"1965-09 to 1966-12; PKI survivor groupings","type":"Scholarly reconstruction citing participant testimony and Imam Soedjono’s later memoir","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"Moderate that Hearman reports this distinction; low-to-moderate as a complete map of all factions or their changing membership.","basis":"The factional interpretation is partly sourced through Soedjono’s 2006 memoir as cited in Hearman’s footnote; it is not based on contemporaneous group minutes. Do not treat either cluster as a fully coherent command chain.","next":"Read Soedjono’s cited pages and Sudisman’s 1966 self-criticism in Indonesian; seek Ismail Bakri/Rewang correspondence, the Jakarta group’s communications and regional committee records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2588","text":"Melvin’s summary of two 14 October 1965 South Aceh military-chronology entries reports four Chinese victims among five deaths at Blang Pidie (80%) and four Chinese victims among ten deaths at Sama Dua (40%). The article says the four named Chinese victims in the Sama Dua entry were all from Blang Pidie.","period":"1965-10-14; Blang Pidie and Sama Dua, South Aceh","type":"Event-level ratios calculated from a historian’s transcription of a military chronology","source_ids":["S612","S855"],"confidence":"High for the printed counts and arithmetic; low for unique-person accounting and underlying event completeness until the original chronology is checked.","basis":"The article calls these separate events but assigns the four named Sama Dua victims to Blang Pidie; residence/origin, event location and possible duplicate listing are not resolved here. Do not add the two rows into a 15-person victim total or call these ratios a district, provincial or national ethnic share.","next":"Inspect Chronologis 1966 p.8 image and full Indonesian wording; determine whether “from Blang Pidie” means residence, origin or attack location; match names, deaths, belongings, burial and family records across both entries.","year":1965},{"id":"C2589","text":"Woolgar’s regional comparison reports that West Java saw Chinese association property attacks and Baperki arrests, but Chinese deaths were rare among the several thousand killings there; it contrasts this with harsher anti-communist/anti-Chinese conditions under northern Sumatra and Aceh commanders, including massacres, detention and forced expulsion. It also describes a broader state crackdown on Chinese organizations and property after March–May 1966.","period":"1965-10 to 1967; West Java, North Sumatra and Aceh","type":"Regional comparative historical synthesis","source_ids":["S231","S1322","S612"],"confidence":"High that Woolgar reports these distinctions; moderate for province-wide comparison because casualty counts and enforcement records are incomplete and regionally non-equivalent.","basis":"This does not provide comparable regional ethnic denominators or establish a national ethnic-victim percentage. Property destruction, detention, murder, organizational closure and expulsion must remain separate outcome fields. Woolgar’s national “not overrepresented” inference depends on approximate 2,000 and 500,000 estimates.","next":"Trace the article’s local press, command orders and citations; seek district-level Chinese/Baperki victim, detention, property and expulsion lists for West Java, Medan and Aceh with a common time window.","year":1965},{"id":"C2590","text":"Hearman reports that when Brawijaya units said local Hansip were passing information about military movements, the Army replaced those guards with Banser members and planned a larger Trisula operation.","period":"1968; South Blitar","type":"Retrospective oral-history and military-publication reconstruction","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports this sequence; moderate-to-low for the asserted information leakage and its causal weight pending the underlying operation file.","basis":"The claim of leakage appears in a military account, while the described replacement is reconstructed by Hearman from military and participant sources. The source does not establish that all Hansip cooperated with PKI or that every Banser member was under identical command.","next":"Inspect Operasi Trisula pp.167 and related orders, guard rosters, intelligence reports and interviews with displaced Hansip, Banser members and village residents.","year":1968},{"id":"C2591","text":"Hearman describes Trisula’s village-control system as replacing local administrators with caretakers (often military), stationing a company in each battalion-assigned village, checking identity and registering residents and cattle, placing checkpoints roughly 300–400 metres apart, and requiring military permission for public gatherings.","period":"1968; South Blitar","type":"Historical reconstruction from the Army’s Trisula history and local oral histories","source_ids":["S1310","S1324"],"confidence":"High that the published study reports these methods; moderate for their exact coverage and village-by-village execution.","basis":"These are counterinsurgency and territorial-control measures, not a casualty count. The underlying military history and complete village registers were not independently inspected; oral accounts and military self-report have different vantage points.","next":"Obtain Operasi Trisula pp.67, 85 and relevant appendices; compare village caretaker appointments, resident/cattle registers, checkpoint maps, movement permits and family testimony.","year":1968},{"id":"C2592","text":"CiNii identifies Pauker’s 1969 RM-5753-PR as prepared for U.S. Air Force Project RAND under contract F44620-67-C-0045. The report is now directly read: pp.61–62 present Pauker’s account of disagreement over rebuilding a mass underground before armed struggle versus isolated actions; p.62 also gives his casualty/fate claims. Hearman separately reconstructs the domestic debate through interviews and military histories.","period":"1969 report analyzing post-1965 PKI; South Blitar debate 1967–1968","type":"Official library catalog metadata plus a later scholar’s quotation of a contemporaneous U.S.-sponsored analysis","source_ids":["S1411","S1358","S1310"],"confidence":"High for the report’s text, title, sponsorship and contract; low-to-moderate for Pauker’s accuracy about covert PKI deliberations because he provides no source at the point of the strategic dispute and his Rewang death claim is demonstrably wrong.","basis":"The official RAND scan (S1358) was opened and printed pp.61–62 visually checked on 2026-11-19. Hearman’s Rewang interview and TAPOL/Amnesty prison reporting independently establish survival. RAND sponsorship identifies an analytical channel, not direct evidence that the U.S. operated or collected through a particular covert mechanism.","next":"Compare the report’s sources/distribution file with emergency-CC minutes, Rewang and Munir accounts, army-history pages, court records and local incident evidence; recover original NYT sources behind its death estimate.","year":1969},{"id":"C2593","text":"Lyon reports that Army Air Command pilot Sudjai recalled being entrusted from 1 October 1965 with personally delivering Suharto’s confidential orders and information to commanders of the three Java military divisions, requiring repeated flights between Jakarta and division headquarters.","period":"1965-10-01 onward","type":"Retrospective participant memoir mediated by a later specialist article","source_ids":["S1412"],"confidence":"High that Lyon reports this and quotes the memoir; moderate for the deliveries and contents because the five memoir installments and flight records were not retrieved.","basis":"Lyon, “Testimony of a messenger,” “A pilot’s testimony” and “Plans for the destruction of the PKI”; source identified as Sudjai’s 2009 five-part memoir.","next":"Retrieve all five 2009 installments and compare Army Air Command logs, dispatches, receipts and regional command diaries.","year":1965},{"id":"C2594","text":"In Sudjai’s account as summarized by Lyon, Java division commanders were initially uncertain and did not know one another’s responses; their dilemma joined hostility to the PKI with loyalty to Sukarno. Sudjai says all three accepted Suharto’s confidential demands by the third week of October.","period":"1965-10, first three weeks","type":"Retrospective participant account and later secondary interpretation","source_ids":["S1412"],"confidence":"High that the article attributes this to Sudjai; moderate-to-low for exact sequence, private motives and timing pending original memoir and command records.","basis":"Lyon, “Commanders’ uncertainty”; includes Sudjai’s recollection of Adji’s repeated fear of arrest, dismissal or demotion. Not a contemporary meeting minute.","next":"Identify all three commanders and obtain diaries, orders, meeting calendars, personnel records and dated operational directives.","year":1965},{"id":"C2595","text":"Lyon says Sudjai recounted a rumor that Suharto permitted West Java commander Adji to maintain good relations with Sukarno as part of an understanding preceding Adji’s agreement to the anti-PKI campaign in West Java.","period":"1965-10; agreement date unspecified","type":"Participant memoir reporting a rumor, mediated by a later article","source_ids":["S1412"],"confidence":"High that Lyon reports the rumor; low for an actual bargain because the memoir itself presents it as rumor and no agreement record is cited.","basis":"Lyon, “Commanders’ uncertainty.” Do not describe this as a documented agreement or direct proof of Sukarno’s role.","next":"Find Adji/Suharto correspondence, Siliwangi orders, meeting records and independent participant testimony.","year":1965},{"id":"C2596","text":"Lyon interprets the confidential instructions carried by Sudjai as containing a plan to destroy the PKI down to local levels and foreshadowing an effort to implicate Sukarno; the public line of support for Sukarno and private instructions reportedly differed.","period":"1965-10, early New Order consolidation","type":"Later author’s interpretation of a participant memoir","source_ids":["S1412"],"confidence":"High that Lyon makes this interpretation; low-to-moderate as a conclusion about the orders because the orders and full memoir were not inspected.","basis":"Lyon, “Plans for the destruction of the PKI”; underlying confidential orders are not reproduced in the article.","next":"Obtain original order texts and recipients’ copies; compare public speech transcript, Sukarno’s orders and regional operational directives.","year":1965},{"id":"C2597","text":"Lyon reports that Sudjai’s memoir describes opportunities for overseas flight training at U.S. Army and U.S. Air Force flight schools in 1960.","period":"1960","type":"Retrospective memoir detail reported by a later specialist article","source_ids":["S1412"],"confidence":"High that the article states this; low-to-moderate for exact program, attendance and financing because memoir and training records were not retrieved.","basis":"Lyon, “A pilot’s testimony”; describes training opportunities, not a documented covert mission or complete aid-program record.","next":"Search U.S. Army/Air Force foreign training rosters, grant/travel records, Indonesian Army personnel files and Sudjai’s service record.","year":1960},{"id":"C2598","text":"Lyon reports Sudjai’s account that the Indonesian Army rushed an order in 1964 for two U.S.-manufactured twin-engine Aero Grand Commander aircraft to strengthen strike and command-and-control capability; Army pilots ferried them from the United States, with Sudjai arriving in Jakarta in late August 1964.","period":"1964-08","type":"Retrospective participant memoir reported by a later specialist article","source_ids":["S1412"],"confidence":"High that the memoir as summarized says this; moderate for the purchase, delivery date and purpose until procurement and transfer records are checked.","basis":"Lyon, “A pilot’s testimony”; no contract, payment, export-license, delivery or unit assignment document was examined. Procurement evidence is not proof of U.S. political action in 1965.","next":"Find Army procurement/budget files, U.S. export and sales records, aircraft serials, delivery documents, training records and 1965 flight logs.","year":1964},{"id":"C2599","text":"Lyon reports that Sudjai recalled flying Suharto on secret night or pre-dawn flights in early October, including a pre-dawn 3 October trip to Semarang after loyalist forces retook it, and flying Suharto to Semarang again around Aidit’s capture in November.","period":"1965-10-03 and 1965-11","type":"Retrospective participant memoir mediated by a later article","source_ids":["S1412"],"confidence":"High that Lyon reports these recollections; moderate for exact dates and details pending memoir, logs and parallel records.","basis":"Lyon, “Suharto’s pilot”; retrospective recollection does not establish the full operational purpose of each flight.","next":"Compare Army Air Command plans, Semarang command records, Aidit capture documentation and other crew/passenger testimony.","year":1965},{"id":"C2600","text":"The 6 October 1965 CIA memorandum says it had “reliably” been reported that the PKI in August reviewed contingency plans to be used if Sukarno died within the next days or weeks; it describes seclusion of senior leaders and protection of party assets by youth and security cadres. The cable gives no source or underlying document.","period":"August 1965, reported 1965-10-06","type":"Contemporaneous intelligence report of an unattributed report","source_ids":["S026"],"confidence":"High that the CIA memorandum records this report; low-to-moderate for the existence and exact content of the alleged contingency plan absent its source record.","basis":"FRUS d149, para.16. The document says “reliably reported” but does not identify the source, meeting, author or written plan.","next":"Find the cited intelligence source, PKI Politburo/secretariat minutes, Aidit papers and contemporaneous participant records; distinguish succession contingency planning from authorization of 30 September operations.","year":1965},{"id":"C2601","text":"The same CIA memorandum hypothesized that news of Sukarno’s apparent illness on the evening of 30 September, combined with partial or garbled knowledge of a contingency plan for his death, may have influenced the movement’s timing; it further guessed militants expected swift action and a new council to force Sukarno and the country to support them.","period":"1965-09-30 to 10-01, assessed 1965-10-06","type":"Contemporaneous intelligence causal hypothesis","source_ids":["S026"],"confidence":"High that the CIA proposed this hypothesis; low for the underlying causal sequence because it is not tied to a named witness or operational document.","basis":"FRUS d149, para.17 uses “plausible view,” “may have,” and “probably”; this is an analytic reconstruction, not a direct record of the planners’ thinking.","next":"Compare Sudisman, Subekti, Supardjo, Untung and Sjam accounts; locate Sukarno’s 30 September schedule/medical record, operation planning notes and communications about his illness.","year":1965},{"id":"C2602","text":"The 6 October CIA memorandum judged that Aidit probably did not approve killing the generals or changing the government, because the political situation appeared favorable to the PKI, while allowing that a few militant Central Committee members might have supported the plan.","period":"1965-10-06 U.S. assessment of events on 1965-09-30/10-01","type":"Contemporaneous intelligence judgment","source_ids":["S026"],"confidence":"High that the memo expresses this judgment; low-to-moderate as a conclusion about Aidit’s actual knowledge or approval because the memo identifies no direct evidence and describes its view as a probability.","basis":"FRUS d149, para.18. This was an early assessment; compare later participant accounts without treating later trials as automatically more reliable.","next":"Compare original party records and testimony by Aidit’s aides, Sudisman, Subekti, Munir, Sjam and Supardjo; seek contemporaneous communications and calendars.","year":1965},{"id":"C2603","text":"The 6 October CIA memorandum reports that Sukarno’s 2 October broadcast said he had assumed personal command of the Army, appointed General Pranoto administrative head, and deputized Suharto to restore security; the memo says a 3 October KOTI broadcast described Pranoto only as “assisting” the president.","period":"1965-10-02 to 10-03","type":"Contemporaneous diplomatic/intelligence report of public statements","source_ids":["S026"],"confidence":"High that the CIA memo reports these broadcasts and wording; moderate for exact wording pending original Indonesian audio/transcripts. It records asserted formal authority, not proof of who controlled units in practice.","basis":"FRUS d149, paras.6–7. The report also records Suharto’s statement affirming Sukarno’s changes; the underlying broadcasts were not directly inspected in this round.","next":"Retrieve RRI and KOTI transcripts/audio, presidential appointment/order, Army headquarters directives and regional receipt/implementation records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2604","text":"The 6 October CIA memorandum reports that Sukarno rejected Army proposals for firm measures against the movement’s leaders and PKI on 4 October, describing the issue as political and asking generals to leave settlement to him; it says Army officers were depressed after the meeting.","period":"1965-10-04, reported 1965-10-06","type":"Contemporaneous intelligence report of a presidential meeting and attributed reactions","source_ids":["S026"],"confidence":"High that the memo records this account; moderate for the exact words and participant reactions until checked against Indonesian minutes, recordings and separate witnesses.","basis":"FRUS d149, para.9. “Depressed” is the memorandum’s reported characterization, not a measured group response.","next":"Find the 4 October meeting attendee list, minutes, Sukarno speech/transcript, commanders’ diaries and orders issued immediately afterward.","year":1965},{"id":"C2605","text":"The CIA memorandum assessed that Sukarno’s repeated broadcasts, together with pro-Sukarno/pro-Army positions attributed to Tjakrabirawa commander Sabur and Central Java commander Surjosumpeno, appeared to reduce Untung’s following; it also said reports of his remaining support varied from 110 troops to several battalions.","period":"1965-10-01 to 10-06","type":"Contemporaneous intelligence assessment with conflicting force estimates","source_ids":["S026"],"confidence":"High that the memo records this assessment and range; low-to-moderate for the amount of support lost because the report calls the information confused and does not explain its source or counting method.","basis":"FRUS d149, para.4. The range itself warns against treating an early CIA count as a unit roster.","next":"Compare Central Java unit rolls, defections, radio logs, command reports and movement records; identify the sources behind each estimate.","year":1965},{"id":"C2606","text":"Davidson and Kammen report that the banned West Kalimantan PKI maintained an urban underground during 1966 and early 1967, established a Singkawang city committee in April 1967, recruited in Pontianak and Sambas, and linked urban cadres to forest-based resistance and training sites.","period":"1966–1967-04; West Kalimantan","type":"Peer-reviewed regional historical reconstruction drawing on military histories, press and interviews","source_ids":["S232"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this organizational sequence; moderate for its full extent and operational continuity because the underlying rosters, minutes and training registers were not examined.","basis":"Davidson and Kammen, “Indonesia’s Unknown War,” pp.59–61; their footnotes cite a 1970 Kodam history and regional press.","next":"Find city committee membership, meeting records, training attendance, village contacts and captured internal correspondence; separate military press claims from participant evidence.","year":1966},{"id":"C2607","text":"Davidson and Kammen say West Kalimantan PKI leader Said Achmad Sofyan and followers left Pontianak for a forested base called Bukit Bara in late 1965 or possibly early 1966, located near rural Chinese communities and remaining coastal cadres.","period":"Late 1965 or early 1966; Bengkayang area","type":"Regional scholarly reconstruction from military records, press and participant materials","source_ids":["S232"],"confidence":"Moderate that the relocation occurred; low-to-moderate for exact date and precise site because the authors themselves state the date is uncertain and Bara does not appear on available maps.","basis":"Davidson and Kammen, pp.59–60 and notes 28–29; cited military manuscript and a 1967 Kompas map/report. Do not infer that residents of nearby Chinese communities were party members or combatants.","next":"Locate the cited unpublished military manuscript, the 1967 Kompas map/article, Sofyan interviews and village-level names/household records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2608","text":"Davidson and Kammen distinguish four origins in the West Kalimantan rebellion—Paraku, PGRS, Indonesian volunteers, and the banned provincial PKI—and describe a post-Konfrontasi convergence into two loose groupings: PGRS/Paraku oriented toward Sarawak, and PKI members with some former volunteers oriented toward West Kalimantan. They say the groups had contact but remained distinct through early 1967.","period":"1965–1967; West Kalimantan–Sarawak border","type":"Peer-reviewed historical synthesis and organizational distinction","source_ids":["S232"],"confidence":"High that the authors make this distinction; moderate for group boundaries and exact contacts, which were fluid and reconstructed from mixed sources.","basis":"Davidson and Kammen, pp.57–60; see also existing C322/C673. The authors argue contemporaneous military and Western framing collapsed distinct groups into a single China-linked insurgency.","next":"Compare party and guerrilla rosters, PGRS/Paraku internal records, Indonesian military reports, Sarawak branch histories and border-crossing/interrogation records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2609","text":"For April 1967, Davidson and Kammen report a meeting between Sofyan’s PKI group and PGRS/Paraku members to plan a joint offensive; they relay a military-history account that about thirty PGRS members left over a strategic dispute and joined Sofyan, while cautioning that the Army’s broad PGRS label could mean some were actually PKI members.","period":"1967-04; Sanggau Ledo subdistrict","type":"Later scholarly reconstruction from military history and an unpublished military manuscript","source_ids":["S232"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the meeting and competing account; low-to-moderate for the thirty-person roster and its organizational identity because source attribution is contested and the underlying records are not open.","basis":"Davidson and Kammen, p.60 and notes 31–32; the authors explicitly preserve the possibility of misidentification in military records.","next":"Retrieve the unpublished manuscript, cited 1975 Kodam history, party/guerrilla rosters, captured correspondence and accounts from both organizations.","year":1967},{"id":"C2610","text":"Davidson and Kammen argue that West Kalimantan PKI survival into the 1970s depended on a combination of forested terrain, a large rural Chinese population, local Dayak resistance to central-state interference, the Malaysian land border and contact with a neighboring guerrilla movement; this is a regional causal interpretation, not a single sufficient cause.","period":"1966–1974; West Kalimantan","type":"Scholarly causal interpretation based on regional archival and interview research","source_ids":["S232"],"confidence":"High that the authors advance this explanation; moderate as a causal account because local support, coercion, logistics and the relative contribution of each factor are not quantified.","basis":"Davidson and Kammen, pp.85–86. Do not generalize these conditions to South Blitar or all Indonesian underground groups.","next":"Compare individual supply, refuge and recruitment evidence with military sweeps, border controls, local conflict and survival histories; contrast with South Blitar’s command, food and population conditions.","year":1966},{"id":"C2611","text":"Davidson and Kammen report that on 16 October 1965 the West Kalimantan provincial government froze the PKI and affiliated organizations and ordered party leaders to report to authorities; two days later anti-PKI demonstrations took place in Pontianak.","period":"1965-10-16 to 1965-10-18; West Kalimantan","type":"Regional scholarly report of local government measures and demonstrations","source_ids":["S232"],"confidence":"High that the article reports these dates and measures; moderate pending the provincial order, local press and police records.","basis":"Davidson and Kammen, “Indonesia’s Unknown War,” p.57; the original provincial decree is not reproduced in the article.","next":"Retrieve the freeze order, distribution list, party leaders’ reporting records, Pontianak newspapers and police/municipal records for 18 October.","year":1965},{"id":"C2612","text":"Davidson and Kammen report that Tanjungpura commander Brigadier General Ryacudu was unwilling to arrest West Kalimantan PKI head Said Achmad Sofyan and provided him refuge in the commander’s official residence after the 16 October freeze.","period":"October 1965; West Kalimantan","type":"Secondary historical report based on cited sources and accounts","source_ids":["S232"],"confidence":"Moderate-to-low for the episode pending identifiable firsthand testimony, residence/security logs and command records; high that the study reports it.","basis":"Davidson and Kammen, p.57 and note 16. This is evidence of a reported local command choice, not proof Ryacudu supported the PKI or opposed all anti-PKI actions.","next":"Find Ryacudu’s diary/orders, household and guard records, Sofyan’s testimony, provincial reports and the sources behind the article’s note 16.","year":1965},{"id":"C2613","text":"Davidson and Kammen say that about two weeks after Ryacudu sheltered him, Sofyan was escorted outside Pontianak and released, according to several “well-placed sources” cited by the authors.","period":"Late October 1965; Pontianak","type":"Retrospective report attributed to unnamed sources","source_ids":["S232"],"confidence":"Low-to-moderate because the source identities and release documentation are not provided; high that the article reports this account.","basis":"Davidson and Kammen, p.58 note 16. The date and meaning of “released” are not independently verified.","next":"Identify the cited sources; find military transport/security records, arrest/release registers, Sofyan’s account and contemporaneous local reporting.","year":1965},{"id":"C2614","text":"In his 21 July 1967 defense, Sudisman identifies “Tegakkan PKI yang Marxis-Leninis untuk Memimpin Revolusi Demokrasi Rakyat” as the self-criticism he had tried to carry out and proposes appending it to his defense so the public could assess it.","period":"1967-07-21; retrospective attribution to 1966","type":"Detained participant’s self-attribution","source_ids":["S1339","S260"],"confidence":"High that the published transcription records this statement; moderate that he was a principal drafter.","basis":"The 1971 scan title leaf attributes the document to the Political Bureau, while the modern transcription labels it “Sudisman.” His testimony supports personal involvement, not sole authorship. It was made in detention at a military tribunal.","next":"Compare original court transcript/audio, 1966 drafts, circulation copies, minutes and testimony by other Politburo members.","year":1967},{"id":"C2615","text":"The September 1966 self-criticism says ongoing “white terror” made a complete analysis difficult, presents urgent main issues first, and asks Party members to discuss and complete it using collective and individual experience.","period":"1966-09; reprinted 1971","type":"Internal party document and publication record","source_ids":["S262","S1331"],"confidence":"High for the text’s wording; unknown whether the requested internal discussion occurred.","basis":"Selected scan pp.91–97 visually checked against the Indonesian transcription. The later scan is a reprint, not the 1966 manuscript; circulation records are absent.","next":"Locate the original September 1966 document, internal circulation and responses.","year":1966},{"id":"C2616","text":"The 1966 self-criticism says that adopting legal and parliamentary struggle in 1951 was basically correct under the objective conditions then, including a receding revolutionary wave and weak mass forces.","period":"1951 as assessed in 1966","type":"Retrospective party diagnosis","source_ids":["S262","S1331"],"confidence":"High that the text makes this distinction; moderate for its reconstruction of 1951 conditions.","basis":"Printed text pp.112–113; visually checked. This does not say that all later legal strategy remained correct or that immediate armed struggle was feasible in 1951.","next":"Compare the 1951 Politburo/CC records, dissenting views, party registration, regional reports and Madiun aftermath.","year":1951},{"id":"C2617","text":"The 1966 self-criticism says the Party never developed a sufficiently clear, party-wide understanding of the principal form of struggle, and that the peaceful/legal route later became a principle even after legality and political rights were taken away.","period":"1951–1965 as assessed in 1966","type":"Retrospective party diagnosis","source_ids":["S262","S1331","S261"],"confidence":"High for the document’s diagnosis; moderate-to-low for how uniformly it describes policy and practice.","basis":"Printed text pp.112–121; checked against the scan and English edition. The 1968 English edition is a translation/reprint, not independent corroboration.","next":"Examine 1956 plenum and 1959/1962/1965 congress and Politburo records, constitutional texts, and provincial implementation.","year":1951},{"id":"C2618","text":"The 1966 self-criticism accuses Party leadership of redirecting peasant unilateral actions against landlords toward cultural, production and anti-pest campaigns rather than building an anti-landlord agrarian struggle.","period":"Late 1950s–1965 as assessed in 1966","type":"Internal party accusation about agrarian strategy","source_ids":["S262","S1331"],"confidence":"High that the later text makes the accusation; low-to-moderate as a verified account of actions across regions.","basis":"Printed text pp.124–127; selected scan pages visually checked. The document does not identify a complete set of local campaigns, orders or outcomes.","next":"Compare BTI and village records, land claims, district instructions, crop/rat-control programs, landlord records and local testimony.","year":1950},{"id":"C2619","text":"The 1966 self-criticism says worker takeovers of Dutch, British and Belgian companies mainly benefited a small bureaucratic-capitalist group and did not improve the workers’ living conditions.","period":"1960–1965 as assessed in 1966","type":"Internal party economic diagnosis","source_ids":["S262","S1331"],"confidence":"High for the text’s claim; low-to-moderate for the distributional conclusion without firm-level records.","basis":"Printed text pp.124–127; selected scan pages visually checked. This is a faction’s post-defeat assessment, not an audited account of the firms or workers.","next":"Identify the firms, takeover committees, ownership transfers, wage rolls, profits and worker interviews.","year":1960},{"id":"C2620","text":"The 1966 self-criticism says later worker actions were curtailed because Party leaders considered government-controlled companies national property.","period":"1960–1965 as assessed in 1966","type":"Internal party causal diagnosis","source_ids":["S262","S1331"],"confidence":"High for what the text says; moderate-to-low for the extent and causal operation in practice.","basis":"Printed text pp.124–127. No firm-level orders or labor minutes were examined; the source gives a retrospective leadership critique.","next":"Find company-level union decisions, government directives, plant minutes and takeover-to-state-ownership records.","year":1960},{"id":"C2621","text":"The 1966 self-criticism calls for armed peasant agrarian revolution under working-class leadership but says it cannot be imposed from outside; it should develop through peasants’ own struggle, consciousness and political education.","period":"1966 programmatic position about the revolution","type":"Internal party programmatic text","source_ids":["S262","S1331","S261"],"confidence":"High for the text’s stated position; no inference about pre-1965 capacity or authorization.","basis":"Printed text pp.112–114, 166–167; visually checked. This is the surviving faction’s 1966 line, not proof that a nationwide armed program existed in 1965.","next":"Compare 1951–1965 party programs, military training records, BTI branch evidence and later regional commands.","year":1966},{"id":"C2622","text":"The September 1966 self-criticism cites a Political Bureau statement dated 17 August 1965 and accuses the leadership of exaggerating mass activity, especially peasant action, discounting contrary district reports, and treating a still-maturing situation as a revolution; it calls this a “left” error that contributed to adventurism.","period":"17 Aug 1965, as assessed in 1966","type":"Retrospective internal causal diagnosis","source_ids":["S262","S1331"],"confidence":"High that the self-criticism makes these claims; low-to-moderate as independently verified reporting and causation.","basis":"Printed text pp.166–167, visually checked. This is a post-defeat account of what leaders said and how they used local reporting; the complete 1965 statement, district reports and meeting records are not in hand.","next":"Retrieve the 17 Aug 1965 statement and dated provincial/district mass-action reports; compare independent land, labor and press records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2623","text":"ANRI’s KOTI finding aid includes item 507, titled as a Political Bureau CC PKI statement to continue revolutionary offensives in all fields and develop the revolutionary situation to its peak.","period":"Catalogue entry; document date unspecified","type":"Archival finding-aid record","source_ids":["S228"],"confidence":"High that the catalogue entry exists and carries this description; low that it is the same document cited in the 1966 self-criticism.","basis":"The catalogue does not provide the item’s date, contents, physical condition or original/duplicate status in the accessible entry. It is a lead, not the statement itself.","next":"Request item 507 and establish date, document identity, provenance, pages and relation to the 17 Aug 1965 citation.","year":null},{"id":"C2624","text":"John Roosa reproduces a Harian Rakjat cartoon and identifies its source as the newspaper’s 17 August 1965 issue; the caption gives the slogan “Perhebat Ofensif Revolusioner Disegala Bidang.”","period":"1965-08-17; reproduced in 2008 Indonesian edition","type":"Secondary reproduction of contemporary newspaper illustration","source_ids":["S252"],"confidence":"High for what Roosa reproduces and how he attributes it; no claim that this is the full Politburo statement.","basis":"Roosa, printed pp.237–238. A cartoon and slogan are not the complete statement; this is not an independent copy of ANRI item 507.","next":"Obtain the 17 Aug 1965 Harian Rakjat full issue and compare with the Politburo document.","year":1965},{"id":"C2625","text":"The 1968 Foreign Languages Press pamphlet’s publisher note distinguishes a Political Bureau statement dated 17 August 1966 from the September 1966 self-criticism and says the latter appeared in issue no.3, described as the January issue.","period":"1966–1968 publication history","type":"Publisher’s note in translated reprint","source_ids":["S261"],"confidence":"High that the 1968 edition says this; medium for the exact first-publication date until the named issue is inspected.","basis":"The original Indonesian Tribune issue no.3 was not obtained. This 1966 statement is distinct from the 17 Aug 1965 statement cited in the self-criticism.","next":"Locate Indonesian Tribune issue no.3, compare its date and text, and inspect the 17 Aug 1966 statement separately.","year":1966},{"id":"C2626","text":"The 1971 Delegation of the CC PKI edition reprints the self-criticism five years after its stated September 1966 date; the volume’s foreword gives a later armed/rural strategic framing that must be distinguished from the 1966 document body.","period":"1966 document; 1971 publication","type":"Publication provenance and editorial framing","source_ids":["S262","S1331"],"confidence":"High for edition/title-leaf/foreword contents in the scanned copy; the foreword is not a 1966 record.","basis":"157-page scanned edition checked at cover, title leaf and foreword. The 1971 reprint is the same publication represented by the online transcription, not an independent witness.","next":"Compare the complete 1968 and 1971 editions with any original 1966 manuscript and trace the Delegation’s authority and circulation.","year":1966},{"id":"C2627","text":"The ISEAS–Yusof Ishak Institute library guide lists Harian Rakjat microfilm holdings for 1953–1965 under call number MFM 4.","period":"Library holdings; access checked 2026-11-18","type":"Library finding-aid record","source_ids":["S1413"],"confidence":"High that the institutional guide lists this holding; unverified that the specific 17 Aug 1965 issue is present.","basis":"The guide gives a year range only; its catalogue-detail link returned 403 and no microfilm was examined.","next":"Request the MFM 4 issue reel or an item-level holdings list for 17 Aug 1965, and neighboring issues.","year":2026},{"id":"C2628","text":"Xiamen University’s Center for Southeast Asian Studies holdings inventory lists Harian Rakjat through September 1965, including a run described as 1962–1965.9.","period":"Library holdings; inventory opened 2026-11-18","type":"University library holdings record","source_ids":["S1414"],"confidence":"High that the inventory prints this date range; low that any single daily issue survives without item-level confirmation.","basis":"The Chinese-language page is a collection-level list, not an issue checklist or digitized issue.","next":"Ask for the exact August 1965 daily issue holdings and copy access terms.","year":2026},{"id":"C2629","text":"John Roosa’s resource page describes the Perpusnas Khastara Harian Rakjat digitization as a work in progress, with only 1951–1952 issues added at the time of the page’s note.","period":"Digitization status reported on undated resource page; checked 2026-11-18","type":"Researcher-curated access note","source_ids":["S1415"],"confidence":"High that the page says this; current platform status remains unverified and may change.","basis":"The note concerns the linked digital subset, not all Perpusnas holdings; the Khastara page itself returned 403 through the web reader.","next":"Recheck the live Khastara manifest and request its item-level catalogue for August 1965.","year":2026},{"id":"C2630","text":"The 2004 oral-history volume reports 260 interviews and says recordings/transcripts were archived at ISSI; its interview list includes Rewang on 19 July 2000 and 27 June 2001 and Muyatno on 12 December 2000.","period":"2000 interviews; volume published 2004","type":"Methodological note and interview-list entry","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that the book prints these details; no audio or transcript independently reviewed.","basis":"The 2004 volume describes a research collection and interviews; it is not itself the interview recordings.","next":"Locate the original Rewang and Muyatno recordings, transcripts, metadata and consent/access files at ISSI.","year":2000},{"id":"C2631","text":"ISSI’s current Arsip Suara page, as indexed, describes roughly 500 oral-history interviews and access restricted to researchers/students with an institutional letter; the Rewang/Muyatno item-level holdings were not identified.","period":"Current archive access page, checked 2026-11-18","type":"Archive-description observation","source_ids":["S1416"],"confidence":"High that indexed page text reports this; no individual recording found.","basis":"The page itself returned a verification challenge. Access terms can change; collection-level description does not establish that any named item is held.","next":"Request item-level catalog entries for Rewang, Muyatno and OHP 1965, then consult under current access rules.","year":2026},{"id":"C2632","text":"TAPOL Bulletin 66 prints “Gatot Lestario (Sutaryo),” calls him chair of the PKI East Java provincial committee, and gives 19 January 1969 as arrest date while leaving location as “Jakarta or East Java.”","period":"1967–1969; report published November 1984","type":"Contemporary advocacy report","source_ids":["S1419"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL prints these words; moderate for office and arrest details absent primary records.","basis":"The bulletin is an advocacy account drawing on press/reporting; the location itself is explicitly uncertain.","next":"Find East Java PKI appointment records, arrest/capture register and the cited military history.","year":1967},{"id":"C2633","text":"Hearman reports that Lestario used the alias “Gatot Sutaryo”; South Blitar interviewees remembered him as intermediary with the base leadership in 1967–1968; the military history recorded him as secretary of the “Project Committee”; Hearman dates his capture to 19 January 1969 in Jakarta.","period":"1967–1969; article published 2016","type":"Secondary historical reconstruction using cited military history and retrospective interviews","source_ids":["S1417"],"confidence":"High that Hearman makes these reports; moderate for the underlying role and capture details.","basis":"The article’s footnotes cite Semdam VIII Brawidjaja’s 1969 Operasi Trisula for the alias, office and capture, and pseudonymous interviews for intermediary status; the book and interview originals were not independently opened.","next":"Obtain Operasi Trisula pp.297–298, interview metadata/audio if accessible, and original arrest/capture papers.","year":1967},{"id":"C2634","text":"Muyatno’s “Gatot Taryo” is probably the same person as the Gatot Sutaryo/Lestario linked in later sources: name form, South Blitar context and committee role overlap, but no source directly equates the shortened “Taryo” with Lestario.","period":"1967–1968; identity cross-check made 2026-11-18","type":"Cross-source identity inference","source_ids":["S1317","S1417","S1419","S1421","S1422"],"confidence":"Moderate; plausible identity, not established identity.","basis":"Muyatno calls Taryo the Kompro chair; Hearman cites the military history for secretary of the Project Committee; TAPOL/Amnesty call Lestario provincial committee chair. Different bodies/levels may explain some role differences, but this is not proven.","next":"Check original interview sound/transcript, party roster, military captured documents and court file for a full-name crosswalk.","year":1967},{"id":"C2635","text":"The National Library of Wales catalogue describes a 0.5 cm “Prisoner file of Gatot Lestario” (2/3) containing 1983–1989 papers about Lestario and wife Pudji Aswati, including letter photocopies; the creation-date field instead says 1983–1986.","period":"Archival description; file dates 1983–1989/creation 1983–1986","type":"Finding-aid record with internal date-range discrepancy","source_ids":["S1418"],"confidence":"High that the finding aid prints these descriptions; file contents remain unreviewed.","basis":"Catalogue metadata is not equivalent to reading the file; date fields differ.","next":"Request the file or digitized item list, then compare letters with IISH holdings and published excerpts.","year":1983},{"id":"C2636","text":"In a published letter dated 5 August 1982, Lestario says he was born 25 November 1925 in Trenggalek, had taught history at Taman Dewasa, and gives his age as 57; by the stated birth date he would have been 56 on that day.","period":"1925–1982","type":"Published first-person letter excerpt with internal age/date discrepancy","source_ids":["S1420"],"confidence":"High that the excerpt prints these details; moderate as civil-biographical proof.","basis":"The editor says the excerpt comes from an unpublished letter; the original is not inspected. Age may reflect a typo or counting convention.","next":"Check birth/education records or original letter and correspondence file.","year":1925},{"id":"C2637","text":"A published excerpt of Lestario’s diary reports that he was executed on 1 July 1985 and that remains were transferred to Pamekasan Prison Cemetery on 2 July.","period":"1985-07-01 to 1985-07-02","type":"Published diary excerpt","source_ids":["S1420"],"confidence":"High that the published excerpt states these dates; execution register not seen.","basis":"Underlying diary manuscript is unpublished and was not inspected; Amnesty’s report records a broader 1–3 July interval.","next":"Locate original diary, execution order, prison register and burial/cemetery record.","year":1985},{"id":"C2638","text":"Hearman’s article reports Lestario’s 1925 birth in Trenggalek, Taman Siswa education, early work as assistant to Amir Sjarifuddin, Education Department work in Malang after 1948, and teaching at a Taman Siswa senior high school in Kediri from 1951.","period":"1925–1951","type":"Secondary biographical reconstruction","source_ids":["S1417"],"confidence":"High that article reports the sequence; moderate pending the cited biographical and military-history sources.","basis":"Footnotes cite McVey and the 1969 military history; the underlying references and civil records were not reviewed here.","next":"Check school, employment and local personnel records; compare party biography and family documents.","year":1925},{"id":"C2639","text":"Hearman reports that Lestario married fellow activist and teacher Pudjiaswati in 1952 and that they had two children, Yanthi and Pradono.","period":"1952 onward","type":"Secondary biographical reconstruction based partly on a cited letter","source_ids":["S1417"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this; moderate pending family or civil documentation.","basis":"The article cites a 1983 letter for some family details; no marriage/civil registry or family interview was opened. Name spelling also varies as Pudji Aswati/Pudjiaswati.","next":"Seek marriage, school, family and prison correspondence records; preserve name variants.","year":1952},{"id":"C2640","text":"Hearman reports Pudji as third chair of the Surabaya Gerwani branch and a leader of the PKI-established People’s University in Surabaya.","period":"1950s–1965","type":"Secondary biographical reconstruction","source_ids":["S1417"],"confidence":"High that the article states these roles; moderate pending cited records.","basis":"These are Pudji’s offices and should not be attributed to Gatot; article cites McVey and letter materials not independently examined here.","next":"Check Gerwani branch rosters, People’s University staff lists and local education records.","year":1950},{"id":"C2641","text":"Hearman reports Lestario became a full-time PKI official responsible for day-to-day operations in East Java while serving as a member of the provincial committee.","period":"Before October 1965","type":"Secondary biographical reconstruction","source_ids":["S1417"],"confidence":"High that the article makes the claim; moderate pending party records.","basis":"This does not by itself establish his specific decisions or authority in the 1967–1968 South Blitar base.","next":"Find East Java provincial committee minutes, staff lists, task assignments and communications.","year":1965},{"id":"C2642","text":"Hearman reports Lestario and Djoko Untung were tried in Blitar in 1975 with Basuki; Amnesty’s 1984 report likewise places the joint trial at the end of 1975 and says Basuki died during trial on 15 December 1975.","period":"1975","type":"Secondary article and contemporary advocacy report","source_ids":["S1417","S1421"],"confidence":"High that both sources report a 1975 trial; the court chronology still requires the file.","basis":"Hearman’s account and Amnesty are not fully independent: Hearman cites Amnesty and the Brawijaya military history for related details. Amnesty’s report cites press and prosecutor statements.","next":"Obtain the court docket, indictment, hearing calendar, judgment and Basuki’s death/custody record.","year":1975},{"id":"C2643","text":"Amnesty’s November 1984 report says Lestario and Djoko Untung were sentenced to death on 2 January 1976; Hearman repeats 2 January 1976, while TAPOL Bulletin 70 reports 9 January 1976 and cites press reports dated 8–9 January.","period":"1976-01-02 / 1976-01-09","type":"Conflicting contemporary reports and later scholarship","source_ids":["S1417","S1421","S1423"],"confidence":"High that the sources disagree; low on the precise verdict date without the judgment.","basis":"The reports may reflect different announcement/sentencing dates or an error; neither date is selected as settled.","next":"Compare the written judgment, hearing minutes, Indonesian Times 8 Jan 1976 and Suara Karya 9 Jan 1976.","year":1976},{"id":"C2644","text":"The 2017 Manifold chapter introduction says Lestario conducted his defense at a Blitar trial in 1978, conflicting with Amnesty/TAPOL/Hearman reports of a 1975 trial and 1976 sentence; 1978 also appears in sources as his transfer/period at Pamekasan.","period":"1975–1978","type":"Publication-level chronology conflict","source_ids":["S1417","S1420","S1421","S1423"],"confidence":"High that the 2017 introduction prints 1978; low that 1978 is the actual trial date.","basis":"The note may conflate Lestario’s trial with his transfer to Pamekasan or Pudji’s 1978 trial; this explanation is an inference, not proven.","next":"Check judgment and Lestario’s 26 December 1975 defense statement; keep the discrepancy visible until then.","year":1975},{"id":"C2645","text":"Amnesty’s 1984 case report gives Pudji Aswati a 15-year sentence; TAPOL Bulletin 70 (1985) says 17 years; TAPOL Bulletin 79 (1987) says 18 years.","period":"1970s–1987","type":"Conflicting contemporary advocacy reports","source_ids":["S1421","S1423","S1424"],"confidence":"High that these publications report different terms; sentence length unresolved.","basis":"The publications may count the sentence or pre-trial detention differently, or contain reporting errors; no judgment was inspected.","next":"Find Pudji’s court judgment, detention credit decision and Malang prison release register.","year":1970},{"id":"C2646","text":"TAPOL Bulletin 70 reports that the Army detained Pudji Aswati and allegedly tortured her to learn Gatot’s location; it says she refused to disclose it.","period":"1967–1968; reported July 1985","type":"Advocacy account drawing on released prisoners and prisoner materials","source_ids":["S1423"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL makes the allegation; independent confirmation not found.","basis":"Serious allegation; no interrogation record, medical evidence or direct interview/audio was reviewed.","next":"Seek Pudji’s trial testimony, detention/interrogation files, family letters and independent witness accounts.","year":1967},{"id":"C2647","text":"Hearman’s article describes Lestario’s letters (1983–1985) to overseas correspondents as part of a grassroots support/advocacy network involving Quakers, Amnesty International and TAPOL; published excerpts include his criticisms of New Order rule and prison conditions.","period":"1983–1985","type":"Historical interpretation based on letters and correspondent interviews","source_ids":["S1417","S1420","S1418"],"confidence":"High that article and excerpts report this network; moderate for its full extent.","basis":"Only selected published excerpts and one finding aid were checked; the underlying IISH and private correspondence files remain to be inspected.","next":"Locate IISH collection ARCH02325 and the Wales prisoner file contents; map specific letters, recipients, censoring and actions.","year":1983},{"id":"C2648","text":"Amnesty’s August 1985 update says it received reports that Lestario, Djoko Untung and Rustomo were executed between 1 and 3 July 1985 in Madura/Pamekasan and that families were reportedly not informed; a diary excerpt reports 1 July and remains transfer on 2 July.","period":"1985-07-01 to 1985-07-03","type":"Contemporary human-rights reporting compared with later published diary excerpt","source_ids":["S1420","S1422","S1424"],"confidence":"High that the sources make these reports; exact date and burial details lack official records.","basis":"Amnesty’s interval and diary excerpt are compatible on 1 July but not an execution certificate; later TAPOL reporting says Pudji learned 22 days afterward.","next":"Seek official execution order/register, prison log, burial/cemetery record and family notification correspondence.","year":1985},{"id":"C2649","text":"TAPOL Bulletin 79 reports Pudji Aswati learned of Gatot’s execution 22 days later after a letter was returned with a note that he was dead.","period":"July 1985; reported February 1987","type":"Contemporary human-rights bulletin relaying a French press account","source_ids":["S1424"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL reports it; underlying letter and family account not inspected.","basis":"The article cites l’Humanité Dimanche (14 November 1986); no returned envelope or prison notification log was seen.","next":"Locate the cited French article, returned letter and family correspondence.","year":1985},{"id":"C2650","text":"TAPOL and Amnesty label Lestario “Sutaryo” or “Sutarjo”; Hearman identifies “Gatot Sutaryo” as an alias, while the 1982 letter excerpt and National Library of Wales finding aid use “Gatot Lestario.”","period":"Identity/name variants; 1960s–1980s","type":"Name-variant cross-check","source_ids":["S1417","S1418","S1419","S1420","S1421","S1422"],"confidence":"High that these variants appear in the sources; official identity crosswalk not found.","basis":"Similar spelling supports but does not alone prove every variant belongs to one person. TAPOL’s Sutarjo spelling may be typographical/transcriptional.","next":"Find civil identity records, court caption, prison register and original manuscript name forms.","year":1960},{"id":"C2651","text":"Hearman’s South Blitar article and the contemporary reports establish several attributed offices, but no source reviewed here gives a primary PKI appointment, Kompro roster or meeting record proving Lestario’s exact decision-making authority.","period":"1967–1969","type":"Evidence-gap observation","source_ids":["S1317","S1417","S1419","S1421","S1423"],"confidence":"High that the available sources are indirect/attributed; no primary appointment file located.","basis":"Do not infer a particular order, armed action or command responsibility from the office labels alone.","next":"Seek party appointment records, captured documents, court exhibits, military interrogations and oral-history originals.","year":1967},{"id":"C2652","text":"Hearman’s 2018 article says Pudji Aswati was tried in 1978 after ten years in custody and received a 15-year sentence; it separately describes Gatot Lestario as a South Blitar base secretary who was sentenced to death and executed in July 1985.","period":"1975–1985","type":"Secondary historical reconstruction","source_ids":["S1425"],"confidence":"High that the article states these details; moderate pending court and prison records.","basis":"Same author and overlapping research as S1310/S1417; not independent corroboration. The 15-year term conflicts with TAPOL reports of 17 and 18 years.","next":"Check Pudji’s Blitar judgment, pre-trial detention credit and Malang prison release register; compare with the trial date of Lestario.","year":1975},{"id":"C2653","text":"The 2017 UCalgary introduction may have assigned Pudji Aswati’s 1978 trial year to Gatot Lestario, because Hearman’s 2018 account dates Pudji’s trial to 1978 while contemporary reports place Lestario’s trial in 1975 and sentence in 1976.","period":"1975–1978","type":"Cross-publication chronology inference","source_ids":["S1417","S1420","S1421","S1423","S1425"],"confidence":"Moderate as an explanation for the discrepancy; not a proven editorial error.","basis":"A direct UCalgary correction or Blitar court file has not been found; keep the 2017 statement and competing dates visible.","next":"Compare the chapter introduction with its editor’s source manuscript, Pudji’s judgment, Lestario’s judgment and Pamekasan transfer record.","year":1975},{"id":"C2654","text":"TAPOL says Gatot Lestario’s defense statement was handwritten on nearly 80 pages and made on 26 December 1975; TAPOL Bulletin 71 publishes selected extracts rather than the complete statement.","period":"1975-12-26","type":"Contemporaneous publication report","source_ids":["S1426"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL reported this and printed extracts; the original manuscript has not been inspected.","basis":"The bulletin’s 1985 English rendering is a partial translation/publication witness; it does not allow verification of omitted passages or exact wording in the Indonesian original.","next":"Locate the 80-page manuscript, the Dutch nearly complete edition and the Blitar court file; collate page order, translation and omissions.","year":1975},{"id":"C2655","text":"In the defense text as excerpted by TAPOL, Lestario said grave PKI internal weaknesses went unaddressed and that some leaders disregarded organizational procedures in joining military adventurism not grounded in mass consciousness; elsewhere in the same argument he said only a few individual PKI leaders were involved and denied that the PKI as an organization planned G30S.","period":"1965 interpreted in 1975","type":"Participant’s retrospective courtroom argument","source_ids":["S1426"],"confidence":"High that the published excerpts make these arguments; low as independent proof of the underlying G30S decision process.","basis":"The original text, complete context and Indonesian wording were not examined. Lestario was a defendant in a capital political trial and had strategic legal and political reasons for his framing.","next":"Compare the complete Dutch edition and original defense manuscript with Sudisman’s defense/self-criticism, other defendants’ statements, contemporaneous Politburo and Special Bureau records, and cross-examined court evidence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2656","text":"Lestario’s defense framed the South Blitar underground and armed resistance as a consequence of the overthrow of Sukarno, outlawing and persecution of communists, and a struggle to survive; he argued that cadres integrated with poor peasants and resisted encirclement.","period":"1967–1968 interpreted in 1975","type":"Participant’s retrospective courtroom argument","source_ids":["S1426"],"confidence":"High that the excerpts contain this interpretation; not independent evidence that all cadres shared it or that every operational detail occurred as described.","basis":"TAPOL prints selected translated excerpts. Claims about military operations, peasant relations and causation need local records and testimonies from multiple positions.","next":"Compare military operation logs and village case files with independent survivor interviews, land/livelihood records, PKI underground correspondence and records from neighboring districts.","year":1967},{"id":"C2657","text":"TAPOL Bulletin 76 reported that the 1985 Dutch-language volume Indonesië: De waarheid omtrent 1965. Suharto staat terecht contained an almost complete, extensively annotated Dutch text of Lestario’s defense plea.","period":"1985–1986","type":"Contemporary bibliographic report","source_ids":["S1427"],"confidence":"High that TAPOL described the book this way; the exact completeness, annotation and translation chain remain unverified.","basis":"TAPOL’s summary is not direct inspection of the volume, and the published TAPOL 71 English extracts are not the same edition.","next":"Locate and inspect a surviving copy; identify translator/editor, pagination, source manuscript and annotation citations; compare against TAPOL 71 and court copy.","year":1985},{"id":"C2658","text":"The 5 June 1986 Attorney General’s decision bans circulation throughout Indonesia of the 1985 Amsterdam book and orders holders or distributors to surrender copies and authorized state bodies to seize them.","period":"1986-06-05","type":"Official legal order, visually checked scan","source_ids":["S1428"],"confidence":"High; both pages of the official two-page scan were visually checked.","basis":"The order establishes what the government directed, not actual nationwide enforcement or the number of copies seized.","next":"Search state-gazette publication, local prosecutor/police enforcement files, customs records and publisher correspondence.","year":1986},{"id":"C2659","text":"Peter Dale Scott’s bibliography independently lists the Amsterdam: Indonesia Media 1985 book and his Dutch-translated essay within it, while contributing no direct description of Lestario’s plea text.","period":"1985","type":"Contributor bibliography","source_ids":["S1429"],"confidence":"High for Scott’s bibliography entry; limited to book and essay identification.","basis":"Scott is a contributor, and the bibliography corroborates bibliographic metadata but not the book’s defense-text completeness or quality of annotation.","next":"Inspect a catalogued copy or publisher/archival file for the full table of contents and production records.","year":1985},{"id":"C2660","text":"The decree’s preamble says the book contained writings and accusations attacking the personal character of Indonesia’s national leader/President and could disturb public order; it cites an opinion from the head of BAKIN dated 11 April 1986, no. R/921/IV/1986/G.","period":"1986-04-11 to 1986-06-05","type":"Official decision text, visually checked scan","source_ids":["S1428"],"confidence":"High that this is the decision’s stated basis; the underlying BAKIN opinion and the accuracy of the decree’s characterization have not been independently examined.","basis":"The asserted threat and attack are the government’s own legal characterization, not a neutral evaluation of the book’s arguments. The named intelligence opinion is referenced but unavailable in this pass.","next":"Obtain BAKIN opinion R/921/IV/1986/G, compare it with the full book and related Attorney General decisions, and look for internal circulation instructions.","year":1986},{"id":"C2661","text":"KEP-044/J.A/6/1986, dated 18 June 1986, prohibits circulation of Harold Crouch’s Militer dan Politik di Indonesia, orders persons holding or distributing copies to surrender them, and authorizes prosecutors, police or other public-order agencies to seize them. Its preamble says the book may harm the national interest and cause public distrust of national leadership and government; it cites a Department of Information recommendation of 9 May 1986, No.268/IJ/K/V/RHS/1986.","period":"1986-05-09 to 1986-06-18","type":"Official legal order; visually checked scan","source_ids":["S1433"],"confidence":"High for the decree’s text and stated rationale; no independent assessment of the book or implementation.","basis":"The cited recommendation is not included in the scan. The legal order documents the government’s action and justification, not the recommendation’s drafting process, its evidentiary basis, or actual nationwide enforcement.","next":"Obtain the Department of Information recommendation, Attorney General file, local seizure reports, publisher correspondence and customs/postal records.","year":1986},{"id":"C2662","text":"ELSAM’s 1999 study reports that in June 1986 a House Commission I hearing with BAKIN included Vice Chair Rusli Desa urging a ban on Harold Crouch’s book, arguing it would undermine public trust in the President. ELSAM says the ban followed “a month later”; the official KEP-044 order is dated 18 June 1986, and the study gives no hearing day, so the stated interval cannot be independently reconciled and may be approximate.","period":"1986-06","type":"Retrospective secondary account contrasted with official order date","source_ids":["S1430","S1433"],"confidence":"High that ELSAM reports the hearing and JDIH dates the order 18 June; low for the exact interval and the hearing’s causal weight.","basis":"No hearing minutes, transcript, attendee list, or Rusli Desa statement was obtained. The hearing, BAKIN’s institutional attendance, and the separate formal recommendation cited by the decree must not be collapsed into one proven decision chain.","next":"Obtain Commission I minutes/audio, BAKIN hearing records, the 9 May Department of Information recommendation, and the full Attorney General review file.","year":1986},{"id":"C2663","text":"Penetapan Presiden No.4/1963 authorized the Minister/Attorney General to prohibit printed matter deemed disruptive of public order; provided for seizure by prosecutors, police or other authorized public-order agencies; allowed prior screening of selected imported printed matter and return of banned foreign material held by postal offices; and required a printer to submit one copy within 48 hours for the listed classes of printed matter.","period":"1963 onward; invoked in the 1986 KEP-041 and KEP-044 orders","type":"Primary legislation and later invocation in official orders","source_ids":["S1431","S1428","S1433"],"confidence":"High for the statutory text and its citation in the two 1986 orders; actual enforcement practice remains case-specific.","basis":"The statute sets legal powers and procedures, not proof that every imported item was screened, every copy surrendered, or each local office complied.","next":"Compare implementation files, State Gazette notices, import/customs and postal records, and local prosecutor/police inventories for the 1985 Amsterdam book.","year":1963},{"id":"C2664","text":"The 1963 decree’s preamble describes foreign influence delivered through imported printed matter as a matter the government should control; its explanatory note gives harm to belief in the revolution, socialism and national leadership as examples of “disturbing public order,” leaving the assessment to the Minister/Attorney General.","period":"1963","type":"Primary legal text and official explanation","source_ids":["S1431"],"confidence":"High that this was the decree’s stated ideological and legal rationale.","basis":"This is the Sukarno-era legal text and explanation, not evidence by itself of the motives behind either 1986 ban or of actual foreign operations.","next":"Read in parallel with the specific 1986 order preambles and the underlying BAKIN/Department of Information recommendations.","year":1963},{"id":"C2665","text":"Jaringan Kerja Budaya/ELSAM reports that New Order book-ban proposals also came from other government bodies and social organizations; that routine political-security coordination meetings chaired by Menko Polkam included the Attorney General and senior government/military officials; and that BAKIN, Bakorstanas, Bais ABRI, Polri and the Ministry of Religion sent views to the Attorney General, while the Attorney General’s intelligence deputy gathered information on “sensitive” books.","period":"1968–1990s, retrospective account published 1999","type":"Secondary institutional reconstruction citing decrees and ban-order recitals","source_ids":["S1430"],"confidence":"High that the book makes this account; moderate as a reconstruction of recurring administrative practice pending primary minutes and correspondence.","basis":"No meeting minutes, submission letters, intelligence files or staffing records were inspected. The listed agencies did not necessarily participate in every individual ban; keep each order’s named inputs separate.","next":"Acquire Menko Polkam meeting records, institutional letters attached to individual ban cases, Attorney General intelligence files and underlying records cited in ELSAM’s footnotes.","year":1968},{"id":"C2666","text":"Keppres No.10/1986 formally defines Muspida as a consultation/coordination forum linking regional governments, ABRI officers and other state apparatus; it tasks the forum with coordinating regional agencies, assessing disturbances to public order and determining prevention/response measures. Its provincial and district membership includes regional executives, military commanders, police chiefs and prosecutors; it also replaces Inpres No.05/1967.","period":"1986-02-27","type":"Primary presidential decree, full text reviewed","source_ids":["S1432"],"confidence":"High for the decree’s text and date.","basis":"A formal framework does not prove a particular meeting addressed books or that local prosecutors acted before a national order. The arrangement predates the 1986 decree in earlier form.","next":"Locate provincial and district Muspida minutes and follow-up orders, including cases involving circulation of books and printed materials.","year":1986},{"id":"C2667","text":"ELSAM reports that local Muspida forums discussed circulation of books labeled dangerous as a security concern, and that local prosecutors sometimes acted on this basis before an Attorney General decision.","period":"1986 onward, retrospective account published 1999","type":"Secondary account of local enforcement practice","source_ids":["S1430","S1432"],"confidence":"High that ELSAM reports this practice; low to moderate for prevalence and exact cases absent local files.","basis":"The decree defines Muspida’s mandate but not this book-specific practice. No particular Muspida minutes, local seizure record or dated pre-national action was obtained.","next":"Find district/provincial meeting minutes, local prosecutor decisions, police inventories and bookshop/publisher records for named cases in ELSAM’s pp.35–36.","year":1986},{"id":"C2668","text":"ELSAM says Bakorstanas replaced Kopkamtib under Keppres 29/1988 and that a cross-agency book-review “clearing house” was formally established under Attorney General decision KEP-114/JA/10/1989 with 19 members. This formalized later review arrangements but came after the June 1986 KEP-041 and KEP-044 bans and cannot explain their procedure.","period":"1988-10 to 1989-10","type":"Retrospective institutional account; primary decision not inspected","source_ids":["S1430"],"confidence":"High that ELSAM reports this sequence; details of the 1989 body await primary-document verification.","basis":"The 1989 clearing house must not be projected backward as the formal procedure for the 1986 bans. No signed KEP-114/JA/10/1989 or Bakorstanas founding order was obtained.","next":"Obtain Keppres 29/1988 and KEP-114/JA/10/1989, membership lists, meeting minutes and case-review records.","year":1988},{"id":"C2669","text":"The official June 1986 orders name different administrative inputs: KEP-041 for the Amsterdam book cites a BAKIN chief opinion dated 11 April 1986, while KEP-044 for Crouch’s book cites a Department of Information recommendation dated 9 May 1986. This establishes multiple formal channels named in the orders, not the relative influence or full informal process behind either decision.","period":"1986-04-11 to 1986-06-18","type":"Cross-reading of two official decision scans","source_ids":["S1428","S1433"],"confidence":"High for the inputs each decree names; low for the causal role and informal contacts.","basis":"The underlying BAKIN and Department of Information memoranda, any associated security-coordination minutes and their evidence have not been obtained.","next":"Request both cited memoranda and trace their routing, attachments, recipients, review notes and implementation records.","year":1986},{"id":"C2670","text":"Hearman reports that former political prisoners smuggled copies of Gatot Lestario’s 26 December 1975 courtroom defense speech out of prison; she says it was later translated into Dutch, introduced by W.F. Wertheim, published in a booklet on the 1965–1966 abuses and retitled Indonesië klaagt aan in explicit reference to Sukarno’s 1930 defense.","period":"1975–1985; reported 2016","type":"Peer-reviewed retrospective account based on letters, archival materials and interviews","source_ids":["S1417"],"confidence":"High that Hearman’s article makes this account; moderate pending inspection of the Dutch booklet and the smuggling/translation records.","basis":"The 1985 Dutch booklet and underlying custody/transfer records were not inspected. The article’s wording does not settle whether the phrase is a separate title page, a heading for the plea, or an editorial retitling within the larger book.","next":"Inspect the full Stichting Indonesia Media book and compare title leaf, contents, plea heading, translator/editor notes, page span, source manuscript and cited interviews.","year":1975},{"id":"C2671","text":"Indonesië klaagt aan is also the alternate Dutch title recorded for a separate 1985 Indonesian-language edition of Sukarno’s defense Indonesia menggugat; CiNii identifies Sukarno as author and records 188 pages and three library holdings. This supports a title collision and does not identify Lestario’s plea as the same work.","period":"1930 defense; 1985 edition and Lestario booklet","type":"Union-catalog bibliographic distinction cross-checked against later scholarly account","source_ids":["S1417","S1434"],"confidence":"High for the CiNii bibliographic record; moderate for Hearman’s stated deliberate allusion until the Lestario booklet is inspected.","basis":"The CiNii entry describes a separate Sukarno edition; it does not describe the title page or contents of Lestario’s Dutch plea.","next":"Compare both physical publications; record publisher, title leaf, contents, pagination and whether Indonesië klaagt aan appears as title, heading or subtitle in the Lestario volume.","year":1930},{"id":"C2672","text":"A specialist private bibliography lists Gatot Lestario’s plea within the 1985 booklet with W.F. Wertheim and Peter Dale Scott; an antiquarian dealer lists that same title but gives 35 pages, while Scott’s bibliography places his essay within it at pp.170–235. The page-count descriptions conflict and do not establish the actual length of the Lestario plea or edition.","period":"1985 publication metadata","type":"Bibliographic discrepancy across catalog/finding-aid and sales listing","source_ids":["S1429","S1435","S1436"],"confidence":"High that the listings conflict; low for which pagination statement or edition is correct until a copy is inspected.","basis":"Peter Schumacher’s direct page timed out and only its indexed text was available; Ardjoeno is a bookseller listing. Scott is a contributor and his own retrospective bibliography is not a physical-copy inspection.","next":"Locate a library or private copy; photograph title leaf, contents, colophon, and pages 1–235; determine whether different editions or separate pamphlets share the title.","year":1985},{"id":"C2673","text":"In a separate March 2001 interview, Maryono told André Liem that soldiers brought roughly forty men to the Ngrejo riverside and shot them; he recalled eight returned to a cave and thirty-two buried in two pits, named several victims from memory, and said he did not know whether the victims were PKI or BTI members.","period":"1968; Ngrejo / South Blitar, precise site unresolved","type":"Retrospective local witness testimony recorded in an oral-history chapter","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that the 2004 chapter records this separate interview; medium for the episode details and identities.","basis":"Printed pp.196–199; Maryono says “if I am not mistaken” about forty. He recalls Sani, Senen Parjono, Wagimin, Suro Kempleng, Gentong, Kamto and Sonto, but names are not cross-checked against civil or party records. Liem’s interview list dates Maryono to March 2001. This is a second retrospective witness within the same project as Paijo, not a contemporaneous independent record.","next":"Find the audio/transcript, confirm place-name variants and burial/cave site, seek relatives and missing-person registers, and compare with military transport/death/detention logs and archaeological evidence.","year":1968},{"id":"C2674","text":"A Blitar resident identified in Liem’s chapter as Harsono recalled that Chinese residents of Blitar city were assembled at Dipoyono cinema and told to support the authorities, especially the military, with money; he said vehicles were borrowed for operations and residents felt business and safety depended on contributions to Kodim.","period":"After October 1965; Blitar city, exact date and duration unspecified","type":"Retrospective ethnic-minority testimony quoted through a later oral-history chapter","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that Liem reproduces the account; low-to-moderate for the full scope and implementation absent other witnesses or records.","basis":"Printed pp.173–174 and footnote citing Jaringan Kerja Budaya, Kumpulan Hasil Diskusi Tahun 2000, pp.128–129; the underlying discussion volume, meeting recording, summons, receipts and vehicle logs were not separately inspected. Harsono says he was not a Baperki member. One account cannot establish a national policy, an exact number of people or payments, or that every Blitar Chinese resident complied.","next":"Locate the cited 2000 discussion volume and recording; identify Harsono and the meeting date; seek Kodim transport/fund accounts, business records and independent Blitar Chinese families’ testimony.","year":1965},{"id":"C2675","text":"Hearman reports that PKI members with agricultural training tried to share cultivation knowledge with South Blitar villagers and improve yields or diversify crops, including techniques suited to local soil and food conditions.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Secondary synthesis of retrospective accounts and agricultural/military materials","source_ids":["S1324"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports these activities; medium-to-low for their reach and results.","basis":"Printed pp.86–89; the article describes agricultural technicians and education in soil/fertilizer, crops and production. It is not an audited program list; no village-by-village participant roster, crop plot records or yield series were inspected. It does not establish that all projects were voluntary or that every cadre shared one approach.","next":"Inspect the article’s cited Rewang and Lies Sari sources, agricultural project and village records, crop/land data and testimony from participating and nonparticipating farmers.","year":1967},{"id":"C2676","text":"Hearman reports that military intelligence, relying on information from local NU allies, alleged that PKI fugitives compelled villagers to grow and supply food as a form of “taxation.”","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Security allegation as reported in later scholarship","source_ids":["S1324"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports the allegation; low that coercive taxation occurred as described.","basis":"Printed p.90 and note 45. The article attributes the information to military intelligence and local NU allies, not a directly identified household witness or contemporaneous tax register. Crop-extension efforts and food-provisioning work are also documented; this allegation cannot be used to characterize them all as forced extraction.","next":"Find the underlying military intelligence report and named sources; compare village testimony, crop transfers, party instructions, food ledgers and evidence of coercion or voluntary exchange.","year":1967},{"id":"C2677","text":"Hearman says Brawijaya commander Muhammad Jasin told the East Java regional parliament that a reforestation project existed in South Blitar under BTI leader Djadi Wirjosubroto.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar / East Java regional parliament","type":"Later scholarly report of a military commander’s parliamentary statement","source_ids":["S1324"],"confidence":"High that the article reports the statement; medium for its exact wording and project details.","basis":"Printed p.90 and note 46. The parliament transcript and referenced military report were not obtained. This establishes what Hearman says Jasin reported, not the project’s full size, coercion, outputs or village participation.","next":"Obtain East Java parliament minutes and the cited Brawijaya file; identify project dates, leadership, sites, labor terms and relation to pre-1965 BTI agricultural programs.","year":1967},{"id":"C2678","text":"Hearman reports that during the 1968 operation villages were evacuated and residents held in camps such as Bakung to obstruct food reaching fugitives; a pseudonymous witness, Zainab, recalled that despite promised camp kitchens and field hospitals residents had to bring their own food and became hungry enough to eat raw sweet potatoes and other crops.","period":"1968; South Blitar / Bakung holding camps","type":"Oral-history testimony and later historian’s reconstruction","source_ids":["S1324","S1310"],"confidence":"High that the article records the account and gives this explanation of camp policy; medium for details pending camp registers and independent testimony.","basis":"Hearman, printed pp.82–83 and 91–92; Zainab is a pseudonym interviewed decades later. The policy purpose is Hearman’s reconstruction, supported by cited military material; no evacuation order, camp roster, ration register or field-hospital log was directly inspected. S1310 and S1324 are the same author’s overlapping research program.","next":"Find the cited military operation pages, Zainab interview record if access permits, village evacuation lists, Bakung camp registers, ration/medical logs and testimony from other camps and villages.","year":1968},{"id":"C2679","text":"Hearman reports that Zainab, a pseudonymous South Blitar resident, returned after evacuation to find rice, a goat and chickens missing from her household; she attributed this to military occupation of the home.","period":"1968; South Blitar after village evacuation","type":"Pseudonymous retrospective oral testimony reproduced in a scholarly article","source_ids":["S1324"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports the testimony; medium for the individual household loss and its actor attribution without an inventory or independent witness.","basis":"Printed p.92. This is one household account and does not quantify looting or establish uniform military conduct across villages. The original interview has not been reviewed.","next":"Locate interview notes/audio, household and village records, and testimony from neighboring families; identify the unit present in the area during the relevant period.","year":1968},{"id":"C2680","text":"Hearman reports that Zainab had to report daily to authorities for several months after returning and cook or perform household chores for soldiers posted in her village.","period":"1968 onward; South Blitar","type":"Pseudonymous retrospective oral testimony reproduced in a scholarly article","source_ids":["S1324"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports the account; medium for the duration and routine pending independent testimony or reporting records.","basis":"Printed p.92. The interview account is retrospective and identifies Zainab by pseudonym; no reporting cards, village orders, duty rosters or officer identities were inspected.","next":"Find the interview source and village daily-report records, identify the local military post and compare other residents’ accounts.","year":1968},{"id":"C2681","text":"Hearman interprets the New Order’s response to South Blitar crop projects as a contest over rural political legitimacy and argues that the PKI was unlikely to have been a de facto government because she estimates the fugitive cadre population at no more than about 150.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar; interpretation published 2016","type":"Author’s causal and scale interpretation","source_ids":["S1324"],"confidence":"High that Hearman advances this interpretation; low-to-moderate for the fugitive headcount and causal weight absent rosters and policy records.","basis":"Printed pp.90–91. Her under-150 figure is an estimate, not a verified census. The legitimacy interpretation is the author’s analysis; it neither establishes that the PKI projects had broad control nor resolves their material effects.","next":"Find village and party rosters, military assessments and local administrative records; compare resource allocation, support, coercion, food access and state policies without treating the author’s interpretation as a final political judgment.","year":1967},{"id":"C2682","text":"Komnas HAM’s Ad Hoc team says it worked from 1 June 2008 to 30 April 2012, examined 349 witnesses/victims and visited some regions, but could not investigate every region or hear every related witness because the events covered a vast geography and the team lacked sufficient budget and faced the passage of time.","period":"2008-06-01 to 2012-04-30; report issued 2012-07-23","type":"Official retrospective investigation’s own methodology and limitation statement","source_ids":["S859"],"confidence":"High for the report’s stated mandate, count and limitations; not a representative sample of national victims.","basis":"Executive summary printed pp.2–3, directly checked in Indonesian. The 349 is the number of examined witness/victim statements, not an estimate of the total affected population. The underlying BAPs, sampling rules and full item-level geography were not inspected.","next":"Obtain the full investigation report and accessible BAPs; map interview location and event location separately; establish how names, duplicates, ethnicity and violation categories were recorded.","year":2008},{"id":"C2683","text":"Komnas HAM’s executive summary describes October 1965 terror in Makassar against PKI members or people considered PKI-related and also ethnic Chinese who were identified with the PRC; it provides no national count or victim-ethnicity percentage for this episode.","period":"1965-10; Makassar and surrounding area; report published 2012","type":"Official retrospective account based on witness evidence; regional scope","source_ids":["S859"],"confidence":"High that the report states this; medium for individual incidents pending underlying witness BAPs and local records.","basis":"The report’s regional narrative identifies a perceived association with the PRC, not proof that all ethnic Chinese shared a political affiliation. Search of the report found no national ethnicity-coded victim table; its local narrative cannot establish national proportions.","next":"Read the relevant witness BAPs and Makassar command, police, detention, property and burial records; identify victims by person and self-identified/community identity without inferring affiliation from ethnicity.","year":1965},{"id":"C2684","text":"Hearman reports that a pseudonymous woman, Suginem, was widowed during the South Blitar operation and was forced by a soldier deployed to her village who later served as village head to meet his sexual demands; the article says her child became severely malnourished.","period":"1968; South Blitar after the Trisula operation","type":"Pseudonymous retrospective oral testimony reproduced in a scholarly article","source_ids":["S1324"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports the account; medium for individual circumstances pending the original interview and independent records.","basis":"Printed pp.92–93 and note 63. Suginem is a pseudonym. The article provides a later oral-history account; no original interview, medical record, complaint, appointment order or village register was inspected. This is an individual reported case, not evidence of uniform conduct by every soldier or village head.","next":"Locate the interview notes/audio and consent metadata; identify the appointed village head and unit from local records; seek family testimony, medical and village records and independent reports of sexual coercion.","year":1968},{"id":"C2685","text":"Liem reports, citing the Brawijaya military history, that the military replaced village administrators in South Blitar with 234 ABRI caretakers as part of efforts to weaken local support for the underground and collect information.","period":"1968; South Blitar / Trisula counterinsurgency","type":"Later oral-history chapter quoting an official military history","source_ids":["S1317","S1390"],"confidence":"High that Liem reports the figure and cited source; medium-to-low for the exact number, period and full replacement coverage without the roster.","basis":"Liem, printed p.182 and note 17, cites Kodam VIII/Brawijaya, Operasi Trisula, p.243. The military book and its 234-name list have not been directly opened. A registered count does not prove that all appointments were implemented simultaneously or in every village.","next":"Obtain Operasi Trisula p.243 and the underlying caretaker roster; compare appointment dates, villages, prior pamong, military posts, and village administrative records.","year":1968},{"id":"C2686","text":"Farmer Ruslan recalled that his village-head uncle ordered him and other local men to carry packs and supplies for an Army group of about forty soldiers while it walked between Tumpakoyot, Ngrejo, Bakung and Sumberdadi to map the area.","period":"Before Trisula; South Blitar, exact date unspecified","type":"Retrospective civilian oral testimony about compelled military logistics and reconnaissance","source_ids":["S1317"],"confidence":"High that Liem records this account; medium for troop size, route and exact purpose.","basis":"Liem, printed p.182, separately describes the movement as an Army survey/mapping trip and records Ruslan’s “if I am not mistaken” estimate of forty soldiers. No patrol diary, map, requisition slip or village order was obtained; the named route points and district boundaries require verification.","next":"Find Ruslan’s interview recording/transcript, village-head records, military patrol maps and route journals; identify the date/unit and compare other residents’ accounts of carrying, provisioning and intelligence mapping.","year":null},{"id":"C2687","text":"Rewang retrospectively blamed impatience and “shortcuts” among some PKI leaders for premature armed activity: he said unfinished, dispersed shelters and party work were overtaken by local weapons seizures and attacks, which alerted military authorities and preceded encirclement and defeat.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar; retrospective account published 2004","type":"Participant’s retrospective internal causal diagnosis","source_ids":["S1317","S1384","S1385"],"confidence":"High that Rewang makes this analysis; medium-to-low as a complete causal explanation of the underground’s defeat.","basis":"Liem, printed pp.179–181, records Rewang discussing Vietnam-inspired long-term organization, “shortcuts,” arms seizure and the resulting military operation. This account is retrospective and comes from one former leader; it does not establish that every cadre agreed, that every raid caused discovery, or that organizational weakness alone explains defeat. Compare with local actions, military intelligence, capture records and broader repression.","next":"Find the cited Rewang memoir and original interview, dated weapons seizures and attacks, military intelligence/encirclement orders, captured correspondence and independent villagers’ accounts; weigh the leadership critique against state force, local support and operational constraints.","year":1967},{"id":"C2688","text":"The reported number of ABRI caretaker village officials in South Blitar in 1968 differs by source: Liem cites 234 registered caretakers from the 1969 Brawijaya history, while the 1978 government history reports 144, citing a 1975 Blitar regency booklet.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Cross-source discrepancy in retrospective official counts","source_ids":["S1317","S1390","S1437"],"confidence":"High that the two publications give these figures; low-to-medium that they count the same territory, posts and date range.","basis":"Liem, printed p.182 note 17, cites Semdam VIII/Brawijaya, Operasi Trisula, p.243 for 234. Sejarah Daerah Jawa Timur, printed p.269 note 574, cites Pemkab Blitar, Selayang Pandang Blitar Selatan (1975), p.1 for 144. Neither underlying roster/booklet was obtained; the figures may differ by date, geographic scope or definition, or one may be wrong.","next":"Obtain both cited books and appointment registers; identify dates, villages, caretaker roles, totals by subdistrict, and whether the number is people appointed, positions filled or cumulative replacements.","year":1967},{"id":"C2689","text":"The 1978 East Java history reports that a civil territorial-development post was established in Bakung on 20 May 1968 under Blitar regent Sanoesi Prawirodihardjo, with a “3P” remit: education, food and infrastructure.","period":"1968-05-20; Bakung, South Blitar","type":"Government-sponsored history citing a local regency publication","source_ids":["S1437"],"confidence":"High that the 1978 book reports this date, chair and remit; medium for the original order and actual implementation.","basis":"Sejarah Daerah Jawa Timur, printed p.269, note 574 cites Pemkab Blitar, Selayang Pandang Blitar Selatan (1975), p.1. That booklet, appointment order, budget and daily reports were not obtained. The post was part of a stated civil-territorial program conducted alongside intelligence operations, not proof that all services were delivered as described.","next":"Obtain the 1975 booklet and the Bakung post’s founding order, staffing roster, supply/budget records, monthly reports and village-level implementation files.","year":1968},{"id":"C2690","text":"Sejarah Daerah Jawa Timur (1978) dates the start of Operation Trisula to 31 May 1968 and says it lasted about two months; it then distinguishes territorial rehabilitation/stabilization through 7 September from civil territorial guidance through 16 December 1968.","period":"1968-05-31 to 1968-12-16; East Java / South Blitar","type":"Official retrospective chronology with named military and local source citations","source_ids":["S1310","S1384","S1410","S1437"],"confidence":"High that the 1978 book states these phases; medium for their exact operational boundaries and coverage.","basis":"Printed p.269 dates Trisula to 31 May; p.270 describes an approximately two-month operation, then a territorial phase through 7 September and a civil-territorial phase through 16 December. Hearman describes the named counterinsurgency as beginning in June and formally ending 7 September; Leksana cites 31 May for the task-force launch. These labels may describe formation, combat, territorial consolidation and formal closure; no authenticated order set or after-action calendar has resolved the stages.","next":"Inspect the 1969 Semdam history, order and phase logs, 1973 presidential accountability address, Trisula monument inscription and local posts’ diaries; distinguish task-force formation, first combat, “physical” phase, handover and ceremonial closure.","year":1968},{"id":"C2691","text":"The 1978 official history describes South Blitar as the East Java underground’s strongest and best-armed Kompro, claims it had many ABRI fugitives/escapees, says two Detga and 17 Gerda were organized by early 1968, and attributes STPR/KKPR training in South Malang with cadres circulated across East Java.","period":"1967–early 1968; East Java / South Blitar","type":"Official retrospective account relaying military-history claims","source_ids":["S1317","S1376","S1390","S1437"],"confidence":"High that the 1978 book makes these claims; low-to-medium for actual strength, armament, school operation and numbers.","basis":"Sejarah Daerah Jawa Timur, printed pp.266–267 notes 566–567 cites Semdam VIII/Brawijaya, Operasi Trisula, pp.5 and 8–11. It calls the Kompro strongest and sufficiently armed; Rewang and Muyatno’s later accounts to Liem and Munir’s 1973 defense instead describe dispersed, underprepared formations and say no regular TPR existed. Those statements concern different organizational categories and vantage points; no authenticated roster, weapon inventory, course register or school record is available.","next":"Obtain the cited military-history pages, captured Kompro documents, original Perjuta/training manuals, Detga/Gerda rosters, weapons inventory, training attendance and independent accounts from local recruits and residents.","year":1967},{"id":"C2692","text":"The 1978 history reports that Brawijaya commander M. Jasin said at a Malang press conference on 9 August 1968 that 850 people classified as PKI detainees had been detained by then and that 37 firearms and four grenades had been seized.","period":"By 1968-08-09; Trisula operation","type":"Retrospective government history quoting a commander’s press-conference report","source_ids":["S1393","S1437"],"confidence":"High that the 1978 book reports these numbers; medium-to-low for the underlying count, classification and completeness.","basis":"Sejarah Daerah Jawa Timur, printed p.270 note 576 cites M. Jasin’s 9 August 1968 press conference as printed in the third edition of Sketsmasa, Operasi Trisula, p.14. The pamphlet, press transcript, detainee register and seizure ledger remain uninspected. “850 detainees” is not a death toll or a complete final operation total.","next":"Obtain Sketsmasa p.14 and the original press transcript; compare the figure with custody registers, camp lists, release/transfer/death records and the itemized seizure ledger.","year":1968},{"id":"C2693","text":"The 1978 history claims that South Blitar residents “practically sided” with the PKI and were instructed to use a “GTM/T-3” response—“do not know, do not know [the person], do not understand”—when questioned by government officials.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Official military-political allegation reproduced in a government history","source_ids":["S1317","S1393","S1437"],"confidence":"High that the book makes the allegation; low-to-medium that this represented an organized instruction across the population.","basis":"Sejarah Daerah Jawa Timur, printed p.267 note 568 cites Jasin’s 9 August press-conference account as reproduced in Sketsmasa’s 1969 third edition, pp.12–13. No text of the alleged instruction, distribution list, military interrogation records, or village-level testimony verifying its breadth was obtained. Liem reports residents providing warnings/support in some cases and a farmer compelled to carry military supplies in another; neither set can represent all residents.","next":"Obtain the conference transcript and pamphlet pages; search captured papers for GTM/T-3 instructions and compare with independent interviews, interrogation transcripts and village records from different sites.","year":1967},{"id":"C2694","text":"The 1978 government history categorizes South Blitar Kompro activity as “armed propaganda” and alleges robbery, kidnapping and retaliatory killings in Blitar and Tulungagung.","period":"Late 1967–1968; South Blitar and Tulungagung","type":"Official military-history allegation repeated in a retrospective government history","source_ids":["S1317","S1390","S1437"],"confidence":"High that the 1978 book reports the allegation; low-to-medium on incident-by-incident attribution, authorization and casualty details.","basis":"Sejarah Daerah Jawa Timur, printed p.267 note 569 cites Semdam VIII/Brawijaya, Operasi Trisula, pp.8–11. Rewang later described some attacks and disarmament as local actions beyond Kompro authorization; Munir’s legal defense characterized named 1968 encounters as compelled self-defense. Neither the military account nor those retrospective statements provide a complete incident ledger.","next":"Identify incidents, dates, victims, suspects, weapons and command links from the military book, police/district reports, court files, local burial records, detainee testimony and party correspondence.","year":1967},{"id":"C2695","text":"The 1978 history cites a “Pedoman Aksi Kota” attributed to Datong Sugiarto and others and dated 10 November 1968 as evidence of a planned mass disruption in Surabaya for March 1968.","period":"Alleged plan: 1968-03; cited document date: 1968-11-10","type":"Retrospective source chronology anomaly / official allegation","source_ids":["S1393","S1437"],"confidence":"High that the 1978 book makes this claim; low that the later-dated document proves a March plan existed before Trisula.","basis":"Sejarah Daerah Jawa Timur, printed pp.267–268 says the mass action was planned for March 1968, but describes the cited document as made on 10 November 1968; note 570 points to Sketsmasa’s 1969 third edition, p.34. The underlying document and pamphlet page are not available. Unless the date or characterization is wrong, the source postdates the alleged plan and cannot establish what authorities knew before the May–June operation.","next":"Obtain the original “Pedoman Aksi Kota,” the 1969 pamphlet p.34 and any capture/interrogation report; authenticate authorship, document date, intended action date, provenance and whether the plan was retrospective or misdated.","year":1968},{"id":"C2696","text":"The December 1968 CIA study reconstructs the first planning meeting on 6 September 1965 at Captain Wahjudi’s home: it says Sjam introduced the Generals’ Council threat and proposed a new government/Revolutionary Council, while the officers asked how the Council would be formed and who would lead it.","period":"1965-09-04 to 1965-09-06; Jakarta","type":"Retrospective intelligence reconstruction based on participant interrogations","source_ids":["S251"],"confidence":"High that the CIA report gives this account; low-to-moderate for the actual meeting details and their completeness.","basis":"CIA study printed pp.109–113, especially its account of 4 Sep contact and 6 Sep meeting. The foreword states that its account of the conspiracy derives entirely from interrogations of participants; no authenticated meeting notes, participant’s contemporary diary or complete uncoerced transcript was checked. The CIA’s account does not establish that the full Politburo or PKI Central Committee authorized the operation.","next":"Obtain the cited Latief/Untung/Wahjudi interrogation and trial records, compare their wording and timing, search Sjam/Pono and military correspondence, and seek any surviving Central Committee or Special Bureau minutes.","year":1965},{"id":"C2697","text":"The 1968 CIA study reports that Subandrio’s interrogation record attributed to Zhou Enlai a January 1965 offer of 100,000 free light arms for a proposed “Fifth Force”; it also says Omar Dani’s mid-September Beijing mission concerned final arrangements, but explicitly states that the arms-to-G30S connection is unclear.","period":"1965-01 to 1965-09; Jakarta and Beijing","type":"Intelligence report about an alleged foreign arms offer and negotiations","source_ids":["S251","S134"],"confidence":"High that the CIA report makes and qualifies this claim; low-to-moderate for the offer/near-final deal as described because the cited Indonesian statement is an interrogation record and direct Chinese approvals, transport and receipts remain absent.","basis":"CIA study printed pp.130–131 and note to p.131 attributes the offer to Subandrio’s interrogation report; it says Dani returned from Beijing on 19 Sep after a secret trip and reports discussion of 100,000 arms. Zhou’s separate study (S134; C118–C119), citing Chinese MFA records and interviews, distinguishes offer, Indonesian Air Force request for 25,000, negotiations and the absence of proof that the large shipment arrived before G30S. Neither study establishes weapon-by-weapon receipt or use.","next":"Obtain Subandrio and Dani trial/interrogation records, the 17 Sep Zhou–Dani minutes if released, Indonesian Air Force request/receipt files, Chinese authorization, aircraft/ship manifests, customs or airfield records, serial numbers and receiving-unit ledgers.","year":1965},{"id":"C2698","text":"A 30 November 1965 U.S. Embassy cable reports that Polish First Secretary Andrzej Gradziuk attributed to an unnamed PKI Central Committee contact the claim that only a small group headed by Aidit knew of the September 30 plan and that its idea originated outside the PKI, possibly with Sukarno.","period":"1965-10-01 account reported to U.S. Embassy on 1965-11-30","type":"Contemporaneous diplomatic report of layered hearsay","source_ids":["S1438"],"confidence":"High that the Embassy cable records this report; low for the underlying statement and source attribution.","basis":"RG 84, Entry P 339, Box 14, Folder 7; scan pages 1–3 visually checked. The alleged PKI source is unnamed, the cable does not provide a signed statement or interview transcript, and the Polish diplomat is reporting an account to U.S. Embassy staff. The U.S. reporting officer explicitly raises possible fabrication.","next":"Seek the Polish Embassy’s original notes and Ministry of Foreign Affairs dispatches, identify the PKI contact if possible, and compare with Aidit/Sjam/Politburo records, participant testimony and contemporaneous party communications.","year":1965},{"id":"C2699","text":"In the same reported account, the unnamed PKI contact allegedly said participants agreed to a limited operation to seize selected generals without killing them and expected Sukarno to make a public statement on 1 October.","period":"1965-09 to 1965-10-01; reported 1965-11-30","type":"Reported intention, hearsay","source_ids":["S1438"],"confidence":"High that the cable records the claim; low that it accurately represents the planners’ actual collective intention.","basis":"The account passes from an unnamed PKI CC contact to Polish First Secretary Gradziuk and then to the U.S. Embassy; the Embassy explicitly says the account may have been doctored. It conflicts in form with public actions and later interrogation accounts but cannot be resolved by treating either public statement or interrogation as transparent access to private intent.","next":"Find the Polish source notes and compare against the exact movement orders, operational plans, records for the capture teams, contemporaneous statements and uncoerced participant accounts.","year":1965},{"id":"C2700","text":"The U.S. Embassy author of Telegram 1025 A explicitly considered that Gradziuk’s account might have been “doctored” and noted uncertainty about whether it nevertheless contained useful information.","period":"1965-11-30; Jakarta","type":"Contemporaneous source-quality assessment","source_ids":["S1438"],"confidence":"High for the wording and existence of the caveat in the scanned cable.","basis":"The last page of the linked telegram includes the author’s comment that the story may have been designed to solicit Embassy opinion and that the source may have been fabricated, while also noting the diplomat’s prior relationship with the source. This is the U.S. officer’s assessment, not independent proof either that the story was true or that it was doctored.","next":"Identify the Embassy drafter and the cited contact history; search Polish, Soviet and Indonesian records for independent documentation of the same account.","year":1965},{"id":"C2701","text":"John Roosa reports that Abdul Latief later testified that he signed the 25 October 1965 military-police interrogation record while severely wounded, medicated, immobilized and barely conscious after capture.","period":"1965-10-11 capture; 1965-10-25 interrogation; later 1978 trial account","type":"Scholarly report of a defendant’s later statement about interrogation conditions","source_ids":["S1439"],"confidence":"High that Roosa reports the later testimony and cites the relevant records; moderate for the precise conditions pending direct review of the original BAP and trial file.","basis":"Roosa, Indonesia no.85, pp.46–48 and notes 53–58; he says he had a copy of the 25 Oct military-police BAP and cites Latief’s 1978 testimony and 2000 printed plea. The 1965 BAP, complete 1978 court record and medical/custody records were not independently accessed in this pass.","next":"Retrieve the original BAP, 1978 hearing transcript, Latief plea, injury/medical and custody records, and the 21/23 Dec 1965 follow-up interrogations.","year":1965},{"id":"C2702","text":"Roosa says that Latief’s 21 and 23 December 1965 follow-up interrogation records reversed the October 25 statement: Latief denied PKI affiliation and denied that the PKI led the September 30 Movement; Roosa says the New Order official account did not cite those later records.","period":"1965-10-25 to 1965-12-23; later publication","type":"Scholarly comparison of contradictory interrogation records","source_ids":["S1439","S251"],"confidence":"High that Roosa reports the contradiction and that the 1968 CIA study foregrounds the earlier interrogation-based narrative; moderate for the precise contents pending direct comparison of all records.","basis":"Roosa, pp.46–49, notes 58–68, cites KOPKAMTIB Team Pemeriksa Pusat records dated 21 and 23 Dec 1965; the original records were not opened. The CIA study was published before those records’ evidentiary history was resolved and states its planning account rests on interrogations. The differing statements cannot be adjudicated without originals and custody context.","next":"Obtain both December records, compare them line by line with 25 Oct and public 1978 testimony; inspect publication citations and trial exhibits to establish which versions were used, omitted or challenged.","year":1965},{"id":"C2703","text":"Hearman reports that some South Blitar fugitives organized three-person cells with separate responsibility for organization, education/outreach, and armed struggle; Tuti worked in a cell’s outreach role seeking former party members and supporters.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Scholarly reconstruction of underground cell organization","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that the article records these interview accounts; moderate for the cell structure’s extent and the division’s consistency across the base.","basis":"Hearman, printed pp.80–81 and notes 100–102, draws on interviews with Winata and Tuti. The third cell member and a contemporaneous roster are not identified; the article says exact activities are difficult to reconstruct beyond regrouping supporters.","next":"Obtain the original ISSI interview recordings/transcripts, underground contact reports and any surviving cell rosters.","year":1967},{"id":"C2704","text":"Hearman reports that the South Blitar network prepared limited weapons-handling training; a military operation history she cites says two captured youth detachments yielded 34 firearms, some old, and several blowpipes, and Hearman assesses the armed capacity as embryonic and short of trainers, weapons and ammunition.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Scholarly report citing a New Order military operational history","source_ids":["S1310","S1390"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports the number and her assessment; low-to-moderate as a complete inventory of weapons or training across South Blitar.","basis":"Hearman, pp.80–81 and notes 103–106, cites the 1969 Brawijaya history pp.27, 34. The military inventory is a report by the operation’s own institution; no complete seizure ledger or independent arms survey was inspected. “34” should not be treated as a comprehensive PKI inventory.","next":"Obtain Operasi Trisula pp.27 and 34, original seizure records, serials, custody logs, training rosters and local arms procurement evidence.","year":1967},{"id":"C2705","text":"In a 1973 defense statement, PKI leader Mohammad Munir attributed the South Blitar defeat partly to rushing to rebuild the party and exposing its presence before preparations for battle were mature.","period":"1967–1973; South Blitar","type":"Participant’s later courtroom explanation quoted by a scholar","source_ids":["S1310"],"confidence":"High that Hearman quotes this argument; moderate-to-low as a complete causal explanation of the defeat.","basis":"Hearman, printed p.82, quotes Munir’s 1973 defense statement. It is a participant’s retrospective and legal argument; no complete original speech, command minutes or independent operational timeline was checked.","next":"Retrieve Munir’s full 1973 defense statement and compare its account with Rewang’s interviews, local attack records, arrests, informant reports and the military’s chronology.","year":1967},{"id":"C2706","text":"Ian Baird’s 2025 article describes the Communist Party of Thailand’s 1961 shift toward rural work and covert base preparation, followed by armed fighting in 1965 and development of northern strongholds by 1967–68, with Hmong and Lua communities joining in those highland areas.","period":"Thailand, 1961–1968","type":"Peer-reviewed secondary chronology with oral-history and ethnographic framing","source_ids":["S1441","S1313","S1321"],"confidence":"High that the article states this sequence; moderate for exact national chronology and the size/representativeness of minority recruitment.","basis":"Baird’s article cites prior histories for the strategic and military chronology and is centered on a later 1970s Hmong/Lua history project and one veteran’s account. The first-clash date conflicts with sources giving 7 August rather than 8 August 1965; CPT records were not inspected.","next":"Compare the article’s cited 1961/1965 records with Thai regional military reports, CPT histories, PLAT records, Voice of the People of Thailand broadcasts and Hmong/Lua community histories.","year":1961},{"id":"C2707","text":"An FCO 168/2193 chart reproduced as a one-page scan depicts the SEAMU coordinator’s link to political, diplomatic and psychological-warfare offices; it lists a black Indonesian newsletter, Indonesia research, PSYOPS services, contacts and weekly meetings between intelligence collectors and users as stated functions or working methods.","period":"British information operations, mid-1960s; chart undated","type":"Contemporary institutional diagram in later archival reproduction","source_ids":["S1440"],"confidence":"High for the labels and organizational relationships visible on the chart; low for whether every stated meeting or function was carried out in practice.","basis":"The chart lists counter-Confrontation exploitation of diplomatic information, guidance to SEAMU/PSYOPS, liaison with British missions, and collector/user roles; the reproduced image gives 1965/1966 budget estimates excluding salaries. It is not a meeting log, distribution list or activity return, and the original TNA file was not directly retrieved.","next":"Request the full FCO 168/2193 file and meeting minutes, staffing/payroll, tasking papers, station reports, newsletter dispatch receipts and PSYOPS activity logs.","year":1960},{"id":"C2708","text":"Telegram 1863 of 8 October 1965 assigned the Information Research Department responsibility for proposed overt outlets, said separate instructions would cover the clandestine “black” channel, and asked “Peck” to consult Kuala Lumpur/BGRAI about how far suitable Malaysians should be brought into confidence.","period":"1965-10-08; Singapore, Kuala Lumpur and London","type":"British information-operation coordination instruction","source_ids":["S573"],"confidence":"High for the telegram’s instructions; no evidence in this source that Malaysian personnel agreed or acted.","basis":"FCO 168/1668, pp.1–2, visually checked. The document outlines internal tasking, not an executed media schedule or confirmed Malaysian participation; the scan is hosted by a documentary reproduction site.","next":"Locate the separately referenced black-channel instructions, Peck’s meeting notes, KL/BGRAI files, broadcast logs and any receipt or posting records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2709","text":"Sudisman’s September 1966 Politburo self-criticism says the Party had not achieved a shared decision about the principal form of struggle; the issue remained with a small leadership/cadre circle, leaving most Party members passive on a strategic question.","period":"1951–September 1966","type":"Post-defeat internal party self-criticism","source_ids":["S1331"],"confidence":"High that the reproduced text makes this diagnosis; moderate-to-low as an independently verified account of all internal deliberations.","basis":"“Pokok-pokok kelemahan” section; Indonesian text and selected pages of the 1971 collection checked. The text does not name all participants or establish what each leader privately believed.","next":"Find Politburo/CC minutes, draft versions, cadre-school papers, provincial reports and correspondence on armed strategy; compare with Sudisman’s 1967 defense, Supardjo’s report and oral histories.","year":1951},{"id":"C2710","text":"The same self-criticism says some party leaders used separate political and organizational channels outside Politburo/CC control; competent collective bodies sometimes approved steps already taken and did so with incomplete information.","period":"PKI organization before September 1965; assessment written September 1966","type":"Internal party diagnosis of decision procedures","source_ids":["S1331"],"confidence":"High that the document says this; moderate as a description of scope and frequency, since no decision register or named cases accompanies the general charge.","basis":"“Pokok-pokok kesalahan organisasi” section, selected reprint pages checked. The text does not identify which channels or actions it means; it cannot by itself prove that a particular G30S order bypassed a specified body.","next":"Identify named decisions, channels and participants in original CC/Politburo records; compare C393, C2317–C2318, C2400 and G30S trial/interrogation files.","year":1965},{"id":"C2711","text":"The September 1966 text criticizes PKI’s long reliance on legal-parliamentary activity as its principal path and says the Party did not prepare members and mass organizations for a violent counterattack; it treats the post-1951 emphasis on legality as organizational vulnerability.","period":"1951–September 1966; retrospective diagnosis","type":"Internal party strategic and organizational self-criticism","source_ids":["S1331","S260","S261"],"confidence":"High that the self-criticism states this; low-to-moderate as a complete causal explanation of the Party’s collapse. Sudisman’s 1967 courtroom statement separately denies that the Party as an institution decided G30S, so the two documents must be compared in their different settings.","basis":"Text sections on the peaceful/parliamentary path and organizational legalism, reprint pages checked; compare Sudisman’s detained-defendant speech. The statement does not establish that legal mass work was inherently a mistake or that earlier arms preparation could have defeated the Army.","next":"Compare PKI statutes/congress resolutions, 1956/1959/1962/1965 records, provincial security plans, army repression plans, mass-organization instruction and internal debate before/after 1 October.","year":1951},{"id":"C2712","text":"The 1966 self-criticism says rapid membership expansion was prioritized beyond the Party’s capacity to educate and supervise new members; it repeats a Party figure of more than three million members and says cadre development drew senior organizers toward state/semi-state posts and away from rural work.","period":"Late 1950s–1965, as assessed in September 1966","type":"Internal party organizational self-criticism; membership figure is the document’s own reported figure","source_ids":["S1331"],"confidence":"High that the document reports these points; moderate-to-low for the exact membership count and national representativeness because no audited roll is supplied.","basis":"The text contrasts membership plan targets and cadre training and says village cadres were drawn toward cities/central offices. These are politically charged post-defeat diagnoses by surviving leaders, not a membership census or independent employment survey.","next":"Obtain annual member rolls and branch returns, party-plan reports, cadre placement lists, education records, organizational statutes and provincial mass-organization records.","year":1950},{"id":"C2713","text":"The September 1966 program calls for the party to operate wholly clandestinely, rebuild rural bases and prepare armed peasant struggle; this marks the surviving leadership’s post-defeat program and should not be projected backward as proof that the pre-1965 legal PKI already had an operational guerrilla plan.","period":"September 1966 onward","type":"Post-defeat party programmatic statement","source_ids":["S1331","S1310","S1317"],"confidence":"High for the program’s stated direction; low for how far it was implemented nationally. South Blitar’s later cell, arms and authorization evidence remains localized and contested.","basis":"“Jalan Keluar” section; compare pre-G30S party practice and later South Blitar interviews. Do not infer from ideological declaration that countrywide armed structures existed.","next":"Find distribution/circulation records for the 1966 document, subsequent central/provincial directives and evidence of implementation outside East Java.","year":1966},{"id":"C2714","text":"A CIA-hosted 2016 retrospective article by Bob Bergin distinguishes the Communist Party of Thailand’s 1952 declared commitment to armed struggle from the onset of an insurgency in 1965; Baird’s 2025 account separately describes a 1961 shift toward covert rural organizing and base preparation. These are distinct stages, not necessarily contradictory dates.","period":"Thailand, 1952–1965; retrospective accounts published 2016 and 2025","type":"Cross-source chronology reconciliation","source_ids":["S1442","S1441"],"confidence":"High that the two articles present these stages; moderate for the precise chronology pending CPT congress records and contemporaneous Thai incident files.","basis":"Bergin, Studies in Intelligence 60(2), pp.25–26, says doctrine commitment 1952 but insurgency began 1965; Baird gives the post-1961 rural transition. Neither opened a CPT congress minute or complete operational log.","next":"Retrieve CPT congress resolutions and regional instructions, and Thai police/military incident records for 1961–65; distinguish strategic declaration, cadre deployment, first clash and sustained insurgency.","year":1952},{"id":"C2715","text":"Bergin’s 2016 article reports, citing a former senior Thai intelligence official, that CPT membership probably did not exceed about 2,500 at its peak and that the organization used small, highly secretive cells with much of its leadership hidden in the jungle or China.","period":"Thailand, mainly 1960s–1980s; retrospective report","type":"Retrospective security-service estimate and organizational assessment","source_ids":["S1442"],"confidence":"High that Bergin reports this estimate and description; low-to-moderate for the number and its definition because it is not based on a published roster and “member” is not defined.","basis":"Bergin pp.26–27 relies on interviews with former Thai intelligence leaders and cites the party’s secrecy as a collection difficulty. Do not compare the number directly with PKI mass membership, armed combatants or the U.S. 1968 NIE categories.","next":"Find CPT membership estimates by year, internal cadre/branch records, Thai arrest and defector rosters and independent regional oral histories.","year":1960},{"id":"C2716","text":"Bergin reports that Chinese support to the CPT included some finance, arms and ammunition, leadership training in Beijing and VOPT broadcasts said by Thai intelligence to originate in Kunming; the same account describes the CPT as substantially self-financed and dependent on captured arms.","period":"Thailand, early 1960s onward; retrospective accounts","type":"Retrospective foreign-assistance and broadcast-channel report","source_ids":["S1442"],"confidence":"High that the article reports these claims; moderate-to-low for the specific aid quantities, timing and broadcast-site attribution pending Chinese and CPT records.","basis":"The passage quotes a former Thai intelligence official; its VOPT-location note refers to Thai government direction-finding and an NIA interview. This is a comparator for channel types, not evidence about assistance to Indonesia.","next":"Check Chinese party/diplomatic archives, VOPT program recordings and transmitter logs, CPT supply accounts and Thai direction-finding/intercept records.","year":1960},{"id":"C2717","text":"Keppres No.48/1962 established nationwide civil defense as a non-military component of national defense and placed central planning/coordination under the Minister of National Security; it directed central and regional governments to mobilize national resources.","period":"Signed 19 Feb 1962; retroactive to 19 Dec 1961","type":"Official legal rule","source_ids":["S1443"],"confidence":"High for the statute’s text and dates; no inference about a particular 1965 unit or course.","basis":"Keppres 48, operative clauses 1–4, PDF pp.1–2. It created a general framework, not a Fifth Force or named Lubang Buaya program.","next":"Compare with implementing regulations, district orders, budgets and local civil-defense registers.","year":1962},{"id":"C2718","text":"Keppres No.128/1962 assigned central planning, implementation, coordination, control and supervision of civil defense and popular resistance to the First Deputy Minister for Defense/Security, with regional activity governed under that authority.","period":"Signed 4 Apr 1962; effective retroactively 6 Mar 1962","type":"Official legal rule","source_ids":["S1444"],"confidence":"High for the decree’s delegation; actual local implementation unverified.","basis":"Keppres 128, clauses 1–4. The decree also references the general mobilization regulation PERPU 1/1962.","next":"Locate regulations issued under the decree and any 1965 AURI/base-specific delegation.","year":1962},{"id":"C2719","text":"UU No.14/1962 made general mobilization a presidential/Supreme Commander decision after hearing the National Defense Council and stated that organized, trained popular resistance could assist armed forces and perform civil-defense duties.","period":"Law enacted 6 Sep 1962; effective retroactively 19 Dec 1961","type":"Official legal rule","source_ids":["S1445"],"confidence":"High for statutory wording; the text does not establish that general mobilization was declared or that a particular course was authorized.","basis":"UU 14/1962, arts.1–2, PDF pp.3–4; BPK metadata.","next":"Find any presidential mobilization declaration, call-up notices, implementing regulations and 1965 course authorization.","year":1962},{"id":"C2720","text":"UU No.14/1962 allowed healthy Indonesian citizens of either sex aged 18–50 to be summoned for active popular resistance, assistance to armed forces, civil-defense duties and related training.","period":"From 19 Dec 1961 under UU 14/1962","type":"Official legal eligibility rule","source_ids":["S1445"],"confidence":"High for text; low for any claim that a given person was called or trained under it.","basis":"UU 14/1962, arts.3, 6–8, PDF pp.4–5.","next":"Find actual call-up, health screening, training and exemption records.","year":1961},{"id":"C2721","text":"The reviewed 1962 legal texts establish general civil-defense/popular-resistance and mobilization mechanisms but do not name an “Angkatan Kelima” service or authorize the specific Lubang Buaya/HANREV course by name.","period":"1962 statutes; comparison with reported 1965 course","type":"Textual scope observation","source_ids":["S1443","S1444","S1445"],"confidence":"High for the reviewed text; it is not evidence that no separate 1965 order or instruction existed.","basis":"Read the full three legal instruments; they distinguish general legal authority from a particular policy proposal or operational order.","next":"Find specific 1965 AURI orders, legal opinions and Fifth Force command/organization papers.","year":1962},{"id":"C2722","text":"A 1994 Sekretariat Negara history, available here only through a blog transcription, asserts that Omar Dhani issued a 10 March 1965 instruction to establish Hansip at each air-base command and that Sujono sent coded radiograms to Korud battalions directing training teams and priority for labor/farmer organizations.","period":"Purported order 10 Mar 1965; recounted in 1994","type":"Later official narrative / reported order","source_ids":["S1451"],"confidence":"High that the accessible transcription makes this assertion; low that the original order and radiograms said or did this because none were obtained.","basis":"Chapter IV transcription, paragraphs beginning “Pelatihan Kemiliteran PKI...” and quoted radio-order text. It is not the March instruction or radio record itself.","next":"Retrieve the signed 10 March instruction, coded radio text, sender/receiver logs, Korud acknowledgements and base training reports.","year":1965},{"id":"C2723","text":"The same 1994 narrative says plans to train at Kalijati, Tasikmalaya and Semplak/Bogor were blocked by Koramil reactions and that Sujono therefore concentrated Hansip training at Lubang Buaya.","period":"1965; recounted in 1994","type":"Retrospective official narrative about inter-service/local response","source_ids":["S1451"],"confidence":"High that the narrative reports this; low for the claimed Koramil actions and causal reason without contemporaneous records.","basis":"Same Chapter IV transcription, paragraph on proposed bases and centralization. The military-trial testimony reviewed here does not independently confirm this specific sequence.","next":"Search local Koramil/Kodim correspondence, AURI base plans, cancelled course registers and Sujono’s original statements.","year":1965},{"id":"C2724","text":"In the 16 Feb 1966 Mahmillub testimony, former Major Sujono said he took the initiative to hold training at Lubang Buaya under an earlier Deputy Minister of Operations instruction to add Hansip personnel to air-base defenses and in light of Omar Dhani’s public statements; he also acknowledged there was no formal written order for the initiative.","period":"Training in 1965; testimony 16 Feb 1966","type":"Sworn courtroom testimony about command/authorization chain","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that the published court record reproduces this testimony; moderate-to-low for the complete underlying command chain because the written order is absent and the witness was detained.","basis":"S1353, printed pp.204–207. The text itself distinguishes a prior instruction, ministerial statements and Sujono’s own initiative; do not collapse them into one signed command.","next":"Locate Sri Mulyono Herlambang and Omar Dhani orders, AURI command logs, Sujono’s detention/interrogation file and testimony in another case.","year":1965},{"id":"C2725","text":"Sujono testified that BTI and SERBAUD and other mass organizations requested training, that he called them together and agreed to begin the first cohort on 5 July 1965.","period":"5 Jul 1965; reported 16 Feb 1966","type":"Sworn courtroom testimony","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that the transcript records the statement; moderate for actual start and organizer scope pending cohort registers and applicant letters.","basis":"S1353, printed pp.206–207. The testimony attributes the account to Sujono, not to a participant roster.","next":"Find organizations’ written requests, course-opening report, site diary and attendance lists.","year":1965},{"id":"C2726","text":"Sujono testified that cohort durations were progressively shortened from 12 days to 10, then 7 and finally 5 days as the training cycle advanced.","period":"July–Sep 1965; reported 16 Feb 1966","type":"Sworn courtroom testimony about course duration","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that the transcript records this account; moderate-to-low for each cohort’s exact dates and duration without schedules.","basis":"S1353, printed pp.213–214. The testimony is retrospective and no training calendars were checked.","next":"Compare cohort schedules, trainer logs, meal/supply sheets and participant recall.","year":1965},{"id":"C2727","text":"Sujono described the course as including revolutionary/Pancasila ideological instruction, weapons tactics and technology, revolutionary ethics, and intelligence; he said the final 28–30 September arrivals had only about two days, too little for proper training.","period":"Training through 30 Sep 1965; testimony 16 Feb 1966","type":"Sworn courtroom testimony about curriculum and readiness","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that this is what the transcript records; low-to-moderate for actual attendance, hours, weapons access and competence.","basis":"S1353, printed pp.213–215. The claim does not establish that every trainee received every module or became operationally capable.","next":"Find curriculum sheets, trainer assignment lists, firing-range logs, ammunition issues and individual course records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2728","text":"Sujono testified that a group of about 1,500 arrived suddenly at Lubang Buaya on 28 September 1965 and said he could not refuse them.","period":"28 Sep 1965; reported 16 Feb 1966","type":"Sworn courtroom testimony about a late arrival","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that this statement appears in the testimony; low-to-moderate for the count and identity of the full group because no nominal list or independent count was found.","basis":"S1353, printed pp.212–213. This refers to a late arriving group, not necessarily all people ever trained at Lubang Buaya.","next":"Obtain site entry/meal rolls, transport and organization dispatch records, and independent witness accounts.","year":1965},{"id":"C2729","text":"Under cross-examination, Sujono said that from 28 September he had only received his representative’s report of “1,000-something” candidates; he said he had not personally seen the whole group and had not set the exact number.","period":"28 Sep 1965; testimony 16 Feb 1966","type":"Sworn courtroom testimony clarifying information basis","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that the text records this qualification; low for the actual count. This is a distinct information basis from the approximately 1,500 arrival estimate in the same testimony.","basis":"S1353, printed pp.223–225. The OCR wording is “1.000 sekian”; do not convert it into an exact figure or average it with 1,500/2,000 claims.","next":"Check the representative’s identity and report, the other witness’s notes, attendance list and trial page images.","year":1965},{"id":"C2730","text":"Sujono testified that Lubang Buaya trainees had not been formally constituted as an AURI Hansip unit and were supposed to return to their home organizations after training.","period":"1965; testimony 16 Feb 1966","type":"Sworn courtroom testimony on formal status","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that the witness made the statement; moderate-to-low for organizational/legal status without AURI establishment orders and unit rolls.","basis":"S1353, printed pp.212–213. A statement about formal unit recognition does not mean no AURI officers controlled the site or supplied resources.","next":"Find AURI unit-formation orders, personnel rosters, pay/supply registers and post-course return records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2731","text":"Sujono testified that Captain Suradi provided 100 Garand rifles intended for Hansip use.","period":"1965; testimony 16 Feb 1966","type":"Sworn courtroom statement about weapon transfer","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that the testimony says this; low-to-moderate for delivery, count, custody or use because no issue/receipt ledger was examined.","basis":"S1353, printed pp.214–215. The witness describes what he said was received; no serial-number list, armory entry, ammunition issue or return record was found.","next":"Search Suradi and Sujono statements, Halim armory books, requisition/receipt/return vouchers and weapon serials.","year":1965},{"id":"C2732","text":"Sujono testified that organization-appointed intermediaries Kasiman, Nico/Nicolas and Djohar gathered trainees and arranged food, accommodation and transport, while Major Gatot Sukresno represented Sujono at the site and reported arrival information to him.","period":"1965; reported 16 Feb 1966","type":"Sworn courtroom testimony about coordination channels","source_ids":["S1353"],"confidence":"High that these names and roles appear in the published statement; moderate-to-low for exact spellings, formal roles and all functions pending rosters and correspondence.","basis":"S1353, printed pp.212–213 and 223–225. The account separates mass-organization selection, Suradi’s intermediary role and Sujono’s information through Gatot.","next":"Find named individuals’ personnel/organization records, correspondence, transport/food logs and Major Gatot’s reports.","year":1965},{"id":"C2733","text":"Humaidi’s 2008 thesis reports that a first-course report to Omar Dhani categorized five trainees as nationalist, about 2,000 as communist and none from invited religious organizations; it says Omar requested changes in curriculum, time allocation and political balance.","period":"July 1965; reported in 2008","type":"Secondary account of Sujono/Omar reports and later defense material","source_ids":["S1447","S1450"],"confidence":"High that Humaidi reports these figures; low-to-moderate for the count and its meaning because the cited source chain is Katoppo/Omar’s later defense and it is unclear whether these were plans, cumulative participants or graduates.","basis":"Humaidi, printed pp.67–68; note the different category and counting basis from Sujono’s late-September courtroom estimate.","next":"Obtain the report, Omar Dhani defense pp.240–241, Katoppo pp.29–36 and cohort lists; define whether numbers mean planned, enrolled, attended or graduated.","year":1965},{"id":"C2734","text":"Humaidi describes the second course (August 1965) as having shifted from Dwikora volunteer training to a PKI-cadre-oriented “Hada Hanrev” course and lists mass-organization representatives and CC PKI figures as organizers/instructors.","period":"Aug 1965; retrospective account published 2008","type":"Secondary interpretation and reported personnel assignments","source_ids":["S1447","S1450"],"confidence":"High that Humaidi presents this account; low-to-moderate for actual control, political purpose and each person’s role because the key footnote leads to Katoppo rather than course rosters or orders.","basis":"Humaidi, printed pp.67–68 and note citing Katoppo 1999, pp.29–36. The course name and political interpretation should be kept separate from actual curriculum and command.","next":"Find course roster, lesson plan, instructor appointment and participant records; compare with Sujono testimony and AURI documents.","year":1965},{"id":"C2735","text":"Humaidi reports that Komodor Dewanto ordered the training closed after an early-September meeting and that implementation occurred on 26 September 1965.","period":"Early Sep and 26 Sep 1965; reported in 2008","type":"Secondary account of AURI meeting/closure","source_ids":["S1447","S1450"],"confidence":"High that Humaidi reports this sequence; moderate-to-low for the actual order and execution date because the footnote points to Katoppo 1999, pp.34–35 and no signed closure order was read.","basis":"Humaidi, printed p.68 and note 54. A separate 1994 official narrative also gives 26 September, but is not an independently traced signed order.","next":"Locate Dewanto’s meeting minutes, AURI closure order, Korud messages, site handover and participant departure records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2736","text":"The 1994 state-history transcription says Rawabinong, roughly one kilometre from Lubang Buaya, held an August special course for 120 cadres from West Java PKI CDB and 80 Jakarta BTI cadres, followed in early September by a 26-person central cadre course including Nico, Djohar and Kasiman.","period":"Aug–early Sep 1965; recounted in 1994","type":"Later official narrative; archive locator not supplied in the excerpt","source_ids":["S1451"],"confidence":"High that the accessible transcription reports these numbers; low for actual enrollment and course details without lists or source notes.","basis":"Chapter IV blog transcription, paragraphs on Rawabinong. Do not merge this separate location/course with the regular Hansip cohorts or the 28 September arrival count.","next":"Retrieve the original 1994 book pages and Rawabinong site, BTI/CDB dispatch, instructor and participant records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2737","text":"The 2012 chapter reports 200 Rawabinong trainees in August and 26 central cadres in September, citing J.B. Soedarmanto’s 2004 Harry Tjan biography, pp.103–106.","period":"Aug–Sep 1965; reported in 2012","type":"Secondary account with indirect citation chain","source_ids":["S1448","S1449"],"confidence":"High that the chapter attributes the numbers to this book; low-to-moderate for the underlying counts because the cited biography pages and named rosters were not obtained.","basis":"Ambarwulan/Kasdi, printed pp.160–161, note 63. The 200 total aligns with 120+80 in the 1994 account, but dependence between the two sources is unknown.","next":"Read Soedarmanto pp.103–106 and compare names, original interview notes and ANRI records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2738","text":"The 2012 chapter attributes to an anonymous source called “Bung So” the statement that Halim-area military training was discussed in PKI meetings; it later describes him as a CGMI activist trusted to serve Politburo meetings and as Harry Tjan’s source.","period":"Reported through a 2004 biography and repeated in 2012","type":"Anonymous informant and retrospective biographical reporting","source_ids":["S1448","S1449"],"confidence":"High that the 2012 chapter makes this attribution; low for identity, access, exact words and the claim that Politburo meetings discussed the training because the informant’s notes/recording and identity are unavailable.","basis":"Ambarwulan/Kasdi, printed pp.160 and 187–188, notes 63 and 104, both tracing to Soedarmanto pp.103–106. Do not treat the anonymous identity or report as a Politburo minute.","next":"Obtain the biography and its underlying interview notes, identify “Bung So” only if the source permits, and locate meeting minutes/attendee accounts.","year":2004},{"id":"C2739","text":"Humaidi and Ambarwulan/Kasdi both cite Aristides Katoppo’s Menyingkap Kabut Halim 1965, pp.29–36, for core July–September Lubang Buaya training claims; the two later works therefore do not constitute independent corroboration for those claims.","period":"Evidence chain for 1965 training; publications 1999, 2008, 2012","type":"Source-dependency observation","source_ids":["S1447","S1448","S1450"],"confidence":"High for the explicit footnote chain; the content and reliability of Katoppo’s underlying evidence remain unassessed because the book pages were not obtained.","basis":"Humaidi footnotes and Ambarwulan/Kasdi note 62 both point to Katoppo pp.29–36.","next":"Read Katoppo directly; trace each factual statement to any original witness, document or interview in the book’s notes.","year":1965},{"id":"C2740","text":"The 1994 state-history transcription claims that by 28 September about 2,000 people (four battalions) had arrived at Lubang Buaya and assigns them to Pasopati, Bimasakti and Gatutkaca; this differs in wording and counting basis from Sujono’s testimony of a sudden late group of about 1,500 and an indirect “1,000-something” report.","period":"28 Sep 1965; later accounts in 1994 and 1966","type":"Conflict across a retrospective official narrative and courtroom testimony","source_ids":["S1451","S1353"],"confidence":"High that the texts report these distinct counts and categories; low for a single actual headcount or unit assignment without rosters, transport records and command documents.","basis":"1994 transcription gives a cumulative 4-battalion/2,000 figure and unit assignments; Sujono’s 1966 account refers to late arrivals and a representative’s estimate. Do not average or reconcile them without the underlying lists.","next":"Find 28 September attendance/transport records, battalion and unit rosters, participant testimony and the source behind the 1994 narrative.","year":1965},{"id":"C2741","text":"The 1994 official-history transcription dates a CC PKI decision sent to Njono linking training to the movement to 28 August 1965, while the 2012 chapter describes a Njono/Politburo decision or letter dated 28 September 1965; the available texts differ in date and document direction.","period":"28 Aug vs 28 Sep 1965; accounts published 1994/2012","type":"Unresolved documentary/date discrepancy","source_ids":["S1451","S1448"],"confidence":"High that the two accessible texts give these different dates/descriptions; low as to whether they refer to the same paper or separate decisions.","basis":"1994 transcription paragraph 211; Ambarwulan/Kasdi printed p.162 and note 64. Neither original letter/decision was obtained.","next":"Find the purported CC-to-Njono letter, Njono decision, dispatch/receipt logs and any court exhibit; compare dates and signatories.","year":1965},{"id":"C2742","text":"ANRI’s official service page states that users may request archive search or reproduction through email, PPID, LARISSA or SIKS; the LARISSA portal says users need registration/member status to access holdings and ANRI may charge reproduction fees.","period":"Access procedure current as checked 18 Nov 2026","type":"Official archival-service procedure","source_ids":["S1446"],"confidence":"High for the current published process; availability or release of specific BTI files 257–259 remains unknown.","basis":"Official ANRI service page, online-service steps; LARISSA outer page. No request was made and no specific file status was confirmed.","next":"Use the archive number and guide RA 112 in a formal request; record response, access decision, reproduction fee and copy provenance.","year":2026},{"id":"C2743","text":"ANRI’s Sukarno speech inventory lists item 750 as a one-sheet Indonesian presidential lecture at Lemhannas on 31 May 1965, and items 779–781 as three distinct one-sheet speeches dated 17 August 1965 at different events.","period":"31 May and 17 Aug 1965; catalog accessed 2026-11-18","type":"Official archival catalog observation","source_ids":["S1452"],"confidence":"High for the inventory descriptions; no speech text is thereby established.","basis":"ANRI inventory entries 750 and 779–781. Item 780 concerns publication of the book 20 Tahun Indonesia Merdeka; the catalog does not show that any one item is the same text as the 51/48-page TAKARI booklet.","next":"Request item 750 and 779–781; compare title, event, version, and physical foliation to the printed TAKARI booklet.","year":1965},{"id":"C2744","text":"A 2023 Unhan RI Press speech-list table hosted by BPIP identifies the 31 May 1965 Lemhannas lecture as entry 219 and the 17 August Tahun Berdikari speech as entry 223.","period":"Speech dates 31 May and 17 Aug 1965; list published 2023","type":"Later institutional bibliography / locator","source_ids":["S1454"],"confidence":"High for what the later list prints; no content verification.","basis":"Table entry numbers are not ANRI catalog numbers and are not contemporaneous document identifiers.","next":"Obtain the listed texts and identify their provenance and edition.","year":1965},{"id":"C2745","text":"In the accessible transcript of his 17 August 1965 Tahun Berdikari address, Sukarno calls the Fifth Force an idea he had put forward himself.","period":"17 Aug 1965; online transcription","type":"Attributed first-person public statement","source_ids":["S1453"],"confidence":"High that the transcript contains this self-attribution; it does not establish historical priority or exclude earlier proposals by Aidit, the PKI, or Chinese interlocutors.","basis":"Wikisource lines 442–443; original booklet not visually collated. The statement is Sukarno’s own account of authorship in a public speech.","next":"Collate the passage with the 1965 Deppen booklet and earlier January–May proposals and diplomatic records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2746","text":"The same transcript says Sukarno, as Supreme Commander, would decide about the Fifth Force after considering the matter further.","period":"17 Aug 1965","type":"Attributed first-person statement on a pending decision","source_ids":["S1453"],"confidence":"High for the transcription; moderate for exact fidelity to the printed text. It indicates the speech itself did not announce a final decision at that point.","basis":"A statement that a decision is pending does not prove no planning or training was underway through other institutions.","next":"Compare the original speech copy with later signed orders, cabinet minutes, and armed-forces plans.","year":1965},{"id":"C2747","text":"In the same passage Sukarno describes ABRI as the core of popular defense and says that, if necessary, workers, peasants, and others who remain in production could temporarily bear rifles.","period":"17 Aug 1965","type":"Attributed first-person defense-policy statement","source_ids":["S1453"],"confidence":"High that this is the wording printed in the transcript; it does not prove recruitment, unit formation, weapons issue, or operational orders.","basis":"Transcript lines 444–446; separate popular defense language from a constituted fifth service and from AURI HANREV training evidence.","next":"Compare with the original booklet, 31 May Lemhannas lecture, service chiefs’ written plans, and actual training / weapons records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2748","text":"Sukarno’s 17 August speech publicly claimed that the United States had supported BPS too quickly and described US aid to Malaysia as active assistance to neocolonialism.","period":"17 Aug 1965","type":"First-person public political allegation / evidence of presidential framing","source_ids":["S1453"],"confidence":"High that the available transcript contains these allegations; not evidence that the alleged US actions occurred as described.","basis":"The passage does not identify the cited US newspaper or provide documentary support. Treat as Sukarno’s contemporaneous public framing; test the specific BPS and Malaysia claims against US records, Indonesian BPS records, and contemporaneous press.","next":"Identify the US newspaper reference and compare it with US embassy/USIA records, BPS founding and funding records, and Malaysia-policy files.","year":1965},{"id":"C2749","text":"Keppres 256/1965 established KOTARI to carry out economic development on a self-reliance basis, placed it directly under the President, and assigned implementation to existing government departments and agencies rather than creating new operating bodies.","period":"31 Aug 1965","type":"Official legal act","source_ids":["S1455"],"confidence":"High for the decree text as reproduced by BPK.","basis":"This is a concrete economic implementation of the TAKARI program, not evidence of Fifth Force implementation.","next":"Locate implementation reports, budget and staff records, ministerial orders, and economic outcomes.","year":1965},{"id":"C2750","text":"Keppres 257/1965 appointed Brigadier General Achmad Jusuf, then domestic-trade minister / acting foreign-trade minister, as KOTARI chief of staff.","period":"2 Sep 1965","type":"Official appointment","source_ids":["S1456"],"confidence":"High for the published decree text.","basis":"Appointment to KOTARI does not establish a military role in a civilian armed force.","next":"Trace the chief of staff’s files and subsequent KOTARI records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2751","text":"Keppres 258/1965 created a KOTARI core staff with finance, infrastructure, production, trade, and mass-media fields, headed by ministers and accountable through the chief of staff directly to the President.","period":"3 Sep 1965","type":"Official institutional design","source_ids":["S1457"],"confidence":"High for the decree text.","basis":"The decree describes the economic command’s intended structure; it does not itself demonstrate implementation or staffing of every sector.","next":"Find appointment decrees, meeting minutes, staff rosters, budgets, and field reports.","year":1965},{"id":"C2752","text":"Catalog records for the 1965 Tahun Berdikari speech booklet disagree on extent: Google Books lists 51 pages while CiNii lists 48 pages and notes the item was quoted from a second printing matching the delivered text.","period":"1965 booklet; catalog records checked 2026-11-18","type":"Bibliographic discrepancy","source_ids":["S1453"],"confidence":"High that the two catalogs give different page counts and edition notes; unresolved as to whether this reflects separate editions, wrappers, or cataloging.","basis":"Neither record substitutes for inspecting the physical or digitized booklet.","next":"Compare a scan of the University of Michigan copy with the second printing held by Kyoto Sangyo University and document pagination.","year":1965},{"id":"C2753","text":"Keppres 259/1965 appointed the five heads of KOTARI’s finance, infrastructure, production, trade and mass-media fields; the decree dates from 3 September 1965.","period":"3 Sep 1965","type":"Official appointment decree","source_ids":["S1458"],"confidence":"High for the reproduced decree text.","basis":"BPK PDF lists the five appointments. The decree establishes appointments on paper, not subsequent attendance or performance.","next":"Locate appointment acceptance, meeting records, staffing and implementation reports.","year":1965},{"id":"C2754","text":"The KOTARI decree identifies four of five sector heads with military ranks: Brigadier General Achmad Soekandro, Brigadier General Mohd. Jusuf, Brigadier General Achmad Jusuf, and Major General Achmadi; the finance head is Minister for Central Bank Affairs Jusuf Muda Dalam.","period":"3 Sep 1965","type":"Official civil-military staffing record","source_ids":["S1458"],"confidence":"High for the titles and appointments stated in the decree; lower for conclusions about actual command practice.","basis":"The appointment list shows military-ranked officials holding economic portfolios alongside a civilian banking official. It does not by itself show that the command functioned as a military institution or that all sector heads were active-duty in the same administrative sense.","next":"Check individual service status and subsequent attendance, authority, budgets and records of decisions.","year":1965},{"id":"C2755","text":"Dake reports that Sukarno’s 31 May 1965 Lemhannas address to senior service commanders urged serious consideration of arming workers and peasants and invited proposals; Dake cites an Antara item dated 31 May 1965.","period":"31 May 1965","type":"Later account with trace to contemporary news-agency item","source_ids":["S1459"],"confidence":"High that Dake says this and gives the Antara citation; moderate/low for the address wording until the wire and speech are inspected.","basis":"Dake, ch.1 p.9 and appendix I pp.191–192. Neither the original speech nor Antara text has been accessed. Dake’s citation is not a second independent witness.","next":"Obtain ANRI item 750, the original Antara dispatch and Dake’s cited full context; compare audience, venue, date and exact verb for “asked” versus “ordered.”","year":1965},{"id":"C2756","text":"Dake’s Appendix I records a competing 1 June date for the Fifth Force address in Mortimer p.382 and cites Suluh Indonesia dated 2 June 1965.","period":"31 May–2 June 1965","type":"Retrospective source-chain/date discrepancy","source_ids":["S1459"],"confidence":"High that Dake’s note records these references; unresolved whether they describe the same address or a separate follow-up.","basis":"Mortimer p.382 and the cited Suluh Indonesia issue have not been inspected; Dake’s note is a retrieval lead, not verification of their contents.","next":"Retrieve Mortimer p.382 and the 2 June 1965 Suluh Indonesia issue; compare text, speaker, audience and event with ANRI item 750.","year":1965},{"id":"C2757","text":"Ricklefs’s Indonesian history states that on 31 May Sukarno discussed Zhou Enlai’s offer to arm the people and ordered all four armed services to submit implementation plans.","period":"31 May 1965; later edition","type":"Secondary historical narrative; exact source note unresolved","source_ids":["S1460"],"confidence":"High that the inspected translation says this; moderate/low for the underlying event wording until a pinpoint citation or original record is located.","basis":"Printed p.549; no pinpoint note was identified. Crouch and Mortimer appear among the chapter’s general references, so this account is not independent corroboration by itself.","next":"Trace the exact English-edition sentence and its citations; compare with Crouch p.90, Mortimer p.382, Antara and service-chief records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2758","text":"Liang’s thesis repeats the 31 May “submit plans” account and cites Sekretariat Negara’s 30 Tahun Indonesia Merdeka 1965–1973, pp.20–22.","period":"31 May 1965; thesis published 2008","type":"Secondary account with explicit citation chain","source_ids":["S1461","S1465"],"confidence":"High for the thesis citation and catalog metadata; the state-history content remains unverified.","basis":"Liang pp.217–218, endnote 189. The 1981 state history is located only through a library catalog and was not read; this is not independent corroboration.","next":"Inspect the state history pp.20–22, identify the notes/source base, and compare its date and wording with the 1965 speech and Antara.","year":1965},{"id":"C2759","text":"Soedarmo and Ginanjar repeat the May 31 order-for-plans account with an explicit citation to Ricklefs 2007 p.424.","period":"31 May 1965; article published 2014","type":"Secondary account with direct dependence","source_ids":["S1462","S1460"],"confidence":"High that the article cites Ricklefs and reproduces the account; no independent corroboration is added.","basis":"Printed p.135. Its separate June 1 statement is addressed in C2760.","next":"Use the article as a citation-chain map only; seek the cited contemporary dispatch and primary military records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2760","text":"Soedarmo and Ginanjar separately say that on 1 June Sukarno emphasized to territorial commanders that the proposal should be seriously considered.","period":"1 June 1965; article published 2014","type":"Later secondary narrative; source not specified in examined passage","source_ids":["S1462"],"confidence":"High that the article states this; low/moderate for whether this was a distinct meeting or a date drift.","basis":"The examined sentence has no citation. It may refer to a follow-up event distinct from the 31 May Lemhannas address.","next":"Find June 1 Indonesian press reports, presidential speech/meeting inventories, and territorial-command minutes; compare audience and wording.","year":1965},{"id":"C2761","text":"Komnas HAM’s executive summary dates a presidential order to arm/train a Fifth Force to 1 June 1965.","period":"1 June 1965; report published 2012/2014 compilation","type":"Retrospective human-rights investigation summary","source_ids":["S859"],"confidence":"High for the report’s narrative; moderate/low for the date and exact order absent a cited original at the passage.","basis":"Printed p.22, executive summary; no footnote or source citation appears at the passage. It is not a 1965 record.","next":"Trace the cited commission investigation files/BAPs and compare the June 1 claim with ANRI item 750, Antara and contemporary newspaper issues.","year":1965},{"id":"C2762","text":"The same Komnas HAM passage attributes differing reactions to the service chiefs: Omar Dani supportive, Navy chief Edy Martadinata not wholehearted, and Army chief Ahmad Yani opposed.","period":"1 June 1965; retrospective report","type":"Attributed retrospective account of senior-officer positions","source_ids":["S859"],"confidence":"High for what the report says; low/moderate for the underlying statements until contemporaneous records are checked.","basis":"Printed p.22 gives no pinpoint citations in the passage; distinguish each attributed response from an established decision or service policy.","next":"Seek contemporaneous press statements, service-chief meeting minutes and written proposals/replies; verify names/titles on the date.","year":1965},{"id":"C2763","text":"An unauthenticated Scribd-hosted copy of a Perpusnas microfilm list assigns English Antara News Bulletin May 1965 to reel 212/PN/M/94 and Jan 1965–Oct 1966 to 75/PN/M/94; it separately lists Indonesian Warta berita Jan–Jun 1965 as reel 120 and Jul–Dec 1965 as reel 121.","period":"1965 holdings; catalog copy accessed 2026-11-19","type":"Microfilm catalog locator; provenance unverified","source_ids":["S1463"],"confidence":"High for what the accessible catalog copy lists; low for the copy’s provenance and current reel availability; no newspaper content established.","basis":"The English Antara Bulletin and Indonesian Warta berita are separate listed serials. No reel or dispatch was inspected.","next":"Verify reel numbers against an official Perpusnas catalog or finding aid and obtain the May 31–June 2 issue images with full mastheads and page sequence.","year":1965},{"id":"C2764","text":"Antara’s 2020 announcement says the Antara–Perpusnas digitization agreement covered January 1966–December 1985, with the 2020 installment covering 1975–1985.","period":"Digitization agreement announced 15 Dec 2020","type":"Institutional project-scope record","source_ids":["S1464"],"confidence":"High for the stated agreement scope; no conclusion about all current digital copies is warranted.","basis":"The release describes a digitization plan/project and anticipated JPEG output; it does not document completion or online access.","next":"Locate Perpusnas/Antara digital outputs and determine whether other digitization projects cover 1965.","year":2020},{"id":"C2765","text":"The Foreign Ministry library catalog locates a 1981, 270-page edition of the state history 30 Tahun Indonesia Merdeka 1965–1973 at Museum Konferensi Asia Afrika.","period":"Catalog accessed 2026-11-19; edition dated 1981","type":"Official-library catalog metadata","source_ids":["S1465"],"confidence":"High for catalog metadata; the book’s content and cited pp.20–22 remain unread.","basis":"Catalog is a physical-item locator; it identifies a team led by Nugroho Notosusanto under Sudharmono’s coordination.","next":"Inspect pp.20–22 and surrounding notes, then trace any primary citations.","year":2026},{"id":"C2766","text":"ISEAS’s Indonesia guide lists Indonesian Warta berita Antara holdings for 1964–1993 at MFM 200 and English News bulletin (Antara Organization) for 1959–1994 at MFM 1.","period":"Holdings guide accessed 2026-11-19","type":"Institutional microfilm-holdings metadata","source_ids":["S1466"],"confidence":"High for the published guide’s stated ranges and call numbers; issue-level completeness and digital access are unknown.","basis":"The broad ranges do not prove that a particular day’s dispatch exists or that reels are remotely available.","next":"Request/inspect the relevant 1965 reels and record actual issue gaps, page identifiers and preservation condition.","year":2026},{"id":"C2767","text":"ISEAS’s microfilm title list assigns call no.1 (374 items) to Antara News Bulletin, no.4 (25) to Harian Rakjat, no.11 (22) to Suluh Indonesia, nos.14–15 to Warta Bhakti, and nos.139/145 to Angkatan Bersendjata.","period":"Catalog accessed 2026-11-19","type":"Institutional microfilm title-list locator","source_ids":["S1467"],"confidence":"High for title/call-number/item-count entries; not evidence of exact issue coverage or content.","basis":"The list’s “item count” is not a dated completeness statement; physical reels were not checked.","next":"Use call numbers to identify exact 1965 reel coverage and compare contemporaneous editions across political alignments.","year":2026},{"id":"C2768","text":"Taomo Zhou’s 2014 article reports that Colonel Lapase’s three-part Angkatan Bersenjata series (29 June–1 July 1965) argued that social/political conditions differed from China and rejected a separate Fifth Force while allowing for arming people.","period":"29 June–1 July 1965; article published 2014","type":"Secondary reconstruction citing contemporary military newspaper","source_ids":["S134"],"confidence":"High that the scholarly article reports this; moderate/low for the original editorials until the issues are inspected.","basis":"Zhou calls it part of a systematic Army press effort and cites the three dates; original newspaper articles were not opened.","next":"Obtain Angkatan Bersenjata nos. for 29 June, 30 June and 1 July 1965; compare exact wording, author attribution and editorials surrounding them.","year":1965},{"id":"C2769","text":"Taomo Zhou’s 2014 article reports that on 29 June 1965 PLA chief of staff Luo Ruiqing told a visiting Indonesian volunteer delegation that China’s militia experience was a reference and Indonesia should adapt any approach to its own conditions.","period":"29 June 1965; article published 2014","type":"Secondary account citing Chinese Foreign Ministry archival minutes","source_ids":["S134"],"confidence":"High that the article attributes this statement to archival minutes; moderate for the exact exchange until the minutes are inspected.","basis":"Zhou cites Chinese MFA archive file 105-01689-02; the archive minutes were not opened. The delegation’s exact membership and mandate require the cited file and related 23 June/July records.","next":"Obtain file 105-01689-02 and related delegation records 105-01689-01/-03; identify participants, mandate and translation provenance.","year":1965},{"id":"C2770","text":"The date “31 May 1965” is supported by ANRI’s speech-inventory locator, Dake’s citation to a same-day Antara item and Ricklefs’s later narrative; June 1 appears in Komnas HAM and in a later article’s separate territorial-command statement. Current evidence does not establish whether June 1 is a distinct follow-up event, a publication/reporting date, or a one-day date drift.","period":"31 May–1 June 1965","type":"Cross-source date discrepancy and unresolved event identity","source_ids":["S1452","S1459","S1460","S1462","S859"],"confidence":"High that the sources present these different date/audience claims; low/moderate for a single reconstructed event until original records are compared.","basis":"ANRI is a catalog; Dake cites an unseen Antara dispatch; Ricklefs lacks a pinpoint note; Komnas passage is retrospective and uncited; Soedarmo’s June 1 audience statement is uncited. Avoid selecting one date without the missing records.","next":"Compare ANRI item 750, Antara 31 May–2 June bulletins, Suluh Indonesia 2 June, Mortimer p.382, official history pp.20–22, service chiefs’ plans and June 1 territorial-command records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2771","text":"Mahmud’s 2025 thesis lists a press sequence on the Fifth Force proposal: Warta Bhakti (14 Jan), Harian Rakyat (15 Jan and 18 Jan), Antara (18 Jan), Warta Bhakti (23 Jan), and Harian Rakyat (26 and 30 Jan). Its Chapter 1 says the Warta Bhakti and Harian Rakyat items were consulted at Perpusnas, including on microfilm.","period":"14–30 Jan 1965; thesis published 2025","type":"Secondary issue inventory and archival retrieval lead","source_ids":["S1468"],"confidence":"High that the thesis lists these titles and describes its access; moderate as a locator pending direct inspection of the newspaper issues.","basis":"The listed titles and author’s source criticism are in Chapter 1; the newspaper pages themselves have not been viewed in this research pass. The abstract’s political interpretation is not treated as established fact.","next":"Request the exact dates, reel identifiers, mastheads and page images from Perpusnas; verify each title and read the full articles.","year":1965},{"id":"C2772","text":"Mahmud’s thesis prints the 14 January Warta Bhakti entry as “DN. Aidit dipanggil Presiden PKI usulkan 15 juta massa tani dan buruh dipersenjatai” and describes the issue as a Perpusnas microfilm item.","period":"14 Jan 1965; thesis published 2025","type":"Secondary transcription/title variant and archive locator","source_ids":["S1468"],"confidence":"High for the thesis transcription; unresolved for the original headline and the meeting’s exact sequence until the issue is inspected.","basis":"This title wording differs from later paraphrases and abbreviated headline forms. A bibliography entry is not the newspaper page.","next":"Compare the original Warta Bhakti issue with Indonesian Observer and Harian Rakyat reports from 14–15 January.","year":1965},{"id":"C2773","text":"Dake says Aidit spoke to Sukarno and then gave a radio interview; he reports the candidate groups as 10 million workers and 5 million farmers and cites Indonesian Observer dated 14 January 1965.","period":"14 Jan 1965; retrospective account published 2006","type":"Secondary narrative with contemporary press citation","source_ids":["S1459","S1466"],"confidence":"High that Dake’s appendix I pp.185–186 says this and gives the citation; moderate/low for the reported count and interview wording until Indonesian Observer is inspected.","basis":"Dake’s count is the reverse of Zhou’s later account of the 15 January Harian Rakjat speech. The Indonesian Observer original has not been read; the ISEAS guide lists its 1954–1992 holdings at MFM 20, which is a retrieval lead only.","next":"Retrieve Indonesian Observer, 14 January 1965 (ISEAS MFM 20), and compare with the 15 January Harian Rakjat issue and the Warta Bhakti page.","year":1965},{"id":"C2774","text":"Zhou’s article cites Harian Rakjat, 15 January 1965, via Crouch and says Aidit named 10 million peasants and 5 million workers; the 1968 CIA report repeats this category split, which is opposite Dake’s account.","period":"14–15 Jan 1965; later sources 1968 and 2014","type":"Conflicting secondary quotations of contemporary press","source_ids":["S134","S1470","S1459"],"confidence":"High that the cited secondary sources use these different splits; unresolved for whether the newspapers report distinct statements or one transcription/counting discrepancy.","basis":"Zhou’s footnote points to Crouch p.87 and Harian Rakjat 15 Jan; that original issue and Crouch’s cited page were not read in this pass. CIA repetition is not independent corroboration.","next":"Read the 14 January Indonesian Observer and 15 January Harian Rakjat originals; inspect Dake’s underlying citation and Crouch p.87.","year":1965},{"id":"C2775","text":"A 9 April 1968 CIA intelligence report says Beijing probably encouraged Aidit’s 14 January proposal and interprets it as enhancing PKI influence; the same passage says Sukarno apparently turned the proposal down.","period":"1968 report about Jan 1965","type":"Retrospective intelligence assessment; explicit hedging","source_ids":["S1470","S134"],"confidence":"High for what the CIA report assessed; low as proof of a Chinese channel or a final Indonesian decision.","basis":"The text uses “probably” and “apparently”; no contemporaneous contact record is cited at the encouragement sentence. Zhou’s later archival study documents Chinese endorsement and arms discussions but does not prove a pre-14 January instruction to Aidit.","next":"Search Chinese Embassy/Aidit contacts, PRC Foreign Ministry minutes, and relevant Indonesian records before treating the causal claim as established.","year":1968},{"id":"C2776","text":"An Associated Press article printed by The Daily Iowan on 14 January 1965 identifies CBS correspondent Bernard Kalb as interviewing Sukarno in Jakarta about the U.N. withdrawal; the article does not report an interview with Aidit.","period":"14 Jan 1965","type":"Contemporary press report; channel-boundary check","source_ids":["S1471"],"confidence":"High for the text in the AP-reprinted article; it establishes Kalb’s presence/interview with Sukarno only.","basis":"The article’s Antara quotation concerns Sukarno’s response; absence of Aidit from this story neither proves nor disproves a separate exchange with Aidit.","next":"Locate CBS broadcast/transcript or Kalb’s reporting files and compare with the Jan 14 Indonesian newspapers.","year":1965},{"id":"C2777","text":"The Library of Congress microform catalog’s Warta bhakti entry gives coverage from November 1960 through 2 October 1965 and lists holding institutions with microform holdings.","period":"Serial holdings catalog published 1983","type":"Published catalog locator","source_ids":["S1469"],"confidence":"High for the catalog entry; it establishes a retrieval route, not survival or access to a specific issue.","basis":"Catalog institutions and date ranges do not establish Perpusnas holdings or issue completeness.","next":"Use the entry to identify candidate repositories and request their January 1965 issue-level inventories.","year":1983},{"id":"C2778","text":"Hendi Jo’s 2021 online account repeats the claim that Aidit spoke to Bernard Kalb before meeting Sukarno and presents competing origin narratives using Dake and Rum Aly; this repetition is a secondary citation-chain lead, not independent confirmation.","period":"Article published 18 Oct 2021","type":"Secondary narrative / source-chain map","source_ids":["S1472"],"confidence":"High for the article’s wording and attribution; low as independent evidence of the underlying exchange or idea’s origin.","basis":"The article contains no primary-source reproduction in the examined section; similar later accounts may depend on the same books.","next":"Trace the article’s Dake and Rum Aly citations to the cited passages, then compare with 1964-65 Chinese, BTI and Indonesian presidential records.","year":2021},{"id":"C2779","text":"The 1994 State Secretariat narrative attributes to Omar Dhani a 10 March 1965 instruction to establish Hansip at each air-base command, while Sujono’s 16 February 1966 testimony describes the specific Lubang Buaya course as his initiative under an earlier Sri Muljono base-defense instruction and says there was no formal written order for that initiative. These statements may concern different scopes (general base defense versus one course); the original March instruction and course authorization have not been located.","period":"10 Mar–5 Jul 1965; accounts published 1994 and given in 1966","type":"Cross-source scope comparison; unresolved command-chain question","source_ids":["S1451","S1353"],"confidence":"High that the two sources make these differently scoped statements; unresolved whether they refer to separate documents or how either command was implemented.","basis":"1994 Chapter IV is available only as a third-party transcription; S1353 is an Army-published military-trial record available as third-party OCR. Neither the 10 March instruction nor a course-specific signed authorization was inspected. Do not label them a direct contradiction without the documents.","next":"Obtain the signed 10 March instruction, relevant AURI/Korud radiograms and acknowledgements, Sri Muljono’s orders, and the underlying course file; inspect the physical Mahmillub pages.","year":1965},{"id":"C2780","text":"Harold Crouch reports that Sumatra interregional commander Lt. Gen. Mokoginta ordered one peasant-and-worker company in each Kodim in Sumatra; by July, Crouch says four companies had trained in North Sumatra and six more were being trained.","period":"After Jan 1965 proposal; status reported by Jul 1965","type":"Secondary historical report of regional Army-directed popular-defense training","source_ids":["S1473"],"confidence":"High that Crouch reports these instructions and counts; moderate-to-low for the original order, exact geographic scope and counts pending primary records.","basis":"Crouch, printed p.90. The primary order, Kodim rosters and training logs are absent; “Sumatra” order scope and “North Sumatra” count scope are not identical.","next":"Find Mokoginta’s order, each Kodim company roster, trainer/course records, and the source note behind Crouch’s p.90 statement.","year":1965},{"id":"C2781","text":"Crouch interprets Mokoginta’s implementation of the worker/peasant proposal as a small-scale program kept under Army control, in contrast to a separate force outside Army command.","period":"1965; Crouch’s account published 1978, reprinted 2007","type":"Historian’s political-institutional interpretation","source_ids":["S1473"],"confidence":"High that this is Crouch’s interpretation; not a directly documented intent unless Mokoginta’s orders or correspondence support it.","basis":"Crouch, printed p.90. This is an analytical explanation, not a neutral description of an authenticated order. It should be compared with Sukarno’s speeches, service plans and local implementation files.","next":"Locate Mokoginta’s written instructions and contemporaneous reports; compare provincial/army and AURI command structures and actual trainee lists.","year":1965},{"id":"C2782","text":"Crouch reports that in March 1965 Air Force deputy for operations Sri Muljono Herlambang directed civilian base-defense units because AURI lacked personnel for guard duties; Crouch says the plan envisaged one company of 120 at Halim, while Sujono’s scheme trained more recruits than base protection required.","period":"Mar–Jul 1965; reported in later scholarship","type":"Secondary account based on testimony in the Omar Dhani trial and other sources","source_ids":["S1473","S1353"],"confidence":"High that Crouch reports this account; moderate-to-low for the signed order, intended number and exact scope until the cited Omar Dhani proceedings and AURI files are checked.","basis":"Crouch, printed p.92 and note 71, cites Sri Muljono testimony in Omar Dhani trial vol.III p.489. The cited volume was not obtained. This reported base-Hansip instruction may be related to, but is not identical with, a Fifth Force decision or the later Lubang Buaya course.","next":"Obtain the Omar Dhani trial volume III pp.468–489, Sri Muljono’s signed/court statement, Halim staffing shortage records, the 120-person plan and base-defense orders.","year":1965},{"id":"C2783","text":"Crouch reports that Sujono began training at Lubang Buaya on 5 July, usually ran two courses per month with 200–300 per group, and that a final September group rose to 1,200; this numerical summary must remain attributed to Crouch and must not be merged with Sujono’s separate courtroom statements of about 1,500 arrivals and an indirect “1,000-something” report.","period":"5 Jul–Sep 1965; reported in 1978/2007 and testimony in 1966","type":"Secondary summary compared with a witness’s courtroom account","source_ids":["S1473","S1353"],"confidence":"High that Crouch gives these counts and cites the Njono trial; low-to-moderate for the cohort sizes and definitions without course lists or rechecking the original pages.","basis":"Crouch printed p.92, note 73 cites Sujono at Njono trial pp.207, 214 and 225. The existing courtroom record excerpt distinguishes different information bases; no attendance roll or cohort schedule has been found. “1,200 last group,” “1,500 arrived” and the representative’s indirect “1,000-something” are not interchangeable totals.","next":"Compare the cited trial page images and complete transcript, then obtain course calendars, attendance rolls, transport and supply records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2784","text":"Crouch reports that Air Force deputy Ignatius Dewanto ordered the volunteer training stopped on 21 September 1965 after finding participants were predominantly from the Communist element of Nasakom, with future courses intended for nationalist and religious organizations; Crouch cites Dewanto’s testimony at Omar Dhani trial vol.III p.47.","period":"21 Sep 1965; reported later","type":"Secondary report of a military-court statement about command action","source_ids":["S1473"],"confidence":"High that Crouch attributes this account to Dewanto; low-to-moderate that the order was issued and implemented as stated until the cited testimony and order are obtained.","basis":"Crouch printed p.92, note 74. The Omar Dhani court volume and a written stop order were not inspected. The account does not establish all activity at the site ended on that date.","next":"Obtain vol.III p.47, any Dewanto testimony transcript and the 21 September written/coded order, recipient acknowledgements and the proposed 1 October course schedule.","year":1965},{"id":"C2785","text":"Crouch reports a 21 September stop order but does not establish the date the course actually ceased. The 1994 State Secretariat transcription says training ran from 5 July until the September 30 Movement began; Ambarwulan and Kasdi (2012) report an early-September order and actual closure on 26 September, but the chapter’s nearby footnote to Soedarmanto p.106 follows the preceding “Bung So” sentence and is not clearly attached to the closure paragraph; Anderson and McVey date training to the eve of 1 October. The endpoints may refer to different courses, orders, actual classes, or use of the site; the issue remains unresolved.","period":"21 Sep–1 Oct 1965; accounts published 1978/1994","type":"Unresolved timeline divergence across a historian’s synthesis and an official retrospective narrative","source_ids":["S1473","S1451","S1474","S1475","S1476"],"confidence":"High that the four accounts differ in phrasing and date; low regarding the actual order, final class, participant activity or site closure.","basis":"Crouch cites Dewanto’s Omar Dhani trial testimony vol.III p.47; that volume is unavailable. The official account is a third-party transcription. Ambarwulan/Kasdi’s surrounding subsection has a note to Soedarmanto p.106, but its scope is unclear; the page was not inspected. Anderson/McVey is an explicitly provisional reconstruction partly dependent on late-1965 press. “Order issued,” “class ended,” “training program ceased” and “site cleared” are distinct events.","next":"Obtain Omar Dhani trial III p.47 and any written stop order; inspect Soedarmanto p.106 and identify its scope, informant/document; locate daily course and site records, final attendance/return and weapons logs; compare the 1994 original and Anderson/McVey cited press.","year":1965},{"id":"C2786","text":"Anderson and McVey’s 1971 interim report reconstructs a summer 1965 AURI–PKI arrangement for field training lower-level PKI cadres, with local PKI chiefs apparently selecting candidates and volunteers signing up on party instruction; it dates training from early July to the eve of 1 October.","period":"Summer 1965; reconstruction prepared Jan 1966 and published 1971","type":"Early scholarly reconstruction based on press and post-event reporting","source_ids":["S1474"],"confidence":"High that the report makes this reconstruction; moderate-to-low that it establishes a formal agreement or centrally authorized party decision.","basis":"Printed pp.19–20 and notes 14, 29–31. The preface says the analysis was preliminary and was based on late-1965 metropolitan and provincial press. Training details partly rely on post-event newspaper “confessions”; these are not independent course or command records.","next":"Locate AURI/PKI correspondence, local party dispatches, trainee sign-up lists, full course roster and the newspaper reports/confessions cited in notes 14 and 29.","year":1965},{"id":"C2787","text":"Anderson and McVey explain the AURI–PKI training relationship through institutional rivalry with the Army, the Air Force leadership’s alignment with Sukarno, and differing outlooks among senior and mid-level officers; they explicitly do not treat the Air Force as a politically uniform or simply PKI-ideological actor.","period":"1964–1965; interpretation published 1971","type":"Historian’s institutional and factional interpretation","source_ids":["S1474"],"confidence":"High that this is the report’s interpretation; not proof of individual officers’ private beliefs or a unitary Air Force policy.","basis":"Printed pp.19–20. The report is an early, explicitly provisional reconstruction and relies heavily on contemporaneous press; compare with Crouch and trial records, noting citation overlap.","next":"Compare individual officers’ statements and personnel/command records with Army–AURI correspondence, service doctrine, contemporaneous press and full trial records.","year":1964},{"id":"C2788","text":"Ambarwulan and Aminuddin Kasdi’s 2012 chapter reports that Air Force deputy Dewanto convened an early-September meeting about Lubang Buaya training and ordered it closed; the authors state that the training was actually closed on 26 September 1965. A nearby note 63 to J. B. Soedarmanto’s 2004 biography of Harry Tjan Silalahi, p.106, follows the preceding “Bung So” sentence; it is unclear whether this note also supports the Dewanto/closure account.","period":"Early–26 Sep 1965; reported in 2012","type":"Retrospective secondary report and citation-chain observation","source_ids":["S1475","S1476"],"confidence":"High that the 2012 chapter states these details; low-to-moderate for the meeting, order and actual closure until its evidentiary basis and dated course records are checked.","basis":"Read printed pp.160–161 through third-party OCR. No contemporary meeting minute, signed stop order, course register or page image was obtained. Soedarmanto p.106 was identified through a nearby footnote but not directly inspected, and that note’s scope is unclear; the cited book is not counted as independent confirmation.","next":"Inspect Soedarmanto p.106 and surrounding pages; establish whether it supports the closure account, identify informant/document, then locate Dewanto meeting minutes, order recipients and dated closing/attendance records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2789","text":"Ambarwulan and Kasdi interpret a banner displayed at the 26 September training closure reading “Tunggu Apa Lagi” (“What are you waiting for?”) as a signal that the PKI was ready to begin a rebellion.","period":"26 Sep 1965; interpretation published 2012","type":"Retrospective interpretation of an attributed sign/banner","source_ids":["S1475"],"confidence":"High that the chapter makes this interpretation; low that the banner existed as reported or conveyed that meaning, because no photograph, contemporary report, witness deposition or organizational instruction was inspected.","basis":"The banner and its interpretation appear in the same retrospective narrative; no separate source note for the banner/meaning is visible in the examined passage. Treating the phrase as an operational signal is the authors’ inference, not evidence of a PKI order, shared understanding or planned start date.","next":"Find the cited biography passage and its source trail; seek original photographs/film, Air Force information-service logs, contemporaneous press, witnesses and PKI/AURI instructions. Compare whether the phrase was a camp slogan, ceremony banner, or later recollection.","year":1965},{"id":"C2790","text":"Hill reports interviews with more than fifty Indonesian exiles in seven countries and seven returnees; the primary materials he used were in Indonesian, and he states that he has no facility in Chinese.","period":"Research published 2020; interview base dates mainly 2007–2012","type":"Research-method and source-scope statement","source_ids":["S1477"],"confidence":"High that the article states this methodology; it does not independently validate each interview claim.","basis":"Hill, printed pp.338–340 and note 1. Interviews are retrospective, and some requested anonymity; IISH archival documents are cited but not all directly reproduced.","next":"Inspect the article's interview appendix if available, locate cited IISH papers and recordings, and compare Chinese-language records through qualified translations.","year":2020},{"id":"C2791","text":"Hill identifies PKI Politburo/Central Committee member Jusuf Adjitorop as born Josef Simanjuntak, sent to China for medical treatment on 26 June 1964, and later known by the name adopted for underground organizing in North Sumatra; Hill reports a birth-date discrepancy between 19 December 1918 and the later 20 June 1920 date used on a school certificate.","period":"1918/1920–1964","type":"Biographical account based on participant papers and secondary scholarship","source_ids":["S1477"],"confidence":"High that Hill reports these facts and the date discrepancy; moderate for the underlying biographical particulars pending direct inspection of the autobiography and civil/school records.","basis":"Hill, printed pp.349–350 and note 30; he says the biography relies primarily on Adjitorop's 12-page autobiography in IISH Bundle IV, Item 23, which was not directly read here.","next":"Obtain IISH Bundle IV, Item 23; compare school certificate, party biography, medical/travel documentation and Indonesian identity records.","year":1918},{"id":"C2792","text":"Hill quotes Adjitorop's autobiography as saying that on 22 February 1966 he received a Politburo decision authorizing him to lead the PKI Central Committee Delegation abroad.","period":"1966-02-22","type":"Retrospective autobiographical statement transmitted by scholarship","source_ids":["S1477"],"confidence":"High that Hill reports this statement; moderate-to-low that the quoted decision occurred in precisely this form until the cited paper is inspected.","basis":"Hill, printed p.350, note 31 cites Jusuf Adjitorop Papers, Indonesian Exiles of the Left collection, IISH, Bundle IV, Item 23, p.12; the original is not directly inspected.","next":"Retrieve the autobiography, any written Politburo decision, Delegation appointment notice, and early 1966 communications to party branches.","year":1966},{"id":"C2793","text":"Hill reports that Beijing recognized the CC-PKI Delegation as the sole legitimate representation of the PKI abroad; it sent representatives to sympathetic countries and authorized overseas publications, giving it a role in external representation and exile communications.","period":"1966 onward","type":"Scholarly reconstruction of party-in-exile functions","source_ids":["S1477"],"confidence":"High that Hill describes these functions; moderate for the underlying institutional arrangements because the cited evidence is mainly exile interviews and secondary scholarship.","basis":"Hill, printed pp.352–353 and notes 34–39. Recognition and overseas representation do not establish that the Delegation commanded domestic Emergency CC, Kompro cells, or operations inside Indonesia.","next":"Locate Delegation appointment/authorization papers, publication mastheads and correspondence with Albania, Vietnam and other branches; compare home-party messages and host-state records.","year":1966},{"id":"C2794","text":"Hill reports that a 7 August 1966 declaration attributed to the PKI Politburo called for a united front under PKI leadership and development of armed struggle against the Suharto–Nasution regime.","period":"1966-08-07","type":"Published political declaration reported through secondary citations","source_ids":["S1477"],"confidence":"High that Hill reports this wording and political program; moderate-to-low for the declaration's exact authorship, circulation and relation to domestic operations.","basis":"Hill, printed p.353, cites Simon 1969, pp.173–174, via Sukma 1999, p.50. The original declaration was not opened. It is an exile political program, not proof that a domestic armed operation was authorized or implemented.","next":"Find the original declaration, date-stamped copies, publication issue, recipient lists and any home-branch response.","year":1966},{"id":"C2795","text":"Hill reports that around a year after the September 1965 upheaval the Beijing Delegation published a new PKI program known as the Auto-criticism, but his supporting references are secondary publications and a 2000 reprint rather than the original publication inspected in this pass.","period":"1966–1967","type":"Publication-history claim transmitted by scholarship","source_ids":["S1477"],"confidence":"High that Hill reports the publication; moderate for exact publication date, edition and circulation.","basis":"Hill, printed p.353, note 37 cites van der Kroef 1970 and Sudisman 2000, pp.81–139. This publication claim must be kept separate from the September 1966 Politburo self-criticism written by surviving leaders inside Indonesia.","next":"Compare overseas publication scans, Sudisman 2000 reprint, original Indonesian manuscript and correspondence about editorial changes.","year":1966},{"id":"C2796","text":"Hill's exile-history reconstruction says the Chinese Communist Party, as host, determined key conditions such as where Indonesian exiles lived and worked and what funding they received; contact with Chinese authorities was conducted primarily, perhaps exclusively, through the Delegation.","period":"China, 1966 onward","type":"Retrospective reconstruction of host-state administration","source_ids":["S1477"],"confidence":"High that Hill makes this account; moderate for the arrangements as reported, pending Chinese and Indonesian administrative records.","basis":"Hill, printed pp.354–359, drawing on exile interviews and memoirs. It concerns the living and political conditions of exiles after 1965; it is not evidence of Chinese direction of G30S, arms delivery for the September operation, or command over domestic PKI networks.","next":"Seek Chinese Foreign Ministry/CCP liaison, residence, work-assignment and funding records; compare named exile testimony and Delegation correspondence.","year":1966},{"id":"C2797","text":"Hill documents internal opposition to Adjitorop's call for cadres abroad to regroup in China and a 1968 attempt by the BPKK, chaired by Tom Iljas with A.S. Munandar as deputy, to convene a cadres' conference; Hill says the attempt failed after most Delegation members withdrew support and Chinese authorities denied venue or infrastructure access without Delegation authorization.","period":"1967–1968","type":"Exile organizational history from retrospective interviews and memoirs","source_ids":["S1477"],"confidence":"High that Hill reports the dispute and failed conference attempt; moderate for exact motives and decision procedure absent minutes or host-government papers.","basis":"Hill, printed pp.356–358 and notes 48–53. One opposition group was called Cingkan Syang; Hill cites exile memoirs/interviews. The conflict describes a party-in-exile and must not be collapsed into domestic PKI leadership debates.","next":"Find BPKK proposals/minutes, conference invitations and venue requests, Delegation replies, host correspondence, and interviews from participants on opposing sides.","year":1967},{"id":"C2798","text":"An unsigned April 1978 political document cited by Hill says the overseas CC-PKI Delegation was part of the PKI Central Committee at home and could not replace it; Hill assesses that it had no effective communication with the domestic Central Committee destroyed by the New Order.","period":"1978-04","type":"Unsigned internal political document and scholarly interpretation","source_ids":["S1477"],"confidence":"High that Hill reports the document and his interpretation; moderate that the document accurately states the Delegation's internal mandate; low for the exact extent of contact without correspondence.","basis":"Hill, printed p.366, note 74 cites an unsigned 20-page document in IISH, Adjitorop Papers, Item 49, p.20; the document was not directly read. Hill's domestic emergency-committee detail in note 75 expressly cites Hearman 2010a, so it is derivative of S1310 rather than independent corroboration.","next":"Retrieve IISH Item 49 and any transmission logs; compare with domestic Central Committee and South Blitar records, surviving couriers and correspondence.","year":1978},{"id":"C2799","text":"Academic citations to the Omar Dani Mahmilub case identify different transcript configurations: Oey Hong Lee cites Vol. II, pp.131 and 140, while van der Kroef cites book I, pp.3, 287 and 320 for the 23 December 1966 case file; these references cannot yet be reconciled.","period":"1966 trial; citations published 1972 and 1976","type":"Bibliographic comparison and retrieval lead","source_ids":["S1478","S1479"],"confidence":"High for what the two articles cite; no conclusion about whether these are separate editions, transcript sections, or a multi-book set.","basis":"Oey also notes name spellings Dani/Dhani; van der Kroef says Oejeng Soewargana supplied the mimeographed transcripts. Neither author citation gives us the underlying pages.","next":"Locate and inspect the cited copies, title pages, volume statements, pagination, table of contents and file provenance; compare with Cornell and Mahkamah Agung holdings.","year":1966},{"id":"C2800","text":"ANRI's Sukarno-era defense/security guide describes KOTI inventory item 63 as a one-folder 1966 copy of the Mahmilub verdict file in the Omar Dani case; this is a judgment-file finding aid, not evidence that ANRI holds the multi-volume trial transcript cited by scholars.","period":"1966 record; guide published 2024","type":"Official archival finding-aid description","source_ids":["S118"],"confidence":"High for the guide's item description; no confidence assigned to the unseen file contents.","basis":"ANRI guide, KOTI item 63, directly rechecked. Item title and folder count locate the file only; its contents were not opened.","next":"Request the folder, confirm file title/page count and whether judgment, attachments, hearing minutes or transcript copies are present; then search separate transcript holdings.","year":1966},{"id":"C2801","text":"ANRI's 1966–1967 photo inventory places the Mahmilub proceedings against former Air Force commander Omar Dhani at Bappenas in Jakarta from 5 to 24 December 1966 and lists individual courtroom photographs; one caption uses the name form “Omar Said,” which should not be silently normalized to Omar Dani/Dhani.","period":"1966-12-05 to 1966-12-24","type":"Official photographic finding aid and caption metadata","source_ids":["S1338"],"confidence":"High for the inventory's dates, captions and item numbers; no claim about what the photographs prove.","basis":"ANRI photo inventory printed p.555; items 4679, 4680 and adjacent captions refer to Omar Dhani and the presiding judge; item 4689 says “Men/Pangau Omar Said.” Negatives and prints were not inspected.","next":"Request photo items/negatives and verify each caption against the image, trial chronology, court roster and contemporary reporting.","year":1966},{"id":"C2802","text":"A 2003 Hukumonline table gives Omar Dani's Mahmilub judgment number as PTS-017/MLB/XIV/OD/1966, dated 23 December 1966, and states that its list of tried figures comes from the 1994 State Secretariat history.","period":"1966 judgment; reported 2003","type":"Secondary legal-history summary and locator","source_ids":["S1482"],"confidence":"High that the article prints the number/date and identifies its source; the original judgment and the 1994 book were not inspected here.","basis":"Hukumonline's table is a secondary transcription of a 1994 state-history list, with possible copying or typographical errors; it is not a primary court record.","next":"Obtain the judgment, docket and 1994 history pages and compare the case number, date, spelling, offense, sentence and source note.","year":1966},{"id":"C2803","text":"Presidential Instruction No. 5/1979 ordered the State Secretary to perfect a White Book on G30S/PKI already prepared by KOPKAMTIB, allowed a cross-agency team, set the task deadline at the end of December 1979, and charged costs to the State Secretariat budget.","period":"1979-03-17 to 1979-12-31","type":"Primary presidential instruction establishing official narrative-production process","source_ids":["S1480"],"confidence":"High for the instruction's wording and institutional procedure; it does not establish the accuracy or exact later edits of the narrative.","basis":"Official two-page PDF, clauses 1–4 and preamble. The instruction proves a revision mandate and state funding route, not the historical truth of claims in a resulting white paper.","next":"Locate the initial KOPKAMTIB white-book edition, the State Secretariat revised edition and cross-agency drafting files; collate text changes and contributor records.","year":1979},{"id":"C2804","text":"Mardanas Safwan's state-published biography recounts an expanded PKI Politburo meeting on 28 August 1965 and says the meeting delegated full authority to Aidit; its notes cite the 1978 KOPKAMTIB publication G 30 S/PKI, pp.77 and 121.","period":"Event claimed for 1965-08-28; narrative published 1981/1982","type":"Official military/state narrative with an identified citation chain","source_ids":["S1481"],"confidence":"High that the biography makes the claim and names its citation; low-to-moderate that the meeting and delegation occurred exactly as narrated without contemporaneous minutes or direct reading of the cited book.","basis":"Safwan, printed pp.67–78; p.59 note cites KOPKAMTIB 1978 p.77, and p.78 note 6 cites p.121. In the account, silence in the meeting is interpreted as full delegation and Aidit is said to set out an operation plan. The 1978 source itself was not read, and the 1979 Inpres shows a later revision process without disclosing its exact edits.","next":"Obtain the 1978 KOPKAMTIB book and 1979 revised White Book, compare page-level wording, and seek 28 August attendance, minutes, correspondence and dispatch records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2805","text":"Safwan's attributed 28 August account conflicts with later courtroom accounts: Njono testified that the Politburo did not approve the officers' initiative to act first, while Munir described the briefing as ending without a decision or assent/dissent; the full meeting procedure remains unresolved.","period":"1965-08-27/28; testimony reported 1966 and 1973; state narrative 1981/82","type":"Cross-source conflict between defendant testimony and official retrospective narrative","source_ids":["S1353","S1376","S1481"],"confidence":"High that the sources present different accounts; low confidence in selecting one account absent contemporaneous minutes, dispatches and independent participants.","basis":"Compare C2400 and C2439 with Safwan pp.72–74. The court statements are later testimony under military prosecution; Safwan's account is state-published and cites KOPKAMTIB. The terms 'briefing', 'expanded Politburo meeting' and later Daily Board decisions may refer to different moments; do not flatten them into a single unanimous decision.","next":"Find Politburo attendance lists/minutes, daily-board notes, the alleged letter to Sukarno, courier/radio records, and all attendees' testimony in original transcript form.","year":1965},{"id":"C2806","text":"In a 23 May 1960 speech excerpt published in New Age, Aidit said the PKI’s lessons from the 1945–48 revolutionary period included combining rural guerrilla struggle, workers’ revolutionary action in occupied towns and work within the opposing armed forces; in the same excerpt he called for consolidating a National Front and building a broad mass party.","period":"1960-05-23 speech; excerpt published 1960-06-19","type":"Contemporaneous party-program statement","source_ids":["S1484"],"confidence":"High that the excerpt publicly states this line; low as evidence of 1960s implementation or operational military capacity.","basis":"The New Age item identifies itself as an excerpt from the concluding portions of Aidit’s speech. It records the party’s retrospective theory and public program; it does not show what units, training, weapons, orders or actual activity existed in 1960 or 1965.","next":"Locate the full Indonesian speech and official PKI publication, compare New Age translation with Indonesian text and the 1966 Political Bureau self-criticism; seek training, liaison and armed-force records to establish implementation.","year":1960},{"id":"C2807","text":"Aidit’s public 1960 discussion of three forms of struggle coexisted with the PKI line that Lin and Galway characterize for 1950–65 as cross-class cooperation and a nonviolent parliamentary strategy; the difference requires separate examination of doctrine, actual organizational channels and operational capacity.","period":"1950–1965","type":"Historiographical comparison / analytical distinction","source_ids":["S1484","S1325","S261","S1329"],"confidence":"Medium: the statements and interpretation are documented, but how doctrine translated into practice varied by period and organizational unit.","basis":"Aidit’s 1960 text is a partial public speech excerpt; Lin and Galway’s 2023 argument is an intellectual-history interpretation of leadership writings and speeches. The juxtaposition does not settle whether a policy was militarized, peaceful, or inconsistently applied in every locality; it prevents inferring a standing guerrilla force from strategic language alone.","next":"Compare the complete Aidit speech, PKI program and congress decisions, 1966 self-criticism, Fifth Force records, Biro Chusus evidence and provincial training/weapon records in date order.","year":1950},{"id":"C2808","text":"Ho Hui Ling reports that the CPM Central Committee announced resumption of armed struggle on 1 June 1968 and that the first renewed attack inside Peninsular Malaysia occurred on 17 June near Kroh after crossing from southern Thailand; the article situates this in the 1948–60 Emergency-era war and a continuing cross-border organization.","period":"1948–1968","type":"Comparative party chronology reported by secondary scholarship","source_ids":["S1483","S1314","S1379"],"confidence":"Medium-high for the dates as reported in the reviewed literature; primary CPM orders and Malaysian incident records were not read.","basis":"Ho Hui Ling’s full article describes the 1 June decision, 17 June ambush and Thai-border base. It is a secondary reconstruction, partly drawing on government white papers and later histories. Do not treat this as evidence about the PKI or equate the CPM’s armed structure with South Blitar.","next":"Compare the CPM Central Committee declaration, Malaysia’s 1968 white paper and police/army incident reports for Kroh; locate Chin Peng/Musa Ahmad records and Thai-border camp/recruiting evidence.","year":1948},{"id":"C2809","text":"Ho Hui Ling reports estimates of nearly 800 CPM fighters by 1964, compared with fewer than 300 in 1960, and about 2,047 before the 1968 renewed campaign, including a reported composition across Malaysian and Thai Chinese, Malay, Orang Asli and other recruits.","period":"1960–1968","type":"Attributed organizational strength estimate","source_ids":["S1483"],"confidence":"Low for the underlying counts and ethnic breakdown; high that the article reports them.","basis":"The figures are attributed to Ward and Miraflor (2003) and Mohd Reduan (1993), not to a contemporaneous roster. Definitions of fighter/member/recruit and the counting date are not fully transparent in the excerpt reviewed. Do not use as a directly comparable ratio against PKI party membership, South Blitar cells or U.S. estimates.","next":"Find the cited books and their source notes; search CPM regimental lists, Thai and Malaysian security files, demobilization records and individual veteran rolls; retain range and date definitions.","year":1960},{"id":"C2810","text":"Ho Hui Ling describes CPM external channels through China that reportedly included protection for leaders in Beijing, training, a radio station, advice and financial assistance; her account cites Ho (2010), Miller (1972) and Chin Peng’s memoir.","period":"1956–1974, as reported in a 2015 article","type":"Foreign-support mechanism reported by secondary scholarship","source_ids":["S1483","S1486"],"confidence":"High that the two later studies report CPM channels to China including advice, training, radio facilities and financial assistance; low-to-moderate for exact dates, amounts and executed transfers without underlying records.","basis":"Ho Hui Ling’s article cites Ho (2010), Miller (1972) and Chin Peng’s memoir for Beijing protection, training, broadcasting/advice and finance. Ong’s preliminary paper quotes Chin Peng’s memoir about an initial transmitter request, a 1967 assurance from Mao and the 1969 VMR launch, then analyzes VMR transcript material accessed through Singapore ISD. Neither study presents the Chinese authorization or payment ledger here. The reported CPM channel is comparative, not evidence that PRC supplied equivalent support or operational control to the PKI.","next":"Inspect Ho 2010, Miller 1972, Chin Peng memoir and relevant Chinese/CPM records for authorization, dates, recipients, broadcasts and actual transfers.","year":1956},{"id":"C2811","text":"The Malaysian government’s 1971 white paper states that its purpose was to inform the public and strengthen cooperation with the government; it frames the CPM threat as a reason for parallel security measures and economic/social integration, and says its account continues the November 1968 white paper.","period":"1968–1971; state publication dated 1971-10-01","type":"Contemporaneous official state-policy statement","source_ids":["S1485"],"confidence":"High that the document states this purpose and framing; it is not independent evidence that all CPM accusations are correct.","basis":"Foreword and Introduction, printed pp.v–vi and paras.1–2. The document directly evidences Malaysian government public messaging and its stated strategy; it has an explicit security-mobilization purpose and should be compared with CPM primary documents and neutral/independent records.","next":"Obtain the original November 1968 White Paper and its production files; compare the 1968 edition with the 1971 reprint and extract which claims were new.","year":1968},{"id":"C2812","text":"The 1971 Malaysian white paper quotes an alleged captured CPM directive instructing small local units of three to five members to organize underground cells; suitable recruits would receive several days’ training and pistols, mines or grenades, with temporary withdrawal to Thai territory considered if an assault unit faced serious danger.","period":"Directive quoted in 1971; relates to CPM build-up from 1968","type":"Government publication quoting alleged captured operational document","source_ids":["S1485"],"confidence":"High that the white paper prints this excerpt; low-to-moderate that the redacted excerpt is complete, authentic and representative without the original document and custody record.","basis":"White paper para.21 reproduces a “captured document” and refers to infiltration-group instructions. The original item, translation, capture circumstances and unredacted context are unavailable in the reviewed publication; this is not proof every local cell was armed or trained as directed.","next":"Find the captured paper, Malay/Chinese original, seizure inventory, forensic/authentication report, interrogation transcript and evidence of delivery/receipt of weapons.","year":1971},{"id":"C2813","text":"The 1971 Malaysian white paper quotes arrested or surrendered persons describing village contacts who purchased and stored food at home for later collection by CPM units, alongside small-group reconnaissance, propaganda and recruitment.","period":"1968–1971, as described in a 1971 state paper","type":"Official report of attributed detainee/surrender accounts","source_ids":["S1485"],"confidence":"High that the paper publishes these accounts; low-to-moderate for independent verification of individual cases and their representativeness.","basis":"White paper paras.18–20 and 36–38 quote anonymized statements and report food stores, local suppliers, movement intelligence and logistics. Interrogation conditions, dates of statements, full original transcripts and local corroboration were not available; do not generalize to all villages or supporters.","next":"Locate the individual statements and trial files, compare with village testimony and household/market records, and identify precise anonymized locations from archive originals.","year":1968},{"id":"C2814","text":"In his memoir as quoted by Ong, Chin Peng said an earlier effort to obtain Chinese transmitter equipment for a Vietnam-based station was rejected, while after a late-January 1967 meeting with Mao he was told that CPM would receive radio-station facilities.","period":"Before 1964–1967; recalled in a memoir published 2003","type":"Attributed memoir statement about foreign-support negotiation","source_ids":["S1486"],"confidence":"High that Ong quotes the memoir this way; medium-to-low for the underlying meeting, request and authorization pending the cited memoir and Chinese records.","basis":"Ong, p.2, quotes Chin Peng, My Side of the Story, pp.442–449. No Chinese meeting record, facilities agreement or transmission-equipment procurement file was consulted. The chronology is a participant’s retrospective account.","next":"Read Chin Peng’s cited pages directly; find Chinese Communist Party international liaison, radio engineering, budget, base-access and equipment files and Vietnam-side correspondence.","year":1964},{"id":"C2815","text":"Ong reports that the CPM’s Voice of the Malayan Revolution began broadcasts on 15 November 1969 from a restricted military base in Hunan under the code Project 691, in Malay, Chinese, English and Tamil; he attributes station staffing above 80 to a 2004 Chin Peng interview volume.","period":"1969-11-15 onward","type":"Foreign-supported propaganda-channel chronology","source_ids":["S1486"],"confidence":"Medium for the date and station details as reported; the project file, facility logs, broadcast recordings and staff list were not directly opened.","basis":"Ong, p.2, cites Chin Peng 2003 pp.442–449 for negotiations and Chin & Hack 2004 p.368 for station location/workforce; he says transcript material was accessed through the Singapore Internal Security Department. A separate US$50 billion facilities-cost estimate in this paper is from a website and is explicitly excluded as unverified.","next":"Locate VMR original recordings/transcripts, engineering and station logs, Hunan base records, personnel records, budget and procurement files; compare with Malaysian interception logs and the 1971 white paper.","year":1969},{"id":"C2816","text":"In a 1987 oral-history interview, Marshall Green said the United States provided Indonesia medicines under an arrangement he described as secret but lawful, and that Indonesia sold some it did not need to earn foreign exchange.","period":"Late 1965–1966 as recalled in 1987; shipment and resale dates unspecified","type":"Retrospective first-person account of medical assistance and resale","source_ids":["S807"],"confidence":"High that Green made this statement; medium for the underlying shipments and resale because the account is retrospective and no transaction records are present.","basis":"LOC/ADST Green transcript, digital lines 131–134, after his account of first private contact with Suharto; interview conducted by Robert J. Martens on 12 May 1987. Green gives no product list, shipment date, quantities, invoices, buyer or recipient record. His account does not resolve the separate November 1965 covert commercial-cover and credit dispute in FRUS d170–d181.","next":"Find the final medical list, credit/financing plan, invoices, customs/shipping records, Army or state-store receipts, resale accounts and Green’s cited report to Washington; compare Soemantri/Sukendro and Bunnell testimony.","year":1965},{"id":"C2817","text":"Green recalled that the U.S. Embassy team had no direct count or eyewitness confirmation of the killings when asked for an estimate in early February 1966; he said the 300,000 figure was an average of country-team members’ guesses and later said a range of roughly 50,000–550,000 would have represented the uncertainty better.","period":"Estimate made early February 1966; recalled 12 May 1987","type":"Retrospective account of how a diplomatic death estimate was generated","source_ids":["S807"],"confidence":"High that Green described this method and uncertainty; the actual worksheets, individual estimates and underlying local reports have not been found, so this does not establish the true death toll.","basis":"LOC/ADST Green transcript, digital lines 152–156. Green said reports were vague and that Embassy staff had seen no bodies or met a witness; these are his 1987 recollections, not proof that no better information existed elsewhere in the U.S. government or in Indonesia.","next":"Locate Green’s February 1966 briefing cable, country-team estimate worksheets, consular/mission reports and Washington briefing records; compare the estimate’s geographic/time denominator with Indonesian and local victim documentation.","year":1966},{"id":"C2818","text":"In the 1987 interview Green attributed the PKI’s rapid collapse partly to its mass membership being spread thin, insufficient cadre training and discipline, and inability to withstand a crisis; this is his retrospective diagnosis, not an independently demonstrated causal account.","period":"1965 collapse as recalled 12 May 1987","type":"Former U.S. ambassador’s retrospective causal interpretation","source_ids":["S807"],"confidence":"High for what Green said; low for its explanatory weight absent independent PKI membership, training, discipline and branch-level evidence.","basis":"LOC/ADST Green transcript, digital lines 140–145. His answer follows an interviewer’s comparison with Chinese revolutionary warfare; it remains a memory-based diagnosis, not a neutral measure of party discipline or branch capability.","next":"Compare PKI member/cadre records, training and branch reports, emergency leadership correspondence, provincial party breakdowns, survivor accounts and other contemporary analyses.","year":1965},{"id":"C2819","text":"In the same interview Green agreed that the PKI’s reliance on Sukarno left it vulnerable after his authority weakened and added that association with the Chinese Communist Party made it more exposed because he viewed Chinese people as unpopular in Indonesia.","period":"1965 collapse as recalled 12 May 1987","type":"Former U.S. ambassador’s retrospective political interpretation","source_ids":["S807"],"confidence":"High for the interview content; low for the causal claims until tested against Indonesian public opinion, party records, Indonesian–Chinese community distinctions and regional evidence.","basis":"LOC/ADST Green transcript, digital lines 146–147. The reliance-on-Sukarno proposition was introduced by interviewer Martens and endorsed by Green; Green added the CCP association explanation. Do not collapse the PRC government, ethnic Chinese Indonesians and PKI into a single group.","next":"Find PKI/Sukarno correspondence and public statements; regional evidence on attitudes to China and Chinese Indonesians; compare membership, repression and survival patterns across regions.","year":1965},{"id":"C2820","text":"Green recalled that roughly eleven or twelve Army battalions he described as pro-communist were sent to outer islands and “never heard of again”; he named no units, dates, destinations, orders or personnel outcomes.","period":"1965–1966 as recalled 12 May 1987","type":"Retrospective military personnel-transfer lead","source_ids":["S807"],"confidence":"High that Green made this statement; low for the number, unit identity, movement, motive or subsequent fate because no operational records or unit names are supplied.","basis":"LOC/ADST Green transcript, digital lines 203–205. This is an undated recollection by a U.S. ambassador; the label “pro-communist” is Green’s and requires unit-specific verification. FRUS d176 (S470) separately reports one brigade returned from the Malaysia confrontation theater to Central/East Java in Nov 1965; it does not identify the units or corroborate Green’s 11–12 battalions-to-outer-islands recollection. This is a distinct movement lead, not confirmation of the same claim.","next":"Identify battalions transferred to outer-island commands in late 1965–1966; seek movement orders, rosters, relief/replacement records, command histories and family/personnel tracing. Compare separately with the one-brigade Central/East Java redeployment in S470.","year":1965},{"id":"C2821","text":"A U.S. Embassy cover report dated 28 February 1968 says it received a translated 17 February report from Indonesian Army General Staff I/SUAD-I, based on captured documents and interrogation of PKI captives, via businessman and political activist Husein Ahmaduddin, who had contacts in SUAD-I.","period":"1968-02-17 to 1968-02-28; East Java","type":"Document provenance and intelligence-transmission chain","source_ids":["S1487"],"confidence":"High that the Embassy record describes this provenance and transmission; the underlying interrogation and captured documents were not accessed.","basis":"Embassy cover note at PDF/OCR lines 14–16; the cover identifies Ahmaduddin as intermediary and attributes the enclosure to SUAD-I. It does not provide the original TNI file, source identities or interrogation protocol.","next":"Obtain the original SUAD-I file and distribution log, Ahmaduddin’s transmission context, captured-document inventory, and the interrogation records and conditions.","year":1968},{"id":"C2822","text":"The translated TNI report attributes to captive Subandi the statement that the South Blitar KOMPRO was headed by Tarmudji, described as a former East Java CDB member; it assigns Subandi to supplies/logistics, Ucup to PP, Parto to GRMAS, Suwarno to the armed-struggle section, and Sono to BAGITROP.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar","type":"Captured-person testimony reproduced in military intelligence report","source_ids":["S1487"],"confidence":"High that the report makes these attributions; low-to-moderate for exact identity, authority and actual duties without independent rosters or party records.","basis":"S1487, enclosure section III, PDF/OCR lines 124–145. The report inconsistently spells the section as PERDJUTA/PERMUTA and the subsection as GRMAS/GERMAS; aliases and real-name key are unavailable.","next":"Find Tarmudji, Subandi and Ucup/Parto/Sono/Suwarno identity keys, Kompro rosters, captured papers, local trial files and independent East Java CDB accounts.","year":1967},{"id":"C2823","text":"The 17 February TNI report describes East Java Kompro and city-trio structures under the slogan “villages surround the cities,” with three organizational, armed-struggle, and agitation/propaganda sections; it lists multiple mountain-region Kompro and says a Wilis-area unit is only suspected.","period":"1967–1968; East Java","type":"Contemporaneous military intelligence description of clandestine organization","source_ids":["S1487"],"confidence":"High that the report presents this structure; low-to-moderate for how broadly or consistently it existed because the report relies on captives and captured documents and gives no complete rosters or execution records.","basis":"S1487, enclosure sections I–II and IV, PDF/OCR lines 57–123, 168–194. Distinguish the report’s asserted organizational map from independently confirmed operational capacity.","next":"Find region-by-region membership, courier, meeting, finance, and training records; compare captured documents with Rewang, Liem, Hearman and provincial trials.","year":1967},{"id":"C2824","text":"The TNI report says a planned general conflict for early 1968 did not begin because concrete readiness reports from other regions were absent; Rewang later recalled that no start date had been set and that an armed campaign required approval by a meeting of provincial committees. The decision chronology is unresolved.","period":"1967–1968; South Blitar and other regions","type":"Conflict between interrogation-based military intelligence and retrospective participant testimony","source_ids":["S1487","S1310","S1317"],"confidence":"High that the two sources make these different claims; neither establishes a single nationwide order or actual launch date.","basis":"S1487, enclosure section IV(4)(a), PDF/OCR lines 184–187; Hearman, “Guerrillas, Guns, and Knives?”, printed pp.80–81 and note 104, based on her Rewang interview; Liem’s 2004 interview-based account is separately registered in S1317. The TNI report is a translated account based on captives/captured papers; Rewang’s statements are later participant recollections. No minutes, regional readiness reports or original captured papers resolve whether the sources describe different stages or conflict.","next":"Locate the cited captured documents, any national conference agenda/minutes, regional readiness reports, emergency-committee instructions and Rewang’s original interview recording.","year":1967},{"id":"C2825","text":"The TNI report attributes to captive-derived material a split between a Sudisman bloc described as Maoist and a Sakirman bloc labeled “Modern Revisionist”; no PKI factional minutes or correspondence confirming this formulation were obtained.","period":"1967–1968; underground PKI","type":"Interrogation-derived account of alleged internal political divisions","source_ids":["S1487"],"confidence":"High that this wording appears in the report; low for the faction labels, boundaries and consequences as an accurate party-wide map.","basis":"S1487, enclosure section III, PDF/OCR lines 168–170. These are the Army report’s labels, not neutral or independently verified names for stable organized factions.","next":"Compare Sudisman, Sakirman, Ismail Bakri and Rewang correspondence, trial defenses, party documents, and separate participant interviews.","year":1967},{"id":"C2826","text":"The TNI report says captives described PKI political education as condemning the earlier military-commissar approach because officers cultivated by the party had instead exploited the party during the September 30 Movement; this is a reported post-defeat diagnosis, not proof of the earlier system’s full operation.","period":"1965 and retrospective account in 1968; East Java","type":"Captured-person account of PKI internal criticism of military-political relations","source_ids":["S1487"],"confidence":"High that the report reproduces this criticism; moderate-to-low that it accurately reflects a collective party decision or the actual reach of the earlier commissar system.","basis":"S1487, enclosure section IV(4)(f), PDF/OCR lines 188–194; the report does not attach the cited curriculum, meeting minutes or military-cultivation roster.","next":"Compare with 1966 PKI self-criticism, Fifth Force/armed-forces advisory proposals, Biro Chusus papers, officer recruitment lists and regional military records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2827","text":"The U.S. Embassy cover report says it lacked collateral information to confirm that the reported Blitar “revenge killings” were committed by a cohesive PKI group; it notes local religious/social tension and undisciplined, underpaid soldiers involved in robbery as alternative explanations.","period":"1967–1968; Blitar and Java","type":"Contemporaneous U.S. Embassy assessment explicitly limiting attribution","source_ids":["S1487"],"confidence":"High that the Embassy made this caveat; it does not identify the actual perpetrators of any specific killing.","basis":"S1487, Embassy cover report, PDF/OCR lines 27–41. The Embassy’s alternatives are analytical possibilities, not established alternative perpetrators; distinguish these caveats from the TNI enclosure’s allegations.","next":"Identify each killing by date, location, victim, witness, arrest file and unit; compare local police, military, village, family and court records.","year":1967},{"id":"C2828","text":"The TNI report recommended intensified territorial/intelligence work, special combat troops because local forces were said to be insufficient, tighter firearms/ammunition controls and improved ordinary-soldier welfare; these are recommendations, not proof the measures were implemented.","period":"1968-02-17; South Malang and Blitar","type":"Indonesian Army General Staff operational recommendation","source_ids":["S1487"],"confidence":"High for the recommendations printed in the translated report; implementation and the underlying assessment of local units remain unverified.","basis":"S1487, enclosure section VI, PDF/OCR lines 211–219. The report is an Embassy translation; no signed implementation order or follow-up log is attached.","next":"Find Kodam VIII/Brawijaya operational orders, unit deployments, arms-control directives, welfare/pay records and subsequent situation reports.","year":1968},{"id":"C2829","text":"ANRI’s diplomatic-archives guide lists Sukarno’s opening address to KIAPMA at Hotel Indonesia on 17 October 1965 as one stencilled sheet, inventory No.817.","period":"1965-10-17; Jakarta","type":"Archival catalogue fact","source_ids":["S1501"],"confidence":"High that the finding aid contains this entry; it does not establish access to or full content of the underlying speech.","basis":"Official guide, printed p.31, item 234.","next":"Retrieve and inspect ANRI record No.817 and its file cover.","year":1965},{"id":"C2830","text":"ANRI’s virtual exhibition caption attributes an English-language one-page typescript for Sukarno’s KIAPMA opening address to inventory No.817; the official inventory instead describes No.817 as a one-sheet Indonesian-language stencilled text. The relation between the two witnesses is unresolved.","period":"1965 speech; 2023 gallery scan","type":"Conflicting source description","source_ids":["S1488","S1501"],"confidence":"High that the gallery image and catalogue entry differ in language/description; low for whether the image is a translation, alternate copy or mismapped item.","basis":"Visual gallery inspection; ANRI guide p.31/item 234. Do not treat the English image as the original or complete transcript.","next":"Request underlying No.817, provenance of gallery scan, Cabinet Secretariat number 1181/65 and any English translation file.","year":1965},{"id":"C2831","text":"The Dutch National Archives finding aid describes a physical folder on KIAPMA in Jakarta dated 17–21 October 1965.","period":"1965-10-17 to 1965-10-21; Jakarta","type":"Archival catalogue fact","source_ids":["S1489"],"confidence":"High that the finding aid gives this folder title and span; no conference act or diplomatic view can be inferred from the title.","basis":"Archive 2.05.188, printed p.58, item 761; folder contents not opened.","next":"Request item 761 and identify its creator, enclosures and actual dates.","year":1965},{"id":"C2832","text":"Peking Review reported KIAPMA took place 17–20 October 1965 and had more than 200 delegates from 55 countries/regions and four international organizations.","period":"1965-10-17 to 1965-10-20; Jakarta","type":"Contemporary published report and attendance estimate","source_ids":["S1490"],"confidence":"High that the periodical reports these dates and numbers; moderate-to-low for attendance count because no roll or independent count was found.","basis":"Peking Review No.45, pp.16–18; ideological report.","next":"Locate attendance lists, proceedings, invitations and independent Indonesian/foreign press coverage.","year":1965},{"id":"C2833","text":"World Outlook quoted Le Figaro correspondent J. Jacquet-Francillon reporting 160 delegates representing 54 nations at KIAPMA.","period":"1965-10; Jakarta","type":"Contemporary press report quoted by a later outlet","source_ids":["S1498","S1490"],"confidence":"High that World Outlook prints this attribution; low-to-moderate for the count and its comparability with Peking Review because the original Le Figaro issue and counting definitions are missing.","basis":"World Outlook No.31, pp.6–7; its quotation is a mediated report, not an independent second count.","next":"Retrieve the original Le Figaro dispatch and compare registered delegates, organizations, observers and countries/regions.","year":1965},{"id":"C2834","text":"Peking Review reported military and armoured security around KIAPMA, a curfew and restrictions on delegates’ telephone/telegraph access, and arrests of Indonesian conference workers.","period":"1965-10-16 to 1965-10-21; Jakarta","type":"Contemporary participant/party-state report","source_ids":["S1490","S1491","S1498"],"confidence":"High that the publications made these reports; moderate-to-low for their scope and details until rosters, police/military records and independent delegate accounts are checked.","basis":"Peking Review No.45 pp.16–18; Alley is a participant account with strong political framing; World Outlook quotes Le Figaro.","next":"Find conference staff lists, arrest/detention files, hotel logs, press restrictions and independent participants’ notes.","year":1965},{"id":"C2835","text":"Peking Review reported that Indonesian troops searched the Chinese Commercial Counsellor’s Office on 16 October and that China’s delegation protested; it said delegation head Tang Ming-chao did not attend Sukarno’s opening.","period":"1965-10-16 to 1965-10-17; Jakarta","type":"Foreign-government allegation and reported diplomatic reaction","source_ids":["S1490","S1494"],"confidence":"High that PRC publication and Reuters issue preview report the allegation/protest; the precise object searched and the reason for the delegation head’s absence are not independently established.","basis":"Peking Review No.45 p.17; Straits Times Reuters preview says China protested an alleged search.","next":"Obtain Chinese diplomatic protest, Army unit records, Indonesian MFA property register and conference attendance record.","year":1965},{"id":"C2836","text":"Peking Review No.46 quoted an Army Strategic Reserve Command statement acknowledging that a 328th Battalion unit entered and searched the Commercial Counsellor’s Office on 16 October, while saying that nothing improper was found.","period":"1965-10-16; Jakarta","type":"Military statement quoted in a foreign publication","source_ids":["S1493"],"confidence":"High that the PRC publication reproduces this attributed statement; moderate for the exact statement until the original Army release is found.","basis":"Peking Review No.46; the Army release itself was not retrieved.","next":"Locate the Army press statement, its original Indonesian wording, issuing authority and any search/property record.","year":1965},{"id":"C2837","text":"Peking Review No.46 reproduced an Indonesian Foreign Ministry note dated 30 October reporting that military administrator Umar Wirahadikusumah apologized to Sukarno on 28 October over the 16 October search and promised no repeat.","period":"1965-10-28 to 1965-10-30; Jakarta","type":"Diplomatic report quoted by foreign publication","source_ids":["S1493"],"confidence":"High that the periodical reports this note and apology; moderate-to-low for exact wording and scope without the note itself.","basis":"This is the note as reproduced in a PRC party-state publication; the Indonesian original, delivery registry and diplomatic-property register were not obtained.","next":"Retrieve the original Foreign Ministry note, its delivery registry, Chinese response and Indonesian property/protocol records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2838","text":"Arthur J. Dommen’s 1966 article states that the Chinese Commercial Counsellor’s house and office were not officially listed as Embassy property at the Indonesian Foreign Ministry.","period":"1965; Jakarta","type":"Contemporaneous scholarly assertion about diplomatic property registration","source_ids":["S1495"],"confidence":"High that Dommen makes the footnote assertion; low-to-moderate for the underlying legal fact until the register and relevant Vienna-era diplomatic status rules are checked.","basis":"Dommen, note 16; full article text and Ministry register not reviewed. The statement does not itself settle immunity or legality.","next":"Locate Indonesian MFA diplomatic-property/protocol register and the relevant note verbales and site designations.","year":1965},{"id":"C2839","text":"Rewi Alley’s December 1965 essay says he was in Jakarta from 13–23 October to attend KIAPMA and describes raids, arrests and restrictions he says he witnessed or learned about.","period":"1965-10; Jakarta","type":"Participant’s contemporary published testimony","source_ids":["S1491"],"confidence":"High that the hosted text makes these statements; moderate for attendance, lower for specific events without identifying which were personally observed and independent corroboration.","basis":"Transcription of The People’s Voice; strong anti-imperialist and pro-PKI framing; original print issue not obtained.","next":"Locate original issue, named co-delegates, diaries and police/arrest records; separate firsthand observation from hearsay.","year":1965},{"id":"C2840","text":"Taomo Zhou reports AAJA was established in Jakarta in 1963 after a conference attended by journalists from 47 countries, and later had a Jakarta office and publication/training activities.","period":"1963–1965; Jakarta and transnational network","type":"Scholarly synthesis of organizational history","source_ids":["S1496"],"confidence":"High that the study reports these organizational features; moderate-high pending original statutes, membership lists and issues.","basis":"Peer-reviewed article; trace its references to AAJA publications and records.","next":"Obtain the 1963 Jakarta Declaration, founding meeting record, membership register and early Afro-Asian Journalist issues.","year":1963},{"id":"C2841","text":"Zhou reports that the Indonesian Army searched the Jakarta AAJA headquarters in December 1965, confiscated documents, cut external communications and arrested three people.","period":"1965-12; Jakarta","type":"Scholarly account of repression of a transnational press organization","source_ids":["S1496"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this sequence; moderate for occurrence and individual identities because cited support includes an AAJA publication and a later interview.","basis":"Zhou cites Afro-Asian Journalist 1966a p.39 and interview with Liz Morrison; no Indonesian search inventory/arrest file retrieved.","next":"Find the AAJA magazine article, Indonesian police/military files, office lease and arrest/detention records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2842","text":"An AAJA cable reproduced in a 15 January 1966 U.S. Embassy file protested the Indonesian Armed Forces’ arrest of Joesoef, identified in the cable as the Association’s acting secretary-general.","period":"1966-01-15; Beijing / Jakarta","type":"Archival record of an organization’s protest","source_ids":["S1497"],"confidence":"High that the cable and allegation appear in the Embassy file; the cable alone does not independently establish the circumstances or legal basis of arrest.","basis":"NARA RG84 Entry P339 Box24 scan, first page; OCR/name spelling uncertain.","next":"Find the arrest warrant, detainee registry, court file, original AAJA cable and Joesoef’s full identity record.","year":1966},{"id":"C2843","text":"The AAJA representatives’ 15 January press-conference statement said direct calls and letters from Jakarta had not reached the secretariat and the office telephone was disconnected; it announced a temporary withdrawal of the secretariat from Jakarta.","period":"1965-10 to 1966-01; Jakarta / Beijing","type":"Organizational statement recorded in a U.S. diplomatic file","source_ids":["S1497"],"confidence":"High that the statement reports these conditions and a temporary withdrawal; moderate for communication failure and its duration pending carrier/telegraph logs.","basis":"NARA RG84 scan pp.1–3; noisy OCR. Statement is interested testimony.","next":"Locate telephone/telegraph service records, AAJA minutes, correspondence and exact December departure date.","year":1965},{"id":"C2844","text":"The same press-conference account said the All-China Journalists’ Association offered temporary premises/facilities to the AAJA secretariat in Beijing.","period":"1966-01; Beijing","type":"Organization’s report of host assistance","source_ids":["S1497"],"confidence":"High that AAJA publicly reported this offer; this proves the stated offer, not how long the facilities were supplied or the degree of PRC control.","basis":"NARA file is an Embassy rendering/report of the conference, not a Chinese association contract or budget.","next":"Find All-China Journalists’ Association records, space/funding agreements and later AAJA office administration files.","year":1966},{"id":"C2845","text":"The AAJA fourth enlarged Secretariat plenary met in Beijing on 20–24 April 1966; its published communiqué says it supported temporary withdrawal from Jakarta and kept the Secretariat’s working seat in Beijing until the next conference.","period":"1966-04-20 to 1966-04-24; Beijing","type":"Formal communiqué published by Xinhua/People’s Daily","source_ids":["S1506","S1499"],"confidence":"High for the dates and decisions reported in the communiqué; moderate for actual implementation beyond what later periodicals report.","basis":"People’s Daily, 25 Apr 1966 p.3; Le Monde/AFP independently reports 19 country representatives and Beijing seat.","next":"Find meeting minutes, attendance list, motion wording and Algerian 1967 conference file.","year":1966},{"id":"C2846","text":"Le Monde’s AFP report said the April 1966 AAJA plenary adopted anti-U.S./Vietnam and anti-Indonesian-regime resolutions but adopted no anti-revisionist text despite efforts by Chinese delegates.","period":"1966-04-20 to 1966-04-24; Beijing","type":"Contemporary news-agency report about conference debate","source_ids":["S1499"],"confidence":"High that AFP/Le Monde reported this; moderate for the internal debate because minutes, amendments and individual votes are unavailable.","basis":"Subscription-restricted short report; compare with official communiqué but neither supplies the full debate transcript.","next":"Retrieve the draft resolutions, minutes, delegate interventions and correspondence among member organizations.","year":1966},{"id":"C2847","text":"Djawoto’s 16 April 1966 press statement says he had requested release from his ambassadorial post about two months earlier, received no reply from Jakarta, and then announced on his own initiative that he was no longer ambassador from 6 a.m. that day.","period":"1966-02 to 1966-04-16; Beijing / Jakarta","type":"Contemporaneous self-report by ambassador","source_ids":["S1500","S1502"],"confidence":"High that Djawoto publicly said this; moderate for the timing and receipt of his cable until the original transmission and Indonesian response records are found.","basis":"Watch65/GerakIngatan English PDF; Peking Review reproduces related statement.","next":"Find outgoing cable, Indonesian MFA incoming register, Sukarno recall instruction, status of credentials and passport/citizenship actions.","year":1966},{"id":"C2848","text":"Djawoto’s statement explicitly presents the claim that the CIA and other imperialists played an important role in Indonesia’s post-October political shift as his “personal conviction.”","period":"1966-04-16; Beijing","type":"Contemporaneous political allegation attributed to speaker","source_ids":["S1500","S1502"],"confidence":"High that he made the allegation and marked it as personal conviction; this is not direct evidence of a CIA operation or specific causal mechanism.","basis":"Statement’s politically committed framing; no operation-specific evidence is attached.","next":"Test each proposed U.S. channel independently through FRUS/CIA records, Indonesian recipients, payments, instructions and timing.","year":1966},{"id":"C2849","text":"Hill reports Ibrahim Isa served as Indonesia’s resident representative at AAPSO in Cairo (1960–1965), led a pro-Sukarno Indonesian delegation to the January 1966 Tricontinental Conference in Havana and joined the Beijing CC-PKI Delegation staff after arriving from Havana.","period":"1960–1966; Cairo / Havana / Beijing","type":"Scholarly biography linking international solidarity and PKI exile organizations","source_ids":["S1503"],"confidence":"High that the article reports these posts; moderate-high pending travel, appointment and organizational files.","basis":"Hill cites McGregor/Hearman, Manap and Hearman; OISRAA, AAPSO, Tricontinental delegation and CC-PKI role are distinct bodies.","next":"Retrieve AAPSO appointment files, Havana delegate list, travel/passport record, OISRAA bulletins and Beijing Delegation roster.","year":1960},{"id":"C2850","text":"Jusuf Adjitorop’s autobiography, as cited by Hill, says he was sent to China for medical treatment in June 1964 and received a Politburo decision on 22 February 1966 authorizing him to lead the PKI Central Committee Delegation abroad.","period":"1964-06 to 1966-02-22; China","type":"Later participant autobiography cited in peer-reviewed research","source_ids":["S1503"],"confidence":"High that the autobiography as cited gives this account; moderate for its complete institutional authorization because the manuscript/minutes were not directly inspected.","basis":"Hill cites IISH Jusuf Adjitorop Papers, Bundle IV, Item 23, pp.12; the archive item is not directly retrieved.","next":"Request the IISH item, medical/travel record, original Politburo decision and Delegation roster.","year":1964},{"id":"C2851","text":"Hill reports Beijing recognized the CC-PKI Delegation as the sole legitimate PKI representation abroad and that it coordinated exile regrouping, publications and overseas party contacts.","period":"1966 onward; Beijing and exile networks","type":"Scholarly account of an external political-organizational channel","source_ids":["S1503"],"confidence":"High that Hill reports the recognition and activities; moderate for the scope of external authority; it does not establish control over domestic underground cells.","basis":"Article combines archival and exile sources; distinguish representation abroad from domestic command.","next":"Find Chinese recognition/liaison records, Delegation mandates, publication files and communication with specific Indonesian regional groups.","year":1966},{"id":"C2852","text":"Hill reports that the Chinese Communist Party host determined major conditions of Indonesian exiles’ residence, work and funding, while communications with Chinese authorities generally passed through the CC-PKI Delegation.","period":"1966 onward; China","type":"Scholarly interpretation of host-state administration and party gatekeeping","source_ids":["S1503"],"confidence":"High that this is the article’s documented interpretation; moderate for consistency across all exiles and years. It supports material/communications leverage but not by itself Chinese control of PKI doctrine or Indonesian operations.","basis":"Hill draws on interviews, memoirs and archives and also notes that exiles themselves ran much of their criticism/re-education.","next":"Seek Chinese liaison, residence, assignment and funding records alongside exiles who opposed or bypassed the Delegation.","year":1966},{"id":"C2853","text":"Hill reports that some exiles and military participants criticized Adjitorop’s call for cadres abroad to regroup in China as a strategic mistake that produced disputes and lacked a clear plan.","period":"1966–1968; exile networks","type":"Retrospective intra-party criticism","source_ids":["S1503"],"confidence":"High that the cited participants made this criticism; low-to-moderate as an objective assessment of the strategy because it is retrospective testimony from a divided exile community.","basis":"Hill cites Suhario Padmodiwiryo 2005 and interviews; record as factional testimony, not settled consensus.","next":"Read Padmodiwiryo pp.205, 214, Cingkan Syang documents, and interviews from Delegation supporters and opponents.","year":1966},{"id":"C2854","text":"Hill reports that an exile-organized BPKK attempted for more than six months in 1968 to convene a global cadres’ conference; after the Delegation withdrew support, Chinese authorities denied venues/infrastructure and recognized communications only when authorized by Adjitorop’s Delegation, so no conference occurred.","period":"1968; China","type":"Scholarly account of intra-exile dispute and host-state access mechanism","source_ids":["S1503"],"confidence":"High that the article reports this sequence; moderate pending the meeting request, host decision and correspondence.","basis":"Based on named exile interviews; it shows a reported effect on exile politics, not Chinese control over domestic PKI leadership.","next":"Find BPKK invitations/minutes, venue requests, Chinese-host replies and Delegation directives.","year":1968},{"id":"C2855","text":"Hill cites a later CPM history to report that several hundred Malayan Communist Party cadres completed political and military training at Nanjing Military Academy in the early 1960s; Hill separately says the identity of the Indonesian exile compound as that academy is inferred, not named by his interviewees.","period":"Early 1960s; China","type":"Secondary comparative lead about a Southeast Asian communist party","source_ids":["S1503"],"confidence":"High that Hill reports this comparison; moderate-to-low for the underlying number and facility identification until CPM/Chinese records are checked. It does not prove the PKI exiles received equivalent military training.","basis":"Hill cites Chin 2004 for CPM trainees and Zhou 2014 for the academy identification.","next":"Check Chin’s cited page, CPM participant records, Nanjing Academy archives and separate Indonesian exile training accounts.","year":1960},{"id":"C2856","text":"A Chinese Foreign Ministry note reproduced in Peking Review says China had sent more than 8,200 tons of equipment/materials for the Banjaran mill, exceeding 70% of the total, and had dispatched construction experts.","period":"1961–1966; Banjaran, West Java","type":"PRC government statement about economic aid","source_ids":["S1502"],"confidence":"High that the Ministry’s published note makes these claims; moderate for the quantities pending contracts, shipping/customs records and Indonesian receipts.","basis":"The 18 Apr 1966 note is reproduced in a PRC publication; not independently verified in this pass.","next":"Locate 1961 cooperation agreement, credit and equipment schedules, shipment manifests, port records and Indonesian plant inventories.","year":1961},{"id":"C2857","text":"The same Chinese Foreign Ministry note says China stopped its assistance to the Banjaran mill and withdrew its experts after the 15 April 1966 attack on its Jakarta Embassy.","period":"1966-04-15 to 1966-04-18; Jakarta / Banjaran","type":"PRC government statement on aid suspension and its stated reason","source_ids":["S1502"],"confidence":"High that the note declares suspension and withdrawal; moderate for the completion/withdrawal timeline and the causal explanation as a sole cause.","basis":"A unilateral diplomatic statement; no bilateral response or personnel departure/shipping logs retrieved.","next":"Find expert rosters, project progress reports, shipment contracts, Indonesian/Chinese minutes and later restart decisions.","year":1966},{"id":"C2858","text":"A 1991 JICA technical study reports Banjaran First Mill construction began in 1965, financed partly with Japanese reparations but mainly with Chinese financial aid; operations began at 30,000 spindles in 1967 and full-scale operation in 1968.","period":"1965–1968; Banjaran, West Java","type":"Later technical survey and retrospective industrial history","source_ids":["S1504"],"confidence":"High that the JICA report states this and documents a 1991 field survey; moderate-high for the mill timeline pending commissioning logs and Indonesian company records.","basis":"JICA field team surveyed the plant in 1991; later full operation followed the 1966 Chinese announcement of aid suspension and requires an intervening project history.","next":"Find PT Industri Sandang I board minutes, commissioning records, Japanese reparations accounting and resumed Chinese/Indonesian supply records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2859","text":"JICA’s 1991 field report lists Chinese-made 1964–65 machinery still in Banjaran First Mill equipment and reports production in 1989–90 using mainly Chinese-made machinery.","period":"1991 survey; machinery dated 1964–1965","type":"Later on-site equipment observation","source_ids":["S1504"],"confidence":"High that the survey reports these machines and production; moderate for precise provenance until equipment serials and plant inventories are checked.","basis":"A field observation 26 years later supports delivery/use continuity, not the exact 1965 shipment quantities or 1966 aid mechanism.","next":"Retrieve machinery inventory, serial plates, Chinese supplier records and post-1966 maintenance/replacement logs.","year":1991},{"id":"C2860","text":"The September 1966 Political Bureau self-criticism reprinted by the PKI Delegation in 1971 says the leadership committed “adventurism” by violating party organization principles and involving itself in the September 30 Movement without high mass consciousness, thereby isolating the Party from the masses.","period":"1966-09 retrospective account of 1965; Central Java / national party","type":"PKI Political Bureau self-criticism as republished by the exile Delegation","source_ids":["S1505"],"confidence":"High that the text makes this internal accusation; low-to-moderate as a complete factual description of G30S decision-making because it is retrospective, factional and not supported here by underlying minutes.","basis":"1971 compilation reproduces a text dated Central Java, September 1966; it frames the account within a post-defeat Maoist line.","next":"Compare with the August 1966 statement, Supardjo postmortem, Sjam/Untung/Aidit records, party minutes and regional command evidence.","year":1966},{"id":"C2861","text":"The September 1966 self-criticism says that after the September 30 Movement’s defeat the Party leadership adopted “Right opportunism” by entrusting the fate of the Party and revolutionary movement to President Sukarno’s policy.","period":"After 1965-10-01; PKI leadership","type":"PKI Political Bureau’s retrospective self-criticism","source_ids":["S1505"],"confidence":"High that the text makes this diagnosis; it is not an independently tested explanation of all PKI decisions or Sukarno’s actual capacity.","basis":"Text printed in the Delegation’s 1971 compilation, original 1966 text not directly inspected.","next":"Compare Sukarno–Aidit correspondence, Politburo instructions, public appeals, military orders and surviving regional reports.","year":1965},{"id":"C2862","text":"The September 1966 self-criticism interprets the PKI’s earlier goal of achieving people’s democracy through parliamentary elections/peaceful means as a Rightist deviation, and its later political programme calls for armed peasant struggle and rural revolutionary bases.","period":"1951–1966 retrospective doctrinal review; Indonesia","type":"PKI post-defeat party line and political self-interpretation","source_ids":["S1505"],"confidence":"High that the compilation’s texts present this diagnosis and future programme; low as proof that pre-1965 practice was simply peaceful or that the new programme was implemented nationwide.","basis":"The compilation is a factional 1971 presentation; doctrinal self-description must be compared with pre-1965 party speeches, organizational practice and regional military evidence.","next":"Compare complete 1954/1959/1962/1965 resolutions, Aidit’s 1960 speech, volunteer training, Biro Chusus records and post-1966 domestic underground evidence.","year":1951},{"id":"C2863","text":"The September 1966 self-criticism itself says the terror prevailing at the time made it difficult to produce a complete criticism and asks members to continue examining the party’s ideological, political and organizational errors.","period":"1966-09; Central Java","type":"Contemporaneous self-description of limits under repression, reprinted later","source_ids":["S1505"],"confidence":"High that the text states this; it flags incompleteness of the document’s own account.","basis":"Text appears in the 1971 compilation; original distribution and readership are not established.","next":"Locate original text copies, discussion notes and evidence of how the claimed criticism was circulated inside Indonesia.","year":1966},{"id":"C2864","text":"Taomo Zhou reports that Beijing-era AAJA channels supported publications and journalists’ work connected with Indonesian exiles, while Hill reports that OISRAA bulletins and Radio Peking’s Indonesian-language service carried anti-New Order material.","period":"1966 onward; Beijing / international network","type":"Scholarly account of foreign media and organizational channels","source_ids":["S1496","S1503"],"confidence":"High that the scholarship reports these channels; moderate for extent, audience and control of specific content until programme logs and budgets are found.","basis":"AAJA, OISRAA, the CC-PKI Delegation and Chinese media are distinct entities; shared publication space does not alone prove unified command.","next":"Find journal editorial files, broadcast scripts/transmission logs, OISRAA bulletins, Chinese media instructions and funding records.","year":1966},{"id":"C2865","text":"World Outlook quotes Le Figaro correspondent J. Jacquet-Francillon reporting that five KIAPMA secretariat staff were arrested on 16 October 1965.","period":"1965-10-16; Jakarta","type":"Contemporary press report quoted by a later outlet","source_ids":["S1498"],"confidence":"High that World Outlook prints this attribution; low-to-moderate for the arrest count and identities until the original Le Figaro report and arrest records are found.","basis":"The original Le Figaro issue was not located; World Outlook is politically aligned and the report does not provide names or custody records.","next":"Retrieve the original Le Figaro dispatch, conference staff list, Indonesian police/military detention records and individual names.","year":1965},{"id":"C2875","text":"Melvin’s 2013 study argues that violence affecting ethnic Chinese in Aceh comprised three distinguishable waves: PKI-linked Chinese targeted with other PKI members; Baperki members targeted for alleged PKI links; and later violence against broader ethnic Chinese/pro-Beijing communities culminating in an expulsion order.","period":"1965-10 to 1966-08; Aceh","type":"Scholarly regional interpretation based on documents and oral histories","source_ids":["S612"],"confidence":"High that the article presents this periodization; moderate for its reconstruction because several underlying military documents and oral accounts are not independently available here.","basis":"This regional typology does not yield a national victim-ethnicity proportion and should not be generalized to every province.","next":"Compare the cited Aceh military chronology, Baperki documents, local government reports and independent survivor/family accounts.","year":1965},{"id":"C2877","text":"An Aceh North District report reproduced by Melvin says that around 18–19 April 1966 students detained 304 people labeled “RRT Chinese” and handed them to the North Aceh military commander; the report gives no known fate for them.","period":"1966-04-18 to 1966-04-19; Lhokseumawe, North Aceh","type":"Contemporary district report quoted by a later scholar; fate unresolved","source_ids":["S612"],"confidence":"High that the article quotes a report with the 304 figure and says their fate is unknown; low for a final unique-person count or subsequent outcome.","basis":"RRT Chinese is the source’s administrative label; the 304 are detainees, not a verified death count. The underlying district report and names are not in hand.","next":"Retrieve the April 1966 Perkembangan Di Bidang Politik/Keamanan report and list; trace custody transfer, later release, death, flight and family accounts.","year":1966},{"id":"C2878","text":"Melvin reports that Aceh Military Commander Ishak Djuarsa’s 8 May 1966 order required “alien Chinese” to leave Aceh; the article cites earlier scholarship for roughly 10,000 people fleeing by August 17.","period":"1966-05-08 to 1966-08-17; Aceh / North Sumatra","type":"Administrative-order and displacement reports in later scholarship","source_ids":["S612"],"confidence":"High that Melvin reports the order and attributed approximate displacement; moderate for exact wording and count pending the original directive and departure records.","basis":"Expulsion/displacement is not a death count. The 10,000 figure is not a list of unique refugees and may exclude people who stayed or departed by other routes.","next":"Find Djuarsa’s signed directive, checkpoint/pass lists, convoy records, port/ship manifests, refugee registration and family/community lists.","year":1966},{"id":"C2879","text":"Chandra’s demographic study uses available census data to map population impacts in East Java and local political context, while explicitly saying it cannot apportion agency or responsibility among perpetrators.","period":"1965–1966; East Java","type":"Peer-reviewed demographic analysis, abstract-level verification","source_ids":["S042"],"confidence":"High for the publisher’s abstract; lower for methods and detailed geographic findings until the full article is read.","basis":"Population change is not a named death list, ethnicity dataset or direct attribution of responsibility.","next":"Read the complete article, tables and methods; compare 1961/1971 census, migration and district event records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2880","text":"Pauker’s 1969 RAND report says Rewang was killed during the 1968 South Blitar campaign; Hearman’s interview-based biography and 1990–91 TAPOL and Amnesty reports identify the same leader as arrested in 1968, tried and imprisoned, then released on 24 July 1991. The report’s fate statement is wrong.","period":"1968-07 to 1991-07; South Blitar, Semarang/Jakarta","type":"Contemporaneous analytical report contradicted by participant and near-contemporary human-rights reporting","source_ids":["S1358","S1310","S1508","S1509","S1510"],"confidence":"High that Pauker printed the erroneous fate and that Rewang survived imprisonment to 1991; exact capture day remains disputed.","basis":"Pauker printed p.62 says Rewang was killed. Hearman (2010) reports an interview with Rewang on 14 Feb 2009 and a 1971 life sentence; TAPOL 1990 records trial/appeal/commutation and continued detention; TAPOL 1991 and Amnesty confirm the 1991 release. No original trial judgment or prison register has yet been obtained. This establishes a specific report error, not that every RAND estimate is false.","next":"Obtain Rewang’s Semarang trial docket and judgment, 1975 appeal, 1985 commutation, Cipinang intake/release registers, family correspondence and the Brawijaya capture log; resolve the 13/14/20/21 July date variants.","year":1968},{"id":"C2881","text":"The South Blitar “2,000” figure does not have one stable category: Pauker says about 2,000 underground PKI members/leaders were killed; Hearman 2010/2018 and the derivative 1969 press account describe captured-or-killed/arrested totals; Hearman 2017 states 2,000 killed plus thousands detained/displaced. The original NYT reports and any person-level roster remain unavailable.","period":"1968-04 to 1968-09; reports published 1968–2018","type":"Conflicting casualty categories and dependent newspaper citation chain","source_ids":["S1358","S1310","S1394","S1396","S1397","S1425","S1507"],"confidence":"High for the differences in wording across the sources read; low for a reconciled number or death count.","basis":"Pauker p.62 cites NYT July 12 and October 29, 1968 but supplies no method. Hearman 2017 cites NYT 11 Aug 1968 for “2,000 killed”; her other accounts use captured/killed categories. Evans (3 Mar 1969) says the October Times report gave 2,000 killed or captured and a mid-February Times follow-up cut the figure in half. The same author’s later accounts and the derivative reports are not independent counts. The 2010 footnote’s 13 Feb 1968 year is likely anomalous, not yet corrected.","next":"Acquire the NYT July 12, Aug 11, Oct 29 1968 and mid-February follow-up through TimesMachine/ProQuest/microfilm; inspect army briefings and Trisula casualty, detention, court, displacement and village records; tabulate each person and outcome once.","year":1968},{"id":"C2882","text":"Hearman’s 2017 account reports that Trisula deployed about 5,000 soldiers from six battalions and 3,000 militiamen across three operation zones in an area of roughly 3,000 km²; it describes army caretaker administration, population registration, village evacuation and screening, crop removal, forced village patrols, burial of the dead and detained villagers’ compulsory roadbuilding labour.","period":"1968-05 to 1968-09; South Blitar, East Java","type":"Peer-reviewed historical reconstruction of counterinsurgency scale and civilian control, partly attributed to Army reporting and oral histories","source_ids":["S1394","S1425","S1310"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports these mechanisms and force figures; moderate for exact force totals and individual coercion patterns absent original orders and unit logs.","basis":"The article distinguishes reported TNI numbers, oral histories and later Army narrative. These figures are deployment estimates, not a verified unit muster. Forced work, crop removal, evacuation and detention are distinct from combatant deaths and cannot be folded into casualty totals.","next":"Obtain six-battalion operational orders, muster/payroll and militia rolls, village caretaker and screening registers, evacuation/camp lists, food supply records, patrol tasking, road work records, and family interviews from each operation zone.","year":1968},{"id":"C2883","text":"A U.S. interagency estimate dated 14 March 1968 judged likely Chinese aid to Burmese Communist and ethnic insurgents to be limited and forecast containment over the next year; the published source note records disagreement among participating agencies over the Chinese threat and Burmese Army capacity, with the final judgment described as a compromise under inadequate information.","period":"1968-03-14 to 1969-03; Burma / U.S. intelligence assessment","type":"Contemporaneous U.S. interagency intelligence estimate and editorial account of agency disagreement","source_ids":["S1511","S1312"],"confidence":"High for the estimate’s text and its documented interagency disagreement; lower for the intelligence judgments about actual Chinese transfers and insurgent capacity.","basis":"SNIE 51-68 assesses Chinese support as an intelligence estimate, not a receipt record. It is useful alongside the January 1968 CPB offensive and other Southeast Asian party trajectories, but force categories and source quality are not comparable across countries.","next":"Compare with CPB and PRC records, local party/ethnic armed-group material, UCDP chronology and later scholarship; distinguish Chinese weapons/training, local insurgent forces, ethnic allies and U.S. estimates.","year":1968},{"id":"C2884","text":"Hearman reports Pudji Aswati, a former teacher and Gerwani member, was detained for about ten years before a 1978 trial, sentenced to 15 years without credit for that detention, and released in 1989 while gravely ill.","period":"1967 to 1989; South Blitar and Malang","type":"Retrospective scholarly account of an individual political-prisoner case","source_ids":["S1425","S1394"],"confidence":"High that Hearman reports these facts; moderate for exact sentence computation and dates pending the court and prison file.","basis":"The accessible author-written account is not the judgment, custody log or release order; it does not allow a generalization to all Trisula detainees.","next":"Obtain Pudji Aswati’s arrest/custody chronology, 1978 indictment and judgment, sentence calculation, prison medical file and 1989 release register; seek family and former-prisoner testimony.","year":1967},{"id":"C2885","text":"Hearman reports Gatot Lestario, described as a secretary of the South Blitar base and husband of Pudji Aswati, was sentenced to death and executed in July 1985; the couple’s two children had been cared for by relatives after the parents went underground in 1967.","period":"1967 to 1985; South Blitar, East Java","type":"Retrospective scholarly account of an individual execution and family separation","source_ids":["S1425","S1394"],"confidence":"High that Hearman’s account reports this sequence; moderate until the conviction, execution and family records are obtained.","basis":"The reported role/title is not an independently verified command position; the article is not a trial or execution record. The case indicates long-term punishment of the 1968 network but does not establish a count of executions.","next":"Locate the court judgment, clemency decision, execution order and civil death record; seek prison correspondence and family-care documentation.","year":1967},{"id":"C2886","text":"Chandra’s Central Java study uses aggregate population counts from the 1961, 1971, and 1980 censuses, local election returns from 1955 and 1957, and administrative/geographic data to estimate one-time population changes associated with the 1965–66 violence across 29 regencies and six cities.","period":"1961–1980 data; population change associated with 1965–1966","type":"Published demographic reconstruction and method","source_ids":["S1512"],"confidence":"High that the article uses these inputs and method; not an independent replication of the underlying census tables.","basis":"The author describes the census dates, election data and administrative-boundary adjustments. Each area has only three census observations; the method assumes a smooth population trajectory outside the 1965–66 shock and adjusts pre-1965 growth.","next":"Obtain the cited provincial census volumes and 1955/1957 official returns; reproduce the calculations and boundary adjustments.","year":1961},{"id":"C2887","text":"The study reports three Central Java population-loss clusters: Pekalongan in the northwest, Demak in the northeast, and Surakarta in the southeast. All cities show estimated population declines; rural regencies show both declines and gains. Pekalongan city is estimated to have declined 13 percent and Tegal city 9 percent.","period":"1965–1966; Central Java","type":"Published regional estimate from census-derived population change","source_ids":["S1512"],"confidence":"High that these are the article’s reported findings; the figures are estimated net population changes, not verified deaths.","basis":"The article identifies clusters from its model and notes that the urban/rural pattern may reflect both violence and movement. The ResearchGate text extraction did not render the complete table image for independent row-by-row recalculation.","next":"Inspect the article’s table and underlying census tables; compare the estimates with district-level burial, detention, missing-person and refugee records.","year":1965},{"id":"C2888","text":"Chandra interprets the regional pattern as reflecting different combinations of local political opposition and Army deployment: the PNI is assigned a prominent role in the Pekalongan cluster, NU in the Demak cluster, and the RPKAD a comparatively larger direct role in the Surakarta cluster, including PKI strongholds near its forward headquarters.","period":"1965–1966; Central Java","type":"Author’s political-geographic interpretation","source_ids":["S1512"],"confidence":"High that the article makes this interpretation; moderate-to-low that census/election correlations alone establish the actual agency or relative responsibility in each locality.","basis":"The argument compares 1957 vote shares, estimated population changes, prior local studies and reported RPKAD deployments. The author explicitly cautions that associations and the simplified aliran model cannot establish causality; party support and religious-cultural identification were not identical.","next":"Compare original RPKAD movement and operation orders, local Army logs, PNI/NU records, detention lists, trial records and village testimony for each cluster.","year":1965},{"id":"C2889","text":"The Central Java census reconstruction does not supply a national or regional percentage distribution of victims by ethnicity. Its data are population totals by administrative area plus election/geographic variables, not individual victim identity records; population gaps can also reflect refuge-seeking and other movement.","period":"1965–1966; Central Java and national victim estimates","type":"Data-scope finding and limit on ethnic-percentage inference","source_ids":["S1512","S231","S1322"],"confidence":"High that these sources do not provide a victim-level ethnic distribution; no reliable national victim-level ethnic breakdown was located in this search.","basis":"Chandra’s method is ecological and aggregate. Woolgar’s West Java study and Cribb/Coppel address estimates and regional variation in anti-Chinese targeting, but their rounded estimates do not create a national named-victim dataset or an audited ethnic percentage.","next":"Seek victim-level records with identity fields from Komnas HAM, YPKP, local cemeteries, detention facilities, civil registration and community archives; assess each region separately and state missingness.","year":1965},{"id":"C2890","text":"Chandra reports that between the 1955 national and 1957 local elections in Central Java, PKI votes increased by 800,340 while PNI votes fell by 335,944; he calculates a district-level correlation of about −0.53 between changes in their vote shares.","period":"1955–1957; Central Java","type":"Aggregate electoral change reported by a secondary study","source_ids":["S1512"],"confidence":"High that the paper reports these values; underlying official election returns and the causal interpretation were not independently checked.","basis":"The paper cites Hindley’s 1966 table for vote totals and presents the cross-area relationship as evidence of rapid PKI gains and PNI losses. Aggregate change cannot show that specific individuals switched parties or prove that electoral competition caused later violence.","next":"Inspect Hindley’s cited table and official election returns; compare local party organization records and contemporary PNI/PKI correspondence.","year":1955},{"id":"C2891","text":"The 30 April 1921 Soeara Ra'jat no.8 pp.5–6 has a separate appeal for founding/funding a Sarekat Islam school, ending ‘A.n. Bestuur S.I. Semarang’ and signed SEMAOEN as Voorzitter (chairman) and BOEDISOETJITRO as Secretaris (secretary). These signatures belong to the school appeal, not the preceding party-discipline essay.","period":"1921-04-30","type":"Contemporary SI Semarang school appeal and officers' signatures","source_ids":["S891"],"confidence":"High for original printed signatures and school appeal; actual donations, class start date and school financing not independently proven.","basis":"S891 original scan p.5 lower column to p.6, separated from the earlier article ending on p.5. Signatory offices are at the time and local branch only; not evidence for SI national executive office.","next":"Check original donation/payment ledger, 1921 June school intake/class register, and subsequent newspaper receipts.","year":1921},{"id":"C2892","text":"The Indonesian Ministry of Culture's historical encyclopedia summarizes the 29 November 1946 SOBSI founding conference as selecting Harjono as chair, Setiadjit as deputy chair, and Njono/Nyono as general secretary; it cites later labor histories and an ANRI inventory, not the original conference ballot or signed attendee roll.","period":"1946-11-29","type":"Government-hosted retrospective report of SOBSI founding officer slate","source_ids":["S1536"],"confidence":"High that the Ministry's webpage presents this slate; moderate for exact historical offices until 1946 original conference record is examined.","basis":"S1536 website section on 29 November 1946 Yogyakarta federation conference and early officers; the article refers to Lestari, Glassbuner, and ANRI SOBSI inventory 151, not direct 1946 signatures. Names, exact terms and multiple sources need cross-verification.","next":"Obtain original 29 Nov 1946 SOBSI conference minutes, named delegates, union affiliation/dues and contemporary newspapers; compare Tedjasukmana 1958 and the Nyono entry.","year":1946},{"id":"C2893","text":"Hearman's peer-reviewed trade-union study reports that SOBSI held its first congress in Malang, East Java, in 1947 and voted to affiliate with the World Federation of Trade Unions (WFTU), initially to seek international labor support for Indonesia's struggle for independence against the Dutch.","period":"1947; Malang","type":"Retrospective scholarly reconstruction of early SOBSI congress and international affiliation","source_ids":["S845"],"confidence":"High for the scholar's published account; congress voting record, individual delegates and initial intentions have not been independently audited.","basis":"Vannessa Hearman, International Review of Social History (2024), section on post-1945 labor and WFTU; reports initial independence rationale from earlier labor scholarship. Not a first-hand congress minute.","next":"Find the 1947 Malang SOBSI original congress resolution, voting records and WFTU correspondence, and compare post-1949 international affiliations.","year":1947},{"id":"C2894","text":"Hearman's labor-history article describes the formation of rival SOBRI, aligned with the Murba Party, in 1951, and Sarbumusi, aligned with Nahdlatul Ulama, in 1955; these were distinct union organizations and not SOBSI branch renamings.","period":"1951–1955","type":"Retrospective scholarly account of rival independent-era labor federations","source_ids":["S845"],"confidence":"High for the published chronology; actual local chapters and precise patronage differ by region and require union charters.","basis":"Hearman 2024, section on proliferation of national trade-union federations after sovereignty transfer; names, links and founding years given as scholar's synthesis.","next":"Locate 1951 SOBRI and 1955 Sarbumusi constitutive statutes, regional membership and enterprise elections; do not infer every worker's party affiliation.","year":1951},{"id":"C2895","text":"Hearman cites Indonesian Ministry of Labour figures that more than 1,500 national, regional and local trade unions existed in 1955 and about half had no affiliation to a national federation; these are ministry figures cited secondhand, not verified individual union registers.","period":"1955","type":"Secondary citation of a historical government trade-union count","source_ids":["S845"],"confidence":"High that scholarly study prints the figures, moderate for unduplicated union count and exact organizational scope without Ministry source.","basis":"Hearman 2024 text cites the original Ministry of Labour study; classification and counting by local/national entities may duplicate nested branches.","next":"Find original 1955 Indonesian Ministry of Labour union-registration tables and check double counting, local registered membership, inactive unions and organizational autonomy.","year":1955}]);
